/*
 * Pearson Edexcel GCSE Geography B (1GB0) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Real Pearson Edexcel GCSE Geography B (1GB0) questions are
 * built on resources (described figures, tables, maps and photos) and named case
 * studies, with fieldwork questions on sampling, presentation and evaluation.
 * Every item here opens with a fully described resource or a located context.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Part tariffs follow the real mix taken from the site's own past-paper
 * data for this specification.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Board style: point-marked items one point per line; extended
 * items give indicative content and the board's level ladder, including SPaG
 * where the board awards it.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const GEOGRAPHY_EDEXCEL_GCSE_PRACTICE = {
  '1.1': {
    name: 'Global Circulation and Climate Change',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 1: a simplified cross-section of the global atmospheric circulation in the northern hemisphere. Air rises at the equator, moves north high in the atmosphere and sinks at about 30°N before returning to the equator along the surface.\n\nWhich one of the following describes the surface conditions at about 30°N?\nA  Low pressure and rising air\nB  High pressure and sinking air\nC  Low pressure and sinking air\nD  High pressure and rising air`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (high pressure and sinking air) (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 2: a world map of climate zones. One zone forms a belt between about 15° and 30° north and south of the equator, crossing the Sahara, the Arabian Peninsula and central Australia. It is shaded to show annual rainfall of less than 250 mm.\n\nIdentify the climate zone shown by this belt.\nA  Tropical rainforest\nB  Temperate\nC  Hot desert (arid)\nD  Polar`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (hot desert / arid) (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 3: climate data for a weather station at 25°N in the Sahara (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C), January to December: 14, 16, 20, 25, 30, 33, 34, 34, 31, 26, 20, 15.\nMean monthly rainfall (mm), January to December: 2, 1, 3, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3.\n\nIdentify the coldest month shown in Figure 3.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `January (14 °C) (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 1 shows the three-cell model of the global atmospheric circulation. In the northern hemisphere, one cell lies between about 30°N and 60°N; at the surface, air in this cell moves towards the pole and is turned by the Earth's rotation to form westerly winds.\n\nName the cell found between 30°N and 60°N.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Ferrel cell (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nScientists use several sources of evidence to reconstruct climate during the Little Ice Age (about 1300 to 1850).\n\nWhich one of the following is an example of historical evidence of past climate?\nA  An ice core drilled in Antarctica\nB  A painting of a frost fair held on the frozen River Thames in 1684\nC  The growth rings of a bristlecone pine tree\nD  Pollen grains found in lake sediments`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (painting of a Thames frost fair) (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nDuring the Quaternary period (the last 2.6 million years) the Earth's climate has switched many times between cold glacial periods and warmer periods. We are living in one of the warmer periods now, which began about 11,700 years ago.\n\nDefine the term interglacial.`, marks: 1, command: 'Define', markScheme: `A warmer period between two glacial periods, when ice sheets retreat (1).\nAccept: a warm phase within an ice age when temperatures are similar to today's.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 4: annual mean concentration of carbon dioxide (CO₂) in the atmosphere measured at Mauna Loa, Hawaii (data written for this practice question, close to measured values).\n2012: 393.9 ppm\n2013: 396.7 ppm\n2014: 398.8 ppm\n2015: 400.8 ppm\n2016: 404.2 ppm\n\nIdentify the first year in Figure 4 in which the annual mean CO₂ concentration was above 400 ppm.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `2015 (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 5: a photograph of flooded rice paddies in the Ganges delta, Bangladesh. Bacteria in the waterlogged soil break down plant material without oxygen.\n\nWhich one of the following greenhouse gases is released in large amounts by rice paddies?\nA  Methane\nB  Oxygen\nC  Nitrogen\nD  Water vapour`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `A (methane) (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 3: climate data for a weather station at 25°N in the Sahara (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C), January to December: 14, 16, 20, 25, 30, 33, 34, 34, 31, 26, 20, 15.\n\nCalculate the annual temperature range shown in Figure 3. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `34 − 14 (1)\n= 20 °C (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with or without working.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 4: annual mean atmospheric CO₂ concentration at Mauna Loa, Hawaii (data written for this practice question): 1960 — 316.9 ppm; 2020 — 413.2 ppm.\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) increase in CO₂ concentration between 1960 and 2020. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(413.2 − 316.9) ÷ 316.9 × 100 or 96.3 ÷ 316.9 × 100 (1)\n= 30.4% (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with or without working.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 1: a cross-section of the Hadley cell. At the equator the Sun's rays strike the surface almost vertically all year, and a band of tall cumulonimbus clouds is shown above the Intertropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ).\n\nExplain one reason why air rises at the equator.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development.\n• The Sun's energy is concentrated on a small area of the surface at the equator (1), so the ground heats the air above it, which becomes less dense and rises (1).\n• Trade winds from both hemispheres meet / converge at the ITCZ (1), so air is forced upwards, creating low pressure (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe Atacama Desert in Chile and the Sahara in North Africa both lie close to latitude 25°.\n\nExplain one reason why many of the world's hot deserts are found at about 30° north and south of the equator.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development.\n• Air in the Hadley cell sinks at about 30° (1), so it warms and becomes drier, meaning clouds cannot form and there is little rain (1).\n• Sinking air creates a belt of high pressure (1), so skies are clear and rainfall is very low (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 1: a world map of surface winds. Arrows show air moving from the subtropical high-pressure belt at about 30°N towards the equator; the arrows bend so that they come from the north-east.\n\nDescribe the movement of the trade winds in the northern hemisphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per descriptive point, max 2.\n• They blow from high pressure at about 30°N towards low pressure at the equator (1).\n• They blow from the north-east / are north-easterly winds (1).\n• They are deflected / curved by the Earth's rotation (Coriolis effect) (1).\n• They are surface winds that form the lower part of the Hadley cell (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 6: a map of the North Atlantic Ocean. A warm current, the Gulf Stream, flows north-east from the Gulf of Mexico and continues as the North Atlantic Drift towards the west coast of the UK. Winter temperatures in Plymouth, UK (50°N) are shown as about 8 °C higher than in places at the same latitude in eastern Canada.\n\nExplain one way ocean currents affect the climate of the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development.\n• The North Atlantic Drift carries warm water from the tropics towards the UK (1), so winters are milder than in other places at the same latitude (1).\n• Prevailing south-westerly winds blow over the warm current (1), bringing warm, moist air that gives the UK mild, wet weather (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 7: a photograph of a scientist holding an ice core drilled from the Antarctic ice sheet at Vostok. Small bubbles of air can be seen trapped in the ice.\n\nExplain one way ice cores provide evidence of past climate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development.\n• Air bubbles trapped in the ice can be analysed for CO₂ (1); higher CO₂ concentrations match warmer periods (1).\n• The ratio of oxygen isotopes in the ice can be measured (1), which shows the temperature when the snow fell (1).\n• Each year's snowfall forms a layer (1), so layers can be counted to date climate changes over hundreds of thousands of years (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 8: a cross-section of a tree trunk from a forest in northern Sweden, showing growth rings of different widths.\n\nExplain one way tree rings can be used as evidence of past climate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development.\n• A tree grows one ring each year (1), so rings can be counted to date the climate of each year (1).\n• Wider rings show faster growth (1), which suggests warmer / wetter growing seasons; narrow rings suggest cold or dry years (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe diary of the London writer John Evelyn describes the River Thames frozen solid in January 1684, with shops, coaches and a bull-baiting held on the ice.\n\nExplain one limitation of using historical sources such as diaries as evidence of past climate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a limitation and 1 mark for development.\n• They describe only one place (1), so they may not show what happened to the climate at a global scale (1).\n• They may be exaggerated or subjective (1), because the writer was not using instruments to record measurements (1).\n• Records only go back a few hundred years (1), so they cannot show long-term changes such as glacial periods (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nBetween 1645 and 1715, astronomers recorded very few sunspots on the Sun's surface. This period, the Maunder Minimum, came during one of the coldest parts of the Little Ice Age in Europe.\n\nExplain one way changes in solar output can affect the Earth's climate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development.\n• Fewer sunspots mean the Sun gives out slightly less energy (1), so less heat reaches the Earth and temperatures fall (1).\n• More sunspots / higher solar output means more energy reaches the Earth (1), which can cause warmer periods such as the Medieval Warm Period (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nIn June 1991 Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines erupted, sending about 15 million tonnes of sulphur dioxide into the stratosphere. Global average temperatures fell by about 0.5 °C over the following year.\n\nExplain one way large volcanic eruptions can cause global temperatures to fall.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development.\n• Sulphur dioxide forms tiny droplets of sulphuric acid / aerosols in the stratosphere (1), which reflect incoming sunlight back into space so less reaches the surface (1).\n• Ash and gas are spread around the world by high-level winds (1), so the cooling effect is global and can last one to two years (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 9: sea level measured by the tide gauge at Newlyn, Cornwall (data written for this practice question). Between 1916 and 2016, mean sea level rose by 180 mm.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of sea-level rise at Newlyn, in millimetres per year. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `180 ÷ 100 (years) (1)\n= 1.8 mm per year (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with or without working.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 10: a photograph of a dairy farm in Cheshire, UK, showing a herd of cows and a tractor spreading nitrogen fertiliser on a field.\n\nExplain one way farming increases the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development.\n• Cattle digest grass in their stomachs (1), releasing methane, a powerful greenhouse gas (1).\n• Nitrogen fertilisers break down in the soil (1), releasing nitrous oxide into the atmosphere (1).\n• Machinery burns diesel (1), which releases carbon dioxide (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nChina's annual carbon dioxide emissions rose rapidly after 2000 and by 2006 China had become the world's largest emitter.\n\nExplain one reason why global emissions of carbon dioxide have increased since 1850.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development.\n• Industrialisation, first in Europe and North America and later in emerging countries such as China (1), has increased the burning of coal, oil and gas in factories and power stations (1).\n• Rising population and wealth (1) has increased demand for energy, cars and air travel, which burn fossil fuels (1).\n• Deforestation, for example in the Amazon (1), removes trees that absorb CO₂ and releases carbon when they are burned (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 4 shows annual mean CO₂ concentrations at Mauna Loa, Hawaii, for every year from 1960 to 2020, presented in a table.\n\nDescribe one other suitable method of data presentation to show these CO₂ concentrations.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a method and 1 mark for description / justification.\n• A line graph (1), with year on the x-axis and CO₂ concentration (ppm) on the y-axis, so the trend over time is easy to see (1).\n• A bar chart (1), with one bar per year / decade, so values can be compared (1).\nDo not credit pie chart, scatter graph without a time axis, or choropleth map.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nIn July 2022 a temperature of 40.3 °C was recorded at Coningsby, Lincolnshire — the first time 40 °C had been reached in the UK. Climate projections suggest heatwaves like this will become more frequent.\n\nExplain one way projected climate change may affect people in the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for an impact and 1 mark for development.\n• More frequent heatwaves (1) increase deaths from heat stroke, especially among elderly people in cities (1).\n• Wetter winters (1) increase the risk of flooding to homes and businesses, raising insurance costs (1).\n• Drier summers (1) could lead to water shortages and hosepipe bans, especially in south-east England (1).\n• Rising sea levels (1) threaten coastal settlements and farmland, for example in East Anglia (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) gives a range of projections for global temperature in 2100 rather than a single figure.\n\nExplain one reason why projections of future global temperature are uncertain.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development.\n• We do not know how much greenhouse gas will be emitted in future (1), because this depends on population growth, economic growth and government policies (1).\n• Natural processes are not fully understood (1), for example feedbacks such as melting permafrost releasing methane could speed up warming (1).\n• Climate models are simplifications of a complex system (1), so different models give different results (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 11: a photograph of bleached, white coral on the Great Barrier Reef, Australia, taken during a marine heatwave in 2016.\n\nExplain one way climate change may affect ecosystems.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for an impact and 1 mark for development.\n• Warmer seas cause coral to expel the algae living in it (1), so coral bleaches and may die, reducing biodiversity on the reef (1).\n• Arctic sea ice is shrinking (1), so species such as polar bears lose their hunting habitat (1).\n• Climate zones shift towards the poles (1), so species that cannot move or adapt quickly enough may become extinct (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 12: CO₂ concentration of air bubbles in the Vostok ice core, Antarctica (data written for this practice question). At the peak of the last glacial period: 180 ppm. During the last interglacial: 280 ppm.\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) increase in CO₂ concentration from the glacial peak to the interglacial. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(280 − 180) ÷ 180 × 100 or 100 ÷ 180 × 100 (1)\n= 55.6% (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with or without working.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 13: global mean surface temperature compared with the 1850–1900 average (data written for this practice question).\n1880: 0.0 °C\n1910: −0.1 °C\n1940: +0.2 °C\n1970: +0.3 °C\n1990: +0.6 °C\n2010: +0.9 °C\n2020: +1.2 °C\n\nDescribe the trend shown in Figure 13. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per descriptive point; 1 mark reserved for use of data, max 3.\n• Overall, global temperature has increased (1), by 1.2 °C between 1880 and 2020 (1 for data).\n• There was a small fall at first, to −0.1 °C in 1910 (1).\n• The rate of warming has increased since 1970 (1), rising 0.9 °C between 1970 and 2020 compared with 0.3 °C between 1880 and 1970 (1 for data).\nMax 2 without data.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nLondon (51°N) has a mean January temperature of about 5 °C, while Winnipeg in central Canada (50°N) has a mean January temperature of about −16 °C.\n\nExplain one reason why the UK has a milder winter climate than many places at the same latitude.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, plus up to 2 marks for development.\n• The UK is affected by the North Atlantic Drift (1), a warm ocean current that brings water from the Gulf of Mexico (1), which warms the prevailing south-westerly winds blowing onto the UK in winter (1).\n• The UK is surrounded by sea (1), which cools down more slowly than land in winter (1), so it releases heat and keeps coastal temperatures higher than places in the middle of a continent such as Winnipeg (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nIn the 1920s the scientist Milutin Milanković calculated that regular changes in the Earth's orbit and tilt could explain the timing of glacial and interglacial periods during the Quaternary.\n\nExplain one way changes in the Earth's orbit can cause a glacial period.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, plus up to 2 marks for development.\n• Eccentricity: the Earth's orbit changes from almost circular to more elliptical over about 100,000 years (1); this changes the amount of solar energy the Earth receives at different times of year (1), so when summers in the northern hemisphere receive less energy, snow does not melt and ice sheets build up (1).\n• Axial tilt changes between about 22° and 24.5° over about 41,000 years (1); a smaller tilt gives cooler summers at high latitudes (1), so winter snow survives the summer and ice sheets grow (1).\n• Precession (wobble) changes the time of year the Earth is closest to the Sun over about 26,000 years (1), which changes the strength of the seasons (1) and can lead to cooler summers and glacial advance (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 4: an incomplete line graph of annual mean CO₂ concentration at Mauna Loa, Hawaii. The x-axis shows years from 1960 to 2020 in steps of 10 years; the y-axis shows CO₂ from 300 ppm to 425 ppm in steps of 25 ppm. Points have been plotted and joined for 1960 to 2000. Two values have not been plotted: 2010 — 389.9 ppm; 2020 — 414.2 ppm (data written for this practice question).\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 4 by plotting the values for 2010 and 2020.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per descriptive point, max 3.\n• Find 2010 on the x-axis and move up to just under 390 ppm (between 375 and 400, slightly closer to 400) and mark a point / cross (1).\n• Find 2020 on the x-axis and move up to just above 400 ppm, a little over halfway to 425 ppm (414.2), and mark a point (1).\n• Join the 2000 point to the 2010 point and the 2010 point to the 2020 point with straight lines, in the same style as the rest of the graph (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe IPCC projects that global mean sea level will rise by between about 0.3 m and 1.0 m by 2100, depending on future greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nExplain one reason why sea level is projected to rise.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, plus up to 2 marks for development.\n• Thermal expansion (1): as the oceans absorb heat from a warming atmosphere, the water expands (1), so its volume increases and sea level rises even without extra water (1).\n• Melting land ice (1): glaciers and the Greenland and Antarctic ice sheets are melting faster (1), adding water that was stored on land to the oceans (1).\nDo not credit melting sea ice alone without explanation (floating ice does not raise sea level).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 2: a world map of climate zones. Tropical rainforest lies close to the equator, hot deserts at about 15°–30°, temperate climates at about 40°–60° and polar climates beyond about 66°.\n\nExplain two ways the global atmospheric circulation leads to the different climate zones shown in Figure 2.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way and 1 further mark for development of each, 2 × (1 + 1).\n• Rising air at the equator creates low pressure (1), so warm, moist air cools and condenses, giving heavy rainfall all year and tropical rainforest (1).\n• Sinking air at about 30° creates high pressure (1), so there are few clouds and very little rain, giving hot deserts (1).\n• Rising air at about 60° where warm and cold air meet at the polar front (1) causes frequent depressions and rain, giving temperate climates such as the UK (1).\n• Cold air sinks at the poles creating high pressure (1), so polar areas are cold and dry (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nSingapore (1°N) receives about 2,300 mm of rainfall a year, spread across every month.\n\nExplain two reasons why places near the equator receive high rainfall.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• Intense heating from the overhead Sun (1) makes air rise rapidly by convection, so it cools, water vapour condenses and heavy convectional rain falls, often in afternoon thunderstorms (1).\n• The trade winds converge at the ITCZ (1), forcing moist air upwards and creating low pressure and clouds (1).\n• High temperatures cause high rates of evaporation from oceans and transpiration from rainforest (1), so the air holds a lot of moisture that falls as rain (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe global atmospheric circulation is often shown as a three-cell model for each hemisphere.\n\nDraw an annotated diagram to show the three-cell model of the global atmospheric circulation in the northern hemisphere.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `1 mark per correct, labelled feature, max 4.\n• Hadley cell between 0° and 30°N with air rising at the equator and sinking at 30°N (1).\n• Ferrel cell between 30°N and 60°N with surface air moving poleward (1).\n• Polar cell between 60°N and 90°N with air sinking at the pole (1).\n• Pressure labelled correctly: low at 0° and 60°, high at 30° and 90° (1).\n• Surface winds labelled: north-east trade winds, westerlies, polar easterlies (1).\n• Annotation linking pressure to climate, e.g. low pressure at equator = heavy rain; high pressure at 30° = deserts (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nDuring the Quaternary period the Earth has experienced more than 50 glacial and interglacial cycles. The most recent glacial maximum was about 20,000 years ago, when ice covered most of Scotland and northern England.\n\nExplain two types of evidence that show the climate has changed naturally during the Quaternary.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• Ice cores from Antarctica and Greenland (1) contain trapped air and oxygen isotopes that record temperature and CO₂ for over 800,000 years (1).\n• Tree rings (1) show the conditions in each year by ring width, going back several thousand years (1).\n• Historical sources such as diaries, paintings and harvest records (1) describe events like frozen rivers during the Little Ice Age (1).\n• Landforms / sediments left by glaciers, e.g. U-shaped valleys and moraines in the Lake District (1), show that ice covered areas that are now ice-free (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe eruption of Mount Tambora, Indonesia, in 1815 was followed in Europe by the "year without a summer" in 1816. Around the same time, sunspot numbers were unusually low (the Dalton Minimum, about 1790–1830).\n\nExplain two natural causes of climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• Volcanic eruptions (1): sulphur dioxide and ash in the stratosphere reflect sunlight, cooling the Earth for one to two years, as after Tambora (1).\n• Changes in solar output (1): fewer sunspots mean less energy from the Sun, linked to cold periods such as the Maunder Minimum during the Little Ice Age (1).\n• Orbital changes / Milankovitch cycles (1): changes in eccentricity, tilt and precession alter how much solar energy the Earth receives, causing glacial and interglacial periods over thousands of years (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 14: a diagram of the greenhouse effect. Short-wave radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere and heats the Earth's surface. The surface gives off long-wave (infrared) radiation, some of which is absorbed by greenhouse gases and re-radiated back towards the surface. A second panel shows the same diagram with a thicker layer of greenhouse gases labelled "after 1850".\n\nExplain two differences between the natural greenhouse effect and the enhanced greenhouse effect.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• The natural greenhouse effect is caused by naturally occurring gases (1), whereas the enhanced effect is caused by extra gases released by human activity such as burning fossil fuels (1).\n• The natural effect keeps the Earth about 33 °C warmer than it would otherwise be (about 15 °C instead of −18 °C), making life possible (1), whereas the enhanced effect traps more long-wave radiation, causing global warming beyond natural levels (1).\n• The natural effect has existed for millions of years (1), while the enhanced effect has developed since the Industrial Revolution, about 1850 (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 15: share of global greenhouse gas emissions by sector (data written for this practice question, based on published estimates).\nEnergy used in industry: 24%\nAgriculture, forestry and land use: 18%\nEnergy used in buildings: 18%\nTransport: 16%\nOther energy uses (e.g. fuel extraction): 15%\nIndustrial processes and waste: 9%\n\nUsing Figure 15, explain two ways human activities increase greenhouse gas emissions.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1); the way should be linked to Figure 15.\n• Energy used in industry is the largest sector at 24% (1), because factories burn coal, oil and gas for heat and power, releasing CO₂ (1).\n• Agriculture, forestry and land use is 18% (1): cattle and rice paddies release methane, and clearing forest releases stored carbon and removes a carbon sink (1).\n• Transport is 16% (1): cars, lorries and aircraft burn petrol, diesel and jet fuel, releasing CO₂ (1).\n• Energy used in buildings is 18% (1): electricity for heating, lighting and air conditioning is often generated by burning fossil fuels (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nBangladesh is a lower-income country in South Asia. Much of the country is a low-lying delta formed by the Ganges and Brahmaputra rivers, and more than 160 million people live there.\n\nExplain two projected impacts of climate change on people living in Bangladesh.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• Rising sea levels (1) will flood low-lying coastal land, forcing millions of people to migrate to cities such as Dhaka (1).\n• Salt water moving into soils and rivers (1) will reduce rice harvests and contaminate drinking water (1).\n• More intense tropical cyclones and storm surges (1) will increase deaths and damage to homes and infrastructure (1).\n• Heavier monsoon rainfall / more glacial melt in the Himalayas (1) will increase river flooding (1).\n• Higher temperatures (1) may increase the spread of diseases such as dengue fever (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 16: IPCC projections of global warming by 2081–2100, compared with 1850–1900, for different emissions scenarios. Very low emissions scenario: 1.0 °C to 1.8 °C. Intermediate scenario: 2.1 °C to 3.5 °C. Very high emissions scenario: 3.3 °C to 5.7 °C.\n\nUsing Figure 16, explain two reasons why there is uncertainty about future climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• We do not know which emissions scenario will happen (1); Figure 16 shows warming could be as little as 1.0 °C or as much as 5.7 °C depending on how much countries cut emissions (1).\n• Future population and economic growth are unknown (1), and these affect demand for fossil fuel energy (1).\n• Even within one scenario there is a range, e.g. 3.3 °C to 5.7 °C (1), because scientists do not fully understand feedbacks such as melting ice reducing albedo or permafrost releasing methane (1).\n• Natural factors such as volcanic eruptions or changes in solar output cannot be predicted (1), so they could temporarily cool or warm the climate (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 17: global mean surface temperature compared with a long-term average (data written for this practice question).\n1990: +0.45 °C\n1991: +0.40 °C\n1992: −0.05 °C\n1993: +0.10 °C\n1994: +0.30 °C\nMount Pinatubo, Philippines, erupted in June 1991.\n\nSuggest two reasons for the changes in global temperature shown in Figure 17.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• Pinatubo released sulphur dioxide into the stratosphere (1), forming aerosols that reflected sunlight, so temperatures fell by 0.45 °C between 1991 and 1992 (1).\n• High-level winds spread the aerosol cloud around the globe (1), so the cooling affected global rather than local temperature (1).\n• The aerosols gradually fell out of the atmosphere over one to two years (1), so temperatures recovered to +0.30 °C by 1994 (1).\n• Greenhouse gas concentrations kept rising during this period (1), so once the volcanic effect ended the warming trend continued (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe Met Office's UK climate projections suggest that by 2070 UK summers could be up to 5.4 °C warmer and up to 47% drier, and winters up to 4.2 °C warmer and up to 35% wetter, under a high emissions scenario.\n\nExplain two ways climate change may affect the natural environment in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• Hotter, drier summers (1) could cause droughts, low river flows and more frequent wildfires on moorland (1).\n• Species may move north or to higher ground (1), so cold-climate species such as mountain plants in the Cairngorms could disappear (1).\n• Wetter winters (1) increase river flooding and soil erosion (1).\n• Rising sea levels and stormier seas (1) increase coastal erosion and flood salt marshes and coastal habitats, e.g. on the Norfolk coast (1).\n• New pests and diseases may survive warmer winters (1), damaging trees and crops (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 18: carbon dioxide emissions per person, 2021 (data written for this practice question, based on published estimates). USA: 14.9 tonnes; UK: 4.7 tonnes; China: 8.0 tonnes; India: 1.9 tonnes; Ethiopia: 0.2 tonnes.\n\nExplain two reasons why CO₂ emissions per person vary between countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• Level of development / wealth (1): people in high-income countries such as the USA own more cars, appliances and take more flights, so use more energy (14.9 t compared with 0.2 t in Ethiopia) (1).\n• Type of energy used (1): countries relying on coal, such as China and India, emit more CO₂ per unit of energy than countries using more renewables or nuclear (1).\n• Economic structure (1): emerging countries with lots of manufacturing industry emit more, whereas the UK's economy is mainly services and it imports many manufactured goods (1).\n• Climate and size of country (1): very hot or cold countries need more energy for heating or air conditioning, and large countries such as the USA have long travel distances (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 6: a world map of major ocean currents. Warm currents such as the Gulf Stream flow away from the equator; cold currents such as the Canary Current and the Humboldt Current flow towards the equator along the west coasts of continents.\n\nExplain two ways ocean currents transfer heat around the world.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 + 1).\n• Warm surface currents such as the Gulf Stream move water away from the equator (1), carrying heat to higher latitudes and warming places like north-west Europe (1).\n• Cold currents such as the Humboldt Current return cold water towards the equator (1), cooling coastal areas and reducing rainfall, e.g. the Atacama Desert (1).\n• Surface currents are driven by prevailing winds (1), so heat transfer is linked to the atmospheric circulation (1).\n• Cold, salty water sinks in the North Atlantic and flows deep along the ocean floor (thermohaline circulation) (1), drawing warm water northwards at the surface to replace it (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 2: a world map of climate zones (tropical rainforest near the equator, hot deserts at about 15°–30°, temperate zones at about 40°–60°, polar zones beyond about 66°). Figure 6: a map of ocean currents showing the North Atlantic Drift reaching the UK and the cold Humboldt Current along the coast of Chile.\n\nAssess the importance of the global atmospheric circulation in creating the world's climate zones.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Atmospheric circulation: rising air at the equator and 60° (low pressure, rain); sinking air at 30° and the poles (high pressure, dry); Hadley, Ferrel and polar cells; surface winds (trade winds, westerlies) move heat and moisture.\n• It explains the broad pattern of zones by latitude: rainforest, desert, temperate, polar.\n• Other factors: latitude and angle of the Sun's rays (more concentrated energy at the equator); ocean currents (North Atlantic Drift warms the UK; Humboldt Current makes the Atacama very dry); distance from the sea (continentality); altitude; mountain barriers (rain shadow).\n• Judgement: the atmospheric circulation is the main reason for the large-scale pattern of zones, but ocean currents and local factors explain variations within zones.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate and relevant knowledge of the circulation and other factors. Applies understanding to produce a balanced, coherent assessment. Judgement on importance fully supported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly relevant and accurate knowledge. Partially balanced; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated, sometimes inaccurate knowledge. Unbalanced or descriptive; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nScientists use ice cores (records over 800,000 years), tree rings (records over several thousand years) and historical sources such as diaries, paintings of Thames frost fairs and harvest dates (records over several hundred years) to reconstruct past climate.\n\nAssess the reliability of different types of evidence for natural climate change.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Ice cores: very long record; direct samples of past air (CO₂) and isotopes for temperature; but mainly from polar regions, dating deep layers is harder as ice is compressed.\n• Tree rings: precise annual dating; but only a few thousand years; ring width affected by factors other than temperature (rainfall, soil, disease); trees grow only in some climates.\n• Historical sources: detailed descriptions of local events (e.g. frost fairs during the Little Ice Age); but short time span, local, subjective, may be exaggerated; frost fairs also linked to the old London Bridge slowing the river.\n• Reliability increases when different sources agree, e.g. evidence for the Little Ice Age from tree rings, records and glaciers.\n• Judgement: ice cores most reliable for long-term change; combining sources gives the most confidence.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate and relevant knowledge of several types of evidence. Balanced, coherent assessment of strengths and limitations. Judgement on reliability supported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; descriptive list of evidence with little assessment; judgement unsupported.\n0: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 13: global mean surface temperature has risen by about 1.2 °C since 1850–1900. Over the same period, atmospheric CO₂ concentration has risen from about 280 ppm to about 420 ppm. Satellite measurements show no upward trend in the Sun's energy output since the 1950s.\n\nAssess the relative importance of natural and human causes of climate change since 1850.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Human causes: burning fossil fuels for energy, industry and transport; deforestation; farming (methane, nitrous oxide); enhanced greenhouse effect; CO₂ up 50% (280 to 420 ppm) matching the temperature rise.\n• Rate of warming has increased since 1970, when emissions grew fastest, including from emerging countries.\n• Natural causes: volcanic eruptions (e.g. Pinatubo 1991) cause short-term cooling of 1–2 years only; solar output shows no upward trend since the 1950s so cannot explain recent warming; orbital changes act over tens of thousands of years, too slowly.\n• Natural factors may explain small variations, e.g. slight cooling to 1910.\n• Judgement: human causes are much more important for warming since 1850, especially since 1950; natural factors explain short-term variability.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate and relevant knowledge of both natural and human causes. Balanced, coherent assessment using the resource. Judgement on relative importance fully supported.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; one-sided; judgement unsupported.\n0: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nOver the last 2.6 million years the Earth's climate has changed from glacial to interglacial periods many times. Shorter changes have also happened, such as the Little Ice Age (about 1300–1850) and the cooling after the eruption of Mount Tambora in 1815.\n\nEvaluate the importance of different natural causes of climate change in the past.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Orbital changes (eccentricity ~100,000 years, tilt ~41,000 years, precession ~26,000 years): explain the timing of glacial and interglacial cycles over the Quaternary; act very slowly but produce the largest changes.\n• Solar output: sunspot cycles and minima such as the Maunder Minimum (1645–1715) linked to the Little Ice Age; changes in energy are small, so effect is moderate.\n• Volcanic eruptions: sulphur aerosols reflect sunlight; Tambora 1815 led to the 1816 "year without a summer"; effects last only one to two years unless eruptions are clustered.\n• Interactions: e.g. low solar output plus several eruptions may together have caused the Little Ice Age.\n• Judgement: orbital changes most important over long timescales; solar and volcanic activity more important for short-term change.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate and relevant knowledge of orbital, solar and volcanic causes. Balanced, coherent evaluation considering timescale and scale of impact. Judgement fully supported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; description of one or two causes; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 19: projected impacts of global warming of 2 °C (information written for this practice question, based on IPCC reports): more than 99% of tropical coral reefs lost; Arctic Ocean ice-free in summer at least once every ten years; more frequent droughts in southern Africa and the Mediterranean; sea level rise threatening low-lying deltas such as the Ganges–Brahmaputra and the Nile.\n\nAssess the projected impacts of climate change on people and the environment.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Environment: coral bleaching and loss (Great Barrier Reef); loss of Arctic sea ice and habitat for polar bears; shifting climate zones and species; more wildfires; melting glaciers.\n• People: sea level rise flooding deltas and coastal cities (Bangladesh, Nile delta); drought reducing food and water supply (southern Africa, Mediterranean); heatwaves increasing deaths; health impacts (spread of malaria, dengue); migration.\n• Uneven impacts: LICs such as Bangladesh have less money to adapt than HICs such as the UK; some places may benefit (longer growing seasons in Canada or the UK).\n• Uncertainty: size of impacts depends on emissions scenario.\n• Judgement: e.g. impacts on people are most serious in low-lying, poorer places; environmental impacts may be irreversible.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate and relevant knowledge of impacts on people and environment using examples. Balanced, coherent assessment. Judgement fully supported.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; list of impacts; judgement unsupported.\n0: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 16: IPCC projections of global warming by 2081–2100 compared with 1850–1900. Very low emissions: 1.0–1.8 °C. Intermediate: 2.1–3.5 °C. Very high emissions: 3.3–5.7 °C. Projected sea level rise by 2100 ranges from about 0.3 m (very low emissions) to about 1.0 m (very high emissions).\n\nEvaluate the reasons why there is uncertainty about the future impacts of climate change.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Future emissions are unknown: depend on population growth, economic development (especially in emerging countries), technology and international agreements such as the Paris Agreement (2015); this gives the biggest range in Figure 16 (1.0 °C to 5.7 °C).\n• Scientific uncertainty within each scenario: feedbacks (permafrost methane, reduced albedo from melting ice), behaviour of the Antarctic ice sheet, cloud effects; models are simplifications.\n• Natural factors: volcanic eruptions and solar variation cannot be predicted.\n• Impacts also depend on how well people adapt (flood defences, drought-resistant crops), which depends on wealth.\n• Judgement: human choices about emissions are the largest source of uncertainty; scientific uncertainty increases at higher warming.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate and relevant knowledge. Balanced, coherent evaluation using the resource. Judgement fully supported.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; judgement unsupported.\n0: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 20: projected changes in UK climate by 2070 under a high emissions scenario (Met Office projections): summers up to 5.4 °C warmer and up to 47% drier; winters up to 4.2 °C warmer and up to 35% wetter; sea level in London up to 1.15 m higher by 2100. Hot summers like 2018 could happen every other year by 2050.\n\nAssess the extent to which climate change will have negative impacts on the UK.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Negative: heatwaves and heat-related deaths (40.3 °C in 2022); drought and water shortages in the south-east; winter flooding (e.g. Somerset Levels-type events); sea level rise threatening London, East Anglia and the Humber; coastal erosion; loss of upland species; new pests and diseases; pressure on the NHS.\n• Positive: longer growing season and new crops (e.g. vineyards in southern England); fewer cold-related deaths in winter; possible increase in UK tourism; lower heating demand.\n• Extent varies: worse for low-lying coasts and the south-east; depends on emissions scenario and adaptation (Thames Barrier, flood defences); the UK as an HIC can afford adaptation better than LICs.\n• Judgement: overall impacts likely to be mainly negative, but the scale is uncertain and depends on location and adaptation.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate and relevant knowledge of UK impacts. Balanced, coherent assessment of negative and positive impacts. Judgement on extent fully supported.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; one-sided; judgement unsupported.\n0: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section D — Making a Geographical Decision.\nResource information (written for this practice question): Bangladesh is a lower-income country of about 170 million people. Much of the country lies less than 5 m above sea level on the Ganges–Brahmaputra delta. Projections suggest that a 1 m rise in sea level could flood about 17% of the land and displace up to 20 million people. Salt water is already reaching rice fields in the south-west, and tropical cyclones such as Cyclone Sidr (2007) have caused thousands of deaths.\nThree options for Bangladesh's government to respond to the projected impacts of climate change:\nOption 1 — Build and raise coastal embankments and plant mangrove forests along the coast to protect against sea level rise and storm surges.\nOption 2 — Help farmers switch to salt-tolerant rice varieties and floating gardens, and build rainwater storage for drinking water.\nOption 3 — Build more multi-storey cyclone shelters and improve early warning systems for coastal communities.\n\nSelect the option you think Bangladesh's government ought to focus on first. Justify your choice. Use the information above and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer. (12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `No option is better than another; credit any well-justified choice.\nIndicative content:\n• Option 1: protects large areas of land and population from flooding; mangroves are low-cost, natural and store carbon; but embankments are expensive to build and maintain, may fail, and do not stop salt water reaching soils.\n• Option 2: protects food security and livelihoods for the many people working in farming; relatively cheap and can be done by communities; but does not protect lives from storm surges and may not be enough if sea level rises by 1 m.\n• Option 3: saves lives (deaths from cyclones have fallen greatly since 1970 thanks to warnings and shelters); but does not protect homes, crops or land, and people may still lose livelihoods.\n• Links to course: projected impacts and uncertainty of sea level rise; tropical cyclones becoming more intense; Bangladesh's limited wealth; sustainability (social, economic, environmental); short-term v long-term.\n\nLevel 4 (10–12): Accurate geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Makes full use of the resource information and wider knowledge to build a balanced, well-developed argument; considers the chosen option and why it is preferred to the others. Coherent, fully supported judgement.\nLevel 3 (7–9): Mostly accurate knowledge. Good use of the resource information with some wider knowledge. Argument mostly balanced; judgement supported.\nLevel 2 (4–6): Some accurate knowledge. Some use of the information; argument partially developed or one-sided; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3): Isolated knowledge, some inaccurate. Limited use of the information; little or no justification.\n0: No rewardable material.\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate performance (2–3): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; uses a good range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nThreshold performance (1): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning and any errors do not significantly hinder meaning; uses a limited range of specialist terms as appropriate.\n0: No marks awarded / writes nothing / response does not relate to the question.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.2': {
    name: 'Tropical Cyclones',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 1 is a cross-section through a mature tropical cyclone. It shows towering cumulonimbus clouds spiralling around a narrow central area of clear sky, where cool air sinks and winds are light.\n\nState the name given to the central area of a tropical cyclone shown in Figure 1.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for:\n• the eye (1)\nDo not accept "eye wall".` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nTyphoon Haiyan struck the Philippines in November 2013. Tropical cyclones are given different names in different ocean basins.\n\nWhich one of the following is the name used for a tropical cyclone in the western North Pacific Ocean?\nA  hurricane\nB  typhoon\nC  willy-willy\nD  tornado`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B — typhoon (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 2 shows the Saffir–Simpson scale. Category 1: 119–153 km/h; Category 2: 154–177 km/h; Category 3: 178–208 km/h; Category 4: 209–251 km/h; Category 5: 252 km/h or more. Hurricane Katrina had sustained winds of about 200 km/h when it made landfall in Louisiana on 29 August 2005.\n\nIdentify the category of Hurricane Katrina at landfall.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Category 3 (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 3 shows the number of named tropical storms in the North Atlantic in each month of one season (data written for this practice question): June 2, July 3, August 9, September 14, October 6, November 2.\n\nIdentify the month with the most named tropical storms.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `September (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 4 shows air pressure readings (data written for this practice question). Normal air pressure at sea level is 1013 mb. The lowest pressure recorded in the eye of one tropical cyclone was 902 mb.\n\nCalculate the difference between normal sea-level pressure and the pressure in the eye of this tropical cyclone.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `111 mb (1)\n(1013 − 902 = 111)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 5 is a world map of sea surface temperatures in September. Tropical cyclones form only where the ocean is warm enough.\n\nWhich one of the following is the minimum sea surface temperature usually needed for a tropical cyclone to form?\nA  16 °C\nB  21 °C\nC  26.5 °C\nD  35 °C`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C — 26.5 °C (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 6 is a world map of the tracks of tropical cyclones between 1985 and 2015. Tracks are found over the western North Pacific, the North Atlantic and Caribbean, the Bay of Bengal and Arabian Sea, the South Indian Ocean and around northern Australia. There are almost no tracks within 5° of the Equator, in the South Atlantic or along the west coast of South America.\n\nDescribe the distribution of tropical cyclones shown in Figure 6.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each descriptive point, up to 2 marks:\n• Found between about 5° and 30° north and south of the Equator / in the tropics (1)\n• Mainly over oceans / along the eastern coasts of continents, e.g. the Caribbean, Philippines, Bay of Bengal (1)\n• Most frequent in the western North Pacific (1)\n• Absent near the Equator / in the South Atlantic / off western South America (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 6 shows that almost no tropical cyclones form within 5° of the Equator, even though sea temperatures there are above 27 °C.\n\nExplain one reason why tropical cyclones do not form close to the Equator.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• The Coriolis effect is too weak near the Equator (1) so the rising air does not begin to spin / a rotating low-pressure system cannot develop (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nTyphoon Haiyan crossed the central Philippines on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds of over 300 km/h. After crossing the islands, wind speeds had fallen.\n\nExplain one reason why tropical cyclones lose strength when they move over land.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• They are cut off from their supply of warm, moist air from the ocean (1) so there is less latent heat released by condensation to power the storm (1)\n• Friction with the land surface increases (1) which slows the winds (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 3 shows the number of named tropical storms in the North Atlantic in each month of one season (data written for this practice question): June 2, July 3, August 9, September 14, October 6, November 2.\n\nCalculate the percentage of named tropical storms that formed between August and October. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Working: total = 36 and August–October = 9 + 14 + 6 = 29; 29 ÷ 36 × 100 (1)\n• 80.6% (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 7 is a table of maximum sustained wind speeds of five tropical cyclones (data written for this practice question): Storm A 185 km/h; Storm B 220 km/h; Storm C 160 km/h; Storm D 250 km/h; Storm E 205 km/h.\n\nCalculate the mean maximum sustained wind speed of the five storms. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Working: 185 + 220 + 160 + 250 + 205 = 1020; 1020 ÷ 5 (1)\n• 204 km/h (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 3 shows that most North Atlantic tropical storms happen between August and October.\n\nExplain one reason why tropical cyclones are seasonal.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• Sea surface temperatures are highest in late summer and autumn (1) because the oceans have been heated by the overhead sun for several months, so they are most likely to be above 26.5 °C (1)\n• The ITCZ / zone of low pressure moves north or south with the overhead sun (1) bringing the rising air needed for storms to that hemisphere in its summer (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nCyclone Nargis made landfall in the Irrawaddy (Ayeyarwady) Delta of Myanmar on 2 May 2008. A storm surge about 4 m high flooded large areas of the delta.\n\nExplain one way in which a storm surge causes coastal flooding.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• Very low air pressure allows the sea surface to rise / dome upwards (1) so sea level is higher than normal when the cyclone reaches the coast (1)\n• Strong onshore winds push water towards the coast (1) so water piles up and floods low-lying land such as deltas (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nTyphoon Haiyan (2013) brought more than 200 mm of rain to parts of the Philippines in 24 hours. Many villages are built on steep, deforested hillsides.\n\nExplain one reason why landslides are a hazard during tropical cyclones.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• Intense rainfall saturates the soil / regolith (1) making it heavy and reducing friction so it slides down steep slopes (1)\n• Deforested slopes have no roots to bind the soil (1) so saturated soil moves more easily, burying homes and roads (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 8 shows Bangladesh. Much of the country is a low-lying delta formed by the Ganges and Brahmaputra rivers, and the Bay of Bengal narrows towards its coast.\n\nGive two physical reasons why Bangladesh is vulnerable to tropical cyclones.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason, up to 2 marks:\n• Large areas are low-lying / less than a few metres above sea level (1)\n• The funnel shape of the Bay of Bengal concentrates / increases the height of storm surges (1)\n• It borders warm seas where cyclones form (1)\n• Many islands / delta channels allow flood water to spread far inland (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe US National Hurricane Center tracked Hurricane Katrina for several days before it reached New Orleans in August 2005.\n\nExplain one way that satellite technology helps countries prepare for tropical cyclones.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development:\n• Satellites track the path / position of a cyclone (1) so forecasters can predict where it will make landfall and warn those areas in advance (1)\n• Satellite images show cloud patterns and help estimate the storm's strength (1) so the right level of warning / evacuation can be given (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nCyclone Nargis (2008) killed or left missing about 138,000 people in Myanmar. Many villages in the delta received little or no warning.\n\nSuggest one reason why the death toll from Cyclone Nargis was so high.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• Poor communication / few radios or phones in rural delta villages (1) so people did not know to evacuate (1)\n• Mangroves had been cleared for rice farming / shrimp farms (1) so there was no natural barrier to reduce the storm surge (1)\n• Few cyclone shelters / weak homes of wood and bamboo (1) so people had nowhere safe to go (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nAfter Hurricane Katrina (2005) about 80% of New Orleans was flooded and tens of thousands of people sheltered in the Louisiana Superdome.\n\nExplain one reason why the response to Hurricane Katrina was criticised.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• The federal agency FEMA was slow to send help (1) so people were stranded for days without food, water or medical care (1)\n• Poorer residents without cars could not evacuate (1) so many were trapped when the levees broke (1)\n• Conditions in the Superdome were poor / overcrowded (1) with shortages of water and sanitation (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe following statements are about how tropical cyclones form.\n\nIdentify the two correct statements.\nA  They form over cold ocean currents.\nB  Warm, moist air rises rapidly, creating an area of low pressure.\nC  They form directly on the Equator.\nD  Condensation of water vapour releases heat that powers the storm.\nE  They need strong winds high in the atmosphere to form.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1)\nD (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 9 is a table of deaths from three tropical cyclones in Bangladesh (figures rounded): 1970 Bhola cyclone about 300,000; 1991 cyclone about 138,000; 2007 Cyclone Sidr about 3,400.\n\nDescribe one other suitable method of presenting the data in Figure 9.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a method and 1 mark for development:\n• A bar chart (1) with one bar for each cyclone, year on the x-axis and number of deaths on the y-axis (1)\n• Proportional symbols on a map of Bangladesh (1) with circles sized according to the number of deaths, placed where each cyclone struck (1)\nDo not accept line graph (data are not continuous).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 1 is a cross-section through a mature tropical cyclone, showing warm, moist air spiralling in at sea level and rising rapidly in the eye wall.\n\nExplain one reason why warm oceans are needed for tropical cyclones to form.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and up to 2 further marks for development:\n• Warm water (above 26.5 °C) causes rapid evaporation (1) so the air above contains a lot of water vapour (1). As this air rises and cools, the vapour condenses, releasing latent heat that makes the air rise even faster and powers the storm (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 9 shows deaths from tropical cyclones in Bangladesh (figures rounded): 1970 about 300,000; 1991 about 138,000; 2007 about 3,400.\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in deaths between the 1991 cyclone and Cyclone Sidr in 2007. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Decrease = 138,000 − 3,400 = 134,600 (1)\n• 134,600 ÷ 138,000 × 100 (1)\n• 97.5% (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 10 is an incomplete bar chart of the number of North Atlantic named storms in each month of one season (data written for this practice question). The bars for June (2), July (3), August (9), October (6) and November (2) are drawn. The y-axis goes from 0 to 16 in steps of 2. The bar for September (14) is missing.\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 10 by plotting the bar for September.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each step, up to 3 marks:\n• Find September on the x-axis, between August and October (1)\n• Draw the bar up to 14 on the y-axis / one gridline below 16 (1)\n• Make the bar the same width as the other bars with equal gaps, and shade it in the same way (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nYou have studied the preparation for a tropical cyclone in either a developed or a developing country.\n\nExplain one way that evacuation can reduce the loss of life. (State your chosen tropical cyclone.)`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a way and up to 2 further marks for development. Credit any named cyclone, e.g.\n• Typhoon Haiyan (2013): the Philippine government moved about 800,000 people away from coasts before landfall (1); this took them out of the area flooded by the storm surge (1), which caused most of the deaths, so far fewer people drowned than would otherwise have done (1).\n• Hurricane Katrina (2005): a mandatory evacuation of New Orleans was ordered the day before landfall (1); most residents left by car on contraflow highways (1), so they were not in the city when levees failed and 80% of it flooded (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nTyphoon Haiyan struck the Philippines on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds above 300 km/h, heavy rain and a storm surge of up to about 5 m in the city of Tacloban.\n\nExplain two physical hazards caused by tropical cyclones.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each hazard identified and 1 further mark for each development, up to 2 hazards (2 × 2 marks):\n• High winds (1) destroy buildings, uproot trees and bring down power lines (1)\n• Storm surge (1) caused by low pressure and onshore winds raises sea level and floods low-lying coasts, drowning people (1)\n• Intense rainfall / river flooding (1) as rivers overflow (1)\n• Landslides (1) as saturated soil on steep slopes collapses, burying homes (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nTropical cyclones of similar strength often cause many more deaths in developing countries than in developed countries.\n\nExplain two reasons why developing countries are more vulnerable to tropical cyclones.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason and 1 further mark for development (2 × 2 marks):\n• Houses are built from weaker materials such as wood and corrugated iron (1) so they are destroyed by high winds, leaving people exposed (1)\n• Less money for forecasting and warning systems (1) so people may not know a cyclone is coming or when to evacuate (1)\n• Poor infrastructure / few roads (1) so evacuation and emergency aid are slow (1)\n• Many people live in low-lying coastal areas for farming or fishing (1) so they are exposed to storm surges (1)\n• Poverty means people may refuse to leave their homes / possessions (1) for fear of losing their livelihood (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 11 compares two tropical cyclones. Hurricane Katrina, USA, 2005: Category 3 at landfall; about 1,800 deaths; damage about US$125 billion. Cyclone Nargis, Myanmar, 2008: equivalent to Category 4 at landfall; about 138,000 dead or missing; damage about US$10 billion.\n\nUsing Figure 11, suggest two reasons why the number of deaths was much higher in Myanmar than in the USA.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason and 1 further mark for development (2 × 2 marks). Reasons should be linked to Figure 11:\n• Nargis was a stronger storm (Category 4 compared with Category 3) (1) so winds and the storm surge were more powerful (1)\n• The USA is a developed country with better forecasting and warnings (1) so most people had evacuated before landfall (1)\n• Myanmar had fewer cyclone shelters and weaker homes (1) so people in the delta had nowhere safe to go (1)\n• Myanmar's government restricted foreign aid workers after the storm (1) so many survivors died of disease / lack of clean water (1)\nThe higher damage in the USA reflects the greater value of property, not a bigger hazard.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFor a named tropical cyclone that affected a developed country, explain two ways that the country prepared for the cyclone.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way and 1 further mark for development (2 × 2 marks). Must refer to a developed country. E.g. Hurricane Katrina, USA, 2005:\n• The National Hurricane Center used satellites and aircraft to track the storm (1) and issued hurricane warnings for New Orleans about two days before landfall (1)\n• A mandatory evacuation of New Orleans was ordered on 28 August (1) and contraflow on highways let traffic leave the city faster (1)\n• The Superdome was set up as a "refuge of last resort" (1) for people who could not leave the city (1)\n• Levees and floodwalls had been built around New Orleans (1) to protect the city, which is largely below sea level (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFor a named tropical cyclone that affected a developing country, explain two ways in which people and organisations responded after the cyclone.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each response and 1 further mark for development (2 × 2 marks). Must refer to a developing country. E.g.\nTyphoon Haiyan, Philippines, 2013:\n• Countries such as the USA and the UK sent military ships and aircraft (1) to deliver food, water and shelter to areas cut off by damaged roads (1)\n• The government created no-build zones near the coast (1) to stop people rebuilding where storm surges hit (1)\nCyclone Nargis, Myanmar, 2008:\n• The military government at first refused visas to foreign aid workers (1) so aid reached many survivors only weeks later (1)\n• Local monks and volunteers distributed food and shelter (1) because outside help was slow (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nIn Bangladesh, the government and charities have built several thousand raised concrete cyclone shelters along the coast since 1991.\n\nExplain one cost and one benefit of building cyclone shelters to reduce the impacts of tropical cyclones.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a cost and 1 further mark for development; 1 mark for a benefit and 1 further mark for development:\nCost:\n• Expensive to build and maintain (1) which is difficult for a developing country and may rely on foreign aid (1)\n• Some people live too far away to reach them (1) so they are less effective in remote areas (1)\nBenefit:\n• They are raised above storm surge levels and built of reinforced concrete (1) so thousands of people can survive the flooding and winds (1)\n• Can be used as schools when there is no cyclone (1) so they provide benefits all year round (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nIn the Irrawaddy Delta of Myanmar, large areas of mangrove forest were cleared for rice farming and shrimp ponds before Cyclone Nargis in 2008.\n\nExplain two ways in which tropical cyclones affect the natural environment.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each effect and 1 further mark for development (2 × 2 marks):\n• Salt water from storm surges floods farmland (1) making the soil saline so crops cannot grow for several years (1)\n• High winds uproot trees / destroy forests and mangroves (1) removing habitats for wildlife (1)\n• Floodwater carries sewage and chemicals (1) polluting rivers and drinking water (1)\n• Large waves and sediment damage coral reefs (1) which reduces fish numbers (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 1 is a cross-section through a mature tropical cyclone, about 500 km across. It shows bands of cloud spiralling towards the centre, a ring of very tall cumulonimbus clouds around the centre, and a calm, clear centre where air is sinking.\n\nExplain two features of the structure of a tropical cyclone shown in Figure 1.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each feature and 1 further mark for development (2 × 2 marks):\n• The eye is calm and clear (1) because cool air sinks down the centre of the storm, so there is little cloud or wind (1)\n• The eye wall has the strongest winds and heaviest rain (1) because warm, moist air rises most rapidly here and condenses into tall cumulonimbus clouds (1)\n• Spiral rain bands (1) form as air is drawn in towards the area of low pressure and rotates because of the Coriolis effect (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 12 describes the track of Hurricane Katrina in August 2005. It formed over the Bahamas, crossed southern Florida as a Category 1 hurricane, strengthened over the warm Gulf of Mexico to Category 5, then made landfall in Louisiana as a Category 3 hurricane and weakened quickly as it moved north over Mississippi.\n\nUsing Figure 12, explain two reasons why the strength of Hurricane Katrina changed along its track.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason and 1 further mark for development, using Figure 12 (2 × 2 marks):\n• It strengthened over the Gulf of Mexico (1) because the very warm sea provided lots of evaporation / latent heat energy (1)\n• It weakened after landfall in Louisiana / over Mississippi (1) because it lost its supply of warm, moist air from the ocean (1)\n• Friction with the land surface (1) slowed the winds as it moved inland (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nThe Philippines is hit by about 20 tropical cyclones each year. Its government uses forecasts from the national weather agency, PAGASA, to give warnings to coastal communities.\n\nExplain two ways that forecasting and warning systems reduce the impacts of tropical cyclones.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way and 1 further mark for development (2 × 2 marks):\n• Forecasts predict the path and arrival time (1) so people can be evacuated from areas at risk before landfall (1)\n• Warnings on radio, TV and mobile phones (1) give people time to board up windows, store food and water and move possessions upstairs (1)\n• Storm surge forecasts identify the coasts most at risk (1) so emergency services and supplies can be placed nearby in advance (1)\n• Warning levels / signal numbers (1) help people understand how serious the threat is and what action to take (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 11 compares Hurricane Katrina (USA, 2005: about 1,800 deaths, damage about US$125 billion) and Cyclone Nargis (Myanmar, 2008: about 138,000 dead or missing, damage about US$10 billion).\n\nAssess the extent to which a country's level of development affects the impacts of a tropical cyclone. Use examples in your answer.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nAO2 — development and impacts:\n• Developed countries have better forecasting, warnings and evacuation, so fewer deaths (USA about 1,800 vs Myanmar about 138,000).\n• Stronger buildings, insurance and money for reconstruction reduce long-term impacts in developed countries.\n• Economic losses are higher in developed countries because property is worth more (Katrina about US$125 billion).\n• Developing countries have weaker homes, poor communications, rural populations in low-lying deltas and depend on aid.\nAO3 — assessment:\n• Development is not the only factor: storm strength (Nargis Category 4 vs Katrina Category 3), the height of the storm surge and the shape of the coast matter.\n• Governance matters: Myanmar's refusal of foreign aid increased deaths; even in the USA, poorer New Orleans residents without cars were most affected, showing inequality within a country.\n• Developing countries can reduce deaths with low-cost measures, e.g. Bangladesh cyclone shelters.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate, well-developed understanding of the factors; a balanced assessment, with a substantiated judgement on the extent, supported by evidence from examples.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some accurate understanding; partly developed assessment with some use of examples; judgement may be unbalanced or partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated points of knowledge; limited assessment; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nYou have studied tropical cyclones in a developed country and a developing country.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of the responses to a tropical cyclone in a developed country and a developing country that you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (credit any named cyclones, e.g. Hurricane Katrina 2005; Typhoon Haiyan 2013 or Cyclone Nargis 2008):\nAO2 — responses:\n• Katrina: warnings about two days ahead and mandatory evacuation; Superdome as a refuge; Coast Guard rescues; but FEMA was slow, levees failed, poorer residents were left behind; later the levees were rebuilt (about US$14 billion).\n• Haiyan: about 800,000 people evacuated beforehand; international aid (US military, UK); "Build Back Better" plan and coastal no-build zones; but the storm surge was poorly understood by residents and aid was slow to reach remote islands; about 6,300 deaths.\n• Nargis: little warning reached delta villages; government delayed foreign aid; local groups and monks gave help.\nAO3 — evaluation:\n• Effectiveness judged by deaths, speed of aid, recovery and reduced future risk.\n• Developed country responses were well funded but poorly coordinated; developing country responses relied on aid and were limited by infrastructure and governance.\n• A justified conclusion on which response was more effective, and why.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate, well-developed understanding of both responses; balanced evaluation leading to a substantiated judgement supported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some accurate understanding, possibly unbalanced between the two countries; partial evaluation; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited evaluation; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nTyphoon Haiyan (Philippines, 2013) produced sustained winds above 300 km/h, a storm surge of up to about 5 m in Tacloban, and heavy rain that triggered landslides inland. About 6,300 people died, most of them in coastal areas.\n\nAssess the relative importance of the different physical hazards caused by a named tropical cyclone that you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (credit any named cyclone):\nAO2 — hazards:\n• Storm surge: low pressure and onshore winds raise sea level; floods low-lying coasts; main cause of drowning (e.g. Haiyan in Tacloban; Nargis in the Irrawaddy Delta; Katrina's 8 m surge on the Mississippi coast).\n• High winds: destroy weak buildings, crops and power lines; flying debris causes injuries.\n• Intense rainfall and river flooding: damage further inland; contaminate water supplies.\n• Landslides: saturated soil on steep, deforested slopes buries homes and blocks roads.\nAO3 — assessment:\n• Storm surge usually causes the most deaths and is most important on low-lying coasts.\n• Wind damage is widest in area and causes most economic damage to buildings and crops.\n• Importance varies with location: landslides matter in mountainous areas, flooding in deltas; the hazards combine (e.g. levee failure in New Orleans).\n• A substantiated judgement on which hazard is most important.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate, well-developed understanding of the hazards; balanced assessment with a substantiated judgement supported by evidence from the named cyclone.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some accurate understanding; partly developed assessment; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated points; limited assessment; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nFigure 6 shows that tropical cyclones form over warm oceans between about 5° and 30° from the Equator, mostly in late summer and autumn.\n\nAssess the importance of different conditions needed for the formation of tropical cyclones.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nAO1/AO2 — conditions:\n• Sea surface temperature of at least 26.5 °C, to a depth of about 50–60 m, providing evaporation and latent heat energy.\n• Location at least 5° from the Equator so the Coriolis effect makes the air spin.\n• Low wind shear (winds at different heights similar), so the storm is not torn apart.\n• Rising, unstable, moist air / an area of low pressure to start convection.\nAO3 — assessment:\n• Warm water is often seen as most important because it is the energy source; storms weaken over land or cold water.\n• But warm water alone is not enough: near the Equator seas are warm but no cyclones form, showing the Coriolis effect is essential.\n• Conditions work together; seasonality shows storms form only when all conditions are met.\n• A substantiated judgement.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate, well-developed understanding of the conditions; balanced assessment of their relative importance and a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some accurate understanding; partly developed assessment; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated points; limited assessment; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\nIn August 2005 about 80% of New Orleans was flooded after Hurricane Katrina. Much of the city lies below sea level between the Mississippi River and Lake Pontchartrain, and its levees failed in many places. About a quarter of residents lived in poverty and many did not own a car.\n\nAssess the relative importance of physical and human factors in making places vulnerable to tropical cyclones. Use examples in your answer.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nAO2 — factors:\n• Physical: low-lying coasts and deltas (Bangladesh, Irrawaddy Delta, New Orleans below sea level); funnel-shaped bays raising storm surges (Bay of Bengal); steep slopes for landslides; location on common cyclone tracks (Philippines about 20 a year).\n• Human: poverty, weak housing, lack of warnings and shelters; population density in coasts; deforestation of slopes and mangroves; quality of defences (New Orleans levees); governance (Myanmar refusing aid).\nAO3 — assessment:\n• Physical factors determine exposure; human factors determine how far people can cope.\n• Bangladesh shows human action (shelters, warnings) can reduce deaths despite unchanged physical risk.\n• Katrina shows that even in a developed country human factors (poverty, failed levees) increased vulnerability.\n• Human activities can increase physical risk (clearing mangroves).\n• A substantiated judgement.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate, well-developed understanding; balanced assessment of both sets of factors with a substantiated judgement supported by examples.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some accurate understanding; partly developed assessment; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated points; limited assessment; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Making a geographical decision — reducing cyclone risk on the coast of Bangladesh.\nResource information: Bangladesh's coastal districts are home to over 35 million people. Most land is less than 5 m above sea level. Since the 1991 cyclone (about 138,000 deaths) the country has built several thousand cyclone shelters and trained over 70,000 Cyclone Preparedness Programme volunteers; Cyclone Sidr in 2007 caused about 3,400 deaths. Many people still live too far from a shelter, and some are reluctant to leave homes and livestock. Mangroves of the Sundarbans reduced storm surge damage behind them during Sidr.\nThree options for a coastal district council:\nOption 1 — Build more raised concrete cyclone shelters that are also used as schools.\nOption 2 — Plant a wide belt of mangrove forest along the coast and river banks.\nOption 3 — Improve early warnings, sending alerts to every mobile phone and training more volunteers to help people evacuate with their livestock.\n\nSelect the option that you think would be the best way to reduce the impacts of tropical cyclones in this district. Justify your choice. Use the information above and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer. (12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `No option is preferred. Credit any option that is justified.\nIndicative content:\nOption 1 — shelters: protect people from storm surge and wind; dual use as schools gives everyday benefit; but expensive, may be far from some homes, no room for livestock, do not protect property.\nOption 2 — mangroves: natural, low-cost defence that absorbs wave energy and reduces surge height; provides habitats, fish nurseries and timber; sustainable; but takes years to grow, needs land that may be used for farming / shrimp ponds, does not stop very large surges.\nOption 3 — warnings and volunteers: low cost, uses rising mobile phone ownership; gives time to evacuate; volunteers have been effective since 1991; but warnings are useless without somewhere safe to go; remote people without signal / power may be missed.\nGood answers link options to the resource (e.g. 138,000 deaths in 1991 compared with 3,400 in 2007), consider economic, social and environmental factors and short and long term, and explain why the rejected options are less suitable.\nLevels:\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Applies understanding thoroughly to the decision; a comprehensive, balanced evaluation of the chosen option and the alternatives; a fully justified decision supported by well-used evidence from the resources and wider course.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Applies understanding with some depth; a balanced evaluation with some reference to alternatives; a justified decision supported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some application of understanding; a partial evaluation; decision partly justified with limited evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited evaluation; decision stated with little or no justification.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate performance (2–3): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; uses a good range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nThreshold performance (1): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning; any errors do not significantly hinder meaning; uses a limited range of specialist terms.\n0 marks: no response, or the response does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.3': {
    name: 'Tectonic Hazards',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 1: a cross-section through the Earth from the surface to the centre. Four layers are labelled W, X, Y and Z. Layer W is a thin outer shell 5–70 km thick. Layer X is about 2,900 km thick and is made of hot, semi-molten rock. Layer Y is about 2,200 km thick. Layer Z is at the centre, with a temperature of about 5,500 °C.\n\nWhich one of the following describes layer Y?\n\nA  Solid iron and nickel\nB  Liquid iron and nickel\nC  Solid basalt and granite\nD  Semi-molten silicate rock`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1)\nLayer Y is the outer core.\nDo not credit more than one answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nThe San Andreas Fault in California, USA, is where the Pacific plate and the North American plate slide past each other. Earthquakes are common there but there are no volcanoes.\n\nWhich one of the following is the type of plate boundary at the San Andreas Fault?\n\nA  Divergent\nB  Convergent (oceanic–continental)\nC  Conservative\nD  Convergent (continental–continental)`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (1)\nDo not credit more than one answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 2: a photograph of Mauna Loa on the island of Hawaii, in the middle of the Pacific plate. The volcano is very wide with gentle slopes. Long, dark streams of runny basalt lava are flowing down its sides. Hawaii lies thousands of kilometres from the nearest plate boundary.\n\nName the type of volcano shown in Figure 2.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `shield (volcano) (1)\nDo not credit composite / strato-volcano / cinder cone.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 3: a table of the focus depth of five earthquakes recorded along the west coast of South America in one month (data written for this practice question).\n\nEarthquake | Focus depth (km)\nA | 12\nB | 35\nC | 70\nD | 150\nE | 33\n\nUsing Figure 3, identify the median focus depth.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `35 (km) (1)\n(ordered: 12, 33, 35, 70, 150)` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nOn 25 April 2015 a magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck Nepal, a developing country. The epicentre was in the Gorkha district, about 80 km north-west of the capital, Kathmandu.\n\nState one primary impact of the 2015 Nepal earthquake.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Any one from:\n• about 9,000 people killed (1)\n• over 20,000 people injured (1)\n• hundreds of thousands of homes destroyed / collapsed (1)\n• historic buildings in Kathmandu destroyed, e.g. Dharahara tower / Durbar Square temples (1)\n• roads / bridges / water pipes damaged (1)\nDo not credit secondary impacts, e.g. landslides, avalanches, disease, loss of tourism income.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nSeismologists reported that the earthquake off the coast of Maule, Chile, on 27 February 2010 measured 8.8.\n\nWhich one of the following scales is used to measure the magnitude of an earthquake today?\n\nA  Mercalli scale\nB  Moment magnitude scale\nC  Saffir–Simpson scale\nD  Volcanic Explosivity Index`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1)\nDo not credit more than one answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 4: a world map showing the location of active volcanoes. A dense line of dots runs all the way round the edge of the Pacific Ocean, from New Zealand through Indonesia, Japan, Alaska and down the west coast of the Americas to Chile. A thinner line runs down the middle of the Atlantic Ocean through Iceland. A few isolated dots are shown in the middle of the Pacific (Hawaii) and in East Africa. Very few dots are shown in Australia, Russia or Brazil.\n\nDescribe the distribution of volcanoes shown in Figure 4.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 2 marks, e.g.\n• found in long narrow belts / lines (along plate boundaries) (1)\n• most are around the edge of the Pacific Ocean / the Ring of Fire (1)\n• a line runs down the centre of the Atlantic / through Iceland (1)\n• some are isolated in the middle of plates, e.g. Hawaii (1)\n• few or none in the interior of continents, e.g. Australia / Brazil (1)\nCredit use of named places from the figure. Explanation is not required.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nAlong the coast of Chile the Nazca plate (oceanic crust) moves towards the South American plate (continental crust). A deep ocean trench lies just offshore.\n\nExplain one reason why the Nazca plate is subducted beneath the South American plate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Oceanic crust is denser than continental crust (1) so when the plates meet the heavier Nazca plate sinks beneath the lighter South American plate into the mantle (1).\n• Oceanic crust is thinner / made of basalt (1) so it is pulled down at the trench by its own weight (slab pull) (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nMount Fuji in Japan and Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines are composite volcanoes found at convergent plate boundaries.\n\nIdentify two characteristics of a composite volcano.\n\nA  Gentle slopes that cover a very wide area\nB  Alternating layers of ash and lava\nC  Runny, low-viscosity basalt lava\nD  Steep sides with a conical shape\nE  Eruptions that are frequent but rarely explosive`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1)\nD (1)\nIf more than two answers are given, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 3: a table of the focus depth of five earthquakes recorded along the west coast of South America in one month (data written for this practice question).\n\nEarthquake | Focus depth (km)\nA | 12\nB | 35\nC | 70\nD | 150\nE | 33\n\nCalculate the mean focus depth of the five earthquakes. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `12 + 35 + 70 + 150 + 33 = 300 (1)\n300 ÷ 5 = 60 (km) (1)\nAward 1 mark for a correct total or correct method with an arithmetic error.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 5: a diagram of the Earth's interior. Heat is shown rising from the core. Curved arrows show material in the mantle rising beneath a mid-ocean ridge, moving sideways beneath the plates and sinking again near an ocean trench.\n\nExplain one way the movement shown in Figure 5 causes tectonic plates to move.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a way and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Heat from radioactive decay in the core makes mantle rock rise in convection currents (1); as it spreads sideways beneath the crust it drags the plates along with it (1).\n• Rising hot material pushes up and out at mid-ocean ridges (ridge push) (1) forcing the plates on either side apart (1).\nAccept slab pull at trenches developed correctly.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nOn 12 January 2010 a magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck Haiti. Its epicentre was about 25 km from the capital, Port-au-Prince, and its focus was only about 13 km deep. In October 2010 an outbreak of cholera began in camps for homeless people.\n\nExplain one secondary impact of the 2010 Haiti earthquake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a secondary impact and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• A cholera epidemic spread (1) because water pipes and sanitation were destroyed and over a million people lived in crowded camps (1).\n• Unemployment / loss of income (1) as businesses, the port and government buildings were destroyed (1).\n• Children missed education (1) because thousands of schools collapsed (1).\nDo not credit primary impacts (deaths/collapse caused directly by shaking) unless linked to a secondary effect.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nChile has strict building codes. Many tall buildings in Santiago are built with deep foundations, steel frames and rubber bearings at their base.\n\nExplain one way earthquake-resistant building design can reduce the loss of life.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a way and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Rubber bearings / base isolators absorb the shaking (1) so the building moves less and is less likely to collapse on the people inside (1).\n• Steel frames / reinforced concrete can bend or sway (1) rather than snapping, so floors do not pancake (1).\n• Deep foundations anchor the building in solid rock (1) reducing the risk of it toppling (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nScientists at the Hawaiian Volcano Observatory use tiltmeters, GPS stations and gas sensors on Kīlauea volcano.\n\nExplain one way that monitoring can help predict a volcanic eruption.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a method/sign and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Tiltmeters / GPS measure the ground swelling (1) which shows magma is rising and collecting beneath the volcano (1).\n• Gas sensors detect an increase in sulphur dioxide (1) given off as magma nears the surface (1).\n• Seismometers record more small earthquakes (1) as rising magma cracks the rock (1).\n• Thermal imaging detects rising temperatures (1) as magma approaches the surface (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 6: a scatter graph of the magnitude of 12 earthquakes (x-axis, 5.0 to 9.0) against the number of buildings destroyed (y-axis, 0 to 100,000) (data written for this practice question). Most points rise from left to right, but one magnitude 6.0 earthquake with a focus 5 km deep below a large city destroyed 80,000 buildings, much more than the others of similar magnitude.\n\nDescribe the relationship shown in Figure 6.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• A positive relationship: as magnitude increases the number of buildings destroyed increases (1)\n• Recognition of the anomaly: the magnitude 6.0 earthquake destroyed 80,000 buildings / far more than expected (1)\nAllow: the relationship is not perfect / a weak or moderate positive correlation (1).\nExplanation is not required.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFollowing the 2010 earthquake in Haiti, people reported these impacts:\n\nA  Over 200,000 people killed by collapsing buildings\nB  A cholera outbreak killing thousands of people later in the year\nC  The main prison collapsed and prisoners escaped\nD  Around 1.5 million people made homeless as houses were destroyed\nE  Food prices rising as farm supply routes stayed blocked for months\n\nIdentify two primary impacts of the earthquake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `A (1)\nD (1)\nAccept C as an alternative to either A or D (collapse of the prison is a direct impact).\nIf more than two answers are given, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nThe 2010 Haiti earthquake (magnitude 7.0) killed over 200,000 people. The 2010 Chile earthquake (magnitude 8.8) was much stronger but killed about 525 people.\n\nExplain one reason why the Haiti earthquake caused far more deaths than the Chile earthquake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Haiti had poorly built houses / no building codes (1) so many concrete buildings collapsed and crushed people (1).\n• The focus in Haiti was shallow (about 13 km) and close to the capital (1) so shaking was very strong where the most people lived (1).\n• Haiti had few emergency services / little money (1) so trapped people were not rescued in time (1).\n• Chile had earthquake drills and preparation (1) so people knew how to protect themselves (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 7: a table of damage in one district of a city after an earthquake (data written for this practice question).\n\nTotal buildings in the district: 7,400\nBuildings that collapsed: 1,850\n\nUsing Figure 7, calculate the percentage (%) of buildings that collapsed. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1,850 ÷ 7,400 × 100 (1)\n= 25 (%) (1)\nAward 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic error.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nAfter the 2015 Nepal earthquake, search-and-rescue teams arrived from countries including the UK, India and China. Charities set up tents and distributed water purification tablets.\n\nExplain one short-term response to the 2015 Nepal earthquake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a response and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• International search-and-rescue teams were sent (1) to find and free people trapped under collapsed buildings (1).\n• Tents / temporary shelters were provided (1) because hundreds of thousands of homes had been destroyed (1).\n• Water purification tablets / clean water were supplied (1) to reduce the spread of disease such as cholera (1).\n• Helicopters were used to reach remote villages (1) because landslides had blocked mountain roads (1).\nDo not credit long-term responses, e.g. rebuilding schools.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nThe Hawaiian Islands lie near the centre of the Pacific plate, about 3,000 km from the nearest plate boundary. Only the youngest island in the chain, in the south-east, has active volcanoes.\n\nExplain one reason why volcanoes can form in Hawaii even though it is far from a plate boundary.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Hawaii lies above a hotspot / mantle plume (1) where a column of very hot magma rises from deep in the mantle and melts through the crust (1).\nAllow development: the plate moves over the fixed hotspot so new volcanoes form and older ones become extinct (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nEvery year on 1 September, millions of people in Japan take part in earthquake drills as part of Disaster Prevention Day.\n\nExplain one way preparation can reduce the impacts of an earthquake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a way and 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Earthquake drills teach people to drop, cover and hold on (1) so they are less likely to be injured by falling objects (1).\n• Households keep emergency kits with water, food and torches (1) so they can survive until help arrives (1).\n• Evacuation routes and meeting points are planned (1) so people can move quickly to safety, e.g. away from a tsunami (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nMount St Helens in the USA lies near the boundary where the small Juan de Fuca plate (oceanic) moves beneath the North American plate (continental).\n\nExplain why volcanoes form at this type of plate boundary.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 2 further marks for development.\ne.g.\n• The denser oceanic plate is subducted beneath the continental plate (1); as it sinks into the mantle it is heated by friction and the high temperature and melts / releases water that lowers the melting point (1); the molten rock (magma) is less dense so it rises through cracks in the crust and erupts at the surface (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 8: a cross-section across the coast of Chile from west to east (data written for this practice question). An ocean trench lies 100 km off the coast. Earthquake foci are shown as dots. Close to the trench the foci are 0–30 km deep. Beneath the coast they are 50–100 km deep. Beneath the Andes, 300 km inland, they are 150–250 km deep. The dots form a sloping band that dips eastwards beneath South America.\n\nDescribe the pattern of earthquake depth shown in Figure 8. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid descriptive point, with a maximum of 2 marks if no data is used.\ne.g.\n• Earthquakes get deeper from west to east / further inland (1)\n• Shallow earthquakes (0–30 km) near the trench (1)\n• Deep earthquakes (150–250 km) beneath the Andes, 300 km inland (1)\n• The foci form a narrow sloping band / line dipping east (1)\nExplanation (subduction zone) is not required but do not penalise.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nIn 2010 earthquakes struck both Chile (GDP per capita about US$10,000) and Haiti (GDP per capita about US$700).\n\nExplain one reason why the impacts of tectonic hazards are often greater in developing countries than in developed countries.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 2 further marks for development.\ne.g.\n• Developing countries have less money to spend on earthquake-resistant buildings (1) so many people live in poorly built houses made of unreinforced concrete or blocks (1) which collapse during strong shaking, causing many deaths (1).\n• Fewer emergency services / hospitals (1) so injured people cannot be treated quickly (1) and more die from injuries that could be treated (1).\n• Rapid, unplanned urban growth (1) means dense informal housing on unstable slopes (1) increasing the number of people exposed (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nThe San Andreas Fault runs for about 1,200 km through California. The Pacific plate and the North American plate are both moving north-west, but at different speeds.\n\nExplain why earthquakes occur at conservative plate boundaries.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 2 further marks for development.\ne.g.\n• The plates slide past each other (in the same direction at different speeds or in opposite directions) (1); friction makes them stick and pressure / stress builds up over time (1); when the pressure is suddenly released the plates jerk past each other, sending out seismic waves (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nMauna Loa (Hawaii) is a shield volcano. Mount Pinatubo (Philippines), which erupted violently in 1991, is a composite volcano.\n\nExplain two differences between shield volcanoes and composite volcanoes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each difference identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• Shield volcanoes have gentle slopes whereas composite volcanoes are steep-sided (1) because runny basalt lava flows a long way before cooling, while thick, viscous lava cools quickly near the vent (1).\n• Composite volcanoes erupt explosively but shield volcanoes erupt gently (1) because viscous magma traps gas until pressure is released violently (1).\n• Composite volcanoes are made of alternating layers of ash and lava, shield volcanoes mostly of lava (1) because explosive eruptions throw out ash and tephra between lava flows (1).\n• Shield volcanoes form at divergent boundaries / hotspots, composite at convergent boundaries (1) because of the type of magma produced there (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 9: a cross-section of the Earth showing that temperature increases from about 1,000 °C at the top of the mantle to about 5,500 °C at the centre. Arrows show heat flowing out from the core.\n\nExplain two ways that heat from the Earth's interior causes tectonic plates to move.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• Convection currents in the mantle (1): heat from radioactive decay in the core makes mantle rock rise, spread sideways and sink as it cools, dragging the plates above (1).\n• Ridge push (1): rising hot material at mid-ocean ridges forms new, higher crust which slides away from the ridge, pushing the plates apart (1).\n• Slab pull (1): oceanic crust cools and becomes denser away from the ridge, so at a trench it sinks into the mantle and pulls the rest of the plate behind it (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 10: a table comparing two earthquakes in 2010 (figures approximate; estimates vary between sources).\n\n | Haiti | Chile\nDate | 12 January 2010 | 27 February 2010\nMagnitude | 7.0 | 8.8\nFocus depth | 13 km | 35 km\nDeaths | about 230,000 | about 525\nGDP per capita | about US$700 | about US$10,000\n\nUsing Figure 10 and your own knowledge, explain two reasons why far fewer people died in Chile than in Haiti.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• Chile is wealthier (GDP per capita about 14 times higher) (1) so it could enforce strict building codes, meaning fewer buildings collapsed (1).\n• Chile's focus was deeper (35 km compared with 13 km) (1) so less energy reached the surface and shaking was weaker (1).\n• Chile had well-trained emergency services and earthquake drills (1) so people knew what to do and were rescued quickly (1).\n• Haiti's epicentre was close to the densely populated capital, Port-au-Prince (1) where many lived in poorly built housing (1).\nCredit use of data from Figure 10.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nThe 2015 Nepal earthquake was centred in the Himalayas, a steep mountain region. Tourism, including trekking and climbing on Mount Everest, was one of Nepal's main sources of income.\n\nExplain two secondary impacts of the 2015 Nepal earthquake.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each impact identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• Landslides (1) blocked roads, making it hard for aid to reach remote villages / buried villages such as Langtang (1).\n• An avalanche on Mount Everest (1) killed climbers and guides at base camp (1).\n• Fall in tourism (1) as visitors stayed away, reducing incomes for hotels, guides and porters (1).\n• Loss of income / jobs (1) as farmland, livestock and businesses were destroyed (1).\n• Risk of disease (1) because water supplies and sanitation were damaged and people lived in crowded shelters (1).\nDo not credit primary impacts.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFor a tectonic hazard you have studied in a developing or emerging country, explain two long-term responses to the hazard.\n\nName of hazard event: ......`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each response identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2). Responses must be long-term.\ne.g. Haiti 2010:\n• Rebuilding houses to stronger standards (1) so they are less likely to collapse in a future earthquake (1).\n• Building a new cholera treatment and water system (1) to reduce the spread of disease (1).\ne.g. Nepal 2015:\n• The National Reconstruction Authority provided grants to rebuild homes (1) with training for builders in earthquake-resistant methods (1).\n• Rebuilding schools and historic sites (1) to restore education and tourism income (1).\nMax 2 marks if no named event / responses are generic and not linked to a developing or emerging country.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nJapan has a network of over 1,000 seismometers. During the Tōhoku earthquake of 11 March 2011, millions of mobile phones received a warning before the strongest shaking reached Tokyo, and bullet trains were stopped automatically.\n\nExplain two ways that warning systems can reduce the impacts of tectonic hazards.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• Alerts give people seconds to take cover (1) so fewer are injured by falling objects (1).\n• Trains and lifts stop / gas supplies shut off automatically (1) reducing crashes and fires (1).\n• Tsunami warnings allow coastal residents to evacuate to higher ground (1) reducing deaths from flooding (1).\n• Volcano warnings allow evacuation before an eruption, e.g. Pinatubo 1991 (1) so fewer people are killed by pyroclastic flows / ash (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nIceland lies on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where the North American and Eurasian plates are moving apart by about 2 cm each year. Rift valleys and fissure eruptions are common.\n\nExplain two processes that take place at a divergent plate boundary.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each process identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• The plates move apart (driven by convection currents / ridge push) (1) leaving a gap / rift valley as the crust is stretched (1).\n• Magma rises from the mantle into the gap (1) where it cools to form new oceanic crust / basalt (1).\n• Volcanic eruptions (1) are mostly gentle because the basalt magma is runny and low in gas, forming shield volcanoes or fissures (1).\n• Small, shallow earthquakes (1) as the crust cracks and moves apart (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nThe 2011 earthquake in Christchurch, New Zealand, measured magnitude 6.2 with a focus about 5 km deep, yet it killed 185 people. Some deeper earthquakes of greater magnitude have caused far less damage.\n\nExplain two ways that the magnitude and depth of an earthquake affect its impacts.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• Higher-magnitude earthquakes release more energy (1) so the shaking is stronger and lasts longer, causing more buildings to collapse (1).\n• Shallow-focus earthquakes are more damaging (1) because the seismic waves lose less energy before reaching the surface (1).\n• Deep-focus earthquakes spread energy over a wider area (1) so shaking at any one place is weaker (1).\n• Larger magnitude increases the chance of secondary hazards such as tsunamis / landslides / liquefaction (1) adding to deaths and damage (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 11: a map of the Hawaiian–Emperor chain of islands and seamounts. The youngest island, Hawaii, is in the south-east and has active volcanoes. The islands get older towards the north-west: Maui about 1 million years, Oahu about 3 million years and Kauai about 5 million years. An arrow shows the Pacific plate moving north-west.\n\nUsing Figure 11, explain two ways a hotspot forms a chain of volcanic islands.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• A plume of hot magma rises from deep in the mantle at a fixed point (1) and melts through the crust to form a volcano on the sea floor that grows into an island (1).\n• The Pacific plate moves north-west over the stationary hotspot (1) so the volcano is carried away from the plume and becomes extinct (1).\n• A new volcano forms above the hotspot (1) so islands get older in a line to the north-west, e.g. Kauai about 5 million years (1).\nCredit use of evidence from Figure 11.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nYou have studied the effects of and responses to a tectonic hazard in a developed country and in a developing or emerging country.\n\nExplain two differences in the short-term responses in the two countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each difference identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2). Answers should refer to named events.\ne.g. Chile 2010 and Haiti 2010:\n• Chile used its own emergency services and army, whereas Haiti relied on international aid (1) because Haiti's government buildings and services were destroyed and it lacked money and equipment (1).\n• In Chile power and phone lines were restored within days / weeks, but in Haiti this took much longer (1) because Haiti's port and airport were damaged, slowing delivery of supplies (1).\n• Chile quickly set up temporary shelters, but in Haiti over a million people lived in makeshift camps (1) leading to the spread of disease (1).\nMax 2 marks if only one country is discussed.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nIn 1973 a fissure eruption began on the island of Heimaey, Iceland. Lava flowed towards the town and its fishing harbour. Workers pumped millions of tonnes of seawater onto the front of the lava flow.\n\nExplain two ways that protection can reduce the impacts of tectonic hazards.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way identified (max 2) and 1 further mark for development of each (max 2).\ne.g.\n• Spraying lava with water (as at Heimaey) (1) cools and hardens the front of the flow so it slows or stops before reaching buildings / the harbour (1).\n• Building barriers or channels to divert lava (1) away from towns, e.g. on Mount Etna (1).\n• Earthquake-resistant buildings / retrofitting older buildings (1) so they do not collapse and kill people (1).\n• Tsunami walls / sea walls (1) reduce flooding of coastal settlements (1).\n• Strengthened roofs on buildings near volcanoes (1) so they do not collapse under heavy ash (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 10: a table comparing two earthquakes in 2010 (figures approximate; estimates vary between sources).\n\n | Haiti | Chile\nMagnitude | 7.0 | 8.8\nFocus depth | 13 km | 35 km\nDeaths | about 230,000 | about 525\nPeople made homeless | about 1.5 million | about 800,000\nGDP per capita | about US$700 | about US$10,000\n\nAssess the extent to which the level of development of a country determines the impacts of a tectonic hazard.\n\nUse Figure 10 and examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Development affects building quality: Chile's codes meant most modern buildings survived a magnitude 8.8 earthquake; Haiti's unreinforced concrete collapsed.\n• Development affects emergency response and recovery: Chile used its own services; Haiti depended on aid and suffered cholera afterwards.\n• Data: Haiti's earthquake was weaker (7.0 vs 8.8) yet deaths were over 400 times higher.\n• Other factors: focus depth (Haiti shallow, 13 km), distance of the epicentre from large cities (Haiti close to Port-au-Prince), time of day, population density, secondary hazards (Chile's tsunami).\n• Developed countries still suffer large economic losses (Chile about US$30 billion; Japan 2011 tsunami) so development may reduce deaths more than economic cost.\n• Judgement on how far development is the main factor.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited use of the figure. A judgement, if given, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Elements of knowledge and understanding that are mostly relevant and accurate. Some use of the figure and examples to support points. An unbalanced assessment with a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate and relevant knowledge and understanding applied throughout. Uses the figure and named examples effectively. A balanced assessment that weighs development against other factors, leading to a fully supported judgement.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nIn Japan, earthquake hazards are managed by prediction and warning (seismometer networks and phone alerts), protection (building codes and tsunami walls) and preparation (drills on Disaster Prevention Day). In 2011 the Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami still killed over 18,000 people.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of different methods of managing tectonic hazards.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Prediction: earthquakes cannot be accurately predicted in time or place; volcano monitoring is more reliable (e.g. Pinatubo 1991 evacuation saved thousands). Early warning gives only seconds to a minute for earthquakes.\n• Protection: building codes are very effective (most Tokyo high-rises survived 2011) but are expensive, so less used in developing countries; tsunami walls were overtopped in 2011.\n• Preparation: drills and emergency kits are cheap and reduce injuries; hazard maps and land-use planning keep people away from the riskiest areas.\n• Effectiveness depends on wealth, governance and the size of the event: no method stopped the 2011 deaths, but deaths would have been far higher without them.\n• A combination of methods is more effective than any one.\n• Judgement on which method(s) are most effective, and for whom.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited reference to examples. A judgement, if given, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly relevant knowledge of management methods, with some supporting examples. An unbalanced evaluation; the judgement is partly supported.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate and relevant knowledge applied throughout, with well-chosen examples. A balanced evaluation of strengths and limitations of different methods leading to a fully supported judgement.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nIn 1991 Mount Pinatubo, a composite volcano at a convergent boundary in the Philippines, produced one of the largest eruptions of the 20th century, but about 60,000 people had been evacuated beforehand. Eruptions of Kīlauea, a shield volcano above the Hawaiian hotspot, rarely kill anyone, but in 2018 its lava destroyed over 700 homes.\n\nAssess the reasons why some volcanic eruptions are more hazardous than others.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Plate boundary and magma type: convergent boundaries produce viscous, gas-rich magma and explosive eruptions with ash and pyroclastic flows; divergent boundaries and hotspots produce runny basalt lava and gentle eruptions.\n• Volcano type: composite volcanoes are more dangerous to life than shield volcanoes; lava flows are slow so people can usually escape but property is destroyed.\n• Secondary hazards: lahars (e.g. after Pinatubo), ash fall collapsing roofs, gases.\n• Human factors: population density near the volcano, quality of monitoring and warning, ability to evacuate, level of development.\n• Pinatubo shows monitoring can make even a huge eruption less deadly; Kīlauea shows a gentle eruption can still have large economic impacts.\n• Judgement on whether physical or human factors are more important.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge, some inaccurate. Limited use of the information given. Judgement, if given, unsupported.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly relevant knowledge of physical and/or human reasons, with some use of examples. Unbalanced assessment; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate and relevant knowledge of both physical and human reasons applied throughout, using the examples effectively. Balanced assessment with a fully supported judgement.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nYou have studied the responses to a tectonic hazard in a developed country and in a developing or emerging country, for example Chile 2010 and Nepal 2015.\n\nExamine why the short-term and long-term responses to tectonic hazards are different in countries at different levels of development.`, marks: 8, command: 'Examine', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Short-term: developed countries have trained emergency services, equipment and money to respond quickly (Chile restored most power and water within about 10 days); developing countries rely on international aid and NGOs (Nepal received search-and-rescue teams from India, China, UK), which can be slow to arrive.\n• Physical access: remote mountain villages in Nepal cut off by landslides and blocked roads, delaying aid.\n• Long-term: developed countries can fund rebuilding to strict codes and insurance pays for reconstruction; in Nepal reconstruction grants were delayed and many families were still in temporary shelters a year or more later.\n• Governance and corruption, debt, dependence on donors affect long-term planning.\n• Some developing countries respond well with community preparation; some developed countries struggle with very large events.\n• Interconnections between wealth, governance, physical geography and scale of the event.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge, some inaccurate. Generic, with little reference to named examples.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly relevant knowledge with some reference to named events. Some explanation of reasons for differences but connections are partial.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate and relevant knowledge of named events applied throughout. Detailed examination of a range of reasons for differences in both short- and long-term responses, with clear connections.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nFigure 12: a world map of plate boundaries and the location of large earthquakes and active volcanoes. Most earthquakes and volcanoes lie along plate boundaries, for example around the Pacific Ocean and along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. Volcanoes are also shown in Hawaii and Yellowstone (USA), far from any boundary. Large earthquakes are shown in the Himalayas, where two continental plates collide, but there are no volcanoes there.\n\nAssess the extent to which plate boundaries explain the global distribution of tectonic hazards.\n\nUse Figure 12 and your own knowledge.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Most earthquakes and volcanoes occur at plate boundaries: subduction at convergent boundaries (Ring of Fire, Andes, Japan); rifting at divergent boundaries (Iceland); sliding at conservative boundaries (San Andreas) gives earthquakes but no volcanoes.\n• Type of boundary explains which hazards occur: collision boundaries (Himalayas) give large earthquakes but no volcanoes because no crust is subducted and melted.\n• Hotspots / mantle plumes (Hawaii, Yellowstone) cause volcanoes far from plate boundaries.\n• Some earthquakes occur within plates on old faults.\n• Hazards only become disasters where people live, so population distribution affects where impacts occur.\n• Judgement: plate boundaries explain most, but not all, of the distribution.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge, some inaccurate. Limited use of the figure. Judgement, if given, unsupported.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly relevant knowledge of boundaries and their hazards, with some use of the figure. Unbalanced assessment; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate knowledge of different boundary types and hotspots applied throughout, using the figure effectively. Balanced assessment with a fully supported judgement.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nNepal lies where the Indian plate is colliding with the Eurasian plate. Kathmandu's population grew rapidly before 2015, and many new buildings were added without following the national building code. After the 2015 earthquake, governments and charities spent large sums on emergency relief.\n\nEvaluate the view that long-term planning is more important than short-term responses in reducing deaths from tectonic hazards.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Long-term planning (building codes, retrofitting schools and hospitals, land-use zoning, hazard mapping, education and drills) reduces deaths before the event happens; e.g. Chile and Japan.\n• In Nepal, unenforced building codes and rapid urban growth increased deaths; well-built structures survived.\n• Short-term responses (search and rescue, medical care, shelter, clean water) save people trapped or injured and prevent secondary deaths from disease; most people rescued alive are found within the first 72 hours.\n• Long-term planning is costly and difficult in developing countries; aid often favours relief rather than prevention.\n• The two are linked: preparation also improves short-term response (trained rescue teams, stockpiles).\n• Judgement on which matters more, and in which contexts.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge, some inaccurate. Little reference to examples. Judgement, if given, unsupported.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly relevant knowledge of both planning and responses, with some use of examples. Unbalanced evaluation; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate and relevant knowledge applied throughout, using named examples effectively. Balanced evaluation of both, leading to a fully supported judgement.` },
      { q: `Section A — Hazardous Earth.\n\nInformation (data written for this practice question unless stated): Kathmandu, the capital of Nepal, has a population of over 1.5 million and is growing quickly. It lies about 80 km from the epicentre of the magnitude 7.8 earthquake of April 2015, which killed about 9,000 people across Nepal (real figure). Scientists expect another large earthquake in the region, but cannot say when. A survey of the city found that 60% of schools and 40% of hospital buildings are not earthquake-resistant, and 70% of new homes are built without an engineer's inspection. The city council has a limited budget and must choose one priority.\n\nOption 1 — Retrofit schools and hospitals: strengthen existing schools and hospitals with steel bracing and reinforced walls so they remain standing and can be used as shelters.\nOption 2 — Warning and preparation: install a network of seismometers linked to phone alerts and run earthquake drills and first-aid training in every school and neighbourhood.\nOption 3 — Enforce building codes and land-use planning: inspect all new buildings, fine builders who break the national building code and stop building on steep, unstable slopes.\n\nSelect the option that you think would be the best long-term plan for reducing the impacts of a future earthquake in Kathmandu. Justify your choice.\n\nUse the information given and knowledge and understanding from your study of tectonic hazards to support your answer.\n\n(12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `No option is better than another; credit is for the quality of justification.\n\nIndicative content:\nOption 1: protects the most vulnerable (children, patients); schools and hospitals are needed for shelter and treatment after the quake; quick, visible impact; but costly per building and leaves most homes unsafe.\nOption 2: relatively cheap; drills reduce injuries (Japan); but earthquakes cannot be predicted and alerts give only seconds of warning, so deaths from building collapse remain high; depends on phone coverage.\nOption 3: tackles the main cause of deaths (collapsing poorly built homes) as the city grows; long-lasting; but slow to make a difference, needs trained inspectors and may be undermined by corruption or by people unable to afford stronger homes.\nBetter answers compare the chosen option with at least one rejected option, refer to evidence (e.g. 70% of new homes uninspected) and to examples such as Chile, Haiti or Japan, and consider the city's limited budget and timescale.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge, some inaccurate. Limited use of the information. A choice is stated with little or no justification.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant knowledge and partial use of the information. A choice is justified with some reasons but the argument is unbalanced and rejected options are barely considered.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge applied to the information. A clear, reasoned justification that considers some strengths and weaknesses of the chosen option and at least one other.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate and thorough knowledge applied throughout. Information and examples are used effectively. A balanced and coherent justification that evaluates the chosen option against the alternatives, leading to a well-supported decision.\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4 marks): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; uses a wide range of specialist terms precisely.\nIntermediate performance (2–3 marks): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; uses a good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning; uses a limited range of specialist terms.\n0 marks: no response, response unrelated, or errors severely hinder meaning.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.4': {
    name: 'Development Dynamics',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 1 is a table of Gini coefficient values for four countries (data written for this practice question):\nCountry A 0.27; Country B 0.35; Country C 0.48; Country D 0.63\n\nUsing Figure 1, which country has the most unequal distribution of income?\nA  Country A\nB  Country B\nC  Country C\nD  Country D`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `D — Country D (1)\nThe higher the Gini coefficient (closer to 1), the more unequal the distribution of income.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 2 shows the GDP per capita of two countries in 2022 (data written for this practice question):\nCountry X US$42,000; Country Y US$2,100\n\nCalculate the ratio of GDP per capita between Country X and Country Y.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `20 : 1 (1)\n(42,000 ÷ 2,100 = 20)\nAllow 20 or 1 : 20 if clearly the right way round.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 3 lists the five stages of Rostow's model of economic development in order, with one stage left blank:\nStage 1 Traditional society → Stage 2 Preconditions for take-off → Stage 3 ? → Stage 4 Drive to maturity → Stage 5 Age of high mass consumption\n\nWhich of the following is the missing Stage 3?\nA  Dependency\nB  Take-off\nC  Industrial decline\nD  Core–periphery`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B — Take-off (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 4 shows life expectancy at birth in India (data written for this practice question):\n1990: 58.5 years; 2020: 69.9 years\n\nCalculate the increase in life expectancy in India between 1990 and 2020.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `11.4 (years) (1)\n(69.9 − 58.5 = 11.4)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nThe Human Development Index (HDI) combines measures of health, education and income into a single value between 0 and 1. Norway's HDI is above 0.95, while Somalia's is below 0.40.\n\nState one measure of education used in the HDI.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Any one from (1):\n• mean (average) years of schooling of adults\n• expected years of schooling for children\nAllow literacy rate (used in older versions of the HDI).\nDo not accept 'number of schools' or 'education' unqualified.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 5 is a population pyramid for India in 2021 (data written for this practice question). The widest bars are for the 10–14 and 15–19 age groups, each about 9% of the population. Bars narrow steadily above age 30, and the 80+ age group is under 1% of the population.\n\nUsing Figure 5, identify the age group with the largest share of India's population.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `10–14 or 15–19 (1)\nAllow 'young people / teenagers aged 10–19'.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nEconomists studying India use the Gini coefficient to compare the incomes of rich and poor households, alongside GDP per capita.\n\nDefine the term 'Gini coefficient'.`, marks: 1, command: 'Define', markScheme: `A measure of how evenly / unevenly income (or wealth) is distributed within a country (on a scale of 0 to 1, where 0 = perfect equality) (1)\nDo not accept 'a measure of how rich a country is'.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 6 shows India's GDP per capita (data written for this practice question):\n2000: US$440; 2022: US$2,370\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) increase in India's GDP per capita between 2000 and 2022. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (2,370 − 440) ÷ 440 × 100 (1)\n1 mark for answer: 438.6(%) (1)\n(1,930 ÷ 440 = 4.386…)\nAllow 1 mark for 438.63… not rounded / 439.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 7 shows the HDI of seven countries in 2021 (data written for this practice question):\nBangladesh 0.633; Brazil 0.721; Chad 0.488; Kenya 0.585; Mexico 0.802; India 0.690; Switzerland 0.940\n\nCalculate the median HDI of these seven countries. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for putting the values in rank order: 0.488, 0.585, 0.633, 0.690, 0.721, 0.802, 0.940 (1)\n1 mark for answer: 0.690 (1)\nAllow 0.69.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 8 shows India's age structure in 2021 (data written for this practice question):\naged 0–14: 26%; aged 15–64: 67%; aged 65 and over: 7%\n\nThe dependency ratio = (people aged 0–14 + people aged 65 and over) ÷ people aged 15–64 × 100.\n\nUsing Figure 8, calculate India's dependency ratio. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (26 + 7) ÷ 67 × 100 (1)\n1 mark for answer: 49.3 (1)\n(33 ÷ 67 = 0.4925…)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 9 is a table showing the birth rate (per 1,000 people per year) of Niger, India and Japan in 1980, 2000 and 2020.\n\nDescribe one other suitable method of data presentation to show how the birth rates of these three countries have changed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a suitable method and 1 mark for describing how it would be used, e.g.\n• a multiple line graph (1) with year on the x-axis and birth rate on the y-axis, using a different coloured line for each country (1)\n• a compound / grouped bar chart (1) with three bars (one per country) for each year, so heights can be compared (1)\nDo not accept pie chart (data are rates, not parts of a whole).` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIn 2021 Qatar had a GDP per capita of over US$60,000, but many migrant workers there earned low wages and lived in crowded accommodation.\n\nExplain one limitation of using GDP per capita as a measure of development.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a limitation and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• it is an average (mean) (1) so it hides inequality between rich and poor people / regions (1)\n• it only measures income / wealth (1) so it ignores quality of life such as health, education or human rights (1)\n• it misses the informal economy (1) so it may underestimate income in poorer countries where many people work informally (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIn 2021 Sri Lanka and Iraq had similar GDP per capita (about US$4,000–5,000), but Sri Lanka had a much higher HDI.\n\nExplain one reason why the HDI may be a more useful measure of development than GDP per capita.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• HDI is a composite measure combining health, education and income (1) so it gives a broader picture of people's quality of life than income alone (1)\n• HDI includes life expectancy (1) which shows how well a country's wealth is used for healthcare / clean water (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nChad and Mali are landlocked countries in the Sahel region of Africa. Both have a low HDI.\n\nExplain one way in which being landlocked can slow a country's development.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• no direct access to the sea / ports (1) so it is more expensive and slower to export goods and import raw materials (1)\n• it depends on neighbouring countries for trade routes (1) so conflict or tariffs in those countries can cut off trade (1)\n• it is less attractive to TNCs / foreign investors (1) because transport costs are higher (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nBefore independence, many African countries such as Ghana were colonies of European countries. Ghana exported raw cocoa to Britain, where it was processed into chocolate.\n\nExplain one way in which colonialism has contributed to uneven development.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• colonies were set up to export raw materials / primary products (1) so profits from processing (adding value) went to the colonial power, not the colony (1)\n• colonial powers took resources and wealth (1) leaving former colonies with little industry or infrastructure at independence (1)\n• borders were drawn by colonial powers (1) which has led to conflict that slows development (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 10 describes Frank's dependency theory. Developed countries form a 'core' (metropole) and developing countries form a 'periphery' (satellite). Arrows show raw materials and profits flowing from the periphery to the core, and manufactured goods and loans flowing from the core to the periphery.\n\nUsing Figure 10, explain one reason why, according to Frank, periphery countries stay poor.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• periphery countries sell cheap raw materials and buy expensive manufactured goods (1) so wealth flows to the core, keeping the periphery poor / dependent (1)\n• loans from the core create debt (1) so money needed for development is spent on repayments (1)\n• core-based TNCs take profits back to the core (1) so little wealth stays in the periphery (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nRostow's model, published in 1960, was based mainly on the way the UK and the USA developed.\n\nExplain one criticism of Rostow's model of development.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a criticism and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• it assumes all countries follow the same path (1) but countries such as China or India have developed in different ways / skipped stages (1)\n• it ignores colonialism / global trade relationships (1) which may hold developing countries back (as argued by Frank) (1)\n• it measures development only by economic growth (1) and ignores social and environmental change / inequality (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 11 is a map of South Asia. India is shown bordered by Pakistan to the north-west, China, Nepal and Bhutan to the north, and Bangladesh and Myanmar to the east. The Himalaya mountains lie along its northern border, and the Arabian Sea, Indian Ocean and Bay of Bengal surround the peninsula.\n\nUsing Figure 11, describe the location of India.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid locational point, up to 2 marks, e.g.\n• in South Asia / north of the Equator (1)\n• bordered by Pakistan to the north-west (1)\n• bordered by China / Nepal / Bhutan to the north (1)\n• bordered by Bangladesh / Myanmar to the east (1)\n• has coasts on the Arabian Sea (west) and Bay of Bengal (east) / Indian Ocean to the south (1)\n• Himalayas form the northern border (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 12 shows the percentage of India's workforce in each employment sector (approximate data written for this practice question):\n1991 — primary 64%, secondary 15%, tertiary 21%\n2021 — primary 44%, secondary 25%, tertiary 31%\n\nDescribe how India's employment structure changed between 1991 and 2021. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for describing a change and 1 mark for supporting data, e.g.\n• the primary sector fell (1) by 20 percentage points, from 64% to 44% (1)\n• the tertiary sector grew (1) by 10 percentage points, from 21% to 31% (1)\n• the secondary sector grew from 15% to 25% (1)\nMax 1 mark without data.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nMany TNCs, such as IBM, Accenture and Microsoft, have opened offices and IT centres in Bengaluru (Bangalore), India.\n\nExplain one reason why TNCs choose to locate in India.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• large supply of well-educated, English-speaking graduates (1) so call centres / IT services can serve customers in the UK and USA (1)\n• lower wage costs than in HICs (1) which increases the TNC's profits (1)\n• a large and growing middle-class market (1) so TNCs can sell products to Indian consumers (1)\n• government policies since 1991 have opened the economy to FDI / special economic zones (1) making it easier and cheaper to invest (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIn November 2023, air quality in Delhi reached 'severe' levels, and schools were closed for several days.\n\nExplain one environmental impact of rapid economic development in India.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for an impact and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• air pollution (1) from growing numbers of vehicles, factories and coal-fired power stations (1)\n• water pollution, e.g. of the Ganges / Yamuna (1) from untreated industrial waste and sewage from growing cities (1)\n• increased greenhouse gas emissions (1) because economic growth relies heavily on burning coal (1)\n• loss of habitats / farmland (1) as cities, roads and industry expand (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nBarefoot College in Tilonia, Rajasthan, trains women from rural villages, many of whom cannot read or write, to build, install and repair solar lighting systems for their own communities.\n\nExplain one advantage of a bottom-up development project such as Barefoot College.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for an advantage and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• local people are involved in decisions (1) so the project meets their real needs (1)\n• it uses appropriate / intermediate technology (1) which local people can afford and repair themselves, so it lasts (1)\n• it builds skills / empowers women (1) which increases incomes and status in the community (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nThe Sardar Sarovar Dam on the Narmada River in Gujarat, India, was a government-led project completed in 2017. It provides irrigation water, drinking water and hydroelectric power, but its reservoir flooded the land of tens of thousands of families.\n\nExplain one disadvantage of a top-down development project such as the Sardar Sarovar Dam.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a disadvantage and 1 mark for explanation, e.g.\n• local people were displaced / lost homes and farmland (1) often with little say or inadequate compensation (1)\n• very expensive (1) so the country may have to borrow money and take on debt (1)\n• benefits may go to cities / industry / wealthier farmers far away (1) rather than to the poorest people affected (1)\n• environmental damage, e.g. flooding of forests / changes to river ecosystems (1) reducing fish stocks and fertile silt downstream (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIn 2020, India's government spent about 2% of GDP on health care, while the UK spent about 10% (approximate figures written for this practice question).\n\nSuggest one reason why infant mortality rates are higher in India than in the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• less government spending on health (1) so fewer doctors / hospitals / midwives, especially in rural areas (1)\n• less access to clean water and sanitation (1) so more babies die of diarrhoea / waterborne diseases (1)\n• lower incomes / poverty (1) so mothers may be malnourished or unable to afford treatment (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 13 is an incomplete pie chart of the destinations of India's exported goods in 2022 (data written for this practice question). It shows USA (18%), United Arab Emirates (8%), Netherlands (4%) and Other (55%); the remaining 15% is blank.\n\nComplete Figure 13 using this information: China 6%, Bangladesh 5%, UK 4%. Describe how you would divide and label the blank 15% of the circle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for converting percentages to angles / using a protractor: 1% = 3.6°, so China 21.6°, Bangladesh 18°, UK 14.4° (1)\n1 mark for dividing the blank 15% (54°) into three accurate segments measured from the centre, starting at an existing line (1)\n1 mark for labelling / shading each segment and adding it to the key (1)\nAllow segments in any order. Allow ±1° tolerance described.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 14 shows GDP per capita and life expectancy for six countries in 2021 (data written for this practice question):\nNiger US$590, 62 years; Kenya US$2,000, 66 years; India US$2,250, 70 years; Brazil US$7,500, 73 years; Mexico US$10,000, 75 years; UK US$46,000, 81 years\n\nDescribe the relationship shown in Figure 14. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying the relationship: positive — as GDP per capita increases, life expectancy increases (1)\n1 mark for using data, e.g. Niger has the lowest GDP per capita (US$590) and lowest life expectancy (62 years), while the UK has the highest of both (US$46,000, 81 years) (1)\n1 mark for a further descriptive point, e.g. life expectancy rises quickly at low incomes but more slowly at high incomes (an increase of over US$35,000 from Mexico to the UK adds only 6 years) / India has a higher life expectancy than Kenya despite similar GDP per capita (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIndia's total fertility rate (average number of children per woman) fell from about 5.6 in 1971 to about 2.0 in 2021 (approximate figures written for this practice question).\n\nExplain one reason why the fertility rate in India has fallen.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, up to 2 further marks for development / explanation, e.g.\n• more girls stay in education (1) so women marry later and want careers before having children (1), which shortens the time spent having children and reduces family size (1)\n• improved health care has lowered infant mortality (1) so parents do not need to have as many children to ensure some survive (1), which has reduced the desired family size (1)\n• urbanisation (1) — in cities children are an extra cost (housing, schooling) rather than workers on a farm (1), so families choose to have fewer children (1)\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nTata Group began in India and now owns companies around the world, including Jaguar Land Rover in the UK, bought in 2008.\n\nExplain one way in which globalisation has helped India's economy to grow.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, up to 2 further marks for development / explanation, e.g.\n• Indian TNCs such as Tata / Infosys have expanded overseas (1) gaining access to new markets and technology (1), with profits returning to India to be reinvested (1)\n• outsourcing of IT and call-centre work from HICs to India (1) creates millions of jobs for graduates (1), which raises incomes and spending, multiplying growth (1)\n• foreign direct investment from TNCs (1) builds factories and infrastructure (1), creating jobs and tax revenue for the government (1)\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nCountries can be compared using single economic indicators such as GNI per capita, or composite measures such as the HDI.\n\nExplain two limitations of using a single indicator to measure a country's development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each limitation and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• a single indicator only measures one aspect of development (1) e.g. GNI per capita shows income but not health or education, so a rich country may still have poor quality of life (1)\n• averages hide inequality (1) so wealthy elites can raise the figure while many people remain in poverty (1)\n• data may be unreliable in developing countries (1) because of poor record keeping or a large informal economy (1)\n• it can be distorted by a single resource, e.g. oil (1) so a country can appear developed without wider improvements in people's lives (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 15 shows demographic data for two countries in 2021 (data written for this practice question):\nCountry P — birth rate 45 per 1,000; death rate 12 per 1,000; infant mortality 70 per 1,000 live births\nCountry Q — birth rate 9 per 1,000; death rate 10 per 1,000; infant mortality 4 per 1,000 live births\n\nUsing Figure 15, explain two ways in which demographic data can show a country's level of development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• infant mortality reflects health care (1) — Country P's rate of 70 per 1,000 suggests poor access to doctors, clean water and vaccinations, typical of a less developed country, while Country Q's 4 per 1,000 suggests a developed one (1)\n• birth rate reflects education / access to contraception / the role of women (1) — Country P's high birth rate (45) suggests children are needed as farm workers or to support parents, whereas in Country Q (9) women have careers and family planning (1)\n• death rate / natural increase indicates stage of the demographic transition (1) — Country P's population is growing rapidly (natural increase 33 per 1,000), while Country Q's is slowly declining (1)\nCredit use of data from Figure 15.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nMalawi in southern Africa has frequent droughts and floods, and much of its population relies on rain-fed farming of maize.\n\nExplain two physical factors that can cause uneven development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each factor and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• climate / unreliable rainfall / drought (1) causes crop failure, so farmers lose income and food shortages lead to poor health (1)\n• natural hazards such as tropical cyclones / floods / earthquakes (1) destroy infrastructure, and money must be spent on rebuilding rather than development (1)\n• being landlocked (1) makes exporting goods more expensive (1)\n• water-borne / tropical diseases such as malaria (1) reduce the number of people able to work and increase health costs (1)\n• lack of natural resources (1) means less income from exports (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIn 2022, unprocessed cocoa beans made up a large share of Côte d'Ivoire's exports, while most chocolate was manufactured in Europe. Many developing countries also owe large debts to banks and governments in developed countries.\n\nExplain two human (economic or historical) causes of uneven development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each cause and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• dependence on exports of primary products (1) which have low and fluctuating prices, so countries earn little and cannot plan investment (1)\n• trade barriers / tariffs set by developed countries on processed goods (1) discourage developing countries from adding value through manufacturing (1)\n• debt (1) means a large share of government income goes on interest repayments instead of schools and hospitals (1)\n• colonialism (1) left former colonies with economies built around exporting raw materials and little industry (1)\n• conflict / corruption (1) discourages investment and diverts money away from development (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIn 2023, thousands of people from countries such as Guatemala and Honduras tried to migrate north to the USA, where average incomes were many times higher.\n\nExplain two consequences of uneven development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each consequence and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• international migration (1) as people move from poorer to richer countries for jobs and higher wages, which can cause a brain drain in the source country (1)\n• differences in health / life expectancy (1) because poorer countries cannot afford healthcare, clean water and sanitation (1)\n• a widening wealth gap (1) as wealthier countries can invest more in education and technology, so they grow faster (1)\nAllow consequences at a national (regional) scale, e.g. rural–urban migration.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nRostow's modernisation theory and Frank's dependency theory both try to explain why some countries are more developed than others.\n\nExplain two differences between Rostow's and Frank's theories of development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each difference and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• Rostow sees development as a series of five stages all countries can pass through (1), whereas Frank argues developing countries are kept poor and cannot easily 'catch up' (1)\n• Rostow says investment and trade with developed countries help countries take off (1), whereas Frank says trade and investment from the core exploit the periphery and take wealth away (1)\n• Rostow explains poverty by internal factors such as lack of investment or traditional farming (1), whereas Frank explains it by external factors such as colonialism and unfair trade (1)\nA difference must make a clear comparison to gain both marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nSince 1991, India's government has reduced tariffs and opened many industries to foreign investment.\n\nFor a named emerging country, explain two ways globalisation has changed its economy.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), with reference to a named emerging country, e.g. India:\n• growth of the tertiary / quaternary sector (1) as TNCs outsource IT and call-centre work to cities such as Bengaluru and Hyderabad (1)\n• increased FDI (1) which has built factories (e.g. car manufacturing near Chennai), creating jobs and multiplier effects (1)\n• growth of Indian TNCs such as Tata or Infosys (1) which invest abroad and bring profits back to India (1)\n• rapid GDP growth (1) making India one of the world's largest economies (1)\nCredit other named emerging countries, e.g. China, Brazil, Nigeria. Max 2 if no named country.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nUnilever's Indian company, Hindustan Unilever, employs thousands of people in India and sells soap, tea and other products in villages and cities across the country.\n\nExplain two advantages of TNCs for an emerging country you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each advantage and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• create jobs (1) with regular wages that increase spending in the local economy (multiplier effect) (1)\n• bring investment / infrastructure (1) such as roads and power supplies that also benefit local people (1)\n• transfer skills and technology (1) so local workers are trained and can set up their own businesses (1)\n• pay taxes (1) which the government can spend on education and healthcare (1)\nCredit reference to named examples. Max 2 if only generic.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nAccording to the 2011 census, India had about 377 million urban residents. Since then more people have moved to cities such as Mumbai, and more women have entered higher education.\n\nFor a named emerging country, explain two social changes that have resulted from rapid economic development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each change and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• rapid urbanisation (1) as people move from rural areas to cities for jobs in factories and services, leading to growth of informal settlements such as Dharavi in Mumbai (1)\n• improved education / literacy (1) because growing tax revenue funds more schools and parents see the value of qualifications for jobs (1)\n• changing role of women (1) with more women working and marrying later, which lowers the birth rate (1)\n• growth of the middle class / changing culture (1) with more spending on consumer goods, cars and Western-style food (1)\nMax 2 if no named country.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nMaharashtra, the state containing Mumbai, has a GDP per capita several times that of Bihar in north-east India, where most people work in farming.\n\nExplain two reasons why some regions of an emerging country have developed faster than others.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• coastal location / ports (1) so regions such as Maharashtra attract trade and TNCs, creating jobs (1)\n• better infrastructure and services in cities (1) so investors locate there and wages are higher, while rural states lag behind (1)\n• educated workforce in urban regions (1) attracts IT and service industries, which pay more than farming (1)\n• cumulative causation (1) — once a region grows, it attracts more migrants and investment, widening the gap (1)\n• rural regions depend on farming (1), which is affected by monsoon failure and low prices (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIndia is expected to have one of the world's largest working-age populations by 2030, with a median age of under 30.\n\nExplain two ways in which India's population structure may affect its future development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way and 1 further mark for explanation of each (2 × 2), e.g.\n• a large working-age population (demographic dividend) (1) can boost economic growth and tax revenue if enough jobs are created (1)\n• a youthful population needs investment in schools and training (1) which puts pressure on government spending (1)\n• if jobs are not created, unemployment may rise (1) leading to poverty, social unrest or emigration (1)\n• a growing elderly population in future (1) will increase pension and health care costs (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nThe Sardar Sarovar Dam on the Narmada River, Gujarat, is a top-down project. It was planned to irrigate about 1.8 million hectares of farmland and generate up to 1,450 MW of electricity, but tens of thousands of people were displaced.\n\nExplain one advantage and one disadvantage of a top-down development project you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for an advantage and 1 for explanation; 1 mark for a disadvantage and 1 for explanation (2 + 2), e.g.\nAdvantages:\n• provides irrigation (1) so farmers can grow more crops and increase incomes / food security (1)\n• generates hydroelectric power (1) supplying electricity to industry and homes, supporting economic growth (1)\n• provides drinking water to towns and villages in dry areas (1) improving health (1)\nDisadvantages:\n• displacement of people (1) who lose homes, land and livelihoods, often with little compensation (1)\n• very high cost / debt (1) money that could have been spent on smaller local projects (1)\n• environmental damage (1) e.g. flooding of forest and farmland, loss of river habitats (1)\nCredit other named top-down projects.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nFigure 16 shows development data for three countries in 2021 (data written for this practice question):\nCountry R — GDP per capita US$62,000; HDI 0.85; Gini 0.41\nCountry S — GDP per capita US$9,000; HDI 0.77; Gini 0.31\nCountry T — GDP per capita US$1,200; HDI 0.52; Gini 0.39\n\nAssess the usefulness of different measures of development. Use evidence from Figure 16 and your own knowledge.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks). Mark using levels.\nIndicative content:\n• GDP per capita is easy to calculate and compare, but is an average and hides inequality; Country R has the highest GDP per capita but also the highest Gini (0.41), so wealth is not shared evenly\n• HDI is a composite measure (health, education, income) and gives a broader picture of quality of life; Country S has a much lower GDP per capita than R but a relatively high HDI (0.77)\n• the Gini coefficient shows inequality within a country, which neither GDP nor HDI shows; but it does not show how rich or poor a country is overall (Country T and R have similar Gini values but very different incomes)\n• demographic measures (infant mortality, birth rate, life expectancy) reflect health care and the role of women\n• all measures rely on accurate data, which may be hard to collect in developing countries\n• judgement: a combination of measures is most useful; HDI is the most useful single measure because it combines several indicators\nLevel 3 (6–8): thorough assessment of the usefulness of measures; uses Figure 16 data effectively; balanced, well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some assessment of more than one measure; some use of evidence; partially supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited description of measures; little or no use of data; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIndia's economy grew slowly until 1991. Since then, with growing investment, industry and services, it has become one of the world's five largest economies by GDP, although hundreds of millions of people still work in farming.\n\nFor a named emerging country, evaluate how far Rostow's model helps to explain its development.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks). Mark using levels.\nIndicative content:\n• India appears to fit Rostow's stages: a traditional farming society; preconditions such as infrastructure and education; take-off after 1991 economic reforms; drive to maturity with growth of manufacturing and services\n• investment, as Rostow predicts, led to growth, e.g. FDI and the growth of Indian TNCs\n• but India has largely skipped stages, moving straight from farming to services (IT, call centres) rather than through heavy industry\n• development is very uneven: urban states such as Maharashtra may be at a later stage than rural Bihar, so a single stage does not describe the whole country\n• Frank's theory may help explain colonial history (British rule) and continuing dependence on foreign TNCs\n• Rostow focuses on economic growth and ignores inequality, environmental damage and social change\n• judgement: Rostow's model partly explains the pattern of growth but is too simple to explain India's development fully\nLevel 3 (6–8): balanced evaluation of the model's strengths and weaknesses applied to a named country, with detailed support; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some evaluation with partial application to a named country; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): description of Rostow's model with little application; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0: no rewardable material.\nMax Level 2 if no named emerging country.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nNiger, in the Sahel, has a low HDI. It is landlocked, has frequent droughts and has a history of French colonial rule, and much of its export income comes from uranium.\n\nAssess the relative importance of physical and human factors in causing uneven development.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks). Mark using levels.\nIndicative content:\nPhysical factors\n• climate — drought and unreliable rainfall reduce farm output and income\n• landlocked location increases trade costs\n• natural hazards destroy infrastructure and divert spending to rebuilding\n• disease (e.g. malaria) reduces productivity\nHuman factors\n• colonialism left economies dependent on exporting primary products\n• dependence on primary exports such as uranium, with fluctuating world prices\n• debt and unfair trade / tariffs\n• conflict, corruption and poor governance discourage investment\n• judgement: physical factors may be a starting point but human factors often decide whether a country develops — e.g. landlocked Switzerland is highly developed, while human factors such as debt and dependency make physical problems harder to overcome\nLevel 3 (6–8): balanced assessment of both groups of factors, well supported by examples; substantiated judgement on relative importance.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some assessment of both types of factor, or a fuller account of one; partially supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited points about one or two factors; little or no judgement.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nTNCs such as Coca-Cola, Hyundai and Microsoft have invested in India. From 2002, villagers in Plachimada, Kerala, protested that a Coca-Cola bottling plant had reduced and polluted local groundwater; the plant stopped production in 2004.\n\nFor a named emerging country, assess the positive and negative impacts of TNCs.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks). Mark using levels.\nIndicative content:\nPositive\n• create jobs (e.g. car factories near Chennai, IT centres in Bengaluru) with higher wages than farming\n• FDI, infrastructure and tax revenue for the government\n• transfer of skills and technology; growth of Indian suppliers (multiplier effect)\n• consumer goods and services become available\nNegative\n• profits often go back to the TNC's home country\n• some jobs are low-paid, with poor working conditions\n• environmental damage, e.g. water depletion and pollution at Plachimada\n• TNCs may leave if costs rise elsewhere, so jobs are insecure\n• local businesses may be unable to compete\n• judgement: e.g. positives outweigh negatives at national scale (growth, jobs), but negatives can be significant for local communities and the environment; impacts depend on government regulation\nLevel 3 (6–8): balanced assessment of positive and negative impacts with detailed, accurate reference to a named country; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some assessment with some reference to a named country; partially supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited, generic points; little or no judgement.\n0: no rewardable material.\nMax Level 2 if no named emerging country.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nIn India, the government-led Sardar Sarovar Dam cost billions of US dollars and displaced tens of thousands of people, while the Barefoot College in Rajasthan has trained hundreds of rural women as 'solar engineers' at low cost.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of top-down and bottom-up approaches to development in an emerging country.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks). Mark using levels.\nIndicative content:\nTop-down (e.g. Sardar Sarovar Dam)\n• large scale — irrigation, drinking water and hydroelectric power for millions of people\n• supports national economic growth and industry\n• but very expensive, may lead to debt; displaced people often lose out; environmental damage; benefits may go to cities and richer farmers\nBottom-up (e.g. Barefoot College, solar lighting, microfinance, NGOs)\n• low cost, uses appropriate technology, involves local people and meets local needs\n• empowers women and builds skills, so benefits last\n• but small scale; slow to reach the whole population; relies on NGO / charity funding\n• judgement: bottom-up schemes may be more effective at reducing poverty for the poorest people, while top-down schemes are needed for national infrastructure; a combination is most effective\nLevel 3 (6–8): balanced evaluation of both approaches, supported by accurate named examples; substantiated judgement on effectiveness.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some evaluation of both approaches, or a fuller account of one; some use of examples; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited description of one or both approaches; little or no judgement.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nSince 1991, India's GDP has grown rapidly and hundreds of millions of people have moved out of extreme poverty. Over the same period, cities such as Delhi have become some of the most polluted in the world, and the gap between rich and poor has widened.\n\nFor a named emerging country, assess whether the benefits of rapid economic development outweigh the costs.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks). Mark using levels.\nIndicative content:\nBenefits\n• economic: rising GDP and incomes; growth of manufacturing and services; more jobs\n• social: reduced extreme poverty, rising literacy, improved life expectancy, growing middle class\n• political / international: greater global influence, e.g. G20 membership, BRICS\nCosts\n• environmental: air pollution in Delhi, water pollution of the Ganges and Yamuna, rising CO₂ emissions from coal, loss of habitats\n• social: widening inequality between rich and poor and between regions (e.g. Maharashtra vs Bihar); growth of informal settlements such as Dharavi\n• health problems from pollution\n• judgement: e.g. benefits outweigh costs at national scale, but the poorest groups and the environment bear many of the costs; costs may grow in future unless managed\nLevel 3 (6–8): balanced assessment of benefits and costs, with detailed, accurate reference to a named country; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some assessment of benefits and costs with some reference to a named country; partially supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited points; little or no named country detail; little or no judgement.\n0: no rewardable material.\nMax Level 2 if no named emerging country.` },
      { q: `Section B — Development Dynamics.\n\nThe World Bank and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) lend money to developing countries, often with conditions attached. Countries such as the UK also give bilateral aid, and TNCs invest directly in developing countries.\n\nAssess the role of international aid and investment in reducing uneven development.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks). Mark using levels.\nIndicative content:\n• aid can fund schools, health care, clean water and infrastructure, which improve quality of life and HDI\n• emergency aid helps countries recover from disasters\n• IGO loans (World Bank, IMF) fund large projects, but conditions (e.g. cutting government spending) may harm the poor, and loans add to debt\n• aid may be tied to buying goods from the donor, or lost through corruption, and can create dependency\n• FDI by TNCs creates jobs, transfers technology and increases tax revenue, but profits often leave the country\n• bottom-up NGO projects may reach the poorest people more effectively\n• judgement: aid and investment can reduce uneven development, but only if well targeted and well governed; trade reform and debt relief may be more important\nLevel 3 (6–8): balanced assessment of the role of aid and investment, supported by examples; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some assessment of aid and / or investment; partially supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited description of aid or investment; little or no judgement.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Making a Geographical Decision — development choices for Nepal.\n\nResource information (approximate figures written for this practice question): Nepal is a landlocked country in the Himalayas between India and China, with about 30 million people. GDP per capita is about US$1,300 and HDI about 0.60. About 60% of people work in farming, often on steep terraced slopes. Money sent home by Nepalis working abroad equals about a quarter of GDP. Nepal's rivers have great hydroelectric potential, and the 2015 Gorkha earthquake damaged many roads, schools and homes.\n\nThree options for the government to speed up Nepal's development:\nOption 1 — build a large hydroelectric dam funded by a World Bank loan and sell electricity to India (top-down).\nOption 2 — fund small-scale community projects such as micro-hydro schemes, microfinance loans and farmer cooperatives run by NGOs (bottom-up).\nOption 3 — create special economic zones with tax breaks to attract TNCs and foreign direct investment.\n\nSelect the option you think would be best for Nepal's development. Justify your choice. Use the information given and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer.\n(12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (8 marks). Mark using levels. No option is the correct one; credit a well-justified choice that considers the others.\nIndicative content:\nOption 1\n• uses Nepal's river resources; large electricity supply for industry and export income from India\n• but high cost and debt to the World Bank; risk of displacement; vulnerable to earthquakes / landslides; benefits may not reach remote villages\nOption 2\n• low cost, appropriate technology; meets the needs of rural farmers (about 60% of workers); empowers women; micro-hydro brings electricity to remote valleys\n• but small scale, slow, relies on NGO funding; may do little for national GDP\nOption 3\n• jobs, skills, tax revenue and multiplier effects; could reduce the need to migrate abroad\n• but landlocked location and poor roads make Nepal less attractive to TNCs; profits leave the country; low-wage jobs; possible environmental damage\nLinks to theory: Rostow (investment for take-off) vs Frank (dependency on foreign capital); top-down vs bottom-up approaches\nLevel 4 (10–12): a well-justified decision that uses the information and wider knowledge in detail; considers the strengths and weaknesses of the chosen option and why other options were rejected; coherent, fully substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9): a justified decision with clear use of the information; some consideration of other options; mostly supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6): a decision with some justification; limited use of the information or wider knowledge; little consideration of alternatives.\nLevel 1 (1–3): a decision with little or no justification; isolated points copied from the information.\n0: no rewardable material.\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4): spelling and punctuation are consistently accurate; grammar is used to control meaning effectively; a wide range of specialist terms is used adeptly.\nIntermediate performance (2–3): spelling and punctuation are considerably accurate; grammar controls meaning in most cases; a good range of specialist terms is used appropriately.\nThreshold performance (1): spelling and punctuation are reasonably accurate; grammar does not significantly hinder meaning; a limited range of specialist terms is used.\n0: errors severely hinder meaning, or response is not related to the question.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.5': {
    name: 'Challenges of an Urbanising World',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nThe number of cities in the world with a population of 10 million or more grew from 2 in 1950 to 33 in 2018 (UN World Urbanization Prospects).\n\nWhich one of the following is the definition of a megacity?\nA  A city that is the capital of its country\nB  An urban area with a population of over 10 million people\nC  A city that grows by more than 1 million people a year\nD  An urban area with a population of over 1 million people`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (an urban area with a population of over 10 million people) (1).\nNo other answer is acceptable.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 1 is a table showing the estimated annual rate of population growth (% per year) for five cities, 2020–2025 (data written for this practice question):\nKinshasa 4.4 | Lagos 3.9 | Dhaka 3.3 | Mumbai 1.8 | Tokyo −0.1\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the range of the annual growth rates.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `4.5 (percentage points / % per year) (1).\nWorking: 4.4 − (−0.1) = 4.5. Do not accept 4.3 (which ignores the negative sign).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 2 gives estimated population densities in Mumbai, India (figures rounded for this practice question):\nDharavi (informal settlement): 480 000 people per km²\nGreater Mumbai (whole city): 20 000 people per km²\n\nCalculate the ratio of the population density of Dharavi to that of Greater Mumbai.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `24 : 1 (1).\nWorking: 480 000 ÷ 20 000 = 24. Accept 1 : 0.04 or 24 times.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nEvery day, migrants from rural Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh and Bihar arrive at Mumbai's railway stations. Many say they left villages where farm incomes had fallen after years of poor monsoon rainfall.\n\nState one pull factor that attracts migrants to Mumbai.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for any one pull factor, e.g.:\n• more job opportunities / jobs in factories, ports, construction, services (1)\n• higher wages than in rural areas (1)\n• better access to schools / hospitals / healthcare (1)\n• friends or family already living in the city (1)\n• better access to services such as electricity or clean water (1)\nDo not accept push factors such as drought or falling farm incomes.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nBetween 1950 and 2020 the population of Detroit, USA, fell, but since the 2010s new apartments and businesses have opened in its downtown area and some young professionals have moved back into the city centre.\n\nWhich one of the following describes people moving back to live in the centre of a city?\nA  Suburbanisation\nB  Counter-urbanisation\nC  Re-urbanisation\nD  Rural–urban migration`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (re-urbanisation) (1).\nNo other answer is acceptable.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 3 shows the percentage of the population living in urban areas in 2018 for six world regions (UN World Urbanization Prospects 2018):\nNorth America 82% | Latin America and the Caribbean 81% | Europe 74% | Oceania 68% | Asia 50% | Africa 43%\n\nUsing Figure 3, identify the region with the lowest percentage of its population living in urban areas.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Africa (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 4 is an incomplete bar chart of the percentage of the population living in urban areas in five regions in 2018. The vertical axis runs from 0 to 100% with gridlines every 10%. Bars have been drawn for North America (82%), Europe (74%), Latin America (81%) and Africa (43%). The bar for Asia is missing.\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 4 to show that 50% of Asia's population lived in urban areas.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each of:\n• draw a bar the same width as the other bars, in the gap labelled Asia / alongside the other bars (1)\n• bar drawn up to the 50% gridline / exactly halfway up the axis, and shaded in the same way as the other bars (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 5 shows the population of Lagos, Nigeria (UN estimates, rounded):\n1990: 4.8 million\n2020: 14.4 million\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the population of Lagos between 1990 and 2020. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for correct working and 1 mark for the correct answer:\n• (14.4 − 4.8) ÷ 4.8 × 100 / 9.6 ÷ 4.8 × 100 (1)\n• = 200% (1)\nAn answer of 200% with no working = 1 mark only. Do not accept 300% (this is the 2020 population as a % of 1990).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn 1950, 30% of the world's population lived in urban areas. By 2018 this had risen to 55%, with the fastest growth in cities in Asia and Africa.\n\nExplain one reason why the rate of urbanisation is faster in developing countries than in developed countries.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and 1 further mark for explaining it, e.g.:\n• High rates of rural–urban migration (1) because people are pushed out of the countryside by poverty, lack of services or mechanisation of farming, and pulled to cities by jobs in new industries (1).\n• Developed countries are already highly urbanised (1) so there are few rural people left to move to cities / some people are moving out to the countryside (counter-urbanisation) (1).\n• Cities in developing countries have high natural increase (1) because migrants are mainly young adults of child-bearing age, so birth rates are high while death rates are falling (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn Lagos, Nigeria, about 60% of the population is aged under 25, and the city's birth rate is much higher than its death rate.\n\nExplain one way in which natural increase causes the population of a megacity to grow.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid point and 1 further mark for development, e.g.:\n• Many people in the city are young adults of child-bearing age (1) so there are many births each year, which adds more people than are lost through deaths (1).\n• Death rates have fallen due to better healthcare / clean water in cities (1) while birth rates remain high, so more people are born than die (1).\n• Young migrants arrive in the city and then start families there (1) so migration adds to natural increase in the next generation (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nA survey of 40 recent migrants living in an informal settlement in Mumbai found that 31 had moved from rural villages (data written for this practice question).\n\nGive two push factors that may have caused people to leave rural areas in India.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid push factor, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• crop failure / drought / unreliable monsoon rainfall (1)\n• low incomes / poverty from farming (1)\n• lack of jobs / unemployment in rural areas (1)\n• mechanisation of farming reducing the need for labourers (1)\n• lack of services such as schools, hospitals or electricity (1)\n• debt / land shortages / land divided between family members (1)\nDo not accept pull factors (e.g. more jobs in the city).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn many megacities in developing and emerging countries, a large share of people work as street vendors, rickshaw drivers, waste pickers or in small workshops that are not registered with the government.\n\nExplain one reason why so many people work in the informal sector in a megacity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and 1 further mark for development, e.g.:\n• There are not enough formal jobs for the number of migrants arriving (1) so people create their own work in order to earn some income (1).\n• Many migrants lack the qualifications / skills / documents needed for formal jobs (1) so informal work such as street trading, which needs little training, is the only option (1).\n• Informal businesses are cheap and easy to start (1) as they need little money and no licence, so families can begin earning straight away (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nMumbai is in western India. The original city was built on seven small islands that were joined together by land reclamation, and it lies next to a large, deep, sheltered harbour on the Arabian Sea.\n\nDescribe the site of Mumbai.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid point about the site (the land the city is built on), up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• on a peninsula / island (Salsette Island) on the west coast of India (1)\n• next to a natural deep-water, sheltered harbour (1)\n• built on reclaimed land joining seven islands (1)\n• low-lying land, close to sea level / with areas of marshland and creeks (1)\n• surrounded by the sea on three sides, so space to expand is limited (1)\nPoints about situation (e.g. links to the rest of India) are not creditworthy here.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 6 describes a simplified land-use map of Mumbai. The Central Business District (CBD) and the old port are at the southern tip of the peninsula. Older industrial areas and the informal settlement of Dharavi lie in the centre of the city, between two main railway lines. Newer offices are in Bandra-Kurla Complex, just north-west of Dharavi. Large newer residential suburbs stretch north along the railway lines, and the city's main airport is in the north.\n\nUsing Figure 6, describe the location of Dharavi.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid point from Figure 6, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• in the centre / middle of the city (1)\n• between the two main railway lines (1)\n• next to / south-east of the Bandra-Kurla Complex office area (1)\n• north of the CBD / old port (1)\n• surrounded by older industrial areas (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nMumbai is the headquarters of the Reserve Bank of India and the Bombay Stock Exchange. Road and rail routes link it to Pune, Delhi and other Indian cities, and Jawaharlal Nehru Port across the harbour is one of India's busiest container ports.\n\nExplain one way in which Mumbai's situation has helped it to grow.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a feature of situation and 1 further mark for explaining how it led to growth, e.g.:\n• Mumbai is on the west coast facing the Arabian Sea and trade routes to the Middle East and Europe (1) so it became a major port, attracting trading companies and jobs (1).\n• It is linked by rail and road to the rest of India (1) so goods can be moved to and from a huge market, encouraging industry and migrants to locate there (1).\n• It is a national hub for finance and air travel (1) so TNCs and banks set up offices there, creating jobs that attract migrants (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn parts of Dharavi, Mumbai, residents share public taps that run for only a few hours a day, and hundreds of people can share a single toilet block.\n\nExplain one problem caused by a lack of clean water and sanitation in informal settlements.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a problem and 1 further mark for development, e.g.:\n• Spread of water-borne diseases such as cholera, typhoid or diarrhoea (1) because human waste contaminates water supplies and open drains (1).\n• Children miss school / adults lose work time (1) because they have to queue for water or are often ill (1).\n• People must buy water from private sellers (1) which costs far more than piped water and takes money away from food or education (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 7 is a table of the number of people per toilet seat in five districts of an informal settlement in a megacity (data written for this practice question):\nDistrict A 58 | District B 120 | District C 85 | District D 210 | District E 97\n\nCalculate the mean number of people per toilet seat. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for correct working and 1 mark for the correct answer:\n• (58 + 120 + 85 + 210 + 97) ÷ 5 / 570 ÷ 5 (1)\n• = 114 (people per toilet seat) (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 8 shows the population of Detroit, USA (US Census):\n1950: 1 849 568\n2020: 639 111\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in Detroit's population between 1950 and 2020. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for correct working and 1 mark for the correct answer:\n• (1 849 568 − 639 111) ÷ 1 849 568 × 100 / 1 210 457 ÷ 1 849 568 × 100 (1)\n• = 65.4% (1)\nAccept 65.4% only for the answer mark (not 65 or 65.45).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nDetroit, USA, was a major centre of car manufacturing in the 1950s. As factories closed and jobs moved elsewhere, many residents left the city for the suburbs and other states.\n\nExplain one reason why the population of some cities in developed countries falls over time.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and 1 further mark for development, e.g.:\n• Deindustrialisation (1): factories close or move abroad to countries with cheaper labour, so workers leave to find jobs elsewhere (1).\n• Suburbanisation (1): better transport and car ownership allow people to live in larger, cheaper homes in the suburbs and commute (1).\n• Counter-urbanisation (1): people move to rural areas or small towns for a quieter environment, helped by home working / improved internet (1).\n• Decline in quality of the inner city (1): crime, poor housing or pollution push families to leave (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn Mumbai, the women's savings groups Mahila Milan, working with the charity SPARC and the National Slum Dwellers Federation, have designed, built and run community toilet blocks in informal settlements.\n\nDescribe one feature of a bottom-up strategy used to improve the quality of life in a megacity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid feature and 1 further mark for development, e.g.:\n• It is run by local people / community groups or NGOs rather than the government (1), e.g. residents decide where toilet blocks go and look after them (1).\n• It is usually small-scale and low-cost (1), using local labour and materials, so it can be started quickly (1).\n• It improves conditions where people already live (in-situ) (1) so communities and informal businesses are not broken up (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 9 shows the percentage of the world's population living in urban areas (UN World Urbanization Prospects 2018; 2030 and 2050 are projections):\n1950 30% | 1970 37% | 1990 43% | 2010 52% | 2018 55% | 2030 60% | 2050 68%\n\nDescribe the trend shown in Figure 9. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 3 marks. Maximum 2 marks if no data is used.\n• The urban percentage increases throughout / every year shown (1).\n• It rises from 30% in 1950 to 55% in 2018 (1).\n• It passes 50% / more than half of people are urban between 1990 and 2010 (1).\n• It is projected to reach 68% by 2050 (1).\n• The rate of increase is fairly steady at about 6–9 percentage points every 20 years / slows slightly after 2018 (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 10 is an incomplete pie chart showing the employment of working people in an informal settlement in a megacity (data written for this practice question). The segment for 'Formal sector jobs' (25%) has been drawn. The rest of the circle is blank.\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 10 using this information: informal sector 60%, unemployed 15%.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each of the following, up to 3 marks:\n• convert percentages into degrees by multiplying by 3.6: informal sector 60% = 216°, unemployed 15% = 54° (1)\n• use a protractor, starting from the edge of the existing 'Formal sector jobs' segment, to measure 216° and draw a line from the centre to the edge (1)\n• the remaining 54° segment is unemployed / segments drawn in order (largest first, clockwise) (1)\n• shade / colour each segment differently and add a key or labels (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nMumbai Metro Line 1, an 11-kilometre elevated railway built by the state government with a private company, opened in 2014. It links Versova in the west with Ghatkopar in the east, crossing routes that used to take over an hour by road.\n\nExplain one way in which a top-down strategy has improved the quality of life for people in a megacity.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a way and up to 2 further marks for explanation, e.g.:\n• New metro lines reduce journey times (1) so workers spend less time commuting and have more time for family or rest (1), and can reach jobs in other parts of the city, raising incomes (1).\n• Transport schemes reduce traffic congestion (1) which cuts vehicle exhaust emissions (1) and so improves air quality and health (1).\n• Rehousing schemes give families permanent flats with water and toilets (1) reducing the spread of disease (1) and giving security of tenure so people invest in their homes (1).\nAccept other top-down examples (e.g. Lagos BRT bus system, Mumbai Trans Harbour Link) if linked to quality of life.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nThe population of the Mumbai Metropolitan Region grew from about 8 million in 1971 to over 20 million by the 2020s, much faster than new homes, water pipes and sewers could be built.\n\nExplain one challenge that rapid population growth creates for providing services in a megacity.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a challenge and up to 2 further marks for explanation, e.g.:\n• Water supply cannot keep up with demand (1), so many people in informal settlements share standpipes or buy expensive water from vendors (1), and illegal connections lead to leaks and contamination (1).\n• Not enough school or hospital places (1) because the city authorities do not have the money / tax income to build them fast enough (1), so many children leave school early, which limits their job chances (1).\n• Waste collection is overwhelmed (1), so rubbish is dumped on open ground or in rivers such as the Mithi River (1), which blocks drains and increases flood and disease risk (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn 2018, 55% of the world's population lived in urban areas, compared with 30% in 1950. The UN projects that 68% will be urban by 2050.\n\nExplain two reasons why the world's urban population is increasing.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason identified and 1 further mark for each explanation, up to 2 marks per reason (2 × 2).\n• Rural–urban migration (1): people move to cities for better-paid jobs in factories and services and better access to schools and healthcare (1).\n• Natural increase in cities (1): migrants are mostly young adults, so birth rates are high, while better healthcare in cities lowers death rates (1).\n• Industrialisation / economic development in emerging countries such as China and India (1): new factories and TNC investment create jobs concentrated in cities (1).\n• Push factors in rural areas (1): mechanisation of farming, drought or conflict reduce rural jobs, forcing people to leave (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn 1950 there were only two megacities, New York and Tokyo. In 1990 there were 10, and by 2018 there were 33, most of them in Asia, Africa and Latin America, e.g. Delhi, Dhaka, Lagos and São Paulo.\n\nExplain two reasons why most new megacities are in developing or emerging countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason identified and 1 further mark for each explanation (2 × 2).\n• Very large rural populations moving to cities (1): developing countries still have many rural people pushed by poverty and pulled by city jobs, so migration is very high (1).\n• High natural increase (1): young city populations and high birth rates mean the population grows even without migration (1).\n• Rapid economic growth / industrialisation in emerging countries (1): TNCs and manufacturing concentrate in a few large cities, e.g. in India and China, drawing in workers (1).\n• Primate city effect (1): investment and government are concentrated in one dominant city, e.g. Lagos or Dhaka, so it attracts most migrants (1).\n• Developed countries' cities grew earlier and have stopped growing quickly (1) as people move to suburbs or rural areas (counter-urbanisation) (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 11 shows the employment structure of two cities (data written for this practice question):\nCity X (developed country): primary 1%, secondary 14%, tertiary 70%, quaternary 15%; informal sector 5% of workers.\nCity Y (developing country megacity): primary 4%, secondary 30%, tertiary 62%, quaternary 4%; informal sector 65% of workers.\n\nUsing Figure 11, explain two ways in which the urban economies of cities in developed and developing countries differ.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each difference identified from Figure 11 and 1 further mark for explaining it (2 × 2).\n• City Y has a much larger informal sector (65% compared with 5%) (1) because there are not enough formal jobs for the large number of migrants, so people create their own work such as street trading (1).\n• City Y has more secondary (manufacturing) employment (30% compared with 14%) (1) because TNCs have moved factories to developing countries where labour costs are lower (1).\n• City X has more quaternary jobs (15% compared with 4%) (1) because it has a highly educated workforce and universities, attracting research, ICT and finance (1).\nMaximum 2 marks if no use of Figure 11.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFor a named megacity in a developing or emerging country, explain two ways in which its site and situation have helped it to grow.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way identified and 1 further mark for each explanation (2 × 2). Maximum 3 marks if no named megacity or answer is generic.\nExample (Mumbai):\n• Deep-water, sheltered natural harbour on the Arabian Sea (1) allowed the British to develop a major port in the 19th century, attracting trade, cotton mills and migrant workers (1).\n• West-coast situation facing trade routes to the Middle East and Europe (1), especially after the Suez Canal opened in 1869, so Mumbai became India's gateway for imports and exports (1).\n• Good rail and road connections inland to the rest of India (1) meaning goods and people move easily, so industries and banks locate there (1).\nExample (Lagos): coastal lagoon site with a natural harbour (1) made it a major port and former capital of Nigeria, attracting government jobs and businesses (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn Mumbai, average life expectancy in some informal settlements is several years lower than in wealthy areas such as Malabar Hill, where people live in high-rise apartments with private security and piped water.\n\nFor a named megacity in a developing or emerging country, explain two reasons why the quality of life varies in different parts of the city.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason identified and 1 further mark for each explanation (2 × 2). Maximum 3 marks if no reference to a named megacity.\n• Differences in income / type of employment (1): people in the CBD with formal jobs in finance can afford better housing, healthcare and private schools, while informal workers in Dharavi earn low, irregular incomes (1).\n• Differences in access to services (1): informal settlements are often illegal, so the city does not provide piped water, sewers or waste collection, leading to disease (1).\n• Differences in environmental quality (1): informal settlements are often built on hazardous land, such as next to the Mithi River or near the Deonar landfill, exposing people to floods and pollution (1).\n• Differences in housing tenure (1): squatters lack legal ownership, so they do not invest in improving their homes and fear eviction (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nMumbai has grown northwards from the southern tip of its peninsula. New suburbs have developed along the suburban railway lines, while informal settlements have spread onto marshland, beside railway tracks and on the edges of the airport.\n\nExplain two ways in which rapid growth has affected the pattern of land use in a named megacity.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way identified and 1 further mark for each explanation (2 × 2).\n• Growth along transport routes (1): because the peninsula limits space, new housing and industry follow the railway lines north, creating long corridors of development (1).\n• Informal settlements on marginal / hazardous land (1): poor migrants cannot afford formal housing, so they build on any unused land such as marshes or beside railways (1).\n• New business centres away from the old CBD (1): the CBD became congested and expensive, so offices moved to new districts like Bandra-Kurla Complex (1).\n• Sprawl into the surrounding region / satellite towns (1): e.g. Navi Mumbai was planned across the harbour to take population and offices away from the crowded city (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nThe Dharavi Redevelopment Project is a government-led scheme, awarded to the Adani Group in 2022, to replace much of Dharavi's housing with high-rise apartment blocks. Eligible residents are to receive free flats, and new offices, roads and services are planned.\n\nExplain one advantage and one disadvantage of this top-down strategy.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for an advantage identified and 1 further mark for its explanation; 1 mark for a disadvantage identified and 1 further mark for its explanation (2 × 2).\nAdvantages, e.g.:\n• Families get permanent flats with their own water and toilets (1), which reduces disease and gives them legal security of tenure (1).\n• Large investment can provide proper roads, sewers and drainage across the whole area (1), something residents cannot afford themselves (1).\nDisadvantages, e.g.:\n• Residents who cannot prove they lived there before the cut-off date may not get a flat (1), so many people could be evicted or moved to the edge of the city, far from work (1).\n• High-rise flats have no space for the workshops / recycling businesses run from homes (1), so informal jobs and incomes may be lost (1).\n• Residents have little say in the plans (1), so the scheme may not meet their needs and may break up communities (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn Mumbai, the Akanksha Foundation runs after-school classes for children from informal settlements, and Mahila Milan women's groups run savings schemes so that families can pay for housing improvements.\n\nExplain two ways in which bottom-up strategies can improve the quality of life for people in a megacity.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each way identified and 1 further mark for each explanation (2 × 2).\n• Improving education (1): free or low-cost classes improve literacy and skills, so young people can get better-paid formal jobs (1).\n• Improving sanitation (1): community-built toilet blocks reduce open defecation and the spread of diseases such as diarrhoea (1).\n• Access to savings and small loans (1): families can upgrade homes with concrete floors or tin roofs, making them safer and drier (1).\n• Building community involvement (1): residents choose projects themselves, so schemes meet real needs and are looked after, so they last longer (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn 2016, fires at Mumbai's Deonar landfill, one of India's oldest and largest rubbish dumps, covered nearby districts in smoke for weeks. The Mithi River, which flows through the city, is heavily polluted by sewage and industrial waste.\n\nFor a named megacity in a developing or emerging country, explain two environmental problems caused by its rapid growth.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each problem identified and 1 further mark for each explanation (2 × 2). Maximum 3 marks if no reference to a named megacity.\n• Air pollution (1): millions of vehicles, factories and waste fires release particulates, causing smog and breathing problems such as asthma (1).\n• Water pollution (1): untreated sewage and industrial waste flow into rivers such as the Mithi, killing wildlife and spreading disease (1).\n• Waste disposal (1): the city cannot collect all its rubbish, so it is dumped at sites like Deonar, where it burns or leaches toxins into soil and water (1).\n• Increased flood risk (1): building over marshes and mangroves and blocked drains mean rainwater cannot drain away, as in the July 2005 Mumbai floods (1).\n• Loss of green space / mangroves (1): land is reclaimed for housing, destroying habitats that protect the coast (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 12 shows the main reasons given by 50 recent migrants for moving to Mumbai (data written for this practice question):\nFind work / higher wages 28 | Join family already in Mumbai 10 | Education 6 | Escape drought / crop failure 4 | Other 2\n\nUsing Figure 12 and your own knowledge, explain two reasons why rural–urban migration to megacities continues.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason identified and 1 further mark for each explanation (2 × 2). Maximum 3 marks if no use of Figure 12.\n• Work / higher wages is the most common reason (28 of 50 / 56%) (1): even informal jobs in Mumbai usually pay more than farm work, so migrants can send money home (1).\n• Family links (10 of 50 / 20%) (1): relatives already in the city provide a place to stay and contacts for jobs, making the move less risky (chain migration) (1).\n• Education (6 of 50 / 12%) (1): cities have more secondary schools and colleges, so young people move to gain qualifications for better jobs (1).\n• Push factors such as drought (4 of 50 / 8%) (1): crop failure in rural areas leaves people with no income, forcing them to leave (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 13 shows the percentage of the population living in urban areas in two countries (UN World Urbanization Prospects 2018):\nUK: 1950 79% | 2018 83%\nNigeria: 1950 9% | 2018 50%\n\nAssess the reasons why rates of urbanisation differ between developed and developing countries.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2 knowledge and understanding; AO3 application, using Figure 13):\n• Figure 13: Nigeria's urban share rose by 41 percentage points (9% to 50%), while the UK's rose by only 4 (79% to 83%).\n• Developed countries urbanised during the Industrial Revolution, so most people already live in cities and there are few rural people left to move.\n• In developed countries, counter-urbanisation and suburbanisation slow urban growth, as people move to smaller towns and villages, helped by cars and home working.\n• Developing countries have large rural populations pushed by poverty, drought and lack of services, and pulled by jobs in cities, e.g. Lagos.\n• High natural increase in developing-world cities: young migrants and falling death rates.\n• Economic growth and TNC investment in emerging countries concentrate jobs in cities.\n• Assessment: e.g. the stage of economic development is the most important reason, because it controls both how many rural people remain and how many urban jobs exist; natural increase is becoming relatively more important in African cities.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies understanding to deconstruct information, providing a logical and balanced assessment of several reasons. Makes full use of Figure 13 and reaches a supported judgement about which reasons are most important.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies some understanding; assessment is partially balanced and some reasons are explained. Some use of Figure 13; a judgement may be made but is not fully supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of reasons; little or no use of Figure 13; an unbalanced or incomplete assessment.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFor a named megacity in a developing or emerging country, assess the relative importance of migration and natural increase in causing its population to grow.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2; AO3). Credit any named megacity, e.g. Mumbai or Lagos.\n• Migration: national migration from rural areas (e.g. Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh and Bihar to Mumbai), pushed by drought, low farm incomes and lack of services; pulled by jobs in the port, finance, Bollywood, construction and the informal sector.\n• International migration may matter for some cities (e.g. Lagos attracts migrants from neighbouring West African countries).\n• Natural increase: migrants are mainly young adults, so many children are born in the city; better healthcare lowers death rates.\n• Links between the two: migration feeds later natural increase, so their effects cannot be fully separated.\n• Change over time: in the early growth of Mumbai migration was dominant; as fertility in India has fallen and the city has aged, migration and natural increase are now closer in importance. In Lagos, high fertility keeps natural increase very important.\n• Judgement: e.g. migration was the main cause of growth, but natural increase sustains it.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge of a named megacity. Assessment of both factors is logical and balanced, considers links between them and/or change over time, and reaches a well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; both factors considered but explanation is partial or one-sided; some reference to the named megacity; a judgement may be stated but is only partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated, generic points about migration or natural increase; little or no place detail; no meaningful judgement.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn Mumbai, more than 7 million passenger journeys are made every day on suburban trains designed for far fewer people. Over 40% of the city's population is estimated to live in informal settlements, many without a reliable water supply.\n\nFor a named megacity in a developing or emerging country, examine the challenges that rapid growth creates for the people who live there.`, marks: 8, command: 'Examine', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2; AO3). Credit any named megacity, e.g. Mumbai or Lagos.\n• Housing: shortage of affordable housing leads to informal settlements such as Dharavi, with overcrowding, no legal tenure and risk of eviction.\n• Services: lack of piped water and sanitation causes disease; schools and clinics are overcrowded.\n• Transport: overcrowded suburban trains (thousands of deaths a year on Mumbai's railway tracks), severe traffic congestion and long commutes.\n• Pollution: air pollution from traffic and industry; water pollution in the Mithi River; waste fires at Deonar landfill.\n• Employment: many people work in the informal sector with low, irregular pay and no protection.\n• Hazards: building on marshland and blocked drains increase flooding, e.g. July 2005 floods.\n• Examination: challenges are linked (e.g. poor housing and sanitation both cause ill health) and affect the poor most; some challenges, such as transport, affect all residents.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate and detailed knowledge of a named megacity; examines a range of challenges, showing how they are connected and who they affect, reaching a coherent conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; describes and explains several challenges with some place detail; limited links between challenges.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated, generic points about city problems; little or no place detail.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nMumbai's government has invested in large top-down projects, including the Mumbai Metro, the Mumbai Trans Harbour Link (a 21.8 km sea bridge opened in 2024), and the Slum Rehabilitation Scheme, in which developers build free flats for slum dwellers in return for permission to build and sell extra floor space.\n\nFor a named megacity in a developing or emerging country, evaluate the success of top-down strategies in improving the quality of life for its residents.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2; AO3). Credit any named megacity and named top-down strategies.\nSuccesses, e.g.:\n• Metro lines reduce journey times and congestion for commuters, and electric trains reduce air pollution.\n• New bridges and roads (Trans Harbour Link, Coastal Road) improve connections to new jobs and housing in Navi Mumbai.\n• Slum rehabilitation gives some families permanent flats with water, toilets and legal tenure.\nLimitations, e.g.:\n• Very expensive, so they take many years and may not reach the poorest areas.\n• Rehousing towers are often poorly built, crowded together with little light or ventilation, and may lack space for home businesses.\n• Many residents are not eligible and are displaced; communities and informal jobs are broken up.\n• New roads and bridges may mainly benefit car owners / wealthier residents.\n• Evaluation: e.g. top-down strategies are successful at city-wide infrastructure but less successful at improving the lives of the poorest, which may need bottom-up schemes alongside them.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge of named strategies in a named megacity. Balanced evaluation of successes and limitations, considering who benefits, leading to a well-supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; some evaluation, possibly unbalanced (mainly successes or mainly problems); a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated description of strategies with little or no evaluation or place detail.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nIn Mumbai, community toilet blocks built by Mahila Milan and the National Slum Dwellers Federation cost much less than government schemes and are maintained by residents. However, they serve only some neighbourhoods, and many informal settlements still lack sewers.\n\nAssess the extent to which bottom-up strategies are more effective than top-down strategies in improving the quality of life in megacities.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2; AO3). Credit named examples from any megacity in a developing or emerging country.\nBottom-up strengths: cheap, use local labour and knowledge, meet residents' real needs, keep communities and informal businesses together, e.g. community toilets, Akanksha Foundation classes, savings groups, in-situ upgrading in Dharavi.\nBottom-up limitations: small-scale, rely on charity or NGO funding, cannot provide city-wide infrastructure such as sewers, metro lines or water treatment; do not give legal land tenure.\nTop-down strengths: large investment, e.g. Mumbai Metro, Trans Harbour Link, Lagos BRT; can transform transport, water supply and housing for millions.\nTop-down limitations: expensive, slow, may displace the poor, e.g. evictions linked to redevelopment; residents have little say.\nAssessment: e.g. bottom-up strategies are more effective for quick, local improvements for the poorest, but only top-down schemes can solve city-wide problems; the most effective approach combines both (e.g. government funding community-designed sanitation).\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate knowledge of named strategies of both types; balanced, logical assessment of their effectiveness, reaching a well-supported judgement about the extent.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; both types considered but unevenly or with limited examples; a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated, mostly descriptive points about one type of strategy; no meaningful judgement.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nFigure 14 describes how Mumbai's built-up area has changed. In 1900 the city covered only the southern part of the peninsula, around the port, the fort and the cotton mills. By 1970 housing and industry had spread north along the railway lines, and informal settlements such as Dharavi had grown on former marshland. By 2020 the city had spread across the whole of Salsette Island and onto the mainland, with the planned city of Navi Mumbai across the harbour and new office districts such as Bandra-Kurla Complex.\n\nUsing Figure 14 and your own knowledge, examine how and why the land use of a megacity changes as it grows.`, marks: 8, command: 'Examine', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2; AO3 using Figure 14).\nHow (from Figure 14):\n• Growth from the south northwards along railway lines; built-up area expands to cover the whole island and spills onto the mainland.\n• Informal settlements grow on marginal land (marshland, beside railways).\n• New planned settlement (Navi Mumbai) and new business districts (Bandra-Kurla Complex) away from the old CBD.\n• Decline of old industry (cotton mills closed in the 1980s–1990s) and redevelopment of mill land into offices, shopping malls and luxury flats.\nWhy:\n• Peninsula site limits space, so growth follows transport corridors.\n• Rapid migration creates demand for cheap housing faster than formal homes can be built.\n• Congestion and high land prices in the CBD push offices to new districts.\n• Deindustrialisation and growth of services change land use in the inner city.\n• Government planning to relieve overcrowding (Navi Mumbai, new bridges).\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; makes full use of Figure 14 and own knowledge; examines both how and why land use changes, showing clear links between growth processes and land-use patterns.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Mostly accurate; uses Figure 14 and/or own knowledge to describe changes with some explanation; links are partial.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated description of changes, largely lifted from Figure 14, with little or no explanation.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section C — Challenges of an Urbanising World.\nMumbai, India, has a metropolitan population of over 20 million. An estimated 40% of residents live in informal settlements such as Dharavi, where hundreds of people may share one toilet block, water taps run for only a few hours a day, and many families work in recycling and small workshops from their homes. Suburban trains carry over 7 million passenger journeys a day and are dangerously overcrowded.\n\nThree options to improve the quality of life in Mumbai:\nOption 1 — Clear and redevelop informal settlements such as Dharavi, rehousing eligible residents in free high-rise flats built by private developers (top-down).\nOption 2 — Fund community groups and NGOs to upgrade informal settlements where they are, with community toilets, water connections, paved lanes and savings schemes (bottom-up, in-situ).\nOption 3 — Expand the metro and suburban rail network and build new links to Navi Mumbai so that people can live in planned new housing further from the centre (top-down).\n\nSelect the option you think would most improve the quality of life for Mumbai's residents. Justify your choice. Use information from this question and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer.\n(12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2; AO3; AO4 for SPaG). Any option can be chosen; credit is for the quality of justification, use of the information given and own knowledge, and consideration of the other options.\nOption 1: provides permanent flats with water and toilets and legal tenure; frees land for offices and services; but many residents are ineligible and displaced; high-rise flats lack space for home-based industries such as recycling; poor build quality of some rehabilitation towers; breaks up communities; slow and dependent on developers' profits.\nOption 2: cheap and quick; keeps communities and informal jobs (e.g. Dharavi's recycling economy) intact; residents shape and maintain projects (e.g. Mahila Milan / SPARC toilet blocks); but small-scale, depends on funding, does not fix overcrowding, flood risk or city-wide sewers; residents may still lack secure tenure.\nOption 3: tackles severe overcrowding and dangerous trains; reduces congestion and air pollution; connects people to jobs and opens up cheaper land for planned housing (Navi Mumbai); but very expensive and slow; poorest residents may not afford fares or new housing; does not directly improve conditions in existing informal settlements.\nGood answers consider the economic, social and environmental impacts, short and long term, and who benefits; they may suggest combining options while still justifying a first choice.\n\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and thorough geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies this to deconstruct the information provided, giving a coherent, balanced and well-evidenced justification of the chosen option, with clear reasons why the other options were rejected. Makes extensive, well-chosen use of the information and own knowledge.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Justification is logical and mostly balanced, with some consideration of the other options. Good use of the information and some own knowledge.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge. Justification is partial or unbalanced, mainly describing the chosen option, with limited reference to the other options. Some use of the information.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; a choice is made but is poorly justified or simply repeats the information.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4 marks): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate performance (2–3 marks): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; uses a good range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning and any errors do not significantly hinder meaning; uses a limited range of specialist terms as appropriate.\n0 marks: no marks awarded / response not related to the question / errors severely hinder meaning.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.1': {
    name: 'UK Physical Landscapes and Coastal Change',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nFigure 1: a map of the main rock types of the UK. Granite is shown as small, separate areas, for example Dartmoor in south-west England and the Cairngorms in Scotland. Slate and schist cover much of north Wales and the Scottish Highlands. Chalk and clay cover most of south-east England.\n\nIdentify which one of the following is an igneous rock.\n\nA  chalk\nB  granite\nC  clay\nD  slate`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (granite) (1)\nDo not credit more than one answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 2: a photograph of a boulder clay (till) cliff on the Holderness coast near Skipsea, taken after several days of heavy rain. A large curved section of the cliff top has moved downwards and backwards, leaving a curved scar on the cliff face and a tilted block of grassy land part-way down the cliff.\n\nName the type of mass movement shown in Figure 2.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `slumping (1)\nAllow rotational slip / rotational slumping.\nDo not credit rockfall / sliding on its own / soil creep.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 3: a description of a 1:25 000 Ordnance Survey map extract of the coast near Swanage, Dorset. The grid lines are labelled with eastings 03 to 06 and northings 80 to 83. The chalk stack known as Old Harry Rocks lies in the grid square whose south-west (bottom left) corner is where easting 05 meets northing 82.\n\nState the four-figure grid reference of the grid square containing Old Harry Rocks.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `0582 (1)\nDo not credit 8205 (northing given first).` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 4: a description of a 1:25 000 Ordnance Survey map extract of a stretch of the Holderness coast. A narrow, curved ridge of sand and shingle extends out into the mouth of a river estuary. Measured along its centre line, the ridge is 12 cm long on the map.\n\nCalculate the length of this landform on the ground. Give your answer in kilometres.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3 (km) (1)\n(12 cm × 25 000 = 300 000 cm = 3 km)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 5: a photograph of the beach at Bournemouth, Dorset, in winter. A large pipe runs up the beach from a dredger anchored offshore. A mixture of sand and seawater is being pumped out of the pipe onto the beach, and bulldozers are spreading the sand to raise and widen the beach.\n\nName the method of coastal management shown in Figure 5.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `beach nourishment (1)\nAllow beach replenishment / beach recharge.\nDo not credit beach reprofiling on its own / groynes / soft engineering on its own.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nAt Medmerry, West Sussex, the Environment Agency completed a scheme in 2013 in which new embankments were built up to 2 km inland and the old shingle bank was allowed to be breached by the sea, creating new intertidal habitat.\n\nIdentify which one of the following shoreline management plan (SMP) policies was used at Medmerry.\n\nA  hold the line\nB  advance the line\nC  managed realignment\nD  no active intervention`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (managed realignment) (1)\nDo not credit more than one answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 6: a description of a 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map extract of a stretch of the Dorset coast. A narrow, roughly circular cove with a small entrance between two headlands is shown at grid reference 823800. A pointed rock standing in the sea, separated from the cliffs, is shown at grid reference 838797. A long straight beach with a car park behind it is shown at grid reference 805803.\n\nIdentify the feature shown at grid reference 838797.\n\nA  cove\nB  spit\nC  stack\nD  beach`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (stack) (1)\nDo not credit more than one answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nFigure 7: a photograph of a granite tor on Dartmoor. The tor is made of large blocks separated by deep cracks (joints). At its base is a slope of loose, angular rock fragments (scree).\n\nExplain one way in which the granite in Figure 7 has been weathered.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a way, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• freeze–thaw weathering (1) — water gets into joints, freezes and expands by about 9%, so repeated freezing and thawing widens the cracks until angular pieces break off to form scree (1)\n• chemical weathering of the granite (1) — feldspar minerals in the granite are broken down by slightly acidic rainwater into clay, which weakens the rock along the joints (1)\n• biological weathering (1) — plant roots grow into the joints and widen them, prising blocks apart (1)\nDo not credit erosion processes (e.g. abrasion by rivers).` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nFigure 8: a map of upland and lowland areas in the UK. Land above 200 m (upland) covers most of Scotland north of a line from Glasgow to Edinburgh, most of Wales, the Lake District and the Pennines in northern England, and small areas of south-west England such as Dartmoor and Exmoor. Most of the Midlands, East Anglia and south-east England is below 200 m (lowland).\n\nDescribe the distribution of upland areas in the UK shown in Figure 8.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• upland areas are mainly in the north and west of the UK (1)\n• most of Scotland / the Scottish Highlands is upland (1)\n• Wales is mostly upland (1)\n• there is a central upland spine in northern England (the Pennines) (1)\n• there are small, isolated upland areas in the south-west, e.g. Dartmoor / Exmoor (1)\n• the south-east / East Anglia has very little upland (1)\nAllow use of named examples from the figure for development.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 9: a table showing the mean annual rate of cliff retreat at five sites on the Holderness coast between 2000 and 2020 (data written for this practice question).\n\nSite | Mean retreat (m per year)\nBarmston | 1.2\nSkipsea | 2.5\nMappleton (south) | 1.8\nAldbrough | 3.1\nWithernsea | 0.9\n\nCalculate the mean rate of cliff retreat for the five sites. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1.9 (m per year) (2)\n1 mark for working: 1.2 + 2.5 + 1.8 + 3.1 + 0.9 = 9.5 and 9.5 ÷ 5 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 1.9 (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nWaves reaching the coast of Holderness in winter are often driven by strong north-easterly winds blowing across the North Sea.\n\nIdentify which two of the following are characteristics of destructive waves.\n\nA  a strong swash and a weak backwash\nB  a weak swash and a strong backwash\nC  a low wave height and a long wavelength\nD  a high frequency of 10–14 waves per minute\nE  they build up beaches by depositing material`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1)\nD (1)\nDo not credit more than two answers.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 10: a photograph of a wooden groyne on the beach at Swanage. Sand and shingle are piled up high on one side of the groyne, almost reaching its top. On the other side, the beach level is about 1 m lower and the base of the groyne is exposed.\n\nExplain one reason why the beach material has built up on one side of the groyne in Figure 10.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• longshore drift is moving material along the beach (1) — waves approach at an angle with the prevailing wind, so the swash carries sediment diagonally up the beach and the backwash drags it straight back down, moving it in one direction until the groyne traps it on its up-drift side (1)\n• the groyne acts as a barrier to sediment transport (1) — so material moved by longshore drift is deposited against it and less material reaches the down-drift side (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 11: a table showing the mean width of a beach at Sandown, Isle of Wight, before and after a beach nourishment scheme (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | Mean beach width at high tide (m)\nBefore scheme | 18\nOne year after scheme | 45\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) increase in mean beach width. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `150% (2)\n1 mark for working: (45 − 18) ÷ 18 × 100 or 27 ÷ 18 × 100 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 150 (%) (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 12: a fact file about the Holderness coast, East Riding of Yorkshire.\n• The coast is 61 km long, from Flamborough Head in the north to Spurn Head in the south.\n• The cliffs are made of boulder clay (till) deposited by ice during the last glacial period.\n• The coast faces east across the North Sea, which has a long fetch to the north-east.\n• Beaches along much of the coast are narrow.\n\nSuggest one reason why the Holderness coast is eroding rapidly.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• the boulder clay is soft / unconsolidated (1) — so it is easily eroded by hydraulic action and abrasion and slumps when saturated (1)\n• the long fetch across the North Sea (1) — gives powerful destructive waves, especially during winter north-easterly storms (1)\n• the narrow beaches (1) — do little to absorb wave energy, so waves reach the cliff foot more often (1)\n• longshore drift moves eroded material south (1) — so it is not left at the cliff foot to protect it (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nAt Mappleton, on the Holderness coast, two rock groynes and a rock revetment were built in 1991 at a cost of about £2 million to protect the village and the B1242 coast road.\n\nExplain one way in which rock armour (a rock revetment) protects the coast.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• it absorbs / dissipates wave energy (1) — because the gaps between the large boulders break up the waves, so less energy reaches the cliff foot to cause hydraulic action and abrasion (1)\n• it holds back material at the cliff foot (1) — reducing undercutting and so reducing slumping of the cliff (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nFigure 13: a photograph of Kielder Forest, Northumberland. The hillsides are covered in dense, dark-green conifer trees planted in straight rows. Some blocks have straight edges where trees have been felled, leaving bare patches of land. A large reservoir (Kielder Water) fills the valley floor.\n\nExplain one way in which human activity has resulted in the distinctive landscape shown in Figure 13.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• forestry / planting of conifer plantations (1) — fast-growing trees planted in rows for timber give straight-edged blocks of uniform woodland unlike natural mixed woodland (1)\n• clear felling for timber (1) — leaves bare, straight-edged patches on the hillsides (1)\n• building a dam / reservoir (1) — flooded the valley floor to store water, creating a large lake (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nFigure 14: a photograph of the Lake District. A steep-sided, armchair-shaped hollow, partly filled by a small lake (tarn), sits high on a mountainside. Below it, a wide, flat-floored valley with straight, steep sides leads down to a long, narrow lake.\n\nExplain one way in which glacial processes have shaped the landscape shown in Figure 14.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• glacial erosion by plucking and abrasion (1) — ice in a hollow rotated and deepened it to form a corrie, which now holds a tarn (1)\n• a glacier moving down a former river valley (1) — eroded the sides and floor, widening and deepening it into a U-shaped (glacial trough) valley (1)\n• over-deepening of the valley floor by ice (1) — left a hollow that has filled with water to form a ribbon lake (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 15: a description of a 1:25 000 Ordnance Survey map extract of a chalk coastline. The spot height on the cliff top is 60 m. The beach at the foot of the cliffs is at 0 m. The horizontal distance between the spot height and the beach is 300 m.\n\nCalculate the gradient of the land between the cliff top and the beach. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 in 5 / 1:5 (2)\n1 mark for working: 60 ÷ 300 or 300 ÷ 60 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 1 in 5 (1)\nAllow 0.2 or 20% for 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 16: a description of a 1:25 000 Ordnance Survey map extract of Studland Bay, Dorset. It shows a long sandy beach, several car parks, a visitor centre, public toilets, a ferry crossing to Sandbanks, a golf course and many public footpaths, including part of the South West Coast Path. Behind the beach is a large area of heathland and a nature reserve.\n\nIdentify two pieces of map evidence that tourism is important in this area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for each piece of evidence, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• car parks (1)\n• visitor centre (1)\n• public toilets (1)\n• golf course (1)\n• public footpaths / the South West Coast Path (1)\n• a long sandy beach (1)\n• ferry crossing (1)\nDo not credit heathland / nature reserve on its own.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nAt Studland Bay, Dorset, a wide sandy beach is backed by a series of sand dune ridges up to 10 m high. The prevailing winds come from the south-west and the beach dries out at low tide.\n\nExplain one reason why sand dunes have formed at Studland Bay.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• a large supply of sand on a wide beach (1) — which dries out at low tide so it can be blown inland by onshore winds (1)\n• onshore (prevailing) winds (1) — carry dry sand up the beach until it is trapped by obstacles such as driftwood, building up embryo dunes (1)\n• vegetation such as marram grass (1) — traps sand and binds it with its roots, allowing the dunes to grow higher and become stable (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 17: a bar graph showing the length of coastline (km) lost to erosion in four English coastal counties between 2000 and 2020 (data written for this practice question). Bars have been drawn for Norfolk (2.4 km), Suffolk (1.6 km) and Dorset (0.6 km). The bar for East Riding of Yorkshire is missing. The vertical axis runs from 0 to 4 km and is marked every 0.2 km.\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 17 to show that 3.4 km of coastline was lost in East Riding of Yorkshire.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each point, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• draw a bar of the same width as the other bars, in the space labelled East Riding of Yorkshire / with a gap from the other bars (1)\n• draw the top of the bar at 3.4 km on the vertical axis, i.e. two small divisions above 3.0 km (1)\n• label/shade the bar in the same way as the others (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 18: a photograph of chalk cliffs at Flamborough Head, East Riding of Yorkshire. The cliff foot has a small cave entrance, and the rock surface near the waterline is pitted and smoothed.\n\nExplain one way in which chemical processes can weaken chalk cliffs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a process, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• carbonation / solution (1) — rainwater and seawater contain weak carbonic acid that reacts with the calcium carbonate in chalk and dissolves it, widening joints and weakening the cliff (1)\n• salt weathering (1) — seawater evaporates in cracks, leaving salt crystals that grow and put pressure on the rock (1)\nAllow corrosion / solution as an erosional process if linked to dissolving calcium carbonate.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 19: a simplified geology map of the coast between Studland and Durlston Head, Dorset. Three bands of rock run from west to east and meet the coast at right angles. From north to south they are: chalk (Ballard Down, ending at Handfast Point), clays and sands (Swanage Bay) and Purbeck limestone (Durlston Head).\n\nExplain why this stretch of coastline is described as discordant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying the meaning, 1 mark for using the figure, e.g.:\n• the bands of different rock types run at right angles (perpendicular) to the coastline (1)\n• so the coast cuts across alternating bands of resistant (chalk, limestone) and less resistant (clay and sand) rock, e.g. Swanage Bay clays between chalk and limestone headlands (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nIn 1991 rock groynes were built at Mappleton on the Holderness coast. Longshore drift on this coast moves sediment from north to south. Since 1991 the cliffs at Cowden, about 3 km south of Mappleton, have retreated more quickly than before.\n\nExplain one cost of using hard engineering at Mappleton.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a cost, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• increased erosion further down the coast / terminal groyne effect (1) — the groynes trap sediment moving south, so beaches at Cowden are starved of sediment, giving less protection and faster cliff retreat (1)\n• high financial cost (1) — about £2 million spent to protect a small village, money that could not be used elsewhere (1)\n• visual impact (1) — large rocks look unnatural and may reduce the area's appeal to visitors (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nThe Tees–Exe line runs from the mouth of the River Tees in north-east England to the mouth of the River Exe in south-west England.\n\nDescribe the difference in rock type on either side of the Tees–Exe line.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid point, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• north and west of the line rocks are mostly older, harder igneous and metamorphic rocks (e.g. granite, slate, schist) (1)\n• south and east of the line rocks are mostly younger, softer sedimentary rocks (e.g. chalk, clay) (1)\n• so upland landscapes dominate to the north-west and lowland landscapes to the south-east (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 20: a graph of the number of cliff falls recorded each month along a 10 km stretch of the Norfolk coast in one year (data written for this practice question). Most cliff falls (between 6 and 9 per month) were recorded from November to February. Between May and August there were 0 or 1 cliff falls per month.\n\nExplain one reason why more cliff falls happen in winter than in summer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, plus up to 2 marks for development, e.g.:\n• more storms in winter (1) — producing larger, more destructive waves with more energy (1) — which undercut the cliff foot by hydraulic action and abrasion, leaving the cliff unsupported so it collapses (1)\n• higher rainfall in winter (1) — saturates the cliff material, increasing its weight (1) — and lubricates slip planes so slumping/landslides occur (1)\n• freeze–thaw in winter (1) — water in cracks freezes and expands (1) — widening cracks so blocks of rock fall (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nSpurn Head is a spit about 5 km long at the southern end of the Holderness coast. Longshore drift carries sand and shingle south along the coast. At Spurn the coastline changes direction where it meets the mouth of the Humber estuary, and the end of the spit curves back towards the land.\n\nExplain how a spit such as Spurn Head is formed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each linked point, up to 3 marks, e.g.:\n• longshore drift transports sediment along the coast in the direction of the prevailing/dominant waves (1)\n• where the coastline changes direction (at the estuary mouth) the sediment continues to be deposited out into the sea, building a long ridge (1)\n• deposition happens because the water is sheltered/deeper and waves lose energy (1)\n• changes in wind/wave direction, or the river current, curve the end of the spit to form a recurved/hooked end (1)\n• salt marsh forms in the sheltered, low-energy water behind the spit (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 21: a table showing the fetch and the mean height of winter waves at eight locations on UK coasts (data written for this practice question).\n\nFetch (km) | Mean wave height (m)\n50 | 0.4\n120 | 0.7\n300 | 1.1\n450 | 1.3\n800 | 1.8\n1200 | 2.2\n2000 | 2.6\n3500 | 3.4\n\nDescribe the relationship shown in Figure 21. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the relationship, 1 mark for data, 1 mark for a further descriptive point, e.g.:\n• positive relationship — as fetch increases, wave height increases (1)\n• data, e.g. a fetch of 50 km gives a wave height of 0.4 m whereas a fetch of 3500 km gives 3.4 m (1)\n• the rate of increase slows at longer fetches, e.g. wave height rises 0.7 m between 50 km and 300 km but only 0.8 m between 2000 km and 3500 km (1)\n• there are no anomalies / the relationship is strong (1)\nMax 2 marks without the use of data.` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nThe fells of the central Lake District are made of hard volcanic rocks (the Borrowdale Volcanic Group) formed about 450 million years ago, when the area was close to a plate boundary.\n\nExplain one way in which past tectonic processes have influenced the UK's physical landscape.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, plus up to 2 marks for development, e.g.:\n• past volcanic activity at plate boundaries (1) — formed hard, resistant igneous rocks such as those in the Lake District (1) — which resist weathering and erosion and so now form rugged uplands (1)\n• plate collisions (1) — folded and uplifted rocks and caused metamorphism, e.g. forming schist in the Scottish Highlands (1) — giving high mountain ranges which were later shaped by ice (1)\n• magma intrusion (1) — cooled slowly underground to form granite, e.g. Dartmoor (1) — later exposed by erosion to form high moorland with tors (1)\n• lava eruptions as the Atlantic opened (1) — produced basalt, e.g. Giant's Causeway / Isle of Skye (1) — which cooled to form distinctive columns/plateaus (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 22: an incomplete pie chart showing the proportion of a 40 km stretch of English coastline under each shoreline management plan (SMP) policy (data written for this practice question). One segment, labelled 'No active intervention', fills 55% of the circle. The remaining 45% is blank.\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 22 using this information: hold the line 35%, managed realignment 10%.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each point, up to 3 marks, e.g.:\n• start from the edge of the existing segment / a line from the centre (1)\n• convert to angles: hold the line 35% = 126°, managed realignment 10% = 36° (or use a percentage protractor) (1)\n• measure and draw the segments with a protractor, from the centre (1)\n• label each segment or shade/colour it and add to a key (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nThe coastline of the Isle of Purbeck, Dorset, has headlands of chalk (Handfast Point) and limestone (Durlston Head) separated by Swanage Bay, which is cut into clays and sands.\n\nExplain two ways in which geology influences the shape of a coastline.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), e.g.:\n• rock type / resistance (1) — less resistant rocks such as clay are eroded more quickly to form bays, while resistant chalk and limestone are left jutting out as headlands (1)\n• geological structure / orientation of rock bands (1) — where bands run at right angles to the coast (discordant) headlands and bays form; where they run parallel (concordant), as at Lulworth, a straight coast forms with coves where the resistant band is breached (1)\n• faults and joints (1) — lines of weakness are eroded more quickly by hydraulic action, forming caves, arches and stacks (1)\n• dip of the rock layers (1) — layers dipping towards the sea can cause landslides and gentler cliffs, steep/horizontal layers give steep cliffs (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nThe boulder clay cliffs at Aldbrough, on the Holderness coast, are about 15 m high and retreat by an average of more than 2 m per year.\n\nExplain two ways in which mass movement contributes to cliff retreat.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), e.g.:\n• slumping (1) — rainwater soaks into the clay, making it heavy and lubricating a curved slip plane, so large sections slide down and back, moving the cliff edge inland (1)\n• rockfalls (1) — where waves undercut the base of a cliff, the unsupported material above falls, making the cliff retreat (1)\n• sliding / landslides (1) — saturated material moves down a straight slip plane, delivering material to the beach (1)\n• mass movement delivers material to the cliff foot (1) — where it is quickly removed by waves and longshore drift, exposing the cliff to further erosion (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nYou have studied a distinctive coastal landscape in the UK.\n\nFor a named UK coastal landscape, explain two ways in which physical processes have produced its distinctive landforms.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2). Answers must refer to a named UK coastal landscape for full marks (e.g. Dorset/Jurassic Coast, Holderness, North Norfolk). E.g. (Dorset):\n• erosion of joints and faults in the chalk at Handfast Point by hydraulic action and abrasion (1) — has formed caves that grew into arches and collapsed to leave stacks such as Old Harry and stumps (1)\n• differential erosion of bands of rock (1) — the soft clays at Swanage Bay eroded faster than the chalk and limestone, creating a bay between two headlands (1)\n• longshore drift and deposition (1) — has built a sandy beach and sand dunes at Studland Bay (1)\n• wave refraction (1) — concentrates erosion on headlands and deposition in the bays (1)\nE.g. (Holderness): rapid erosion/slumping of soft boulder clay creating a straight, retreating coastline (1) with longshore drift carrying sediment south to build the Spurn Head spit (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nHappisburgh is a village on the coast of Norfolk. Its cliffs are made of soft sands and clays. Old wooden revetments were not replaced when they became damaged, and the shoreline management plan does not include building new defences there. Several homes and part of the main road to the beach have been lost to the sea since the 1990s.\n\nExplain two impacts of coastal recession on people.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each impact (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), e.g.:\n• loss of homes (1) — residents have to move, often without compensation, as properties at risk cannot be insured (1)\n• falling property values (1) — homes near the cliff edge become hard to sell, trapping people (1)\n• loss of farmland (1) — reduces farm incomes as fields are lost to the sea (1)\n• loss of infrastructure, e.g. roads, car parks, slipways (1) — cuts off access, reducing tourism and income for local businesses (1)\n• stress and anxiety (1) — residents worry about losing their homes and community (1)\nCredit impacts from other named UK locations.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nSince 2013 sand dunes at Studland Bay, Dorset, have been managed by fencing off damaged areas, planting marram grass and building boardwalks for visitors.\n\nExplain one cost and one benefit of using soft engineering to manage coastal erosion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a cost and 1 mark for its development; 1 mark for a benefit and 1 mark for its development, e.g.:\nCost:\n• it needs ongoing maintenance, e.g. beach nourishment must be repeated every few years (1) — as waves and longshore drift remove the sand, so long-term costs build up (1)\n• restricts access to parts of the beach/dunes (1) — which can annoy visitors and local people (1)\n• it may not protect against large storms (1) — so property can still be lost (1)\nBenefit:\n• works with natural processes (1) — so it does not cause erosion further along the coast as groynes can (1)\n• looks natural / keeps the beach (1) — so tourism is not harmed (1)\n• creates or protects habitats, e.g. dunes and salt marsh (1) — increasing biodiversity (1)\n• often cheaper than sea walls (1) — e.g. dune fencing costs far less per metre (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nThe coastline from Flamborough Head to the Humber estuary forms part of one sediment cell. Sediment eroded from the Holderness cliffs is carried south by longshore drift and helps to build Spurn Head and beaches along the Lincolnshire coast.\n\nExplain two reasons why integrated coastal zone management (ICZM) is used to manage coastlines such as this.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), e.g.:\n• actions in one place affect other places along the sediment cell (1) — e.g. groynes at Mappleton reduced sediment reaching Cowden, so the whole cell needs to be planned together (1)\n• it balances the needs of different groups (1) — e.g. residents, farmers, tourism businesses, conservation groups and industry such as the Easington gas terminal (1)\n• it takes a long-term, sustainable view (1) — e.g. planning for sea level rise over the next 100 years rather than short-term fixes (1)\n• it allows money to be spent where it gives most benefit (1) — e.g. protecting towns such as Hornsea and Withernsea while allowing farmland to erode (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 23: a line graph showing mean sea level at Newlyn, Cornwall, between 1920 and 2020 (data written for this practice question). The line rises from 0 mm in 1920 to about 190 mm in 2020, rising more steeply after 1990.\n\nExplain two ways in which climate change may increase the risk of coastal erosion and flooding in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), e.g.:\n• rising sea level, due to thermal expansion of seawater and melting land ice (1) — means waves reach further up beaches and attack cliffs and defences more often (1)\n• more frequent / more intense storms (1) — giving more powerful destructive waves and storm surges, e.g. the winter 2013–14 storms that destroyed part of the sea wall at Dawlish, Devon (1)\n• wetter winters (1) — saturate cliffs so slumping and landslides become more common (1)\n• higher sea levels overtop existing defences (1) — so sea walls designed for past conditions may fail, increasing flood risk in low-lying areas such as the Fens/Thames estuary (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 24: a photograph taken at low tide below chalk cliffs at Flamborough Head. A wide, gently sloping, rocky surface with rock pools stretches out from the foot of the cliff towards the sea. A notch has been cut into the base of the cliff.\n\nExplain how the landform at the foot of the cliffs in Figure 24 is formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each linked point, up to 4 marks, e.g.:\n• destructive waves attack the cliff between the high and low tide marks (1)\n• hydraulic action and abrasion erode the base of the cliff to form a wave-cut notch (1)\n• the notch grows deeper until the overhanging cliff above is unsupported and collapses (1)\n• the cliff retreats inland, and the process repeats (1)\n• leaving behind a gently sloping rocky surface — a wave-cut platform — exposed at low tide (1)\n• the platform is smoothed by abrasion and may be weathered by solution/carbonation, forming rock pools (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nDartmoor, in Devon, is high moorland made of granite with tors and boggy upland. The North Downs, in Kent and Surrey, are rolling chalk hills with dry valleys and few surface streams.\n\nExplain two ways in which differences in rock type produce different landscapes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), e.g.:\n• resistance to erosion (1) — hard igneous rocks such as granite are eroded slowly, forming high upland areas, while softer sedimentary rocks such as clay are worn down to form lowland vales (1)\n• permeability (1) — granite is impermeable so water stays on the surface, forming bogs and many streams, whereas chalk is permeable/porous so rainwater soaks in, giving dry valleys (1)\n• jointing and weathering (1) — widely spaced joints in granite are attacked by weathering to leave tors, while chalk is dissolved by carbonation giving smooth, rounded hills (1)\n• soil and land use (1) — granite gives thin, acidic soils used for rough grazing, while chalk gives thin, alkaline soils used for sheep grazing/arable farming (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 25: a table comparing two options considered for managing the coast at Medmerry, West Sussex (figures approximate).\n\nOption | Cost | Expected effect\nRebuild and raise the old shingle bank | about £5 million every few years for maintenance | protection depends on frequent repairs; bank breached during storms\nManaged realignment (chosen, completed 2013) | about £28 million (one-off) | about 7 km of new embankments inland; 183 hectares of new intertidal habitat; protects around 350 properties\n\nUsing Figure 25, explain two reasons why managed realignment was chosen at Medmerry.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), with use of Figure 25, e.g.:\n• lower long-term cost (1) — the one-off £28 million avoids repeatedly spending about £5 million on repairs to a bank that keeps being breached (1)\n• more reliable protection (1) — new embankments set back inland protect around 350 properties more effectively than the failing shingle bank (1)\n• environmental benefits (1) — 183 hectares of new intertidal habitat (salt marsh/mudflats), now an RSPB reserve, replaces habitat lost elsewhere (1)\n• works with natural processes (1) — the salt marsh absorbs wave energy, so it is more sustainable as sea level rises (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nBetween 1897 and 1902 about 650 000 tonnes of shingle were dredged from the sea bed off Hallsands, Devon, to build docks at Plymouth. The beach in front of the village fell in level, and in January 1917 a storm destroyed almost all the houses in the village.\n\nExplain two ways in which human activities can increase the rate of coastal erosion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), e.g.:\n• dredging / removing beach material (1) — as at Hallsands, lowers the beach so waves reach the cliffs/settlement with more energy (1)\n• hard engineering, e.g. groynes (1) — traps sediment moving by longshore drift, starving beaches down-drift and increasing erosion there, e.g. Cowden south of Mappleton (1)\n• building on cliff tops (1) — adds weight and increases surface run-off/drainage into cliffs, making slumping more likely (1)\n• trampling of sand dunes by visitors (1) — destroys marram grass so dunes are blown away and stop protecting the coast (1)\n• dams on rivers (1) — trap sediment that would otherwise feed beaches (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nAt Happisburgh, Norfolk, many residents campaigned for new sea defences to be built. The Environment Agency and the local council argued that the cost of new defences could not be justified for a small number of homes and that defences would reduce the sediment supply to beaches further south.\n\nExplain two reasons why there is conflict over how the coastline should be managed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2), plus 1 mark for development of each (up to 2), e.g.:\n• different groups have different priorities (1) — residents want their homes protected, while the council must spend limited money where it protects the most people and property (1)\n• protecting one place can harm another (1) — defences at Happisburgh would cut sediment supply to beaches down-drift, increasing erosion elsewhere (1)\n• cost–benefit analysis (1) — defences may cost more than the value of the property protected, so funding is refused, angering residents (1)\n• environmental groups may oppose hard engineering (1) — because it damages natural habitats and processes / spoils the landscape (1)\n• lack of compensation (1) — people who lose homes to erosion usually receive no payment, increasing opposition to 'no active intervention' (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 26: a geology map and photographs of the Dorset coast. At Lulworth Cove a narrow band of resistant Portland limestone runs parallel to the coast and has been breached by the sea, behind which a circular cove has been eroded in softer clays. At Swanage, bands of chalk, clay and limestone meet the coast at right angles, forming headlands and a bay. Old Harry Rocks, a chalk stack, stands off Handfast Point. The photographs also show storm waves breaking against the cliffs and a slump scar in the clay cliffs of Swanage Bay.\n\nAssess the importance of geology in the formation of distinctive coastal landforms.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced assessment of the factors, supported by relevant, located evidence. Reaches a judgement that is fully supported by evidence (AO3).\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial assessment that may be unbalanced, with some supporting evidence. Reaches a judgement that is partially supported (AO3).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate (AO2). Limited, generic assessment with little or no supporting evidence; judgement, if present, is unsupported (AO3).\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nIndicative content (AO2/AO3):\n• Rock type: resistant chalk and limestone form headlands and cliffs; less resistant clays and sands form bays (Swanage Bay).\n• Structure: discordant coasts (Swanage) form headlands and bays; concordant coasts (Lulworth) form coves once the resistant outer band is breached.\n• Joints and faults give lines of weakness eroded into caves, arches and stacks (Old Harry Rocks).\n• However, geology only creates potential: marine processes (hydraulic action, abrasion, destructive waves, storms) are needed to erode; sub-aerial processes (weathering, slumping) shape cliff profiles.\n• Wave refraction, fetch and longshore drift matter for depositional landforms (Studland beach, dunes, spits) where geology is less important.\n• Human activity may modify landforms (defences, trampling).\n• Judgement: e.g. geology is the most important factor in explaining where erosional landforms form and their shape, but marine and sub-aerial processes are needed to produce them, and geology is less important for depositional landforms.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nYou have studied the management of a named UK coastline.\n\nEvaluate the success of coastal management for a named UK coastline.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced evaluation, supported by relevant, located evidence. Reaches a judgement that is fully supported by evidence (AO3).\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial evaluation that may be unbalanced, with some supporting evidence. Reaches a judgement that is partially supported (AO3).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate (AO2). Limited, generic evaluation with little or no supporting evidence; judgement, if present, is unsupported (AO3).\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nIndicative content (AO2/AO3), e.g. Holderness:\n• Hard engineering: sea walls and groynes at Hornsea and Withernsea; rock groynes and revetment at Mappleton (1991, about £2 million) protected the village and the B1242 coast road.\n• Rock revetment at the Easington gas terminal protects nationally important infrastructure.\n• However, the terminal groyne effect has increased erosion down-drift, e.g. at Cowden; unprotected farmland and caravan parks (e.g. Skipsea) continue to erode at around 2 m per year.\n• Shoreline management plan: hold the line at towns, no active intervention on most farmland; may be cost-effective but residents/farmers receive little compensation.\n• Spurn Head was breached by the 2013 storm surge — the decision not to repair the road shows management working with natural processes.\n• Credit other named coasts, e.g. Medmerry managed realignment, Swanage sea wall and groynes, Happisburgh.\n• Judgement on success for different groups, economically, socially and environmentally, and in the short and long term.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 27: information about the coast at Skipsea, Holderness.\n• Cliffs are 10–15 m high and made of boulder clay.\n• Storm waves from the north-east have a fetch of over 500 km.\n• Rainfall saturates the cliffs, causing frequent slumps.\n• A caravan park sits on the cliff top; there are no sea defences at Skipsea.\n• Rock groynes at Mappleton, about 15 km to the south, and at Hornsea, about 10 km to the south, protect those places.\n\nAssess the relative importance of physical and human factors in causing rapid coastal recession at a named location.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced assessment of the factors, supported by relevant, located evidence. Reaches a judgement that is fully supported by evidence (AO3).\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial assessment that may be unbalanced, with some supporting evidence. Reaches a judgement that is partially supported (AO3).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate (AO2). Limited, generic assessment with little or no supporting evidence; judgement, if present, is unsupported (AO3).\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nIndicative content (AO2/AO3):\n• Physical: soft, easily eroded boulder clay; long fetch giving powerful destructive waves; winter storms and storm surges; saturation leading to slumping; narrow beaches; longshore drift removing material; sea-level rise.\n• Human: groynes trap sediment and starve beaches down-drift; weight of buildings/caravans and drainage on cliff tops; lack of defences due to cost–benefit decisions; dredging and removal of beach material elsewhere (e.g. Hallsands); trampling.\n• At Skipsea the defences at Mappleton and Hornsea lie to the south (down-drift) so they do not starve Skipsea of sediment — physical factors dominate there; down-drift sites such as Cowden show a stronger human influence.\n• Judgement: e.g. physical factors are the main cause of recession on most of the Holderness coast, but human factors locally increase rates; climate change (partly human) will increase physical processes.` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nFigure 28: a description of a 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map extract of part of the Snowdonia (Eryri) National Park, Wales. Contour lines are very close together on mountain sides rising above 900 m. There are several small lakes in deep hollows high on the mountainsides, and a long, straight lake fills the floor of a steep-sided valley. Scattered farms are found on the valley floor, a slate quarry is marked on one valley side, and conifer plantations with straight edges cover some lower slopes.\n\nAssess the extent to which the UK's upland landscapes are the result of geology rather than other processes.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced assessment of the factors, supported by relevant, located evidence. Reaches a judgement that is fully supported by evidence (AO3).\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial assessment that may be unbalanced, with some supporting evidence. Reaches a judgement that is partially supported (AO3).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate (AO2). Limited, generic assessment with little or no supporting evidence; judgement, if present, is unsupported (AO3).\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nIndicative content (AO2/AO3):\n• Geology: hard, resistant igneous and metamorphic rocks (e.g. Snowdonia's volcanic rocks and slate, Lake District, Scottish Highlands, Dartmoor granite) form the uplands north-west of the Tees–Exe line; past tectonic activity (volcanoes, collisions, uplift) created them.\n• Glacial processes: corries (lakes in hollows), U-shaped valleys and ribbon lakes in the map show ice erosion shaped the uplands.\n• Post-glacial processes: weathering (freeze–thaw scree), mass movement and river erosion continue to shape slopes.\n• Human activity: quarrying of slate, conifer plantations, sheep farming and settlement create distinctive features.\n• Judgement: e.g. geology explains why uplands exist and where, but glacial and post-glacial processes and human activity explain their present appearance.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 29: a table comparing coastal management methods used in England (approximate costs; written for this practice question).\n\nMethod | Approximate cost | Typical lifespan\nSea wall | £5 000–£10 000 per metre | 30–50 years\nRock armour | £1 000–£3 000 per metre | 50+ years\nGroynes | £5 000–£10 000 each (timber) | 15–25 years\nBeach nourishment | £3 000 per metre | 2–10 years\nDune regeneration | £20–£2 000 per metre | depends on maintenance\nManaged realignment | varies; e.g. about £28 million at Medmerry | long term\n\nEvaluate the use of hard engineering compared with soft engineering to manage the UK's coastline.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced evaluation, supported by relevant, located evidence. Reaches a judgement that is fully supported by evidence (AO3).\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial evaluation that may be unbalanced, with some supporting evidence. Reaches a judgement that is partially supported (AO3).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate (AO2). Limited, generic evaluation with little or no supporting evidence; judgement, if present, is unsupported (AO3).\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nIndicative content (AO2/AO3):\n• Hard engineering (sea walls, rock armour, groynes, revetments): effective, immediate protection for high-value land and towns (e.g. Hornsea, Withernsea, Swanage); but expensive (Figure 29), can deflect energy and cause scouring, groynes starve down-drift beaches (Cowden), visually intrusive, may fail in storms (Dawlish 2014).\n• Soft engineering (beach nourishment, dune regeneration, managed realignment): works with natural processes, creates habitat (Medmerry 183 ha), looks natural, supports tourism (Bournemouth, Studland); but needs repeated maintenance (nourishment lifespan 2–10 years), loss of land in realignment, less protection against extreme storms.\n• Sustainability and sea level rise: soft approaches adapt better over time; hard defences become more costly as sea level rises.\n• Often combined in ICZM/SMPs depending on value of land.\n• Judgement: e.g. hard engineering is justified for towns and infrastructure, while soft engineering is more sustainable for most stretches of coast.` },
      { q: `Section A — Coastal Change and Conflict.\n\nFigure 30: a table showing shoreline management plan (SMP) policies for parts of the north-east Norfolk coast for the period 2025–2055 (simplified for this practice question).\n\nLocation | Main land use | Policy\nCromer | town, tourism, pier | hold the line\nOverstrand–Mundesley | small villages, cliffs supplying sediment | managed realignment\nHappisburgh | village, farmland | managed realignment\nBacton | gas terminal | hold the line\nSea Palling | village, low-lying land behind dunes | hold the line\n\nAssess the reasons why shoreline management plan policies vary along a stretch of coastline.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced assessment of the factors, supported by relevant, located evidence. Reaches a judgement that is fully supported by evidence (AO3).\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial assessment that may be unbalanced, with some supporting evidence. Reaches a judgement that is partially supported (AO3).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate (AO2). Limited, generic assessment with little or no supporting evidence; judgement, if present, is unsupported (AO3).\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nIndicative content (AO2/AO3):\n• Economic value of land/assets: towns (Cromer) and nationally important infrastructure (Bacton gas terminal) justify the cost of holding the line; farmland and small villages often do not (cost–benefit analysis).\n• Physical factors: rate of erosion, cliff geology, flood risk of low-lying land (Sea Palling), the role of eroding cliffs in supplying sediment to beaches down-drift (Overstrand).\n• Sediment cell effects: protecting one place can increase erosion elsewhere, so some cliffs are allowed to erode to feed beaches.\n• Environmental factors: protected habitats and landscapes; managed realignment can create habitat.\n• Social and political factors: pressure from residents, tourism businesses; limited government budgets.\n• Climate change: sea level rise makes holding the line everywhere unaffordable in the long term.\n• Judgement: e.g. economic value is the main reason, but physical processes and sustainability are increasingly important.` },
      { q: `Section A — The UK's Evolving Physical Landscape.\n\nFigure 31: a description of a 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map extract of part of the Yorkshire Dales. Limestone pavements and scars (cliffs) are marked on the higher slopes. A large quarry is shown on one valley side. Fields on the valley floor are divided by walls, and isolated barns are scattered across them. A reservoir with a dam is shown at the head of the valley, and areas of rough grassland with sheep pens cover the upland.\n\nAssess the importance of human activities in creating distinctive UK landscapes.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced assessment of the factors, supported by relevant, located evidence. Reaches a judgement that is fully supported by evidence (AO3).\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial assessment that may be unbalanced, with some supporting evidence. Reaches a judgement that is partially supported (AO3).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate (AO2). Limited, generic assessment with little or no supporting evidence; judgement, if present, is unsupported (AO3).\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nIndicative content (AO2/AO3):\n• Agriculture: dry-stone walls, field barns and sheep grazing create the distinctive patchwork of the Dales; upland grazing maintains open moorland; lowland arable farming creates large open fields in East Anglia.\n• Forestry: conifer plantations with straight edges (e.g. Kielder) replace natural woodland.\n• Settlement and industry: quarries, reservoirs and dams, villages and roads change landscapes.\n• Physical factors set the framework: limestone geology (pavements, scars), glacial erosion forming the valleys, climate and weathering — human activity is limited by relief and soils.\n• Judgement: e.g. geology and glaciation explain the landforms, but human activity explains much of the land use and visual character; the balance differs between uplands and lowlands.` },
      { q: `Section D — Making a Geographical Decision: managing the Holderness coast.\n\nResource information (figures written for this practice question unless stated): the Holderness coast is 61 km long and made mostly of boulder clay; it retreats at an average of about 1.8 m per year, among the fastest rates in Europe. Towns such as Hornsea and Withernsea are protected by sea walls and groynes. Mappleton has rock groynes (1991, about £2 million); erosion has increased down-drift at Cowden. Much of the coast is farmland and caravan parks with no defences. Sediment from the cliffs feeds Spurn Head and beaches in Lincolnshire. The local council has a budget of £15 million for the next 20 years.\n\nThree options for managing the coast:\nOption 1 — build new hard engineering (rock armour and groynes) to protect the villages and caravan parks currently undefended.\nOption 2 — continue to hold the line only at the main towns and allow the rest of the coast to erode, paying to help people and businesses relocate inland.\nOption 3 — use soft engineering, such as beach nourishment and cliff drainage, along the most at-risk stretches.\n\nSelect the option that you think would be the best long-term plan for the Holderness coast. Justify your choice. Use information from the resource and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer. (12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout (AO2). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation of the resources and to evaluate the chosen option against the others (AO3). Uses evidence from the resource and wider course throughout. Reaches a well-supported, balanced decision, considering costs and benefits for people, economy and environment, and the short and long term (AO3).\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Sound interpretation of the resources with some evaluation of the chosen option and at least one other. Uses evidence from the resource and course, though not always consistently. Decision is supported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge and understanding. Partial interpretation; considers mainly the chosen option, with limited reference to others. Some use of evidence. Decision partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge and understanding. Limited interpretation; generic points, little or no use of the resource. Decision unsupported.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\nMax Level 2 if the answer does not refer to the resource information.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Option 1: protects homes, caravan parks and tourism jobs; but very costly (budget of £15 million unlikely to cover 61 km), traps sediment and increases erosion down-drift (Cowden effect) and starves Spurn Head/Lincolnshire beaches; unsustainable as sea level rises.\n• Option 2: matches current SMP approach; cost-effective; maintains sediment supply to Spurn and Lincolnshire; works with natural processes; but residents/farmers lose land and homes, relocation is disruptive, and loss of caravan parks affects the local economy.\n• Option 3: looks natural and supports tourism; reduces slumping through drainage; but nourishment must be repeated, sand may be removed quickly by longshore drift and storms; may not work on soft boulder clay with powerful waves.\n• Links to ICZM, sediment cells, climate change and named examples (Medmerry, Happisburgh).\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4 marks): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning overall; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate performance (2–3 marks): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning overall; uses a good range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning and any errors do not significantly hinder meaning overall; uses a limited range of specialist terms as appropriate.\n0 marks: no response, or the response does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.2': {
    name: 'River Processes and Pressures',
    questions: [
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 3: a photograph of the rocky bed of the River Tees above High Force, County Durham. The bed is pitted with smooth, rounded hollows (potholes). Pebbles lie in the bottom of each hollow and are swirled round by the current when the river is high.\n\nIdentify the process of erosion that is mainly responsible for forming these hollows.\nA  Abrasion\nB  Attrition\nC  Saltation\nD  Solution`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark: A (abrasion).\nB is wrong because attrition wears down the load, not the bed; C is a transport process; D removes soluble rock chemically and does not need pebbles.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 4: part of a 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Cockermouth, Cumbria (described for this practice question; grid values simplified). The River Cocker flows north through the town and joins the River Derwent. The confluence lies east of easting 11 and west of easting 12, and north of northing 30 and south of northing 31.\n\nState the four-figure grid reference of the square that contains the confluence of the two rivers.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark: 1130.\nDo not accept 3011 (northing given first).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 1: storm hydrograph data for a gauging station on an upland river in Cumbria, 5–6 December (data written for this practice question).\nRainfall: light rain from 03:00; heaviest rainfall 08:00–09:00 (14 mm in that hour); rain stopped at 13:00.\nDischarge (cumecs): 06:00 — 8; 09:00 — 9; 12:00 — 15; 15:00 — 34; 18:00 — 52; 21:00 — 41; 00:00 — 28; 03:00 — 18; 06:00 — 12.\n\nUsing Figure 1, state the peak discharge of the river.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark: 52 cumecs.\nAllow "52" without units. Do not accept 18:00 (this is the time, not the discharge).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 5: a photograph of the River Severn south of Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire. The river is wide and slow-flowing in a broad, flat valley. Fields stretch for over a kilometre on each side, and there is a raised grassy bank running alongside the channel.\n\nIdentify the landform that is most likely to have formed along the edge of the channel in Figure 5.\nA  Gorge\nB  Interlocking spur\nC  Levée\nD  Waterfall`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark: C (levée).\nA, B and D are landforms of the upper course.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 6: part of a 1:25 000 Ordnance Survey map of an upland valley in mid-Wales (described for this practice question). A river runs along the valley floor from the north-west corner to the south-east corner of the extract. A spot height of 412 m is marked beside the river in the north-west; the river leaves the extract at a contour of 100 m in the south-east.\n\nState the direction in which the river is flowing.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark: towards the south-east / from north-west to south-east.\nAccept SE. Do not accept "downhill" or "north-west".` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 7: a photograph of a new housing estate on former farmland at the edge of a town in the Midlands. The houses have tarmac drives and paved patios, and the roads have drains along the kerbs that lead to a nearby stream.\n\nState one way in which the development in Figure 7 increases surface runoff.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• Tarmac / concrete / paving is impermeable so rain cannot infiltrate.\n• Drains carry water quickly into the stream.\n• Roofs collect rainwater and channel it into drains.\n• Loss of fields / vegetation means less interception and infiltration.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 1: storm hydrograph data for a gauging station on an upland river in Cumbria, 5–6 December (data written for this practice question).\nRainfall: light rain from 03:00; heaviest rainfall 08:00–09:00 (14 mm in that hour); rain stopped at 13:00.\nDischarge (cumecs): 06:00 — 8; 09:00 — 9; 12:00 — 15; 15:00 — 34; 18:00 — 52; 21:00 — 41; 00:00 — 28; 03:00 — 18; 06:00 — 12.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the lag time of this storm hydrograph. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying the two times: peak rainfall 08:00–09:00 (use 09:00 or the mid-point 08:30) and peak discharge 18:00.\n1 mark for the answer: 9 hours (18:00 − 09:00).\nAlso accept 9.5 hours (from the mid-point, 08:30) or 10 hours (from 08:00, the start of the peak rainfall hour) when the working shows the time used.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 1: storm hydrograph data for a gauging station on an upland river in Cumbria, 5–6 December (data written for this practice question).\nRainfall: light rain from 03:00; heaviest rainfall 08:00–09:00 (14 mm in that hour); rain stopped at 13:00.\nDischarge (cumecs): 06:00 — 8; 09:00 — 9; 12:00 — 15; 15:00 — 34; 18:00 — 52; 21:00 — 41; 00:00 — 28; 03:00 — 18; 06:00 — 12.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in discharge between 06:00 on 5 December and the peak discharge. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (52 − 8) ÷ 8 × 100, or 44 ÷ 8 × 100.\n1 mark for the answer: 550%.\nDo not accept 650% (52 ÷ 8 × 100).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 8: part of a 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of the upper River Tees on the slopes of Cross Fell in the North Pennines (described for this practice question). A spring marked "Tees Head" is at 610 m. Further downstream a spot height of 430 m is marked beside the river. Measured along the channel on the map, the distance between these two points is 6 cm.\n\nCalculate the average gradient of the river between the spring and the spot height. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: height difference 610 − 430 = 180 m and horizontal distance 6 cm × 50 000 = 300 000 cm = 3 km = 3000 m.\n1 mark for the answer: 1 in 16.7 (180 ÷ 3000 = 0.06), allow 1:17, 1 in 16.7 or 6%.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 9: part of a 1:25 000 Ordnance Survey map of Upper Teesdale (described for this practice question). The waterfall symbol labelled "High Force" lies exactly on easting 88, four-tenths of the way from northing 28 towards northing 29.\n\nGive the six-figure grid reference of High Force.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 marks: 880284.\n1 mark for 880 (eastings) correct OR 284 (northings) correct, or for 884280 (eastings and northings reversed) — max 1.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nDuring a flood in February 2020, discharge on the River Tees at High Force rose sharply and the water was carrying boulders. The river was flowing over well-jointed limestone and shale.\n\nExplain one way in which hydraulic action erodes the bed and banks of a river.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the process and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• The force of fast-flowing water hits the banks / bed (1) and forces air and water into cracks and joints, so the pressure weakens the rock until pieces break off (1).\n• Air trapped in cracks is compressed by the water (1); when the pressure is released the air expands suddenly, widening the cracks (1).\nDo not credit descriptions of abrasion or attrition.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 10: mean pebble size and shape at three sites along a river in North Yorkshire (data written for this practice question).\nSite 1 (2 km from source): mean long axis 14.2 cm; most pebbles angular.\nSite 2 (15 km from source): mean long axis 7.6 cm; most pebbles sub-rounded.\nSite 3 (40 km from source): mean long axis 3.1 cm; most pebbles rounded.\n\nExplain one reason why the pebbles become smaller and more rounded downstream.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Attrition (1): pebbles knock against each other as they are transported, so sharp edges are chipped off and they break into smaller, rounder pieces (1).\n• Abrasion (1): pebbles rub against the bed and each other as they are moved, which gradually wears them down and smooths them (1).\n• The further the load has travelled (1), the longer it has been worn down by these processes (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 11: a photograph of a meander on the River Wye near Builth Wells. On the outside of the bend there is a steep, bare bank about 2 m high. On the inside of the bend there is a gently sloping bank of sand and gravel.\n\nExplain one reason why deposition takes place on the inside of the meander bend.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• The water is shallower on the inside of the bend so there is more friction (1); velocity falls, so the river loses energy and drops its load, forming a slip-off slope / point bar (1).\n• The fastest flow (thalweg) swings to the outside of the bend (1), leaving slower water on the inside where there is not enough energy to carry sediment (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nThe River Dart drains Dartmoor in Devon. Much of the upper basin is underlain by granite and covered in thin, often waterlogged peat soils.\n\nExplain one way in which the geology of a drainage basin can increase the risk of river flooding.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the way and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Impermeable rock such as granite does not let water soak through (1), so more rain becomes surface runoff and reaches the river quickly, giving a higher peak discharge (1).\n• Impermeable rock means soils soon become saturated (1), so further rain cannot infiltrate and flows overland into the channel (1).\nAllow reverse argument about permeable rock (e.g. chalk) storing water as groundwater.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 12: a photograph of a steep-sided valley in the Brecon Beacons. A small stream flows over boulders on the valley floor. On one valley side, a section of soil and turf has slipped downhill, leaving a curved scar. Below a rocky outcrop higher up there is a slope of loose, angular rock fragments (scree).\n\nExplain one way in which weathering or mass movement shapes the sides of river valleys in the upper course.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the process and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Freeze-thaw weathering (1): water in cracks freezes and expands, breaking off angular fragments that fall downslope as scree, widening the valley sides (1).\n• Slumping (1): after heavy rain the soil becomes saturated and heavy, so it slips downhill along a curved surface, supplying material to the river (1).\n• Soil creep (1): soil particles move very slowly downslope, gradually lowering the valley sides and feeding sediment into the channel (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 13: part of a 1:25 000 Ordnance Survey map of a stream in the Lake District (described for this practice question). On each side of the stream, contours rise from 200 m to 300 m within about 250 m of the channel. The contours are evenly spaced and form tight V shapes pointing upstream. The flat valley floor is too narrow to show on the map.\n\nUsing Figure 13, describe the shape of the valley.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each descriptive point, up to 2, e.g.:\n• V-shaped valley / cross-profile (1).\n• Steep valley sides — the land rises 100 m in about 250 m (1).\n• Narrow / almost no flat valley floor (1).\n• Valley sides are straight / evenly sloping (closely and evenly spaced contours) (1).\nOne mark max without reference to evidence from the map description.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 14: Pickering, North Yorkshire. The "Slowing the Flow" project in the Pickering Beck catchment included building leaky wooden dams across small streams, planting trees and creating a storage area upstream of the town that holds back floodwater and releases it slowly.\n\nExplain one benefit of using soft engineering to manage river flood risk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the benefit and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• It is cheaper than hard engineering (1), so it can be used to protect smaller towns where expensive walls would not be cost-effective (1).\n• It works with natural processes (1), e.g. leaky dams and trees slow runoff so water reaches the river more gradually and the peak discharge is lower (1).\n• It creates habitats for wildlife (1), e.g. wetland and woodland increase biodiversity (1).\n• It looks natural (1), so it does not spoil the landscape for residents or tourists (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nThe Met Office's UK climate projections (UKCP18) suggest that, under a high-emissions scenario, UK winters could be up to about 30% wetter by the 2070s, with more intense rainfall events.\n\nExplain one way in which climate change could increase river flood risk in the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the way and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• More winter rainfall (1) means soils stay saturated for longer, so more rain becomes surface runoff and rivers rise quickly (1).\n• More intense storms (1) deliver rain faster than it can infiltrate, giving higher peak discharges / flash floods (1).\n• Warmer air holds more moisture (1), so storms such as Storm Desmond can deliver record rainfall totals (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 2: storm hydrograph data for two drainage basins of similar size that received the same storm, 40 mm of rain in 6 hours, peak rainfall at 10:00 (data written for this practice question).\nBasin X — 45% of the area is urban (housing, roads, retail park); underlain by clay; time of peak discharge 13:00; peak discharge 40 cumecs.\nBasin Y — 70% of the area is woodland and pasture; underlain by chalk; time of peak discharge 21:00; peak discharge 18 cumecs.\n\nSuggest one reason why Basin X has a shorter lag time than Basin Y.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Basin X is 45% urban (1), so impermeable roads and roofs, plus drains, carry rainwater quickly to the river (1).\n• Basin X is underlain by impermeable clay (1), so little water infiltrates and most reaches the river as fast surface runoff (1).\n• Basin Y has woodland which intercepts rain (1), and permeable chalk which stores water as groundwater, so water reaches the river slowly (1) — reverse argument.\nThe reason must be linked to data in Figure 2 for 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nRivers transport their load in different ways depending on the size of the material and the energy of the river.\n\nIdentify the two statements about river transport that are correct.\nA  Traction is when large boulders are rolled along the river bed.\nB  Suspension is when minerals are dissolved in the water.\nC  Saltation is when pebbles are bounced along the river bed.\nD  Attrition is a process of transportation.\nE  Solution is when fine sand is carried within the water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct answer: A and C.\nB describes solution; D is a process of erosion; E describes suspension.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 1: storm hydrograph data for a gauging station on an upland river in Cumbria, 5–6 December (data written for this practice question).\nRainfall: light rain from 03:00; heaviest rainfall 08:00–09:00 (14 mm in that hour); rain stopped at 13:00.\nDischarge (cumecs): 06:00 — 8; 09:00 — 9; 12:00 — 15; 15:00 — 34; 18:00 — 52; 21:00 — 41; 00:00 — 28; 03:00 — 18; 06:00 — 12.\n\nThe data in Figure 1 is presented as a list. Describe one suitable method of presenting the rainfall and discharge data together.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the method and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• A storm hydrograph (1): discharge plotted as a line graph against time, with rainfall plotted as bars on the same time axis, using a second vertical axis (1).\n• A dual-axis graph (1) with time on the x-axis, rainfall (mm) bars on the left axis and discharge (cumecs) line on the right, so lag time can be read off (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nIn December 2015, Storm Desmond brought 341.4 mm of rain in 24 hours to Honister Pass in Cumbria, a UK record at the time. Rivers including the Eden, Kent and Derwent flooded towns such as Carlisle, Kendal, Keswick and Cockermouth, and the bridge at Pooley Bridge over the River Eamont was destroyed.\n\nExplain one economic impact of river flooding on people living in the affected area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the impact and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Homes and businesses were flooded (1), so owners faced high repair / insurance costs and some businesses closed for months (1).\n• Bridges and roads were damaged or destroyed (1), so journeys became longer and trade / tourism in villages such as Pooley Bridge fell (1).\n• Insurance premiums rose (1), making homes in flood-risk areas harder to insure or sell (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 1: storm hydrograph data for a gauging station on an upland river in Cumbria, 5–6 December (data written for this practice question).\nRainfall: light rain from 03:00; heaviest rainfall 08:00–09:00 (14 mm in that hour); rain stopped at 13:00.\nDischarge (cumecs): 06:00 — 8; 09:00 — 9; 12:00 — 15; 15:00 — 34; 18:00 — 52; 21:00 — 41; 00:00 — 28; 03:00 — 18; 06:00 — 12.\n\nDescribe the shape of the storm hydrograph shown by the data in Figure 1. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each descriptive point, up to 3; at least one must use data for full marks, e.g.:\n• Discharge stays low at first / base flow about 8–9 cumecs until 09:00 (1).\n• Steep rising limb — discharge rises by 43 cumecs in 9 hours, from 9 cumecs at 09:00 to 52 cumecs at 18:00 (1).\n• Peak discharge of 52 cumecs at 18:00 (1).\n• Falling limb is more gentle / longer than the rising limb — it takes 12 hours to fall to 12 cumecs (1).\n• Discharge has not returned to the starting level by 06:00 on 6 December (12 cumecs, compared with 8) (1).\nMax 2 marks without the use of data.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 15: a photograph looking upstream along a narrow river valley in Snowdonia. The river winds from side to side between ridges of high land that jut out alternately from each side of the valley, so that the view up the valley is blocked.\n\nExplain one reason why interlocking spurs form in the upper course of a river.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the reason and up to 2 further marks for development, e.g.:\n• In the upper course the river has a lot of energy for vertical erosion (1) but little energy for lateral erosion, so it cannot cut through areas of more resistant rock (1). It winds around them instead, and as it cuts down a V-shaped valley the high land between the bends is left as spurs that interlock (1).\n• The river follows the easiest route around harder rock (1), eroding downwards through hydraulic action and abrasion (1), so the valley sides on alternate bends are left standing as spurs (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 16: channel measurements at four sites along a river in Northumberland (data written for this practice question).\nSite A (3 km from source): width 2.1 m; mean depth 0.15 m; velocity 0.31 m/s.\nSite B (12 km): width 5.8 m; mean depth 0.42 m; velocity 0.47 m/s.\nSite C (25 km): width 11.4 m; mean depth 0.80 m; velocity 0.62 m/s.\nSite D (38 km): width 19.0 m; mean depth 1.35 m; velocity 0.71 m/s.\n\nDescribe the relationship between distance from the source and the characteristics of the channel. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each point, up to 3; data must be used for full marks, e.g.:\n• Positive relationship — as distance from the source increases, the channel gets wider: from 2.1 m at 3 km to 19.0 m at 38 km (1).\n• Mean depth also increases, from 0.15 m to 1.35 m (1).\n• Velocity increases downstream, from 0.31 m/s to 0.71 m/s (1).\n• The rate of increase in velocity slows downstream — it rises by 0.16 m/s between A and B but only 0.09 m/s between C and D (1).\nMax 2 marks without data.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nIn parts of the Upper Severn catchment in mid-Wales, large conifer plantations have been clear-felled, leaving bare slopes with drainage ditches dug across them.\n\nExplain one way in which deforestation can increase the risk of river flooding.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the way and up to 2 marks for development, e.g.:\n• Removing trees reduces interception (1), so more rain reaches the ground at once (1); with no roots to take up water and less infiltration, more of it flows over the surface into the river, giving a shorter lag time and higher peak discharge (1).\n• Bare soil is easily eroded (1); sediment is washed into the river channel (1), reducing its capacity so it overflows its banks more easily (1).\n• Drainage ditches speed up the flow of water (1) from the slopes to the river (1), so discharge rises more quickly after a storm (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 17: a photograph of High Force on the River Tees. The river drops about 21 m over a band of hard, dark rock (dolerite of the Whin Sill) that lies above softer layers of limestone, sandstone and shale. Below the falls there is a deep plunge pool and a narrow, steep-sided gorge about 700 m long.\n\nExplain how a waterfall and gorge are formed. You may use a labelled diagram.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `1 mark for each valid point, up to 4, or 1 mark for a point and 1 for its development, e.g.:\n• A waterfall forms where a band of resistant rock (dolerite) overlies less resistant rock (limestone / shale) (1).\n• The softer rock below is eroded more quickly by hydraulic action and abrasion (1), creating an overhang of hard rock (1).\n• Swirling water and rocks erode a deep plunge pool at the base (1).\n• The overhang is unsupported and collapses into the plunge pool (1).\n• This is repeated so the waterfall retreats upstream (1), leaving a steep-sided gorge downstream (1).\nA fully labelled diagram can gain full marks.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 18: part of a 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of the River Wear near Durham (described for this practice question). The river flows in a series of large loops across a wide, flat floodplain. On the floodplain about 300 m from the river there is a curved, horseshoe-shaped area of water not connected to the river.\n\nExplain how the horseshoe-shaped lake has been formed. You may use a labelled diagram.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `Identify as an ox-bow lake. 1 mark for each valid point, up to 4, or point plus development, e.g.:\n• Lateral erosion is greatest on the outside of meander bends, where velocity is highest (1), while deposition occurs on the inside bend (1).\n• Over time the neck of the meander becomes narrower as the outside bends erode towards each other (1).\n• During a flood, the river cuts through the neck, taking the shortest / straightest route (1).\n• Deposition seals off the ends of the old meander loop (1), leaving an ox-bow lake that may eventually dry out into a meander scar (1).\nA fully labelled sequence of diagrams can gain full marks.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 19: a photograph of the River Severn in flood near Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire. Brown water covers fields on both sides of the river. The river's raised banks can still be seen as lines of grass and trees above the water.\n\nExplain how floodplains and levées are formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for floodplains and up to 2 marks for levées.\nFloodplain:\n• Meanders migrate across the valley by lateral erosion, widening the valley floor (1).\n• When the river floods, water spreads out, slows down and deposits fine silt (alluvium), building up a wide, flat floodplain over many floods (1).\nLevées:\n• When the river overflows its banks, velocity falls sharply at the channel edge due to friction (1).\n• The heaviest / coarsest sediment is deposited first next to the channel, and repeated floods build these deposits into raised banks (1).\nAccept a valid point plus development for either landform.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nThe River Tees reaches the North Sea through a wide estuary between Middlesbrough and Redcar. At low tide, large areas of mudflat, such as Seal Sands, are exposed, with areas of salt marsh at their edges.\n\nExplain how deposition forms mudflats and salt marsh in an estuary.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid point, up to 4, or point plus development, e.g.:\n• An estuary is the tidal mouth of a river where fresh water meets salt water (1).\n• As the tide comes in, the river's flow is held back, so velocity falls and fine sediment / silt is deposited (1).\n• Fresh and salt water mixing causes clay particles to stick together (flocculation) and sink (1).\n• Over time layers of mud build up and are exposed at low tide as mudflats (1).\n• Salt-tolerant plants colonise the higher mudflats, trapping more sediment, so they build up into salt marsh (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nYou have studied a named UK river basin.\n\nFor a named UK river basin, explain two ways in which the shape of the river valley and channel changes from the upper course to the lower course.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `For each of two ways: 1 mark for the change and 1 mark for the reason. No marks if no river is named; max 2 if no reference to a named river at all.\nExample answer (River Tees):\n• The valley changes from a narrow, steep V shape near Cross Fell (1) because vertical erosion dominates where the river is high above base level and has steep gradient (1), to a wide, flat valley with a floodplain near Yarm (1) because lateral erosion and deposition dominate (1).\n• The channel becomes wider and deeper downstream (1) because tributaries add water so discharge increases, and lateral erosion widens the banks (1).\n• The gradient becomes gentler (1), giving a concave long profile as the river approaches sea level (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 20: a photograph of a town centre in northern England beside a river. Car parks and a shopping centre have been built on the floodplain right up to the riverbank, and the river has been straightened and lined with concrete through the town.\n\nExplain two ways in which human activities can increase the risk of river flooding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `For each of two ways: 1 mark for the activity and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Urbanisation / building on the floodplain (1): impermeable surfaces and drains increase runoff and shorten lag time, so the river rises faster (1).\n• Building on the floodplain (1): removes land that would store floodwater, and puts more people and property at risk (1).\n• Channelisation / straightening (1): water flows faster through the town and can cause flooding further downstream (1).\n• Deforestation (1): less interception and infiltration, so more water reaches the river quickly (1).\n• Farming practices, e.g. ploughing up and down slopes or compacting soil with heavy machinery (1), increase surface runoff (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 2: storm hydrograph data for two drainage basins of similar size that received the same storm, 40 mm of rain in 6 hours, peak rainfall at 10:00 (data written for this practice question).\nBasin X — 45% of the area is urban (housing, roads, retail park); underlain by clay; time of peak discharge 13:00; peak discharge 40 cumecs.\nBasin Y — 70% of the area is woodland and pasture; underlain by chalk; time of peak discharge 21:00; peak discharge 18 cumecs.\n\nUsing Figure 2, explain two physical factors that could explain the differences between the hydrographs of Basin X and Basin Y.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `For each of two factors: 1 mark for the factor (with reference to the figure) and 1 mark for the explanation. Land use (urban / woodland) is a human factor and is not credited here.\n• Geology / rock type (1): Basin X is on impermeable clay so water cannot infiltrate and flows over the surface, giving a lag of 3 hours and a peak of 40 cumecs, while Basin Y's permeable chalk stores water (1).\n• Soil saturation / infiltration (1): clay soils become waterlogged quickly so more rain becomes surface runoff (1).\n• Relief / slope (1): if Basin X is steeper, water flows more quickly to the channel (1). Accept as a suggestion even though slope is not given.\n• Drainage density (1): a basin with more streams per km² carries water to the main river more quickly (1).\n• Groundwater flow (1): in Basin Y water moves slowly through the chalk as throughflow / groundwater flow, so the lag time is 11 hours and the peak is lower (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nAfter severe flooding, some councils in England have used land-use zoning: high-risk parts of the floodplain are kept as playing fields, parks or farmland, and new housing is only allowed on higher ground.\n\nExplain one cost and one benefit of using floodplain zoning to manage river flood risk.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the cost and 1 for development; 1 mark for the benefit and 1 for development.\nCost, e.g.:\n• Restricts development (1), so land values may fall / the town has less space to build new homes and businesses (1).\n• Does not protect buildings already on the floodplain (1), so existing residents still need other defences (1).\nBenefit, e.g.:\n• It is cheap to put in place (1) because there is no building cost, only planning rules (1).\n• Fewer new properties are put at risk (1), so future flood damage and insurance claims are reduced (1).\n• Floodplain land can still store floodwater naturally (1), reducing flooding downstream (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nYou have studied a named river flood management scheme in the UK.\n\nFor a named UK river flood management scheme, explain two ways in which it has reduced flood risk.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `For each of two ways: 1 mark for the method and 1 mark for how it reduces risk. Max 2 if no named scheme or no specific detail.\nExamples:\n• Jubilee River, Maidenhead (completed 2002): an 11.6 km diversion channel (1) takes excess water from the River Thames around Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton, lowering river levels in the towns and protecting about 3,000 homes (1).\n• Jubilee River: weirs and sluice gates (1) control how much water enters the channel during a flood (1).\n• Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme: movable weirs on the River Aire (1) can be lowered to reduce river levels in the city centre during floods (1).\n• Leeds (phase 2): upstream tree planting and flood storage areas (1) slow the flow and reduce peak discharge in the city (1).\n• Pickering "Slowing the Flow": a storage bund holds back floodwater (1), releasing it slowly so peak flow in the town is reduced (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nOn 16 August 2004 a flash flood hit the village of Boscastle in north Cornwall. About 200 mm of rain fell in a few hours over the small, steep catchment of the River Valency and River Jordan, which meet in the village. More than 100 cars were swept away and about 100 people were rescued by helicopter.\n\nExplain two physical reasons why the flood at Boscastle was so severe.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `For each of two reasons: 1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Very intense rainfall (1): about 200 mm in a few hours fell faster than it could infiltrate, so most became surface runoff (1).\n• Steep valley sides / relief (1): water flowed rapidly to the rivers, giving a very short lag time (1).\n• Ground was already saturated from earlier rain (1), so infiltration was limited and runoff increased (1).\n• Small catchment with two rivers meeting in the village (1): their floodwaters combined at the confluence, raising discharge suddenly (1).\n• Impermeable rock / thin soils (1) increased surface runoff (1).\nAccept debris (trees, cars) blocking the bridge to form a temporary dam only if linked to the physical flow of water / load.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 21: number of properties in England at risk of river and sea flooding (data written for this practice question).\n2020: 5.2 million; 2050 (projected): 6.1 million.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the number of properties at risk of river flooding in England may increase in the future.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `For each of two reasons: 1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Climate change (1): wetter winters and more intense storms will increase peak river discharges, so more areas will flood (1).\n• More housing built on floodplains (1) because of demand for new homes in growing towns, so more properties are placed in flood-risk areas (1).\n• Urbanisation (1): more impermeable surfaces increase runoff, so rivers rise faster and higher (1).\n• Ageing flood defences (1) may fail or be overtopped if they are not maintained / upgraded (1).\n• Sea-level rise (1) means tides back up river water in estuaries, increasing flooding in towns near the coast (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nYou have studied a named UK river basin.\n\nExamine how erosion, transportation and deposition produce distinctive landforms along the long profile of a named UK river. (8)`, marks: 8, command: 'Examine', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 knowledge; AO2 understanding):\n• Upper course (e.g. upper Tees): high gradient and turbulent flow; vertical erosion by hydraulic action and abrasion; large angular load moved by traction; landforms — V-shaped valley, interlocking spurs, waterfall and gorge (High Force, 21 m drop over Whin Sill dolerite above softer limestone / shale).\n• Weathering (freeze-thaw) and mass movement (slumping, soil creep) widen the valley sides and supply load.\n• Middle course: discharge rises as tributaries join; lateral erosion on outside bends and deposition on inside bends form meanders (e.g. near Barnard Castle / Yarm); load becomes smaller and rounder through attrition, moved by saltation and suspension.\n• Ox-bow lakes form when meander necks are cut through in floods.\n• Lower course: gentle gradient, wide channel; deposition during floods forms floodplains and levées; at the mouth, tidal deposition forms mudflats and salt marsh in the estuary (Tees estuary, Seal Sands).\n• Examination should show how processes change downstream and how they interact (e.g. erosion upstream supplies the sediment that builds landforms downstream).\nResponses without a named river: max Level 2.\n\nLevels (8 marks):\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence (named examples / data). Makes a balanced, well-supported judgement about the question.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation that is relevant and supported by some evidence. Makes a judgement that is supported by some evidence but may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited, generic interpretation with little evidence. Judgement is absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nYou have studied a named river flood in the UK.\n\nAssess the relative importance of physical and human factors in causing a named UK river flood. (8)`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Physical factors, e.g. Storm Desmond, Cumbria, December 2015: record rainfall (341.4 mm in 24 hours at Honister Pass) after a wet November; saturated ground; steep, impermeable upland catchments; rivers Eden, Kent and Derwent overtopped defences.\n• Or Boscastle, 2004: about 200 mm of rain in a few hours; small, steep catchment; confluence in the village.\n• Human factors: building on floodplains (e.g. parts of Carlisle, Cockermouth town centre); urban surfaces and drains; low bridges trapping debris (Boscastle); upland drainage / overgrazing reducing infiltration; defences built to a lower standard than the 2015 flows.\n• Climate change linked to human activity may have made extreme rainfall more likely.\n• Assessment: physical factors usually trigger the flood, but human factors increase the scale of the impacts; judgement should weigh the two using evidence from the named flood.\nNo named flood: max Level 2.\n\nLevels (8 marks):\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence (named examples / data). Makes a balanced, well-supported judgement about the question.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation that is relevant and supported by some evidence. Makes a judgement that is supported by some evidence but may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited, generic interpretation with little evidence. Judgement is absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 22: information about two UK flood management schemes.\nJubilee River, Maidenhead (hard engineering): an 11.6 km man-made channel, completed in 2002, which diverts water from the River Thames around Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton. It cost about £110 million and protects about 3,000 homes. Some residents of villages downstream said it made their flooding worse in 2003.\nPickering "Slowing the Flow", North Yorkshire (soft engineering): leaky dams, tree planting and an upstream storage area, finished in 2015 at a cost of a few million pounds. The town did not flood on Boxing Day 2015, when nearby places did.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of hard engineering compared with soft engineering in managing UK river flood risk. (8)`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Hard engineering (dams, flood walls, embankments, channelisation, diversion channels) gives a high, predictable level of protection for large urban areas (Jubilee River protects about 3,000 homes); useful where high-value property is concentrated.\n• But it is very expensive (about £110 million); can move the problem downstream (claims of worse flooding downstream in 2003); can fail or be overtopped in extreme events; often unattractive and harms habitats.\n• Soft engineering (floodplain zoning, washlands, afforestation, river restoration, leaky dams) is much cheaper, works with natural processes, creates habitats and is sustainable; Pickering stayed dry in December 2015.\n• But it may not cope with the largest floods, needs large areas of land and landowner agreement, and takes years to become fully effective (trees grow slowly).\n• Many schemes now combine both (e.g. Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme phase 2).\n• Evaluation: a supported judgement on which is more effective, and under what conditions (size of settlement, cost, environmental value).\n\nLevels (8 marks):\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence (named examples / data). Makes a balanced, well-supported judgement about the question.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation that is relevant and supported by some evidence. Makes a judgement that is supported by some evidence but may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited, generic interpretation with little evidence. Judgement is absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 23: part of a 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Cockermouth, Cumbria (described for this practice question). The River Cocker flows north through the centre of the town and joins the River Derwent, which flows west. The main street and many older buildings lie on flat land below the 50 m contour, within 200 m of both rivers. To the north and south, land rises steeply to over 100 m, with newer housing estates on the higher ground. The River Derwent drains a large upland area of the Lake District to the east, including Bassenthwaite Lake. Cockermouth flooded in November 2009 and again in December 2015.\n\nAssess the reasons why parts of Cockermouth have a high risk of river flooding. Use evidence from Figure 23 and your own knowledge. (8)`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Location at the confluence of two rivers: floodwater from the Cocker and the Derwent combines, so discharge rises rapidly.\n• Large, steep, upland catchments with high rainfall (Lake District); impermeable rock and thin soils give fast runoff and short lag times.\n• Town centre and main street on flat floodplain below 50 m and close to both rivers, so these areas flood first; higher housing estates are at lower risk — risk is uneven within the town.\n• Extreme rainfall events: November 2009 (316.4 mm in 24 hours at Seathwaite) and Storm Desmond in 2015.\n• Human factors: historic buildings and businesses built close to the river; bridges that can trap debris; urban surfaces.\n• Defences built after 2009 were overtopped in 2015, showing that risk remains high.\n• Assessment should weigh physical site factors against human factors and reach a judgement, e.g. that the confluence location and upland catchment are the most important reasons, with the building of the town on the floodplain increasing the impact.\n\nLevels (8 marks):\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence (named examples / data). Makes a balanced, well-supported judgement about the question.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation that is relevant and supported by some evidence. Makes a judgement that is supported by some evidence but may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited, generic interpretation with little evidence. Judgement is absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 24: information about future UK flood risk.\n• UKCP18 projections suggest UK winters could be up to about 30% wetter by the 2070s under a high-emissions scenario, with more frequent heavy rainfall.\n• Since 2000 there have been several record-breaking UK flood events, including Storm Desmond in 2015.\n• Thousands of new homes are built on floodplains in England each year (data written for this practice question).\n\nAssess the extent to which climate change will be responsible for increasing river flood risk in the UK. (8)`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Climate change: warmer air holds more moisture, so more intense storms; wetter winters keep soils saturated; more frequent extreme events (e.g. Storm Desmond record rainfall); sea-level rise backs up rivers in estuaries.\n• Uncertainty: projections are ranges, not certainties; natural variability means individual floods cannot all be blamed on climate change; summers may become drier.\n• Other factors: urbanisation and building on floodplains increase runoff and exposure; deforestation and upland drainage; ageing defences; land use change in farming (soil compaction).\n• Human factors also increase the number of people and properties at risk even if rainfall did not change.\n• Management (e.g. new schemes, natural flood management) could reduce future risk.\n• Assessment: a balanced judgement on how far climate change, compared with other factors, will drive increased flood risk, supported by evidence from Figure 24 and own knowledge.\n\nLevels (8 marks):\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence (named examples / data). Makes a balanced, well-supported judgement about the question.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation that is relevant and supported by some evidence. Makes a judgement that is supported by some evidence but may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited, generic interpretation with little evidence. Judgement is absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 25: storm hydrograph data for the same gauging station on a river in the West Midlands, after two storms of similar size (data written for this practice question).\n1975 (catchment 10% urban): lag time 14 hours; peak discharge 22 cumecs; rising limb gentle.\n2020 (catchment 40% urban after a new town and bypass were built): lag time 5 hours; peak discharge 47 cumecs; rising limb steep.\n\nAssess the extent to which human activity explains the change in how this river responds to storms. Use evidence from Figure 25 and your own knowledge. (8)`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Data: lag time fell by 9 hours (14 to 5); peak discharge more than doubled (22 to 47 cumecs, an increase of about 114%); rising limb became steeper.\n• Human activity: urban area rose from 10% to 40%; impermeable surfaces reduce infiltration; drains and sewers carry water rapidly to the river; the bypass and new town may have involved removing hedges / trees (less interception) and channelising streams.\n• Other possible factors: the storms were only "similar" — differences in rainfall intensity or duration; antecedent conditions (saturated ground); season (vegetation cover, frozen ground); changes in farming practices; climate change making rainfall more intense.\n• Physical factors such as geology and relief are unlikely to have changed, which supports a human explanation.\n• Assessment: a judgement on how far urbanisation explains the change, recognising limits of the data.\n\nLevels (8 marks):\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence (named examples / data). Makes a balanced, well-supported judgement about the question.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation that is relevant and supported by some evidence. Makes a judgement that is supported by some evidence but may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited, generic interpretation with little evidence. Judgement is absent or unsupported.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section A — River Processes and Pressures.\nFigure 26: Ashbrook, a market town of 18,000 people in the River Severn basin (information written for this practice question).\n• The historic town centre lies on the floodplain at the confluence of the River Ash and a smaller tributary. It flooded in 2007, 2014 and 2020; 420 homes and 90 businesses are at risk.\n• Upstream, the catchment is steep pasture on impermeable mudstone, with some recently felled conifer plantations.\n• Tourism, based on the historic streets and riverside walks, provides 1 in 5 local jobs.\n• The council has a budget of about £15 million, with possible extra government funding.\nOption 1 — Build flood walls and embankments along the river through the town centre, with demountable barriers at the riverside walk (cost about £14 million).\nOption 2 — Natural flood management upstream: tree planting, leaky dams and two flood storage areas on farmland, paying farmers to take part (cost about £5 million).\nOption 3 — Floodplain zoning and property-level protection: no new building on the floodplain, plus flood doors, air-brick covers and raised electrics for at-risk homes and businesses (cost about £3 million).\n\nSelect the option you think would be the best way to manage flood risk in Ashbrook. Justify your choice. Use information from Figure 26 and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer. (12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Indicative content — any option can be chosen; credit depends on the quality of justification and use of evidence.\nOption 1: highest level of protection for the 420 homes and 90 businesses in the centre; protects tourism jobs; predictable. But uses nearly all the budget, may spoil the historic riverside that tourists visit, may increase flooding downstream, can be overtopped, and does not deal with fast runoff from the catchment.\nOption 2: tackles the cause (steep, impermeable, felled catchment gives short lag time); cheaper; creates habitats and is sustainable (cf. Pickering "Slowing the Flow"); leaves money for other measures. But takes years for trees to grow, needs farmers' agreement and land, may not cope with the largest floods.\nOption 3: cheapest; quick to install; prevents new risk; keeps the historic character. But does not stop flooding — homes and streets still get wet, and tourism is still disrupted; relies on residents fitting and using measures; zoning limits growth of the town.\nStrong answers explain why the chosen option is better than the other two, may suggest combining options (e.g. Option 2 with Option 3), and consider social, economic and environmental factors in the short and long term.\n\nLevels (12 marks):\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation of the information. Makes a well-reasoned, fully supported justification of the chosen option, with clear reference to why the other options were rejected.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Produces a reasonably coherent interpretation using some information from the figure. Justification is supported by evidence but may be unbalanced, with limited comparison with other options.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates some accurate knowledge and understanding. Partial interpretation of the information. Justification is partly supported and generic.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge, some inaccurate. Limited interpretation; justification unsupported or absent.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4 marks): Spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate performance (2–3 marks): Spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; uses a good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): Spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning; uses a limited range of specialist terms.\n0 marks: The learner writes nothing, or the response does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.3': {
    name: 'The UK\'s Evolving Human Landscape',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 1 is a table of population density for four UK regions in 2021 (rounded figures, data written for this practice question):\nLondon — 5,600 people per km²\nNorth West — 520 people per km²\nWales — 150 people per km²\nScotland — 70 people per km²\n\nStudy Figure 1. Identify the region with the lowest population density.\nA  London\nB  North West\nC  Wales\nD  Scotland`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `D (Scotland) (1)\nNo other answer acceptable.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nCambridge has a large number of jobs in university research, biotechnology and software development. The Cambridge Science Park opened on the edge of the city in 1970.\n\nIdentify which one of the following jobs is in the quaternary sector.\nA  a dairy farmer in Cambridgeshire\nB  an assembly worker in a car factory\nC  a scientist developing new medicines\nD  a sales assistant in a city-centre shop`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (a scientist developing new medicines) (1)\nNo other answer acceptable.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 2 shows the mean house price in two UK regions in 2023 (data written for this practice question):\nLondon — £520,000\nNorth East — £160,000\n\nCalculate the ratio of the mean house price in London to the mean house price in the North East.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3.25 : 1 (1)\nAccept 3.3 : 1 or 13 : 4.\n(520,000 ÷ 160,000 = 3.25)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 3 describes a line graph of the percentage of UK workers employed in the tertiary sector (data written for this practice question): 1971 — 55%; 1981 — 62%; 1991 — 70%; 2001 — 76%; 2011 — 80%; 2021 — 84%.\n\nStudy Figure 3. Identify the ten-year period with the largest increase in the percentage of workers employed in the tertiary sector.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1981–1991 (1)\n(Increases: 1971–81 = 7; 1981–91 = 8; 1991–2001 = 6; 2001–11 = 4; 2011–21 = 4 percentage points.)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nThe Outer Hebrides are a group of remote islands off the north-west coast of Scotland. Many young adults leave the islands to live in cities such as Glasgow and Edinburgh.\n\nState one push factor that may cause young adults to leave a remote rural area such as the Outer Hebrides.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark for any one valid push factor, e.g.:\n• lack of well-paid / skilled jobs (1)\n• few further education or university opportunities (1)\n• poor transport links / infrequent ferries or buses (1)\n• limited services, e.g. shops, entertainment, healthcare (1)\n• high cost of housing compared with local wages (1)\nDo not accept pull factors of the city on their own, e.g. 'more jobs in Glasgow'.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nLondon's population fell for several decades after the Second World War, but by the 2021 Census it had grown to about 8.8 million people.\n\nState one reason why London's population has grown since the 1990s.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark for any one valid reason, e.g.:\n• international migration / migrants arriving for work (1)\n• high rate of natural increase because of a young population (1)\n• growth of jobs in finance / business services / the tertiary and quaternary sectors (1)\n• regeneration making inner areas attractive places to live, e.g. Docklands (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nBetween 2001 and 2021 the population of many villages within 20 km of Cambridge grew, as people moved out of the city and travelled back in each day to work.\n\nIdentify the term for the movement of people from urban areas to rural areas.\nA  urbanisation\nB  suburbanisation\nC  counter-urbanisation\nD  re-urbanisation`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (counter-urbanisation) (1)\nNo other answer acceptable.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 4 shows the population of a village 15 km from Cambridge (data written for this practice question):\n2001 — 1,850 people\n2021 — 2,640 people\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) increase in the population of the village between 2001 and 2021. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (2,640 − 1,850) ÷ 1,850 × 100 or 790 ÷ 1,850 × 100 (1)\n1 mark for answer: 42.7% (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer to one decimal place with no working. 42.70% or 43% scores 1 mark.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 1 is a table of population density for four UK regions in 2021 (rounded figures, data written for this practice question): London — 5,600 people per km²; North West — 520 people per km²; Wales — 150 people per km²; Scotland — 70 people per km².\n\nExplain one reason why population density is higher in London than in Scotland.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• London has many jobs in finance and services (1) which attract people to live there from across the UK and abroad (1)\n• Much of Scotland is upland with steep slopes, thin soils and a harsh climate (1) so it is difficult to farm or build on and few people live there (1)\n• London is a major transport hub with airports, ports and rail links (1) so businesses locate there, creating employment that draws people in (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 5 describes the UK's population pyramid for 2021: the bars for ages 50–64 are the widest, the bars for ages 0–14 are narrower than those for ages 25–39, and there are more people aged 65 and over than in earlier censuses.\n\nExplain one reason why the UK's population is ageing.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Life expectancy has increased (1) because of better healthcare, diet and living conditions, so more people survive into old age (1)\n• Birth rates have fallen (1) because women have children later and families are smaller, so the proportion of young people falls (1)\n• The large 'baby boom' generation born after 1945 and in the 1960s (1) is now reaching retirement age (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 6 compares two rural areas in 2021 (data written for this practice question):\nRural Cornwall — 27% of people aged 65 and over\nSouth Cambridgeshire — 19% of people aged 65 and over\n\nSuggest one reason why a remote rural area such as Cornwall has a higher percentage of people aged 65 and over.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Many people retire to Cornwall (1) because of its attractive coastline / quiet environment (1)\n• Young adults move away to cities (1) because there are few well-paid jobs or universities, leaving an older population behind (1)\n• House prices are pushed up by second-home owners (1) so young families cannot afford to stay (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nIn 1986 the Japanese company Nissan opened a car factory near Sunderland in north-east England. It became one of the largest car plants in the UK and many local firms supply it with parts.\n\nExplain one way that foreign direct investment (FDI) has affected the UK economy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• FDI has created jobs in areas hit by deindustrialisation (1) such as the North East after coal mining and shipbuilding declined (1)\n• It has a multiplier effect (1) because local suppliers gain work and workers spend their wages in local shops (1)\n• Profits may go overseas to the parent company (1) so less money stays in the UK economy (1)\n• The UK economy becomes more dependent on decisions made abroad (1) so plants may close or move if global demand falls (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nFigure 7 shows the population and area of an inner London borough in 2021 (data written for this practice question):\nPopulation — 310,300\nArea — 19.8 km²\n\nCalculate the population density of the borough. Give your answer to the nearest 100 people per km². You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: 310,300 ÷ 19.8 (= 15,671.7) (1)\n1 mark for answer: 15,700 people per km² (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 8 is a table showing the percentage of UK workers in each employment sector (data written for this practice question):\n1971 — primary 4%, secondary 42%, tertiary 54%\n2021 — primary 1%, secondary 15%, tertiary 84%\n\nThe data shown in Figure 8 is presented in a table. Describe one other suitable method of data presentation to show the employment structure in 1971 and 2021.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a suitable method and 1 mark for description of how it would be used, e.g.:\n• Two pie charts (1) one for each year, with each sector shown as a segment of 3.6° per 1% (1)\n• Divided (compound) bar chart (1) with one bar for each year divided into the three sectors and a key (1)\n• Triangular graph (1) with one axis for each sector so each year is plotted as one point (1)\nDo not accept line graph for 1 mark without explanation of two separate years.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nIn 2014 the UK government launched the 'Northern Powerhouse' policy to encourage investment in cities such as Manchester, Leeds and Liverpool.\n\nExplain one way that UK government policy has tried to reduce economic differences between regions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Investing in transport links between northern cities (1) to make it easier for businesses to locate outside London and the South East (1)\n• Creating Enterprise Zones / Freeports with tax breaks (1) to attract firms and jobs to areas with high unemployment (1)\n• Moving government departments / the BBC to Salford (MediaCityUK) (1) bringing skilled jobs and spending to the North West (1)\n• Giving more powers and funding to city-region mayors (1) so decisions can be made locally to boost growth (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nLondon grew up at a point where the River Thames could be bridged and where ships could sail inland from the North Sea.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, explain one way in which its site or situation helped it to grow.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a feature of site/situation and 1 mark for development, e.g. London:\n• Located on the tidal River Thames (1) so it became a major port for trade with the rest of the world (1)\n• Lowest bridging point of the Thames (1) so roads met there, making it a centre for trade and government (1)\n• Close to continental Europe (1) which helped it become a centre for international trade and finance (1)\nCredit valid points for any named UK city, e.g. Bristol on the River Avon near the Severn Estuary.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nIn the 2021 Census, more than one third of London's residents had been born outside the UK. Some groups are concentrated in particular areas, for example many people of Bangladeshi heritage live in Tower Hamlets.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, explain one reason why international migrants have settled in particular parts of the city.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Cheaper rented housing in inner-city areas (1) which newly arrived migrants with low incomes could afford (1)\n• Friends and family already lived there (1) giving support, shops and places of worship that meet cultural needs (1)\n• Jobs nearby, e.g. in docks, factories or hospitals (1) so migrants lived close to work (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nFigure 9 shows the mean monthly rent for a two-bedroom flat in five London boroughs in 2023 (data written for this practice question):\nBorough A — £1,450\nBorough B — £2,100\nBorough C — £1,780\nBorough D — £1,320\nBorough E — £2,600\n\nCalculate the mean monthly rent for the five boroughs. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (1,450 + 2,100 + 1,780 + 1,320 + 2,600) ÷ 5 or 9,250 ÷ 5 (1)\n1 mark for answer: £1,850 (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nIn many villages in South Cambridgeshire house prices rose faster than in Cambridge itself between 2000 and 2020.\n\nExplain one reason why house prices have risen in rural areas close to a large city.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• High demand from commuters (1) who want a rural environment but can travel into the city to work (1)\n• Planning restrictions such as green belt limit new building (1) so the supply of houses is low compared with demand (1)\n• Better transport links / fast rail services (1) make the villages more accessible and attractive to buyers (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nMost of London's water is taken from the River Thames and the River Lea, which flow through rural areas before they reach the city.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, explain one way in which the city depends on the surrounding rural area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Water supply from rivers and reservoirs in rural areas (1) needed for the city's millions of residents and businesses (1)\n• Food from farms in the surrounding countryside (1) supplying markets and supermarkets (1)\n• Commuters live in rural areas (1) and provide the city's workforce (1)\n• Countryside provides land for recreation, e.g. country parks (1) improving residents' wellbeing (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nIn the 1960s and 1970s most of London's docks in the East End closed. Thousands of dock workers lost their jobs.\n\nExplain one reason for the closure of the docks in a UK city that you have studied.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Containerisation needed larger ships (1) which could not reach the narrow upstream docks, so trade moved to deep-water ports such as Tilbury (1)\n• Decline of UK manufacturing (1) meant fewer goods were exported through the docks (1)\n• Competition from cheaper ports overseas (1) reduced trade passing through the city (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nThe Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park in Stratford, east London, was built for the 2012 Olympic Games on former industrial land that included contaminated soil and old railway yards.\n\nExplain one environmental benefit of building on brownfield sites in a UK city.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a benefit and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Reduces urban sprawl (1) so greenfield land and the green belt are protected (1)\n• Derelict or contaminated land is cleaned up (1) improving the quality of the local environment, e.g. new parkland and wetlands (1)\n• Homes are closer to jobs and public transport (1) so people make fewer car journeys and pollution is reduced (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nLondon introduced an Ultra Low Emission Zone (ULEZ) in 2019. In 2023 it was extended to cover all London boroughs. Drivers of more polluting vehicles must pay a daily charge.\n\nExplain one way the ULEZ could make urban living in London more sustainable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way and 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Fewer older, polluting vehicles are driven (1) so air quality improves and there are fewer health problems such as asthma (1)\n• People are encouraged to use public transport, walk or cycle (1) reducing traffic congestion and carbon emissions (1)\n• Money raised is reinvested in public transport (1) making sustainable travel easier (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nGreat Shelford is a village about 6 km south of Cambridge with a railway station on the line into the city.\n\nIdentify two features that are typical of a rural area close to a large city.\nA  population is declining rapidly\nB  many residents commute to work in the city\nC  high house prices\nD  most residents work in farming\nE  very few services because of low demand`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1)\nC (1)\nIf more than two answers are given, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect answer.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 10 describes a choropleth map of population density in the UK (2021): the darkest shading (over 1,000 people per km²) covers London, Birmingham, Manchester, Liverpool, Leeds, Newcastle and Glasgow; medium shading (200–1,000 per km²) covers most of south-east England and the Midlands; the lightest shading (under 50 per km²) covers the Scottish Highlands, central Wales and parts of northern England such as Cumbria and Northumberland.\n\nDescribe the distribution of population density shown in Figure 10. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each descriptive point, up to 3 marks. Max 2 marks if no data or named places are used, e.g.:\n• Highest density (over 1,000 per km²) is in major cities / conurbations such as London and Manchester (1)\n• South-east England and the Midlands are generally densely populated (200–1,000 per km²) (1)\n• Lowest density (under 50 per km²) is in upland / remote areas such as the Scottish Highlands and central Wales (1)\n• Density is generally higher in the south and east than in the north and west (1)\n• Pockets of high density occur within sparsely populated areas, e.g. Glasgow / Newcastle (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 8 is a table showing the percentage of UK workers in each employment sector (data written for this practice question):\n1971 — primary 4%, secondary 42%, tertiary 54%\n2021 — primary 1%, secondary 15%, tertiary 84%\n\nDescribe the changes in the UK's employment structure shown in Figure 8. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct descriptive point, up to 3 marks. Max 2 marks without data, e.g.:\n• Tertiary employment has increased from 54% to 84% / by 30 percentage points (1)\n• Secondary employment has fallen sharply from 42% to 15% / by 27 percentage points (1)\n• Primary employment has fallen from 4% to 1% (1)\n• Tertiary has become by far the dominant sector, employing more than five times as many as secondary in 2021 (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nFigure 11 is an incomplete pie chart of the country of birth of residents in an east London borough, 2021 (data written for this practice question). It shows UK 55%, Bangladesh 15% and Other 5%; the remaining 25% is blank.\n\nDescribe how you would plot the following data to complete the pie chart: India 12%, Poland 8%, Romania 5%.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each, up to 3 marks:\n• Convert percentages to degrees by multiplying by 3.6: India 43.2°, Poland 28.8°, Romania 18° (1)\n• Use a protractor to measure each segment from the edge of the last plotted segment, starting with the largest / in order (1)\n• Shade or colour each segment differently and add it to the key / label it (1)\nAccept description of dividing the 25% (90°) blank section in proportion 12 : 8 : 5.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nFigure 12 compares two London boroughs (data written for this practice question):\nTower Hamlets — 35% of children living in poverty; male life expectancy 77 years\nRichmond upon Thames — 12% of children living in poverty; male life expectancy 82 years\n\nExplain one reason why there are differences in quality of life between different parts of a UK city that you have studied.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, plus up to 2 further marks for development, e.g.:\n• Many inner-city areas lost jobs when docks and factories closed (1) so unemployment and low incomes became common (1) meaning families cannot afford healthy diets or good housing, leading to lower life expectancy (1)\n• House prices are much higher in some boroughs (1) so wealthier people concentrate there while lower-income households live in older, overcrowded housing (1) which can lead to poorer health and educational outcomes (1)\n• Access to green space differs (1) leafy suburbs such as Richmond have large parks (1) encouraging exercise and improving mental and physical health (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nFigure 13 describes a scatter graph for 20 neighbourhoods in a UK city (data written for this practice question). The x-axis shows the percentage of adults with no qualifications (from 5% to 35%); the y-axis shows the percentage of households owning a car (from 30% to 90%). The points slope downwards from top left to bottom right: neighbourhoods with 5–10% no qualifications have 75–90% car ownership, those with 30–35% no qualifications have 30–45% car ownership. One neighbourhood with 25% no qualifications has 80% car ownership.\n\nDescribe the relationship shown on Figure 13. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each point, up to 3 marks. Max 2 marks without data:\n• Negative relationship / correlation (1)\n• As the percentage of adults with no qualifications increases, car ownership decreases, e.g. from 75–90% where 5–10% have no qualifications to 30–45% where 30–35% have none (1)\n• The relationship is fairly strong as most points lie close to a line of best fit (1)\n• There is an anomaly: a neighbourhood with 25% no qualifications but 80% car ownership (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nAbout 84% of the UK's population lives in urban areas, while large parts of upland Scotland and Wales have fewer than 50 people per km².\n\nExplain two reasons why the UK's population is unevenly distributed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each reason: 1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g.:\n• Relief and climate (1): uplands in the north and west are steep, wet and cold, making farming and building difficult, so few people live there (1)\n• Employment (1): jobs are concentrated in cities, especially London and the South East, so people move there to work (1)\n• Historical industry (1): cities grew on coalfields and at ports during the Industrial Revolution, e.g. Manchester, Glasgow, creating large populations that remain (1)\n• Transport and accessibility (1): lowland areas have good road, rail and air links, attracting businesses and people (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nKellingley Colliery in Yorkshire, the UK's last deep coal mine, closed in December 2015. Over the same period, jobs in finance, the creative industries and online retail increased.\n\nExplain two reasons why the UK's economy has changed from secondary industry to tertiary and quaternary industry.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each reason: 1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g.:\n• Globalisation (1): manufacturing moved to countries with cheaper labour such as China, so UK factories closed (1)\n• Exhaustion of resources / cheaper imports (1): coal became expensive to mine and cheaper coal and gas could be imported, so mines closed (1)\n• Rising incomes (1): people spend more on services such as leisure, tourism and healthcare, creating tertiary jobs (1)\n• Technology and a skilled workforce (1): universities and research create quaternary jobs in ICT, biotechnology and design (1)\n• Mechanisation (1): machines replaced workers in remaining factories and farms (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nIn May 2004 eight countries in eastern Europe, including Poland and Lithuania, joined the European Union. The UK allowed their citizens to work in the UK straight away, and net migration to the UK increased.\n\nExplain two ways in which international migration has affected the UK's population.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each way: 1 mark for the way and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g.:\n• Population growth (1): net migration has added people directly and accounts for much of the UK's recent population increase (1)\n• Younger age structure (1): most migrants are young adults of working age, slowing the ageing of the population (1)\n• Higher birth rates (1): many migrants are of child-bearing age, so the number of births rises (1)\n• Greater cultural diversity (1): especially in cities such as London, Birmingham and Leicester, with new languages, cuisines and places of worship (1)\n• Uneven distribution (1): migrants settle mainly in cities and some agricultural areas such as Lincolnshire, increasing their populations faster than elsewhere (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nYou have studied two contrasting rural areas: one close to a large city, for example South Cambridgeshire, and one remote rural area, for example Cornwall or the Outer Hebrides.\n\nExplain one change in a rural area close to a city and one change in a remote rural area.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for the rural area close to a city and up to 2 marks for the remote rural area: 1 mark for the change and 1 mark for development.\nNear a city, e.g.:\n• Population growth from counter-urbanisation (1) as commuters move in, increasing house prices / building new estates (1)\n• Village services change (1): local shops may close as commuters shop in the city, while cafés or gastropubs open (1)\nRemote area, e.g.:\n• Out-migration of young people (1) leaving an ageing population and closure of schools / bus services (1)\n• Growth of tourism and second homes (1) bringing seasonal jobs but pricing local people out of housing (1)\n• Farm diversification (1), e.g. holiday lets or farm shops, to make up for falling farm incomes (1)\nMax 2 if only one type of area is addressed.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nLondon's population fell from about 8.6 million in 1939 to under 7 million in the 1980s, but grew to about 8.8 million by 2021.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, explain two reasons why its population has changed over time.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each reason: 1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g. London:\n• Suburbanisation / counter-urbanisation (1): after 1945 people moved to new towns such as Stevenage and Harlow and to outer suburbs, so inner London's population fell (1)\n• Deindustrialisation (1): closure of the docks and factories in the 1960s–80s caused people to leave in search of work (1)\n• International migration (1): migrants arrived for jobs in services, transport and health, increasing the population (1)\n• Natural increase (1): London's young population means births greatly exceed deaths (1)\n• Re-urbanisation (1): regeneration of areas such as Docklands made inner areas attractive again (1)\nCredit any named UK city.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nIn London, the City and Canary Wharf contain some of the most valuable office space in Europe, while nearby parts of Tower Hamlets and Newham have some of the highest rates of child poverty in England.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, explain two ways in which inequality affects people living in different parts of the city.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each way: 1 mark for the way and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g.:\n• Health (1): people in deprived areas have lower life expectancy because of poor diets, overcrowded housing and higher smoking rates (1)\n• Housing (1): low-income families live in overcrowded or poor-quality housing, while wealthy areas have large, expensive homes (1)\n• Education (1): pupils in deprived areas may achieve lower GCSE results, limiting job opportunities (1)\n• Employment and income (1): unemployment is higher in some inner-city areas, so families depend on benefits (1)\n• Environment (1): some areas suffer more air pollution and have less green space, affecting wellbeing (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nThe Lower Lea Valley in Stratford, east London, was an area of derelict factories, railway sidings and polluted land before it was redeveloped as the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park for the 2012 Olympic Games.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, explain two strategies used to regenerate a brownfield area.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each strategy: 1 mark for the strategy and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g. Stratford:\n• Cleaning contaminated land (1) so it could be reused safely for housing and parkland (1)\n• Improved transport links (1), e.g. Stratford International station and the DLR / Elizabeth line, making the area accessible for jobs and visitors (1)\n• New housing (1), e.g. East Village in the former athletes' village, including some affordable homes (1)\n• Retail and employment (1), e.g. Westfield Stratford City shopping centre created thousands of jobs (1)\n• New cultural and education uses (1), e.g. university and museum campuses on the park, attracting investment (1)\nCredit other named examples, e.g. London Docklands, Bristol Harbourside.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nThe London Docklands Development Corporation (LDDC) regenerated the former docks between 1981 and 1998. Canary Wharf became a major financial centre, and the Docklands Light Railway (DLR) opened in 1987.\n\nExplain one benefit and one problem of the regeneration of an area in a UK city that you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for the benefit and up to 2 marks for the problem: 1 mark for each point and 1 mark for development.\nBenefits, e.g.:\n• Thousands of new jobs in finance and services (1) bringing investment and reducing dereliction (1)\n• Better transport such as the DLR and London City Airport (1) improving access to the rest of London (1)\nProblems, e.g.:\n• Many new jobs needed skills that former dock workers did not have (1) so local unemployment stayed high (1)\n• House prices rose sharply (1) so local people were priced out / gentrification broke up communities (1)\nMax 2 if only a benefit or only a problem is given.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nEach weekday, hundreds of thousands of people travel into London by rail from towns and villages in counties such as Kent, Surrey and Hertfordshire. At weekends many Londoners visit the Chilterns and the South Downs for recreation.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, explain two ways in which the city is linked to its surrounding rural area.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each link: 1 mark for the link and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g.:\n• Commuting (1): people live in rural areas and travel into the city for work, supporting the city's economy and raising rural house prices (1)\n• Recreation and tourism (1): city residents visit national parks and the countryside, bringing income to rural businesses (1)\n• Food supply (1): farms in the surrounding area supply markets and supermarkets in the city (1)\n• Water and energy (1): reservoirs and rivers in rural areas supply the city's water (1)\n• Services (1): rural residents travel into the city for hospitals, universities and shopping (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nSince the 1980s UK governments have used a range of policies to influence the economy, including privatising industries such as British Telecom and creating Enterprise Zones with lower taxes for businesses.\n\nExplain two ways that UK government policy has affected the UK economy.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each way: 1 mark for the way and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g.:\n• Privatisation (1): state-owned industries were sold, increasing competition but some jobs were lost through restructuring (1)\n• Enterprise Zones / Freeports (1): tax breaks attract businesses to areas such as Docklands, creating jobs (1)\n• Investment in infrastructure (1), e.g. the Elizabeth line or improved northern rail, making regions more attractive to investors (1)\n• Encouraging foreign direct investment (1): low corporation tax and grants attracted firms such as Nissan, creating manufacturing jobs (1)\n• Openness to migration (1): this helped fill labour shortages in sectors such as farming and health (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nThe Cambridge Science Park opened in 1970 on the northern edge of Cambridge. Today the Cambridge area has hundreds of technology and biotechnology companies.\n\nExplain two reasons why quaternary industries have located near Cambridge.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each reason: 1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g.:\n• Close to a world-class university (1) providing research links and a supply of highly qualified graduates (1)\n• Good transport links (1), e.g. the M11 and fast rail to London and Stansted Airport, for meeting clients and investors (1)\n• Attractive environment and edge-of-city land (1) with space to build modern offices and laboratories with parking (1)\n• Clustering / agglomeration (1): firms locate near each other to share ideas, skilled staff and investors (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nLondon's transport has changed in recent years: the Santander Cycles hire scheme began in 2010, the Elizabeth line opened in 2022 and the ULEZ now covers all London boroughs.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, explain two strategies that have been used to make transport more sustainable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each strategy: 1 mark for the strategy and 1 mark for development, x2, e.g.:\n• Cycle hire schemes and cycle superhighways (1) encourage people to cycle rather than drive, reducing emissions and congestion (1)\n• New rail lines such as the Elizabeth line (1) provide fast, high-capacity public transport, cutting car journeys (1)\n• Congestion charge / ULEZ (1) discourage polluting vehicles from entering the city, improving air quality (1)\n• Electric / hybrid buses (1) reduce air pollution from public transport (1)\nCredit any named UK city, e.g. Bristol's MetroBus.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 14 describes changes in a former coal-mining region of northern England (data written for this practice question): 1980 — 40,000 mining jobs; 2020 — no mining jobs. Unemployment was 18% in 1990 and 5% in 2020. New jobs include call centres, warehouses beside the motorway and a car-parts factory. 30% of young adults left the region between 1990 and 2010.\n\nAssess the impacts of deindustrialisation on the people and places of a UK region.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level-based mark scheme (AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies understanding to produce a full, balanced assessment of a range of impacts, using Figure 14 and own knowledge; a supported conclusion about which impacts are most significant.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies understanding to produce a partial assessment that may be unbalanced; some use of evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge. Limited assessment; little or no use of evidence.\n0: No acceptable response.\nIndicative content:\n• Loss of 40,000 jobs led to high unemployment (18% in 1990) and poverty; negative multiplier effect as shops and services closed\n• Social impacts: out-migration of 30% of young adults, ageing population, health problems and loss of community identity\n• Environmental: derelict land and spoil heaps; but cleaner air and rivers after mines closed, and reclaimed land used for parks\n• Recovery: unemployment fell to 5% by 2020 as new tertiary jobs and FDI arrived, but these may be lower paid / less secure\n• Conclusion may weigh short-term severe impacts against longer-term partial recovery, or differences between groups of people.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nFigure 15 gives information about the London Docklands (data written for this practice question, except where named): before regeneration in 1981, about 40,000 people lived in the area and 60% of land was derelict. By 2020 about 120,000 people worked in Canary Wharf and the population of the area had more than doubled. In 2020 the borough of Tower Hamlets still had one of the highest rates of child poverty in London.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, evaluate the success of the regeneration of one area of the city.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level-based mark scheme (AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate and detailed knowledge of a named regeneration project. Full evaluation weighing successes against failures for different groups, with a substantiated judgement about overall success.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge. Partial evaluation, perhaps one-sided; a judgement that is only partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge, generic points about regeneration; limited or no judgement.\n0: No acceptable response.\nIndicative content (London Docklands):\n• Successes: derelict land reclaimed; Canary Wharf became a global financial centre; DLR, Jubilee line extension and London City Airport improved access; large rise in jobs and population\n• Failures: many jobs went to commuters rather than former dock workers; house prices rose and gentrification pushed out some locals; inequality remains high, e.g. child poverty in Tower Hamlets\n• Environmental: new parks and cleaned-up docks, but high-rise development and increased traffic\n• Judgement: e.g. an economic success but less successful socially for long-standing residents.\nCredit other projects, e.g. Stratford / Olympic Park, Bristol Harbourside.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nIn the 2021 Census, more than 300 languages were spoken in London and over a third of residents had been born outside the UK. Areas such as Brick Lane in Tower Hamlets and Southall in Ealing are well known for their South Asian communities.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, assess the extent to which international migration has changed the character of the city.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level-based mark scheme (AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate, detailed knowledge of the named city. Full assessment of economic, social, cultural and demographic changes, weighing them against other causes of change (e.g. deindustrialisation, regeneration), with a supported conclusion on 'extent'.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge. Partial assessment; may list impacts with little weighing.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; generic statements about migration.\n0: No acceptable response.\nIndicative content:\n• Demographic: younger population, higher birth rates, population growth since the 1990s\n• Economic: migrants fill jobs in health, transport, construction and finance; new businesses\n• Cultural: restaurants, festivals (e.g. Notting Hill Carnival), places of worship, shops; distinctive neighbourhoods\n• Pressures: demand for housing and school places in some boroughs\n• Other factors: changes in the economy and regeneration have also shaped the city, so migration is one of several causes.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 16 compares two rural areas (data written for this practice question):\nSouth Cambridgeshire — population change 2001–2021: +25%; mean house price 12 × local mean salary; 1 village shop closed, 2 new housing estates built.\nA remote area of Cornwall — population change 2001–2021: +3%; 1 in 7 houses are second homes; primary school closed in 2018; seasonal tourism jobs.\n\nAssess the impacts of population change on two contrasting rural areas of the UK.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level-based mark scheme (AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate knowledge of both areas. Full, balanced assessment of positive and negative impacts in both areas using Figure 16 and own knowledge; a supported conclusion comparing the significance of impacts.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge. Partial assessment, perhaps one area only or mainly negative impacts.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; limited use of evidence.\n0: No acceptable response.\nIndicative content:\n• Near a city: counter-urbanisation adds 25% more people; house prices unaffordable for young locals; new estates on greenfield land; traditional services decline as commuters shop in the city, but some services (e.g. schools) are supported\n• Remote area: little growth and an ageing population; second homes leave villages empty in winter; school closure means long journeys; tourism brings income but jobs are seasonal and low-paid\n• Judgement: e.g. impacts are more damaging in the remote area because services are lost, or both suffer from housing affordability.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 17 shows information about UK regions (data written for this practice question):\nLondon — mean weekly earnings £850; unemployment 4.5%\nSouth East — mean weekly earnings £720; unemployment 3.6%\nNorth East — mean weekly earnings £580; unemployment 5.4%\nWales — mean weekly earnings £590; unemployment 4.0%\n\nAssess the extent to which changes in the UK economy have benefited all regions of the UK equally.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level-based mark scheme (AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate knowledge of economic change. Full assessment of how growth and decline have been distributed between core and periphery, using Figure 17 and own knowledge; a substantiated conclusion on 'extent'.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial assessment; some use of data.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; little or no use of data.\n0: No acceptable response.\nIndicative content:\n• Growth of finance, business services and quaternary jobs concentrated in London and the South East (core), so earnings are highest there (£850 vs £580 in the North East)\n• Deindustrialisation hit the North East, Wales, Scotland and the Midlands hardest (coal, steel, shipbuilding)\n• Some peripheral areas gained FDI (e.g. Nissan in Sunderland) and government investment (Northern Powerhouse, Freeports)\n• The core has problems too: very high housing costs reduce real incomes; London unemployment (4.5%) is higher than in Wales (4.0%)\n• Conclusion: benefits have been uneven, favouring the South East, though the gap varies by measure.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nBedZED in the London Borough of Sutton was completed in 2002. It is a housing development built from recycled and local materials with solar panels, rainwater harvesting and a car-sharing club. London also aims to increase the proportion of household waste that is recycled.\n\nFor a UK city that you have studied, evaluate how far urban living in the city has become more sustainable.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level-based mark scheme (AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate, detailed knowledge of sustainability strategies in a named city. Full evaluation of their effectiveness and their limits, with a substantiated judgement on 'how far'.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial evaluation; may describe strategies with little judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated, generic knowledge; limited or no judgement.\n0: No acceptable response.\nIndicative content (London):\n• Transport: Elizabeth line, cycle hire and cycle superhighways, ULEZ and the congestion charge have reduced emissions and car use\n• Housing and energy: BedZED and brownfield regeneration reduce energy use and protect green space\n• Waste: recycling schemes, but recycling rates remain below targets in many boroughs\n• Green space: parks, e.g. Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park; London has many green spaces\n• Limits: small scale of eco-developments; air pollution remains high near busy roads; housing shortage; high consumption\n• Judgement: e.g. progress in transport, but limited in housing and waste, so only partly sustainable.` },
      { q: `Section B — The UK's Evolving Human Landscape.\nFigure 18 shows the percentage of the UK population aged 65 and over (data written for this practice question): 1981 — 15%; 2001 — 16%; 2021 — 19%; 2041 (projected) — 24%.\n\nAssess the impacts of an ageing population on the UK.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level-based mark scheme (AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8): Accurate knowledge and understanding. Full, balanced assessment of social and economic impacts, both positive and negative, using Figure 18; a supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–5): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial assessment, likely one-sided.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Isolated knowledge; little use of data.\n0: No acceptable response.\nIndicative content:\n• The proportion aged 65+ is projected to rise from 15% in 1981 to 24% in 2041\n• Negative: rising pension and NHS / social care costs; fewer workers paying tax relative to dependants; pressure on carers\n• Regional variation: greater impacts in retirement areas such as Cornwall and the south coast\n• Positive: older people volunteer, provide childcare and spend in the 'grey economy'; new jobs in care and leisure\n• Responses: raising the state pension age; migration of young workers helps balance the population\n• Judgement on overall significance.` },
      { q: `Section B — Dynamic UK Cities.\nLondon's population is projected to keep growing and the city needs many more homes. Three options have been proposed (information partly written for this practice question):\nOption 1 — Release some green belt land on the edge of London for new housing estates.\nOption 2 — Build high-density housing on brownfield sites within the city, such as former industrial land in east London.\nOption 3 — Build new garden cities outside London, such as Ebbsfleet in Kent, linked to London by fast rail.\n\nSelect the option you think would be the most sustainable way to provide new housing for London. Justify your choice. Use information from the options and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer.\n(12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level-based mark scheme (AO2 4 marks / AO3 8 marks). No option is better than another; credit is for the quality of the justification.\nLevel 4 (10–12): Applies accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. A full, balanced evaluation of the chosen option across social, economic and environmental sustainability, with reasons for rejecting the other options; a coherent, fully supported decision.\nLevel 3 (7–9): Mostly accurate knowledge. A clear but not fully balanced justification; other options considered briefly.\nLevel 2 (4–6): Some accurate knowledge. A partial justification of the chosen option with limited reference to sustainability or to other options.\nLevel 1 (1–3): Isolated knowledge; a simple choice with little justification.\n0: No acceptable response.\nIndicative content:\n• Option 1: cheaper and quicker to build; family homes with gardens; but loss of farmland, habitats and recreation space; urban sprawl and more car commuting\n• Option 2: reuses derelict land; close to jobs and public transport (e.g. Elizabeth line); protects the green belt; but expensive to clean contaminated land; high density may mean small flats, little green space and high prices, risk of gentrification\n• Option 3: planned infrastructure and green space; relieves pressure on London; but long commutes, loss of greenfield land in Kent, may become dormitory towns\n• Links to population growth, housing shortage and inequality in London.\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate performance (2–3): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; uses a good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning; uses a limited range of specialist terms.\n0: no SPaG marks awarded, or the response does not relate to the question.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.4': {
    name: 'Geographical Investigations',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nA group of students measured the width of a small river at five sites along a 2 km stretch near their school. Figure 1 shows their results (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 1: channel width (m) — Site 1: 3.2; Site 2: 4.1; Site 3: 3.8; Site 4: 4.5; Site 5: 3.9.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the mean channel width.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3.9 m (1)\nWorking: 3.2 + 4.1 + 3.8 + 4.5 + 3.9 = 19.5; 19.5 ÷ 5 = 3.9.` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents investigating beach sediment laid a tape measure from the sea wall to the low-water mark. They collected and measured one pebble every 5 m along the tape.\n\nIdentify the type of sampling the students used.\nA  random\nB  stratified\nC  systematic\nD  opportunistic`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C  systematic (1)\nSamples were taken at a regular interval (every 5 m).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nStudents carried out an environmental quality survey at seven sites in the centre of a UK town. Each site was given a total score from −20 (very poor) to +20 (very good). Figure 2 shows the totals (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 2: Site A 12; Site B −4; Site C 8; Site D 3; Site E 15; Site F −1; Site G 6.\n\nUsing Figure 2, state the median environmental quality score.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `6 (1)\nOrdered: −4, −1, 3, 6, 8, 12, 15 — the 4th of 7 values is 6.` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Changing Rural Settlements.\nA group of students wanted to find out how deprivation varies between two villages in Herefordshire.\n\nGive one secondary source of data the students could use to compare deprivation in the two villages.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for any valid secondary source, e.g.:\n• Index of Multiple Deprivation (IMD) data\n• 2021 Census data (e.g. car ownership, unemployment, qualifications)\n• house price data from a property website\n• parish council / local authority reports.\nDo not credit primary methods such as questionnaires.` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nAt one site, students measured a river channel width of 4.0 m. The mean of their depth readings across the channel was 0.25 m.\n\nCalculate the cross-sectional area of the channel at this site.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1.0 m² (1)\nWorking: width × mean depth = 4.0 × 0.25 = 1.0 m². Units required.` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents measured the long axis of six pebbles at the top of a beach (data written for this practice question).\nPebble long axis (mm): 23, 41, 18, 57, 35, 29.\n\nCalculate the range of the pebble lengths.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `39 mm (1)\nWorking: 57 − 18 = 39.` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nAt Site 3 on a river, students calculated a cross-sectional area of 1.2 m². They timed a float over 10 m three times and worked out a mean velocity of 0.35 m/s (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the discharge at Site 3. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct method: discharge = cross-sectional area × velocity, 1.2 × 0.35 (1)\n1 mark for correct answer with units: 0.42 m³/s (cumecs) (1)` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents measured the width of the same beach, from the sea wall to the low-water mark, on two calm days at low tide (data written for this practice question).\nMay: 32 m.  December: 24 m.\n\nCalculate the percentage change in beach width between May and December. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for method: (24 − 32) ÷ 32 × 100 (1)\n1 mark for answer: −25% / a 25% decrease (1)` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nStudents investigating quality of life in Bristol divided the city's wards into three groups using the Index of Multiple Deprivation (most deprived, middle, least deprived). They then chose two streets in each group to survey.\n\nExplain one reason why the students used stratified sampling.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a reason, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• To make sure every type of area was represented (1) so that the results could be compared fairly across areas with different levels of deprivation (1).\n• To avoid all sites being in similar areas by chance (1), which would make the conclusion about variation in quality of life unreliable (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Changing Rural Settlements.\nStudents recorded the services in three villages in North Yorkshire (data written for this practice question).\nVillage P: primary school, pub, church, bus stop (2 buses per day).\nVillage Q: pub, church, post office, shop, GP surgery, bus stop (hourly buses).\nVillage R: church, bus stop (1 bus per week).\n\nIdentify the two statements that are supported by the data.\nA  Village R has the most services.\nB  All three villages have a church.\nC  Village Q has the best public transport.\nD  Village P has a shop.\nE  Village P has a GP surgery.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1)\nC (1)` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nStudents measured depth and velocity by wading into a river in March, a few days after heavy rain.\n\nExplain one risk the students faced when collecting their data.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a risk, +1 for development (which may include how it was reduced), e.g.:\n• The river may be deeper and faster than usual after heavy rain (1) so students could lose their footing and be swept away; they should stay out of water above knee height (1).\n• Slippery rocks on the bed (1) could cause falls and injury, so students should wear wellingtons/waders and work in pairs (1).\n• Water may be polluted by sewage or farm run-off (1) causing illness, so gloves should be worn and hands washed (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents measured one beach profile from the sea wall to the sea using ranging poles and a clinometer, recording each change in slope. They did this at one point on the beach only.\n\nSuggest one way the students could have improved the reliability of their beach profile data.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid improvement, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• Measure profiles at several points along the beach, e.g. on both sides of each groyne (1), so that one unusual profile does not distort the results (1).\n• Repeat each clinometer reading with different students (1) and use the mean to reduce human error (1).\n• Measure at the same state of tide on each visit (1) so profiles are comparable over time (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nStudents counted pedestrians for 10 minutes at eight sites in Leeds city centre, from the main shopping street out to the edge of the centre. They presented their counts in a table.\n\nDescribe one other suitable method of data presentation to show the pedestrian counts.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a suitable method, +1 for description, e.g.:\n• Proportional symbols on a map of the centre (1), with each circle's size in proportion to the count at that site so the pattern with distance can be seen (1).\n• A bar chart (1) with sites ordered by distance from the main shopping street on the x-axis and pedestrian count on the y-axis (1).\n• Flow lines / a located bar chart on a base map (1) showing where counts are highest (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Changing Rural Settlements.\nStudents asked people in the main street of a Cornish village whether they thought second homes were a problem. They carried out all 30 questionnaires on a Tuesday morning in February.\n\nExplain one reason why the results of the questionnaire may be biased.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a reason, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• On a weekday morning many working-age residents would be at work (1), so the views of retired people may be over-represented (1).\n• In February few second-home owners or tourists would be present (1), so only one side of the issue is likely to be heard (1).\n• People in the main street may not be typical of the whole village (1), e.g. those on outlying farms are missed (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating how drainage basin and channel characteristics influence flood risk for people and property.\n\nState your enquiry question and explain one reason why it was suitable for a geographical investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason why it was suitable, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• It could be answered using measurable data such as width, depth and velocity (1), which allowed results to be compared with the Bradshaw model (1).\n• The river was close to school and shallow enough to be safe (1), so data could be collected at several sites in one day (1).\nThe enquiry question itself is not credited on its own.` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating how and why quality of life varies within urban areas.\n\nExplain one way you used secondary data in your investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a named secondary source and use, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• We used IMD data for each Lower Super Output Area (1) to choose sites in contrasting areas so we could compare quality of life (1).\n• We used 2021 Census data on health and car ownership (1) to check whether our environmental quality scores matched wider measures of quality of life (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents measured the long axis of 11 pebbles at the low-water mark (data written for this practice question).\nPebble long axis (mm), in order: 18, 22, 25, 27, 31, 34, 38, 41, 45, 52, 60.\n\nCalculate the interquartile range of the pebble lengths. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for both quartiles correctly identified: lower quartile = 25 (3rd value), upper quartile = 45 (9th value) (1)\n1 mark for answer: 45 − 25 = 20 mm (1)` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nStudents measured surface velocity by timing how long an orange took to float 10 m downstream.\n\nExplain one limitation of this method of measuring velocity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a limitation, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• The float only measures surface velocity (1), so it does not show the slower flow near the bed and banks caused by friction (1).\n• The float can be blown by wind or caught on rocks/vegetation (1), giving times that are too long or too short (1).\n• Human reaction time on the stopwatch (1) makes short timings inaccurate (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Changing Rural Settlements.\nCensus data shows that the population of a village in the Yorkshire Dales was 412 in 2011 and 380 in 2021 (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage change in the village's population between 2011 and 2021. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for method: (380 − 412) ÷ 412 × 100 (1)\n1 mark for answer: −7.8% / a decrease of 7.8% (1)` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nStudents surveyed environmental quality along a transect from the CBD of Manchester to an inner-city residential area (data written for this practice question).\nScores (−20 to +20): CBD 9; 0.5 km 4; 1.0 km −6; 1.5 km 2; 2.0 km 7.\n\nIdentify the two conclusions that are supported by the data.\nA  Environmental quality is lowest 1.0 km from the CBD.\nB  Environmental quality falls steadily with distance from the CBD.\nC  The CBD has the highest score.\nD  Two sites have a negative score.\nE  The score at 2.0 km is higher than at the CBD.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `A (1)\nC (1)` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nStudents compared environmental quality with the percentage of households without a car at six sites in Sheffield (data written for this practice question).\nSite — environmental quality score; % households without a car:\nS1 — 14; 18%\nS2 — 10; 25%\nS3 — 6; 31%\nS4 — 9; 40%\nS5 — −3; 48%\nS6 — −8; 55%\n\nDescribe the relationship shown by the data. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the overall relationship, 1 for detail, 1 for data/anomaly, e.g.:\n• Negative relationship: as the percentage of households without a car increases, environmental quality falls (1).\n• It is fairly strong, from +14 at 18% to −8 at 55% (1).\n• S4 is an anomaly: 40% without a car but a score of 9, higher than S3 (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nStudents measured channel width at five sites downstream from the source of a stream in the Peak District (data written for this practice question).\nDistance downstream (km) — width (m): 0.5 — 1.2; 1.5 — 2.0; 2.5 — 2.6; 3.5 — 2.4; 4.5 — 4.1.\n\nDescribe how channel width changes downstream. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the overall trend, 1 for data, 1 for an anomaly/rate, e.g.:\n• Width generally increases downstream (1).\n• It rises from 1.2 m at 0.5 km to 4.1 m at 4.5 km, more than three times wider (1).\n• There is a slight decrease at 3.5 km (2.4 m), which does not fit the trend (1), / the biggest increase is between 3.5 and 4.5 km (1.7 m) (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Changing Rural Settlements.\nStudents found that Village X, 8 km from a market town on a main road, had lower IMD deprivation than Village Y, 25 km from the town and served by one bus a week.\n\nExplain one reason why deprivation differs between the two villages.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, up to 2 further marks for development, e.g.:\n• Village X is within easy commuting distance of the town (1), so it attracts wealthier commuters and residents can reach jobs and services (1), raising incomes and lowering deprivation scores (1).\n• Village Y is remote with poor public transport (1), so people without cars struggle to reach work, health care and shops (1); young people may leave, leaving an ageing, lower-income population (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents asked 40 people on the seafront at Swanage whether the new timber groynes were worthwhile. Figure 3 is an incomplete pie chart: the "Don't know" segment (25%) has been drawn and the rest of the circle is blank.\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 3 using this information: Yes 45%, No 30%.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Convert to degrees: Yes 45% × 3.6 = 162°; No 30% × 3.6 = 108° (1)\n• Using a protractor, draw each segment from the edge of the existing segment, in order, largest first / clockwise (1)\n• Label or shade each segment and add it to the key (1)` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents investigated how coastal management at Hornsea, on the Holderness coast, affects coastal processes and the local community. They collected primary data on beach width, beach profiles and sediment size either side of the groynes.\n\nExplain two ways that secondary data would have been useful in this investigation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a way + 1 for development), e.g.:\n• Historic maps / old photographs (1) show how far the cliffs have retreated since the sea wall and groynes were built, giving change over a longer time than one visit (1).\n• East Riding of Yorkshire Council / Shoreline Management Plan documents (1) explain why defences were built and the plan for each stretch, helping to explain conflict over "hold the line" (1).\n• Tide tables / weather records (1) show the conditions on the survey days, so results can be judged as typical or not (1).\n• Google Earth / aerial photos (1) show the build-up of sediment on the updrift side of groynes along the whole frontage (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating flood risk for people and property along a river.\n\nExplain two ways that you collected data to investigate flood risk.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a method + 1 for development linking to flood risk), e.g.:\n• We measured bankfull width and depth with a tape and metre rule at 10 equal intervals (1) to calculate bankfull capacity, which shows how much water the channel can hold before flooding (1).\n• We carried out a land-use survey on the floodplain (1) to see how many homes and businesses were at risk if the river overtopped its banks (1).\n• We measured velocity with a flowmeter three times at each site (1) to calculate discharge and compare it with channel capacity (1).\n• We looked at Environment Agency flood maps (1) to check which properties were in flood zones 2 and 3 (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating how and why quality of life varies within an urban area.\n\nExplain two ways that you reduced bias when collecting your data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a way + 1 for development), e.g.:\n• We used a stratified sample of sites based on IMD data (1), so areas of high and low deprivation were both represented (1).\n• Each environmental quality survey was done by three students and the mean score taken (1), so one person's opinion did not dominate (1).\n• We used clear written criteria for each score on the bipolar survey (1) so different students judged "litter" or "noise" in the same way (1).\n• We asked every fifth person for the questionnaire (1) rather than choosing friendly-looking people (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Changing Rural Settlements.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating how and why deprivation varies within rural areas.\n\nExplain two ways that you presented your data and why each was suitable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a method + 1 for why it suited the data), e.g.:\n• Radar (spider) diagrams of environmental quality scores (1) let us compare all the criteria for each village on one diagram (1).\n• A located bar chart / proportional symbols on an OS map (1) showed how the number of services changed with distance from the market town (1).\n• Divided bar charts of questionnaire answers (1) showed the proportion of residents with each view so villages with different sample sizes could be compared (1).\n• Annotated photographs (1) showed qualitative evidence such as derelict buildings or closed shops (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating the impact of coastal management on coastal processes and communities.\n\nExplain two risks you identified before your fieldwork and how you reduced them.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a risk + 1 for how it was reduced), e.g.:\n• Being cut off by the incoming tide (1) — we checked tide tables and worked only on a falling tide, staying near exits (1).\n• Falling rocks / cliff collapse (1) — we kept away from the base of the cliff (1).\n• Slipping on wet rocks or groynes (1) — we wore suitable footwear and did not climb on the groynes (1).\n• Large waves in bad weather (1) — we checked the forecast and had a cancellation plan (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nTwo groups measured velocity at the same site on the River Lyn on the same morning (data written for this practice question).\nGroup 1 (float, three timings over 10 m): 0.31, 0.29, 0.62 m/s.\nGroup 2 (flowmeter, three readings): 0.41, 0.43, 0.42 m/s.\n\nExplain two reasons why the students' velocity data may be unreliable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a reason + 1 for development using the data), e.g.:\n• Group 1 has an anomalous reading of 0.62 m/s, about double the others (1), possibly because the float was caught in a faster current or the stopwatch was stopped late/early, so the mean is distorted (1).\n• The two methods give different results (float about 0.3 m/s v flowmeter about 0.42 m/s) (1) because a float measures surface flow and may be slowed by wind or snagging (1).\n• Only three readings were taken (1), which is too few to be confident in the mean (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nStudents counted pedestrians for 10 minutes at six sites in Liverpool city centre on a Saturday (data written for this practice question).\nSite 1 (main shopping street) 312; Site 2 205; Site 3 148; Site 4 (outside Lime Street station) 290; Site 5 74; Site 6 51.\nTheir hypothesis was "Pedestrian numbers decrease with distance from the main shopping street." Sites are numbered in order of distance.\n\nSuggest two reasons for the anomaly at Site 4.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a reason + 1 for development), e.g.:\n• The site is outside a main railway station (1), so many commuters and shoppers arrive or leave there, increasing the count above what distance would predict (1).\n• The count may have been taken when a train had just arrived (1), so a 10-minute count gives an unrepresentative peak (1).\n• There may be a bus stop / major attraction / event nearby (1) drawing people to that point (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Changing Rural Settlements.\nStudents investigating rural change in a Lake District village collected quantitative data from a service survey and census data.\n\nExplain two ways that qualitative data would have been useful in this investigation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a way + 1 for development), e.g.:\n• Interviews with long-term residents (1) would explain why they think services have closed, e.g. second homes empty for much of the year (1).\n• Annotated photographs (1) would provide evidence of change, such as a former pub converted to a holiday let (1).\n• Word clouds / perception surveys (1) would show how residents and visitors feel about the village, which numbers cannot show (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents measured beach profiles at Swanage using two ranging poles and a clinometer, recording the angle between the poles at each break of slope from the sea wall to the water's edge.\n\nExplain one strength and one weakness of this method.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a strength + 1 development; 1 mark for a weakness + 1 development, e.g.:\nStrength:\n• The equipment is cheap and simple (1), so several profiles can be measured quickly and compared, e.g. either side of a groyne (1).\n• Measuring at every break of slope (1) records features such as berms accurately (1).\nWeakness:\n• Clinometer readings depend on the user keeping poles upright (1), so human error reduces accuracy (1).\n• Breaks of slope are judged by eye (1), so different groups may choose different points and profiles are not comparable (1).` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nStudents investigated the hypothesis "Quality of life is lower in inner-city Birmingham than in the suburbs." They used only environmental quality surveys at four sites on one afternoon.\n\nExplain two ways the students could improve their investigation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for an improvement + 1 for development), e.g.:\n• Use more sites, chosen by stratified sampling (1), so results are more representative of each area (1).\n• Add secondary data such as IMD scores, life expectancy or Census data (1), because environmental quality only measures one part of quality of life (1).\n• Repeat surveys at different times / days (1), as noise and litter vary, e.g. on bin day or at rush hour (1).\n• Use residents' questionnaires (1) to include people's own views of their quality of life (1).` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating River Processes and Pressures.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating how drainage basin and channel characteristics influence flood risk for people and property.\n\nName your fieldwork location.\nEvaluate the reliability of the conclusions you reached in your investigation.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO3 4, AO4 4):\n• Conclusions about how channel capacity and land use affect flood risk, e.g. bankfull capacity increased downstream but so did the number of properties on the floodplain.\n• Reliability of primary data: number of sites and readings, equipment accuracy (float v flowmeter), human error, anomalies.\n• Conditions on the day: low flow in summer may not represent flood conditions; one visit only.\n• Sampling: whether sites were representative of the whole river; access limited choice of sites.\n• Use of secondary data (EA flood maps, rainfall records, hydrographs) to support or challenge conclusions.\n• A judgement on how far the conclusions can be trusted and how they could be made more reliable.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8): a balanced, well-supported evaluation of the reliability of conclusions, drawing on detailed reference to own fieldwork; a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some evaluation, partly balanced; some reference to own fieldwork; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited, generic points about data or conclusions; little reference to own fieldwork; judgement unsupported or absent.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating the impact of coastal management on coastal processes and communities.\n\nName your fieldwork location.\nAssess the effectiveness of the methods you used to present your data.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO3 4, AO4 4):\n• Methods such as beach profile line graphs, located bar charts of sediment size either side of groynes, pie/divided bar charts of questionnaire results, annotated field sketches/photos.\n• Strengths: e.g. overlaid profiles made differences in beach volume either side of a groyne clear; maps linked data to place.\n• Weaknesses: e.g. pie charts hard to compare between sites; scales differed between graphs; anomalies hidden by means.\n• Suitability for the type of data (continuous v categorical; qualitative v quantitative).\n• Alternatives that would have been better, e.g. dispersion graphs to show spread of sediment sizes.\n• A judgement on overall effectiveness.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8): a thorough assessment with detailed reference to own presentation methods; balanced and with a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some assessment, partly balanced, some reference to own fieldwork; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited assessment, largely descriptive or generic; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating how and why quality of life varies within urban areas.\n\nName your fieldwork location.\nAssess the strengths and weaknesses of your methods of collecting qualitative data.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO3 4, AO4 4):\n• Methods such as interviews, open questions on questionnaires, annotated photographs, field sketches, word association / perception surveys.\n• Strengths: gave reasons behind patterns and residents' views; captured feelings about safety or community that scores miss.\n• Weaknesses: subjective; small samples; respondents may give socially acceptable answers; hard to compare or analyse; time of day/week affects who is interviewed.\n• How qualitative data complemented quantitative data (EQS, pedestrian counts, IMD).\n• A judgement on how useful the methods were for the enquiry question.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8): balanced, detailed assessment of strengths and weaknesses with clear reference to own fieldwork; supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some assessment of strengths and/or weaknesses with some reference to own fieldwork; partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited, generic points; little or no reference to own fieldwork.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Changing Rural Settlements.\nYou have carried out your own fieldwork investigating how and why deprivation varies within rural areas.\n\nName your fieldwork location.\nEvaluate how far your results answered your enquiry question.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO3 4, AO4 4):\n• Statement of enquiry question and main findings, e.g. deprivation was higher in the more remote village with fewer services and poorer transport.\n• Evidence that supported the conclusion: service surveys, environmental quality surveys, questionnaires, IMD/Census data.\n• Evidence that did not fit: anomalies, conflicting views, IMD areas larger than the villages studied.\n• Limitations: small samples, one visit, season, bias in who was asked.\n• What further data would be needed to answer the question fully.\n• A judgement on the extent to which the question was answered.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8): a balanced, detailed evaluation linking findings to the enquiry question with clear reference to own fieldwork; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some evaluation with some reference to own fieldwork; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited, mostly descriptive; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section C1 — Investigating Coastal Change and Conflict.\nStudents visited a beach on the Holderness coast with five wooden groynes. They measured beach width on the north (updrift) and south (downdrift) side of each groyne on one calm day in June (data written for this practice question).\nGroyne — north side width (m); south side width (m):\n1 — 28; 19\n2 — 30; 21\n3 — 27; 22\n4 — 18; 20\n5 — 31; 17\nThey concluded: "The groynes are trapping sediment moved by longshore drift from the north."\n\nAssess the extent to which the data supports the students' conclusion.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO3 4, AO4 4):\n• Support: at four of five groynes the north side is wider; mean north 26.8 m v south 19.8 m (difference 7 m); largest difference at groyne 5 (14 m) — consistent with longshore drift from the north being trapped.\n• Challenge: groyne 4 is the opposite (18 m v 20 m) — may be damaged, shorter, or affected by a stream/outfall.\n• Data limitations: one day only, in calm summer conditions; width alone does not measure sediment volume; no sediment size or direction-of-drift evidence; no repeat readings.\n• Need for other evidence: beach profiles, sediment tracing, secondary data on drift direction (Holderness drift is north to south).\n• A judgement, e.g. data largely supports the conclusion but is not strong enough to prove it.\nCheck: north 28+30+27+18+31 = 134 ÷ 5 = 26.8; south 19+21+22+20+17 = 99 ÷ 5 = 19.8.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8): detailed use of the data, including anomaly and data manipulation, to give a balanced assessment with a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some use of data with partly balanced assessment; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited use of data; mostly descriptive; judgement absent.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section C2 — Investigating Dynamic Urban Areas.\nStudents compared two areas of Newcastle upon Tyne (data written for this practice question).\nArea A (inner city): mean environmental quality score −5; IMD rank in most deprived 10% of England; 35 of 40 residents asked said they felt safe walking at night.\nArea B (suburb, 6 km from centre): mean environmental quality score +11; IMD rank in least deprived 30%; 31 of 40 residents asked said they felt safe walking at night.\nThe students concluded: "Quality of life is lower in the inner city than in the suburbs."\n\nAssess the extent to which the data supports the students' conclusion.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO3 4, AO4 4):\n• Support: environmental quality much lower in Area A (−5 v +11, a difference of 16); IMD places A among the most deprived areas and B among the least.\n• Challenge: more residents in A feel safe at night (87.5% v 77.5%) — community/social aspects of quality of life may be better in A.\n• Limitations: only two areas; EQS is subjective; IMD covers areas larger than the streets surveyed; 40 respondents each is a small sample; time of survey.\n• Quality of life has many dimensions — no data on health, income, access to services.\n• A judgement, e.g. data partly supports the conclusion for environmental and economic measures but not for perceived safety.\nCheck: 35 ÷ 40 = 87.5%; 31 ÷ 40 = 77.5%.\nLevels:\nLevel 3 (6–8): detailed use of all the data including manipulation; balanced assessment with a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5): some use of data; partly balanced; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2): limited use of data; descriptive; judgement absent.\n0: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section C — Geographical Investigations (extended practice item; real Paper 2 fieldwork parts are worth up to 8 marks).\nYou have carried out two fieldwork investigations: one in a physical environment (a coast or a river) and one in a human environment (an urban area or a rural settlement).\n\nName both fieldwork locations.\nEvaluate which of your two investigations produced the more reliable conclusions. Use evidence from your own fieldwork in your answer.`, marks: 12, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO3 6, AO4 6):\n• Physical investigation: mostly quantitative, measurable data (width, depth, velocity, beach profiles, sediment size) — more objective and repeatable, but affected by equipment error, weather, tide/river level and access to sites.\n• Human investigation: mix of quantitative and qualitative data (EQS, pedestrian counts, questionnaires, IMD/Census) — subjective scoring, sampling bias, time of day, small samples; but secondary data add a large reliable dataset.\n• Sampling in each: systematic/random/stratified; number and spread of sites; representativeness.\n• How well conclusions were supported by the data and by geographical theory (e.g. Bradshaw model, longshore drift, urban models, rural deprivation).\n• Anomalies and how they were handled in each.\n• A clear, substantiated judgement on which investigation's conclusions were more reliable and why.\nLevels:\nLevel 4 (10–12): a thorough, balanced evaluation of both investigations, using detailed evidence from own fieldwork; a fully substantiated judgement.\nLevel 3 (7–9): a balanced evaluation with good reference to both investigations; a supported judgement, though some imbalance.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some evaluation, possibly unbalanced or focused on one investigation; partial reference to own fieldwork; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3): limited, mainly descriptive or generic points; little reference to own fieldwork; judgement absent or unsupported.\n0: no rewardable material.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.1': {
    name: 'People and the Biosphere',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): climate summary for weather station X — mean monthly temperature 26–28 °C in every month; total annual rainfall 2,450 mm, with rain in every month; mean daily sunshine about 6 hours all year.\n\nUsing Figure 1, identify the biome in which weather station X is most likely to be located.\nA  Tundra\nB  Tropical rainforest\nC  Hot desert\nD  Temperate deciduous forest`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (Tropical rainforest) (1).\nNo other answer is acceptable.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 2: a world map showing the distribution of major biomes. One biome is shaded as a wide, almost continuous belt between about 50°N and 70°N, stretching across Canada and Alaska, Scandinavia and northern Russia (Siberia). It is not found in the southern hemisphere.\n\nIdentify the biome described in Figure 2.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Taiga / boreal forest / coniferous forest (1).\nDo not accept tundra or temperate forest.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 3 (data written for this practice question): mean monthly temperatures (°C) for a weather station in the taiga of northern Russia.\nJan −20 | Feb −18 | Mar −10 | Apr 0 | May 8 | Jun 14 | Jul 17 | Aug 15 | Sep 8 | Oct 0 | Nov −9 | Dec −17\n\nCalculate the annual range of temperature shown in Figure 3.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `37 °C (1).\nWorking: 17 − (−20) = 37. Units not required for the mark; do not accept 3 or −37.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nThe biosphere provides people with goods (products that can be harvested) and services (natural processes that benefit people).\n\nWhich one of the following is a regulating service provided by the biosphere?\nA  Timber for building\nB  Fish caught from a river\nC  Storage of carbon in trees and soils\nD  Fruit and nuts gathered from a forest`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (Storage of carbon in trees and soils) (1).\nNo other answer is acceptable.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nResource Booklet — the issue: protecting Madagascar's forests. Madagascar is a large island off the south-east coast of Africa. Most of its plants and animals are found nowhere else in the world. Many rural people depend directly on the forest for their daily needs.\n\nUsing your own knowledge, state one good that indigenous or rural people can obtain from a forest such as Madagascar's.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark for any one appropriate good, e.g.\n• food such as fruit, nuts, honey or bushmeat (1)\n• medicinal plants (1)\n• fuelwood / charcoal (1)\n• building materials such as timber, poles or leaves for thatch (1)\n• fibres for weaving or rope (1)\nDo not accept services such as "clean air" or "carbon storage".` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 4: estimated mean net primary productivity (NPP) of selected biomes — tropical rainforest 2,200 g/m²/year; taiga 800 g/m²/year; tundra 140 g/m²/year; hot desert 90 g/m²/year.\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the ratio of the NPP of tropical rainforest to the NPP of hot desert. Give your answer to the nearest whole number.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `24 : 1 (1).\nWorking: 2,200 ÷ 90 = 24.4, rounds to 24. Accept 24.4 : 1. The ratio must be in the order tropical rainforest : hot desert.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 5 (figures rounded): world population was about 3.0 billion in 1960 and about 7.8 billion in 2020.\n\nUsing Figure 5, calculate the percentage (%) increase in world population between 1960 and 2020. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working, 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• (7.8 − 3.0) ÷ 3.0 × 100 or 4.8 ÷ 3.0 × 100 (1)\n• = 160% (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 6 (data written for this practice question): mean monthly temperatures (°C) at a weather station in the Congo Basin.\nJan 26.5 | Feb 26.4 | Mar 26.5 | Apr 26.6 | May 26.8 | Jun 26.7 | Jul 26.9 | Aug 27.5 | Sep 27.9 | Oct 27.8 | Nov 27.3 | Dec 26.9\n\nCalculate the mean annual temperature at this weather station. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working, 1 mark for the answer.\n• 323.8 ÷ 12 (sum of the twelve months divided by 12) (1)\n• = 27.0 °C (1) (26.98 rounded to one decimal place)\nAccept 27 °C for the second mark only if working is shown.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 7: vegetation zones on the slopes of Mount Kilimanjaro, Tanzania (summit 5,895 m). Farmland and cultivated land occupies the lower slopes; montane rainforest grows from about 1,800 m to 2,800 m; heath and moorland lies above the forest; alpine desert lies above about 4,000 m; the summit area has bare rock, ice and snow.\n\nUsing Figure 7, explain one way in which altitude influences the type of ecosystem found on a mountain.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a way (1) and 1 mark for a linked development (1).\nE.g.\n• Temperature falls with height (about 6 °C per 1,000 m) (1) so the growing season becomes shorter and only low, hardy plants such as heather and grasses survive above the forest (1).\n• Higher up, soils are thinner and conditions are windier and colder (1) so trees cannot grow and the forest gives way to moorland and then alpine desert (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 8: a description of two areas of southern England with similar climates. Area P is on chalk; the ground is well drained and the thin, alkaline soil supports short grassland rich in wild flowers. Area Q is on clay; the ground is often waterlogged in winter and supports wet woodland and marsh plants.\n\nUsing Figure 8, explain one way in which local factors can affect the type of ecosystem found in an area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a local factor and its effect (1) and 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• Rock type affects drainage (1): permeable chalk lets water soak through so the surface is dry and only drought-tolerant grasses and flowers grow, whereas impermeable clay stays waterlogged, favouring marsh plants and wet woodland (1).\n• Rock type affects the soil (1): chalk weathers to give thin, alkaline soil, so only plants adapted to low nutrients and high pH, such as certain orchids, thrive (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response. Answers must refer to a local factor (rock type, soil, drainage, altitude), not climate.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nIn the Amazon rainforest, more than half of the rain that falls is returned to the atmosphere by the forest itself.\n\nUsing your own knowledge, explain one way in which vegetation helps to regulate the water cycle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way (1) + 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• Trees transpire large amounts of water vapour through their leaves (1), which adds moisture to the air, forms clouds and keeps rainfall high further inland (1).\n• The canopy intercepts rainfall (1), slowing the rate at which water reaches the ground, so more infiltrates and surface run-off and flooding are reduced (1).\n• Roots and leaf litter increase infiltration into the soil (1), so water is stored and released slowly to rivers, keeping flow steady in dry periods (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nResource Booklet — the issue: development choices for Ghana. In parts of southern Ghana, natural forest has been replaced by farmland and gold-mining sites.\n\nUsing your own knowledge, explain one way in which forests help to maintain healthy soils.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way (1) + 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• Leaf litter falls and is decomposed (1), returning nutrients to the soil so that it stays fertile (1).\n• Tree roots bind the soil together (1), reducing erosion by heavy rainfall and surface run-off (1).\n• The canopy shields the ground from intense rain (1), so there is less rain-splash erosion and fewer nutrients are leached away (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nThe biosphere is sometimes described as a "life-support system" because it regulates the composition of the atmosphere.\n\nExplain one way in which the biosphere regulates the composition of the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way (1) + 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• Plants and phytoplankton photosynthesise, absorbing carbon dioxide (1) and storing carbon in biomass and soils, which reduces the greenhouse effect (1).\n• Photosynthesis releases oxygen (1), keeping oxygen levels in the air at the level needed for animals and people to breathe (1).\n• Peat bogs and forest soils act as long-term carbon stores (1), keeping carbon out of the atmosphere for thousands of years (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 9 (data written for this practice question): energy consumption per person in an emerging country rose from 15 gigajoules in 2000 to 28 gigajoules in 2020, while its GDP per person more than doubled.\n\nSuggest one reason why energy consumption per person increases as a country develops economically.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason (1) + 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• Rising incomes allow more households to buy electrical goods such as fridges, air conditioners and TVs (1), which all need electricity (1).\n• Industrialisation increases the number of factories (1), which use large amounts of energy for manufacturing (1).\n• More people can afford cars or motorbikes (1), increasing demand for petrol and diesel (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nResource Booklet — the issue: protecting Madagascar's forests. The rosy periwinkle, a plant from Madagascar, is the source of drugs (vinblastine and vincristine) used to treat some childhood leukaemias and Hodgkin's lymphoma.\n\nExplain one way in which the biosphere is exploited commercially for medicines.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way (1) + 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• Pharmaceutical companies collect and test plants from biodiverse forests (bioprospecting) (1) and then develop and patent drugs from the chemicals they contain, sold globally for profit (1).\n• Useful plants are grown on commercial plantations (1) so that large quantities of the raw material can be harvested and processed into medicines (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nTwo theories describe the relationship between population and resources.\n\nIdentify which two statements describe Boserup's theory.\nA  Population grows geometrically (1, 2, 4, 8 ...).\nB  "Necessity is the mother of invention."\nC  Famine, war and disease will act as checks on population.\nD  Population growth leads farmers to develop new techniques to increase food supply.\nE  Food supply can only increase arithmetically (1, 2, 3, 4 ...).`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1)\nD (1)\nMaximum 2 marks. If more than two letters are given, award no mark for any additional incorrect letters beyond two.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 10: a table showing net primary productivity (g/m²/year) for six biomes — tropical rainforest 2,200; temperate deciduous forest 1,200; taiga 800; temperate grassland 600; tundra 140; hot desert 90.\n\nDescribe one other suitable method of data presentation to show the data in Figure 10.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a suitable method (1) + 1 mark for a description of how it would be drawn or why it suits the data (1).\nE.g.\n• A bar chart (1) with one bar for each biome and NPP on the vertical axis, so the biomes can easily be compared (1).\n• Proportional symbols on a world map (1), with a circle drawn on each biome sized according to its NPP, showing both location and productivity (1).\nDo not accept a line graph or a pie chart (the data are not continuous and are not parts of a whole).` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nAs incomes rise in countries such as China, diets change. Between 1990 and 2020 meat consumption per person in China more than doubled.\n\nExplain one way in which rising wealth increases the demand for food resources.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way (1) + 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• Wealthier people eat more meat and dairy (1); producing meat needs several kilograms of animal feed per kilogram of meat, so much more cropland and water are needed (1).\n• People can afford more processed and imported food (1), increasing demand for crops such as soya and palm oil grown elsewhere (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFrom the late 1960s India's wheat and rice production rose rapidly during the Green Revolution, even though its population was growing fast.\n\nUsing your own knowledge, explain one way in which technology can increase food supply, as suggested by Boserup.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way (1) + 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• High-yielding varieties of rice and wheat were developed (1), producing more grain from the same area of land (1).\n• Irrigation schemes allow crops to be grown in the dry season (1), so two or three harvests a year are possible (1).\n• Use of chemical fertilisers and pesticides (1) raises yields by adding nutrients and reducing crop losses (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response, e.g. GM crops, hydroponics.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 11: in northern Alaska, mean temperatures are above 5 °C for only about two months each year. The vegetation is mainly mosses, lichens, grasses and low shrubs, with no trees.\n\nExplain one way in which the length of the growing season affects the vegetation of a biome such as the tundra.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way (1) + 1 mark for development (1).\nE.g.\n• The growing season is very short because temperatures are above about 5–6 °C for only a few weeks (1), so plants have little time to grow, flower and set seed, and stay small and low-growing (1).\n• Trees cannot grow tall enough or produce new wood quickly enough in such a short season (1), so the tundra is treeless and dominated by mosses and lichens (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 12: a world map showing the distribution of tropical rainforests. Large areas are shaded in the Amazon Basin of South America and the Congo Basin of central Africa. Smaller areas are shaded in Central America, along the coast of West Africa, eastern Madagascar, and across South-East Asia including Indonesia, Malaysia and Papua New Guinea, and north-east Australia. Almost all shaded areas lie between the Tropic of Cancer (23.5°N) and the Tropic of Capricorn (23.5°S).\n\nDescribe the global distribution of tropical rainforests shown in Figure 12.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each descriptive point, up to 3 marks. Credit use of names and data.\n• Found mainly between the tropics / within about 23.5° north and south of the equator (1)\n• Largest areas are in the Amazon Basin (South America) (1) and the Congo Basin (central Africa) (1)\n• Also found in South-East Asia, e.g. Indonesia / Malaysia (1)\n• Smaller, scattered areas, e.g. Central America, eastern Madagascar, north-east Australia (1)\n• Often in coastal or lowland river-basin areas (1)\n• Mainly in South America, Africa and Asia; absent from Europe (1)\nDo not credit explanation.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 13 (data written for this practice question): a scatter graph of mean annual precipitation (x-axis, 0–3,000 mm) against net primary productivity (y-axis, 0–2,500 g/m²/year) for 12 sites in tropical and subtropical regions. Sites with under 250 mm of rainfall all have NPP below 200 g/m²/year; sites with 1,000–1,500 mm have NPP of 800–1,300; the three sites with over 2,000 mm have NPP of 2,000–2,300. One site with 1,800 mm has NPP of only 600 g/m²/year.\n\nDescribe the relationship shown in Figure 13. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the relationship, up to 2 further marks for data and detail.\n• Positive relationship / as precipitation increases, NPP increases (1)\n• Use of data, e.g. sites with under 250 mm have NPP below 200 g/m²/year, while those with over 2,000 mm have 2,000–2,300 (1)\n• Identification of the anomaly: the site with 1,800 mm has an NPP of only 600 g/m²/year (1)\n• Relationship is fairly strong / most points lie close to a line of best fit (1)\nMaximum 1 mark if no data are used.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 14: Death Valley in the Mojave Desert, USA, receives about 60 mm of rainfall a year, and summer temperatures often exceed 45 °C. Plant cover is sparse and the biomass per square metre is very low.\n\nExplain one reason why hot deserts such as the Mojave have low productivity.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the reason (1) + up to 2 marks for development (1)(1).\nE.g.\n• There is very little and unreliable rainfall (1), so there is not enough water for most plants to photosynthesise and grow for much of the year (1); plants that survive are widely spaced and slow-growing, so total biomass and NPP are low (1).\n• Very high temperatures cause high evaporation rates (1), so the little water that falls is quickly lost from the soil (1), leaving thin, dry soils low in organic matter and nutrients (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 15: an incomplete pie chart showing global freshwater withdrawals by sector. The agriculture sector (70%) has been drawn; the remaining 30% of the circle is blank.\n\nComplete Figure 15 using this information: industry 19%, domestic (household) 11%. Describe how you would divide and label the blank 30% of the circle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct step, up to 3 marks.\n• Convert percentages to degrees by multiplying by 3.6: industry 19% = 68.4° (≈ 68°) and domestic 11% = 39.6° (≈ 40°) (1)\n• Use a protractor, starting from the edge of the agriculture segment, to measure and draw a line at 68° for industry; the remaining 40° (approx.) is domestic (1)\n• Shade/colour each new segment differently and add labels or a key, showing the percentages (1)\nAccept a method that measures the percentages using a pie-chart scale (percentage protractor).` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 16: a world map shows that the location of major biomes broadly follows bands of latitude — tropical rainforest near the equator, hot deserts at about 15–30° north and south, temperate forests in mid-latitudes and tundra in polar regions.\n\nExplain two climatic factors that influence the global distribution of biomes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each factor identified (1) and 1 mark for each development (1), up to 2 marks per factor.\nE.g.\n• Temperature (1): near the equator the sun is high in the sky all year, giving consistently high temperatures that allow plants to grow all year, as in tropical rainforest (1).\n• Precipitation (1): areas of sinking air at around 30° N/S, under high pressure, receive very little rain, so only drought-adapted plants survive and hot deserts form (1).\n• Sunshine hours / insolation (1): polar areas receive little sunlight in winter, limiting photosynthesis, so only low tundra vegetation grows (1).\n• Length of growing season (1): in high latitudes temperatures are above 6 °C for only a few months, so slow-growing conifers dominate in the taiga (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 17 (data written for this practice question): results of a vegetation transect across the Peak District, England. At 150 m on limestone, well-drained soils support grassland and ash woodland. At 350 m on gritstone, acidic soils support heather moorland. At 550 m on flat, poorly drained gritstone plateau, waterlogged peat supports cotton grass and sphagnum moss.\n\nUsing Figure 17, explain two ways in which local factors influence the ecosystems found in an area.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way identified (1) + 1 mark for each development using Figure 17 (1).\nE.g.\n• Rock type (1): limestone produces alkaline, well-drained soils that support grassland and ash trees, whereas gritstone weathers into acidic, nutrient-poor soils where only plants such as heather survive (1).\n• Drainage (1): on the flat plateau water cannot drain away, so the ground is waterlogged, decomposition is slow and peat forms, supporting sphagnum moss and cotton grass (1).\n• Altitude (1): at 550 m it is colder, windier and wetter than at 150 m, so trees do not grow and moorland/bog plants dominate (1).\n• Soil (1): acidic peat soils are low in nutrients, which limits the range of species that can grow (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nThe Yanomami are an indigenous people of about 35,000 who live in villages in the rainforest on the border between Brazil and Venezuela.\n\nExplain two ways in which indigenous people such as the Yanomami use the biosphere to meet their needs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (1) + 1 mark for each development (1), up to 2 marks per way.\nE.g.\n• Food: they hunt animals, fish and gather fruit, nuts and honey (1), using a wide range of species so that no single resource is over-used (1).\n• Shifting cultivation: small forest plots are cleared to grow crops such as plantains and cassava (1), then left after a few years so that the forest and soil can recover (1).\n• Building materials: wood, vines and leaves are used to build communal houses (1), so they do not need to buy materials from outside (1).\n• Medicine: many plants are used to treat illness (1), using knowledge passed down through generations (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 18: Indonesia is the world's largest producer of palm oil. Large areas of lowland rainforest on Sumatra and Borneo have been cleared for oil-palm plantations owned by large companies. Palm oil is exported for use in food, cosmetics and biofuels.\n\nUsing Figure 18 and your own knowledge, explain two ways in which the biosphere is being exploited commercially.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (1) + 1 mark for each development (1), up to 2 marks per way.\nE.g.\n• Food: rainforest is cleared for plantations of commodity crops such as oil palm, soya or cattle ranching (1), producing goods for export and profit for transnational companies (1).\n• Energy: palm oil and other crops are grown as biofuels (1), and wood is harvested as fuel, to meet the energy demand of wealthier countries (1).\n• Timber: commercial logging removes valuable hardwoods (1) for export to make furniture and building materials (1).\n• Medicines: companies bioprospect for plant chemicals (1) and patent drugs made from them (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 19: the Amazon rainforest covers about 5.5 million km² and contains a huge store of carbon. Water vapour transpired by the forest is carried by winds as "flying rivers" that bring rain to farms in southern Brazil.\n\nExplain two ways in which the biosphere helps to regulate water and soil systems.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (1) + 1 mark for each development (1), up to 2 marks per way.\nE.g.\n• Transpiration (1): trees return large amounts of water vapour to the atmosphere, which falls again as rain further inland, maintaining the regional water cycle (1).\n• Interception and infiltration (1): the canopy and leaf litter slow down rainfall so more soaks into the soil, reducing run-off and the risk of flooding (1).\n• Nutrient cycling (1): leaf litter is decomposed quickly by fungi and bacteria, so nutrients are returned to the soil and taken up by roots, keeping the soil fertile (1).\n• Roots binding soil (1): this reduces soil erosion and the amount of sediment washed into rivers (1).\nMaximum 2 marks if only water or only soil is covered.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 20: the United Nations projects that world population will grow from 8 billion in 2022 to about 9.7 billion by 2050.\n\nExplain two reasons why global demand for food is increasing.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (1) + 1 mark for each development (1), up to 2 marks per reason.\nE.g.\n• Population growth (1): there are more people to feed each year, especially in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia where birth rates remain high (1).\n• Rising wealth / economic development (1): as incomes rise in emerging countries such as China and India, people eat more meat and dairy, which needs more land, crops and water to produce (1).\n• Urbanisation (1): urban people buy more processed food and fewer grow their own, increasing commercial food demand (1).\n• Biofuels (1): cropland is used to grow crops for fuel instead of food, increasing demand for crops overall (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 21: India's population passed 1.4 billion in 2023. India is now the world's third-largest consumer of energy, and its energy use has grown rapidly since 2000.\n\nExplain two reasons why demand for energy is rising in emerging countries such as India.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (1) + 1 mark for each development (1), up to 2 marks per reason.\nE.g.\n• Population growth (1): more people means more homes needing electricity for lighting and cooking (1).\n• Industrialisation (1): growth of manufacturing industries such as steel and textiles uses large amounts of electricity and fuel (1).\n• Rising incomes (1): a growing middle class buys cars, air conditioners and other appliances, increasing energy use per person (1).\n• Urbanisation and infrastructure (1): new roads, railways and cities need energy to build and run (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 22: Lake Naivasha is a freshwater lake in Kenya's Rift Valley. Since the 1980s many large flower farms have been built around the lake, growing roses for export to Europe. They extract water from the lake and nearby groundwater for irrigation, and the town's population has grown rapidly as workers have moved in.\n\nUsing Figure 22, suggest two ways in which increasing demand for water could affect the ecosystem of the lake.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (1) + 1 mark for each development (1), up to 2 marks per way.\nE.g.\n• Water abstraction lowers the lake level (1), exposing shoreline habitats such as papyrus swamps that are breeding sites for birds and fish (1).\n• Fertiliser and pesticide run-off from the farms (1) can cause eutrophication, leading to algal blooms that reduce oxygen and kill fish (1).\n• A growing population increases sewage and waste entering the lake (1), lowering water quality for wildlife (1).\n• Pressure on fish stocks from more people (1) may lead to over-fishing and changes in the food web (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nIn 1798 Thomas Malthus argued that population would eventually grow faster than food supply, leading to a "Malthusian catastrophe".\n\nExplain one strength and one weakness of Malthus's theory.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a strength (1) + 1 for development (1); 1 mark for a weakness (1) + 1 for development (1).\nStrength, e.g.\n• Some regions have experienced famine when population pressure combined with drought, e.g. the Horn of Africa (1), suggesting there are limits to how many people the land can support (1).\n• Resources such as fresh water and fertile soil are finite (1), and over-use is already causing soil degradation and falling water tables (1).\nWeakness, e.g.\n• Global food production has grown faster than population since the 1960s (1) because of technology such as the Green Revolution, which Malthus did not foresee (1).\n• Most famines are caused by poverty, conflict or unequal distribution rather than an absolute lack of food (1), so the problem is access, not total supply (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 23: in 2015, fires burned large areas of drained peatland and forest in Indonesia. On some days the fires released more carbon dioxide than the entire US economy.\n\nExplain two ways in which the loss of forest and peatland can affect the regulation of the atmosphere.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (1) + 1 mark for each development (1), up to 2 marks per way.\nE.g.\n• Burning releases stored carbon (1) as carbon dioxide, increasing the enhanced greenhouse effect and global warming (1).\n• Fewer trees means less photosynthesis (1), so less carbon dioxide is absorbed from the atmosphere in future (1).\n• Drained peat dries out and decomposes (1), releasing carbon that has been stored for thousands of years (1).\n• Less transpiration (1) means less water vapour and cloud cover, which can change local and regional climate and rainfall (1).\nAccept any other appropriate response.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 24: a world map of biomes, alongside photographs of Mount Kenya (where forest gives way to moorland and then bare rock with height, even though it lies on the equator) and of an area of chalk grassland in southern England surrounded by woodland on clay.\n\nAssess the extent to which climate is the main factor controlling the distribution of ecosystems.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2 understanding, AO3 assessment):\n• Climate controls distribution at the global scale: temperature, precipitation, sunshine hours and growing season produce broad latitudinal belts (rainforest at the equator, deserts around 30° under high pressure, taiga and tundra in high latitudes).\n• Global atmospheric circulation explains the location of wet and dry zones.\n• At a local scale other factors matter: altitude (Mount Kenya — temperature falls with height, so equatorial location does not guarantee rainforest), rock type, soils and drainage (chalk vs clay).\n• Human activity (farming, deforestation, plantations) also changes the actual vegetation found today.\n• Altitude effects are partly climatic (lower temperatures), so climate still underlies much local variation.\n• Conclusion: climate is dominant globally, but local factors and people explain variation within biomes.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Applies understanding to deconstruct the issue fully and in depth; balanced, well-supported assessment of climate against local factors at different scales, with evidence and a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding applied; partially balanced assessment considering climate and at least one other factor; judgement offered but not fully supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Limited understanding; mainly descriptive, one-sided, with a generic or unsupported judgement.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 25 (figures rounded, data written for this practice question): between 1961 and 2020 world population rose from about 3.1 billion to 7.8 billion (+152%), while world cereal production rose from about 0.9 billion tonnes to 3.0 billion tonnes (+233%). Over the same period about 800 million people remained undernourished, and large areas of soil were degraded.\n\nEvaluate the view that Boserup's theory explains the relationship between population and resources better than Malthus's theory.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Malthus (1798): population grows geometrically, food arithmetically; checks (famine, disease, war) restore balance.\n• Boserup (1965): population pressure stimulates innovation ("necessity is the mother of invention"); intensification raises food supply.\n• Support for Boserup: cereal production grew faster than population (+233% vs +152%); Green Revolution HYVs, irrigation, fertilisers, mechanisation, GM crops.\n• Support for Malthus: 800 million still undernourished; soil degradation, water shortages and biodiversity loss suggest environmental limits; climate change may reduce yields.\n• Hunger is often caused by poverty, conflict and unequal distribution rather than total supply — neither theory fully explains this.\n• Neo-Malthusian concerns about future resource limits as demand for food, water and energy rises with wealth.\n• Judgement: Boserup better explains the past 60 years globally, but Malthus warns of limits that technology may not solve indefinitely.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed understanding of both theories applied to evidence, including data from Figure 25; balanced evaluation leading to a well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding of both theories; some use of evidence; evaluation partially balanced; judgement offered with some support.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Limited or inaccurate understanding; little use of evidence; one-sided with an unsupported judgement.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 26: a diagram of the services provided by the biosphere — provisioning (food, fuel, timber, medicines), regulating (carbon storage, oxygen release, water cycle, soil protection, pollination), supporting (nutrient cycling, soil formation) and cultural (recreation, spiritual value).\n\nAssess the importance of the biosphere as a life-support system for people.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Provisioning: food, fresh water, fuelwood, timber and medicines (e.g. rosy periwinkle) sustain both indigenous people and global economies.\n• Regulating atmosphere: photosynthesis absorbs CO₂ and releases O₂; forests and peatlands store carbon, reducing climate change.\n• Regulating water: transpiration recycles rainfall (Amazon "flying rivers"); interception reduces flooding.\n• Regulating soil: nutrient cycling, roots reduce erosion, maintaining farmland fertility.\n• Some people (indigenous groups, rural poor) depend directly; others depend indirectly through services they do not see.\n• Counter-points: technology can replace some services (fertilisers, reservoirs, flood defences, synthetic drugs) but often at high cost and not at global scale.\n• Loss of services through deforestation and degradation shows how important they are.\n• Judgement on which services are most vital and for whom.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Thorough understanding of a range of goods and services, with examples; balanced assessment of how far people depend on them; well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding of goods and services with some examples; partial assessment; judgement with some support.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Limited, largely descriptive list of goods or services; little or no assessment.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 27: Brazil is one of the world's largest exporters of beef and soya. Much of the land used to produce them was once Amazon rainforest or Cerrado savanna. Brazil also has large hydroelectric dams on Amazon rivers and earns money from timber and mining.\n\nEvaluate the costs and benefits of the commercial exploitation of the biosphere in a country such as Brazil.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nBenefits:\n• Export earnings from beef, soya, timber and minerals boost GDP and government revenue.\n• Jobs in farming, processing, transport and mining; infrastructure such as roads.\n• HEP provides renewable electricity for growing cities and industry.\n• Helps meet global demand for food and energy.\nCosts:\n• Deforestation reduces biodiversity and destroys habitats.\n• Release of stored carbon contributes to climate change; reduced transpiration can lower rainfall, affecting farms.\n• Soil erosion and nutrient loss once forest is cleared; river silting and pollution from mining.\n• Indigenous people lose land and their traditional way of life; conflict over land.\n• Profits often go to large companies and wealthy landowners rather than local people.\nEvaluation: short-term economic gains vs long-term environmental and social costs; costs felt locally and globally; sustainable alternatives.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed, accurate understanding; balanced evaluation of a range of costs and benefits for different groups and scales; well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding; considers both costs and benefits but with imbalance; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Limited understanding; mainly one-sided list; unsupported judgement.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\nFigure 28 (data written for this practice question): global demand projections for 2050 compared with 2020 — food +50%, energy +30%, fresh water +25%. A note explains that most of the increase will come from emerging and developing countries.\n\nAssess the impact of rising demand for food, energy and water on the biosphere.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Food: expansion of farmland into forests and grasslands (e.g. soya and cattle in the Amazon, palm oil in Indonesia) reduces biodiversity; intensive farming causes soil degradation and fertiliser pollution.\n• Energy: fossil fuel extraction (oil in the Amazon, tar sands in the Canadian taiga) destroys habitats; HEP dams flood forest; biofuel crops replace natural vegetation; burning fossil fuels drives climate change, which shifts biomes.\n• Water: over-abstraction lowers lakes and aquifers (e.g. Lake Naivasha), damaging wetlands; dams alter river ecosystems.\n• Using Figure 28: food demand rises most (+50%), suggesting farmland expansion may have the largest impact.\n• Impacts vary: greatest in biodiverse tropical regions where demand and growth are high.\n• Technology and management (higher yields on existing land, renewable energy, water efficiency) could reduce impacts.\n• Judgement on which demand has the greatest impact and whether it can be managed.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Thorough understanding applied to food, energy and water, using evidence from Figure 28 and examples; balanced assessment with a well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding of at least two resources; some assessment; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Limited understanding; generic description of impacts; little or no assessment.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Section D — Making a Geographical Decision.\nResource Booklet — the issue: protecting Madagascar's forests (data written for this practice question unless stated). Madagascar is a low-income country; most of its plants and animals, including all of its lemurs, are found nowhere else. Large areas of forest have been lost since the 1950s, mainly to slash-and-burn farming (tavy) and charcoal making. Most rural households depend on the forest for fuel, food and medicine. Madagascar produces most of the world's vanilla (real). Forest loss causes soil erosion; rivers carry red sediment to the sea.\n\nThree options to manage Madagascar's forests more sustainably:\nOption 1 — Extend national parks and strictly protected areas, funded by international conservation organisations and ecotourism.\nOption 2 — Community forest management: give local villages legal control of nearby forests, with training in agroforestry (growing vanilla, cocoa and fruit trees under the forest canopy).\nOption 3 — Allow commercial companies to develop large timber and cash-crop plantations on already degraded land, using the income to fund rural electricity and fuel-efficient stoves.\n\nSelect the option you think would best protect Madagascar's forests while meeting the needs of its people. Justify your choice. Use information from the Resource Booklet and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer.\n(12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Indicative content (any option can be chosen; credit is for the quality of justification):\nOption 1: protects biodiversity and carbon stores; ecotourism brings income and jobs; but may exclude local people who rely on the forest, causing conflict and illegal clearance; depends on outside funding and tourist numbers.\nOption 2: local people have an incentive to protect forest; agroforestry provides income (vanilla is a high-value export) while maintaining canopy, soil protection and water regulation; uses indigenous knowledge; but needs training and fair prices, may be slow, vulnerable to vanilla price changes.\nOption 3: uses degraded land rather than primary forest; provides jobs and tax revenue; electricity and efficient stoves reduce charcoal demand; but plantations have low biodiversity, profits may leave the country, risk of land grabs and expansion into natural forest.\nStrong answers link to biosphere goods and services (provisioning, regulating atmosphere, water and soil), rising demand for resources, sustainability (social, economic, environmental) and short- vs long-term effects, and explain why rejected options are less suitable.\n\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; full use of the Resource Booklet; thorough, balanced evaluation of the chosen option and reasons for rejecting others; well-supported, substantiated decision.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Accurate knowledge; good use of resources; mostly balanced evaluation; decision supported with some reference to other options.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant knowledge; some use of resources; partial evaluation, mainly of one option; decision with limited support.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Limited knowledge; little use of resources; mainly descriptive; decision unsupported.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4 marks): spelling and punctuation with consistent accuracy; grammar used to control meaning effectively; wide range of specialist terms used adeptly.\nIntermediate performance (2–3 marks): considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): reasonable accuracy; no serious hindrance to meaning; limited specialist terms.\n0 marks: no response, or response does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.2': {
    name: 'Forests under Threat',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 1: a photograph of a forest in northern Canada in winter. Tall, narrow, cone-shaped evergreen trees stand close together; their branches slope downwards and are covered in snow. The ground beneath has a thin layer of mosses and lichens.\n\nWhich one of the following is an adaptation of the trees shown in Figure 1?\nA  Leaves with drip tips\nB  Buttress roots\nC  Needle-shaped leaves with a waxy coating\nD  Lianas climbing towards the light`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (1)\nNeedle-shaped leaves with a waxy coating reduce water loss when the ground is frozen. A, B and D are tropical rainforest features.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 2: a Gersmehl nutrient cycle diagram for a tropical rainforest. It shows three circles: biomass (a very large circle), litter (a small circle) and soil (a small circle). The arrows for litter fall and for nutrient uptake by plants are very thick; the arrow for loss by leaching is thick.\n\nIdentify the largest nutrient store in the tropical rainforest shown in Figure 2.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Biomass (1)\nAccept: the trees / vegetation / living plants and animals.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 3: a Gersmehl nutrient cycle diagram for the taiga. It shows three circles: litter (the largest circle), biomass (a medium circle) and soil (the smallest circle). All transfer arrows are thin.\n\nIdentify the largest nutrient store in the taiga shown in Figure 3.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Litter (1)\nAccept: the layer of fallen needles / dead organic matter on the forest floor.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 4: mean monthly temperature (°C) for a weather station in the Siberian taiga, Russia (data written for this practice question).\nJan −22 | Feb −19 | Mar −11 | Apr −1 | May 8 | Jun 14 | Jul 17 | Aug 15 | Sep 8 | Oct 0 | Nov −11 | Dec −19\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the annual temperature range.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `39 °C (1)\n17 − (−22) = 39 °C. Units not required.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 5 lists international schemes that aim to protect forests. One of them is REDD+, run by the United Nations, which pays countries to keep their forests standing.\n\nUsing your own knowledge, state what the letters REDD stand for.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and (forest) Degradation (1)\nAll four key words needed for the mark (allow minor wording differences).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 6: land use of 1,200 km² of rainforest cleared in one province of Borneo, Indonesia, over ten years (data written for this practice question).\nOil palm plantations 540 km² | Logging concessions left as degraded land 300 km² | Mining 120 km² | Small-scale farming 180 km² | Roads and settlements 60 km²\n\nUsing Figure 6, calculate the percentage (%) of the cleared land that was used for oil palm plantations.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `45% (1)\n540 ÷ 1,200 × 100 = 45` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 7: a cross-section of a tropical rainforest in the Amazon, Brazil. Scattered very tall trees rise to about 50 m above a continuous layer of tree crowns at 30–40 m. Below this are smaller young trees, a layer of shrubs and ferns at 1–5 m, and a dark forest floor covered in leaf litter.\n\nIdentify the layer of the forest shown in Figure 7 that receives the least sunlight.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `The forest floor (1)\nAccept: ground layer. Do not accept shrub layer.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 2 shows a Gersmehl nutrient cycle for a tropical rainforest: a very large biomass store, a small litter store and a small soil store, with a thick arrow showing loss of nutrients by leaching.\n\nExplain one reason why the soil store in the tropical rainforest is small.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• Heavy daily rainfall (over 2,000 mm a year) washes nutrients down through the soil (1) beyond the reach of roots / into rivers, so they are lost from the soil store by leaching (1).\n• Nutrients released by fast decomposition in the hot, wet conditions (1) are taken up very quickly by the dense vegetation, so few remain in the soil (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 8: a photograph of the base of a kapok tree in the Amazon rainforest, Peru. Large triangular roots spread out above the ground up to 3 m from the trunk. The soil is a thin layer over red clay.\n\nExplain one way in which the roots shown in Figure 8 help the tree to survive.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the adaptation, 1 mark for how it helps. For example:\n• Buttress roots spread out above the ground (1) giving support / stability to a very tall tree that has only shallow roots in thin soil (1).\n• The roots spread widely near the surface (1) so the tree can take up nutrients quickly from the thin top layer of soil where decomposition happens (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 9: a photograph of a three-toed sloth hanging upside down from a branch in the canopy of a rainforest in Costa Rica. Its fur has a greenish tinge from algae growing in it.\n\nExplain one way in which the animal shown in Figure 9 is adapted to living in the tropical rainforest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the adaptation, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• Algae in its fur give it green camouflage (1) so it is hidden in the canopy from predators such as harpy eagles (1).\n• It has long, hooked claws (1) so it can hang from branches in the canopy, where it feeds on leaves, for long periods with little effort (1).\n• It moves slowly and has a slow metabolism (1) which saves energy because its leaf diet is low in nutrients (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 3 shows a Gersmehl nutrient cycle for the taiga: a large litter store, a medium biomass store and a small soil store, with all transfer arrows thin.\n\nExplain one reason why the litter store is the largest store in the taiga.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• Temperatures are below freezing for over half the year (1) so decomposers are inactive and fallen needles break down very slowly, building up as litter (1).\n• Conifer needles are tough and acidic (1) so they decompose slowly and accumulate on the forest floor (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 10: annual forest loss in the Brazilian Amazon, 2016–2023, in km² (data written for this practice question).\n2016: 7,900 | 2017: 6,900 | 2018: 7,500 | 2019: 10,100 | 2020: 10,900 | 2021: 13,000 | 2022: 11,600 | 2023: 9,000\n\nUsing Figure 10, calculate the percentage (%) increase in annual forest loss between 2018 and 2021. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `13,000 − 7,500 = 5,500, then 5,500 ÷ 7,500 × 100 (1)\n= 73.3% (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working only if 73.3 is given.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 10: annual forest loss in the Brazilian Amazon, 2016–2023, in km² (data written for this practice question).\n2016: 7,900 | 2017: 6,900 | 2018: 7,500 | 2019: 10,100 | 2020: 10,900 | 2021: 13,000 | 2022: 11,600 | 2023: 9,000\n\nUsing Figure 10, calculate the mean annual forest loss for 2016–2023. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Total = 76,900 km² ÷ 8 (1)\n= 9,612.5 km² (1) (accept 9,612 or 9,613)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 10 is a table showing annual forest loss in the Brazilian Amazon (km²) for each year from 2016 to 2023.\n\nDescribe one other suitable method of data presentation to show the change in forest loss over time.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a suitable method, 1 mark for description. For example:\n• A line graph (1) with years on the x-axis and forest loss in km² on the y-axis, with points joined to show the trend (1).\n• A bar chart (1) with one bar for each year, the height showing km² of forest lost (1).\nDo not accept pie chart or scatter graph.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 11: a satellite image of the Athabasca oil sands near Fort McMurray, Alberta, Canada. Large grey open-pit mines, tailings ponds and access roads cut through dark green boreal forest beside the Athabasca River.\n\nUsing Figure 11, identify two ways in which oil sands extraction is a threat to the taiga.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for each, up to 2 marks:\n• Forest is cleared completely for open-pit mines (1)\n• Tailings ponds may leak toxic waste into the river / groundwater (1)\n• Roads and pipelines fragment the forest / habitats (1)\n• Pollution of the Athabasca River downstream (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 12: a newspaper headline from 2024 — "Amazon drought: rivers at record lows as fires spread through dried-out forest".\n\nExplain one way in which climate change is a threat to tropical rainforests.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the threat, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• Higher temperatures and longer droughts (1) dry out the forest so fires spread more easily and kill trees not adapted to burning (1).\n• Changing rainfall patterns (1) mean some species cannot survive in their current range, reducing biodiversity (1).\n• Drought stress kills trees (1) so the forest stores less carbon and may release it, speeding up warming (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 13: a photograph of clear-felled taiga in Karelia, north-west Russia. Every tree has been cut over a large area, leaving stumps, branches and tracks made by heavy logging machines. Untouched forest is visible on the horizon.\n\nExplain one way in which the activity shown in Figure 13 reduces biodiversity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• Clear-felling removes all the trees at once (1) destroying the habitat / food source of species such as woodland caribou and owls (1).\n• Replanting is often with a single species of conifer (1) so the new forest supports fewer species than old-growth forest (1).\n• Logging tracks fragment the forest (1) so animal populations become isolated (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 14: an advertisement for an eco-lodge in the Monteverde Cloud Forest Reserve, Costa Rica: "Small groups only. Local guides. Solar-powered cabins. Part of every booking funds forest protection."\n\nExplain one way in which ecotourism like that shown in Figure 14 helps to conserve forests.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• It gives local people jobs as guides / lodge staff (1) so they earn more from keeping the forest than from clearing it (1).\n• Money from visitors is used to fund the reserve (1) paying for rangers / buying more land for protection (1).\n• Small group sizes limit damage (1) so trails and habitats are not degraded (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nIn 2010 Norway agreed to pay Indonesia up to US$1 billion if it reduced carbon emissions from forest loss, as part of the UN-backed REDD+ approach.\n\nExplain one way in which REDD+ can reduce deforestation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• Rich countries pay forested countries for the carbon stored in standing forest (1) so keeping the forest becomes more profitable than clearing it for farming (1).\n• Payments depend on satellite monitoring showing reduced forest loss (1) which encourages governments to enforce laws against illegal logging (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 15: a food web from the Amazon rainforest. Brazil nut trees are pollinated only by large orchid bees. The bees need orchids that grow on other trees. Agoutis (large rodents) eat Brazil nuts and bury some, which later grow into new trees.\n\nUsing Figure 15, explain one way in which the Brazil nut tree shows interdependence.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a link, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• The tree depends on orchid bees for pollination (1) and the bees depend on orchids, so if orchids are lost the trees cannot produce nuts (1).\n• Agoutis depend on the nuts for food (1) and the tree depends on agoutis burying nuts to disperse its seeds (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 16: number of tree species in a 1 hectare sample plot — tropical rainforest, Ecuador: 473; taiga, Finland: 4 (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain one reason why the taiga has lower biodiversity than the tropical rainforest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• The growing season is short, only a few months above about 6 °C (1) so only a few hardy species can survive and grow (1).\n• Soils are thin, acidic and nutrient-poor / often frozen (1) so few plant species can establish, which supports fewer animals (1).\n• Low light and cold mean low primary productivity (1) so there is less energy to support many species (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nThe Belo Monte dam on the Xingu River, Pará, Brazil, is one of the largest hydroelectric power (HEP) schemes in the world.\n\nExplain one way in which HEP schemes can lead to deforestation in tropical rainforests.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• The reservoir floods large areas of forest (1) killing trees and destroying habitats (1).\n• Roads and power lines must be built to the site (1) opening up the forest to settlers, loggers and farmers (1).\n• Workers move to the area (1) and new settlements clear forest for housing and farmland (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nThe Trans-Amazonian Highway (BR-230) was started in the 1970s and runs east–west across the Brazilian Amazon. Satellite images show strips of cleared forest branching off it at right angles.\n\nExplain one way in which road building leads to further deforestation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• Roads give access to previously remote forest (1) so loggers, cattle ranchers and settlers can move in and clear land along side roads (1).\n• Roads make it cheaper to transport timber / beef / soya to market (1) so commercial clearance becomes more profitable (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 10: annual forest loss in the Brazilian Amazon, 2016–2023, in km² (data written for this practice question).\n2016: 7,900 | 2017: 6,900 | 2018: 7,500 | 2019: 10,100 | 2020: 10,900 | 2021: 13,000 | 2022: 11,600 | 2023: 9,000\n\nDescribe the trend in forest loss shown in Figure 10. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 3 marks; at least one must use data.\n• Forest loss fell slightly from 7,900 km² in 2016 to its lowest, 6,900 km², in 2017 (1)\n• It then rose each year to a peak of 13,000 km² in 2021 (1)\n• Between 2018 and 2021 it rose by 5,500 km² / about 73% (1)\n• After 2021 it fell, to 9,000 km² in 2023, but was still higher than in 2016–2018 (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 17: climate data for Manaus, Brazil, in the Amazon rainforest (data written for this practice question). Mean monthly temperature 26–28 °C all year; total annual rainfall 2,300 mm, with rain on most afternoons.\n\nExplain one reason why tropical rainforests have very high biodiversity.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, up to 2 further marks for development. For example:\n• Temperatures and rainfall are high all year (1) so there is a year-round growing season and very high primary productivity (1) which provides plentiful food and energy to support many species (1).\n• The forest has several layers (1) each with different light and humidity conditions (1) creating many niches for different species (1).\n• The climate has been stable for millions of years (1) giving a long time for species to evolve (1) and specialise (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 18: a photograph of a hillside in Rondônia, Brazil, two years after the rainforest was cleared for cattle ranching. Gullies have formed in the red soil and the river at the bottom of the slope is brown with sediment.\n\nExplain one way in which deforestation leads to the changes shown in Figure 18.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, up to 2 further marks for development. For example:\n• Removing the canopy means heavy rain hits the ground directly instead of being intercepted (1) so more water flows over the surface as overland flow (1) washing soil downslope into gullies and rivers (1).\n• Tree roots no longer bind the soil (1) so it is easily eroded by rainfall (1) and the sediment makes rivers brown / causes them to silt up (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nBig-leaf mahogany, a valuable hardwood from Central and South American rainforests, was added to Appendix II of CITES (the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) in 2002.\n\nExplain one way in which CITES can help to protect forest ecosystems.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, up to 2 further marks for development. For example:\n• Listing species controls or bans international trade in them, e.g. mahogany can only be exported with a permit (1) so it is less profitable to log them illegally (1) which protects trees and the habitats they support (1).\n• Appendix I bans commercial trade in the most endangered species such as orangutans (1) reducing demand for hunting / capture (1) helping populations recover and keeping the food web intact (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 7 shows the layers of a tropical rainforest in the Amazon: emergent trees up to 50 m, a continuous canopy at 30–40 m, an under-canopy, a shrub layer and a dark forest floor. Leaves in the canopy are large and many have pointed tips.\n\nExplain two ways in which plants are adapted to the tropical rainforest climate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two ways (1 mark for the adaptation, 1 for development). For example:\n• Drip-tip leaves (1) let heavy rain run off quickly, stopping the growth of algae / fungi on the leaf (1).\n• Lianas root in the ground and climb trees (1) to reach sunlight in the canopy (1).\n• Epiphytes grow on branches high in the canopy (1) to reach light and take moisture from the humid air (1).\n• Large leaves in the lower layers (1) capture as much of the limited light as possible (1).\n• Thin, smooth bark (1) because there is no cold or dry season to protect against, and it sheds climbing plants (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 4 shows that winter temperatures in the Siberian taiga fall to −22 °C, the summer peak is 17 °C, and only five months have a mean temperature above 5 °C. Total annual precipitation is 453 mm, much of it falling as snow (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain two ways in which coniferous trees are adapted to the taiga climate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two ways. For example:\n• Evergreen needles (1) mean trees can photosynthesise as soon as temperatures rise in the short growing season, without growing new leaves (1).\n• Conical shape with downward-sloping branches (1) so heavy snow slides off without breaking branches (1).\n• Small needles with a waxy coating (1) reduce water loss when ground water is frozen and unavailable (1).\n• Dark green colour (1) absorbs as much of the low-angle sunlight as possible (1).\n• Thick bark (1) protects against extreme cold and fire (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 2 (tropical rainforest) and Figure 3 (taiga) are Gersmehl nutrient cycle diagrams.\nTropical rainforest: very large biomass store; small litter and soil stores; very thick transfer arrows.\nTaiga: large litter store; medium biomass store; very small soil store; thin transfer arrows.\n\nExplain two differences between the nutrient cycles of the tropical rainforest and the taiga.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two differences (1 for the difference, 1 for the explanation). For example:\n• The rainforest's largest store is biomass but the taiga's is litter (1) because in the rainforest heat and moisture allow fast decomposition and rapid uptake, whereas cold slows decomposition in the taiga (1).\n• Transfers are much faster / larger in the rainforest (1) because the growing season lasts all year, whereas in the taiga it is only a few months long (1).\n• Leaching losses are greater in the rainforest (1) because of very heavy rainfall compared with low precipitation in the taiga (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 6 shows that, of 1,200 km² of rainforest cleared in one province of Borneo, Indonesia, 540 km² became oil palm plantations, 300 km² was logged and 120 km² was mined (data written for this practice question).\n\nFor a named tropical rainforest, explain two causes of deforestation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two causes, linked to a named rainforest (e.g. Borneo, Amazon). For example:\n• Commercial farming: in Borneo forest is cleared for oil palm plantations (1) because Indonesia is the world's largest palm oil producer and demand for it in food and fuel is high (1).\n• Logging: valuable hardwoods such as meranti / mahogany are cut (1) for export, which also opens the forest to further clearance (1).\n• Mining: gold / coal / bauxite mines need forest cleared (1) and roads built to remove the ore (1).\n• Cattle ranching in the Amazon (1) to supply growing global demand for beef (1).\nMaximum 3 marks if no named example.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 19: products from Canada's boreal forest (taiga) — softwood timber for construction; wood pulp for paper and tissue; oil from the Alberta oil sands; electricity from HEP dams in Quebec and Manitoba; furs and game.\n\nExplain two reasons why the taiga is being exploited.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two reasons. For example:\n• Softwood conifers grow straight and are found in large single-species stands (1) so they are cheap to harvest for timber and pulp, which are in global demand (1).\n• The taiga contains large reserves of oil, gas and minerals such as the Alberta oil sands (1) which earn export income and create jobs (1).\n• Large, fast-flowing rivers (1) are dammed to produce HEP for cities further south (1).\n• Low population density means little opposition (1) and land is cheap to exploit (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 20: a 2023 report on Canada's boreal forest stated that the wildfire season was the worst on record, with over 150,000 km² burnt, and that the mountain pine beetle had killed large areas of pine in British Columbia since the 1990s because winters were no longer cold enough to kill the beetle larvae.\n\nExplain two ways in which climate change is a threat to the taiga.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two ways. For example:\n• Warmer, drier summers (1) increase the number and size of wildfires, which destroy large areas of forest (1).\n• Milder winters (1) allow pests such as the mountain pine beetle to survive and spread, killing trees (1).\n• Rising temperatures (1) mean some tree species cannot survive at the southern edge of the taiga, which shrinks or changes to grassland (1).\n• Thawing permafrost (1) makes ground unstable so trees lean and fall / releases methane, speeding up warming (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nAbout half of the rain that falls on the Amazon rainforest is returned to the atmosphere by evapotranspiration from the trees. The rainforest also stores very large amounts of carbon.\n\nExplain two ways in which deforestation of tropical rainforests can affect climate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two ways. For example:\n• Fewer trees means less evapotranspiration (1) so less moisture returns to the atmosphere and rainfall decreases locally / regionally, making droughts more likely (1).\n• Burning and decay of cleared trees releases carbon dioxide (1) which adds to the enhanced greenhouse effect / global warming (1).\n• Fewer trees means less carbon dioxide is absorbed by photosynthesis (1) so the forest's role as a carbon sink is reduced (1).\n• Bare ground reflects / absorbs heat differently and lacks shade (1) so local temperatures rise (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 21: a label on a pack of garden furniture sold in the UK: "FSC certified — from responsibly managed forests". The Forest Stewardship Council (FSC) checks that logging companies replant, protect wildlife and respect local people's rights.\n\nExplain two ways in which sustainable forestry can help to manage forests.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two ways. For example:\n• Selective logging removes only mature trees (1) so the canopy stays largely intact and the forest regenerates naturally (1).\n• Replanting after felling (1) means timber can be harvested again in the future without permanent loss of forest (1).\n• Certification lets consumers choose sustainable timber (1) so companies have a financial reason to follow the rules (1).\n• Monitoring / tagging of logs (1) makes it harder to sell illegally logged timber (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 22: Costa Rica fact file. Forest cover fell to about one-fifth of the country in the 1980s, mainly because of cattle ranching. In 1996 a law banned clearing forest without a permit, and from 1997 landowners were paid to protect forest (payments for environmental services). Around a quarter of the country is now in national parks and reserves. Tourism is now one of the largest sources of foreign income. Forest now covers about half of the country.\n\nUsing evidence from Figure 22, suggest two reasons why forest cover in Costa Rica has increased.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two reasons, using evidence from Figure 22. For example:\n• Landowners have been paid to protect forest since 1997 (1) so keeping or regrowing trees earns money that might otherwise come from cattle (1).\n• The 1996 law banned clearance without a permit (1) so further losses were reduced and abandoned pasture could regrow (1).\n• About a quarter of the land is protected in national parks (1) where logging and farming are not allowed (1).\n• Tourism earns a lot of income (1) so the forest is more valuable standing as an attraction than cleared (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 7 shows a tropical rainforest with five layers (emergent, canopy, under-canopy, shrub layer, forest floor). Figure 1 shows the taiga, with tall conifers of a few species above a ground layer of mosses and lichens.\n\nExplain two ways in which the structure of the taiga differs from the structure of the tropical rainforest.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two differences. For example:\n• The taiga has fewer layers, usually just a tree layer and a ground layer (1) because the cold, short growing season limits the plants that can grow beneath the conifers (1).\n• Taiga trees are shorter and of only a few species, e.g. spruce, pine, fir, larch (1) whereas the rainforest has emergents up to 50 m and hundreds of tree species, because of its year-round warmth and rainfall (1).\n• The taiga has little undergrowth (1) because dense evergreen needles block light and the acidic litter makes poor soil (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nWood Buffalo National Park, on the border of Alberta and the Northwest Territories, Canada, is the country's largest national park (about 44,800 km²) and a UNESCO World Heritage Site. It protects nesting grounds of the endangered whooping crane. The Athabasca River flows into the park from the oil sands region upstream.\n\nExplain two reasons why it can be difficult to protect wilderness areas in the taiga.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each of two reasons. For example:\n• Pollution from outside the protected area, e.g. from oil sands upstream, travels into the park by river (1) so a boundary alone cannot protect it (1).\n• Protected areas are vast and remote (1) so it is expensive and difficult to patrol / monitor them (1).\n• Governments earn large revenues from oil, minerals and timber (1) so there is pressure to allow development close to or within wilderness areas (1).\n• Climate change effects such as fires and pests (1) cannot be stopped by park rules (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 6 shows that, of 1,200 km² of rainforest cleared in one province of Borneo, 45% became oil palm plantations, 25% was logged, 15% was used for small-scale farming, 10% for mining and 5% for roads and settlements (data written for this practice question).\n\nAssess the relative importance of different causes of deforestation in tropical rainforests.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Commercial farming (oil palm in Borneo, cattle and soya in the Amazon) is often the largest cause by area, driven by global demand.\n• Logging, legal and illegal, removes valuable hardwoods and opens up the forest with tracks, leading to further clearance.\n• Mining (e.g. gold, bauxite, coal) clears smaller areas but causes pollution and needs roads.\n• HEP schemes such as Belo Monte flood forest and bring roads and settlers.\n• Road building (e.g. Trans-Amazonian Highway) is a trigger that makes other causes possible.\n• Population growth and subsistence farming / shifting cultivation are locally important but clear less than commercial activities.\n• Causes are interlinked; importance varies between places and over time, e.g. with government policy and commodity prices.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Well-developed understanding of a range of causes, with balanced, well-supported assessment of their relative importance, using evidence from Figure 6 and examples. Reaches a supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding of several causes; partial assessment, some use of evidence; conclusion may be unbalanced or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge of one or two causes; little or no assessment.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nCITES came into force in 1975 and controls international trade in endangered species, including timber such as big-leaf mahogany and rosewood. REDD+ pays countries such as Brazil and Indonesia to reduce carbon emissions from forest loss.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of global actions in protecting forests.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• CITES: bans or controls trade in listed species, reducing demand for illegal timber and wildlife; very wide membership. But it only covers international trade, depends on national enforcement and on species being listed, and illegal trade continues.\n• REDD+: puts a financial value on standing forest; funds monitoring, enforcement and alternative livelihoods (e.g. Norway's pledges to Brazil and Indonesia). But payments depend on political will, can be paused when governments change policy, may displace clearance elsewhere ("leakage"), and may not reach local / indigenous people.\n• Other global factors: certification (FSC) and consumer pressure, debt-for-nature swaps.\n• Global actions work best when combined with strong local and national management; deforestation rates have varied with government policy rather than global agreements alone.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed, accurate knowledge of global actions; balanced evaluation of strengths and limitations, supported by examples; well-reasoned judgement on effectiveness.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some accurate knowledge; evaluation is partial or one-sided; judgement present but not fully supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated, basic points; little or no evaluation.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 20: in 2023 Canada's boreal forest had its worst wildfire season on record. Other pressures on the taiga include softwood logging, oil sands mining in Alberta, HEP dams in Quebec, and acid rain from smelters.\n\nAssess the extent to which climate change is the greatest threat to the taiga.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nClimate change as the greatest threat:\n• Warmer, drier summers increase wildfire area; milder winters let pests such as the mountain pine beetle spread; species ranges shift north; permafrost thaws.\n• Affects the whole biome, including protected areas, and is hard to manage locally.\nOther threats:\n• Commercial forestry clear-fells large areas for softwood timber and pulp, replacing old growth with plantations.\n• Mining and oil / gas extraction (Alberta oil sands) remove forest completely and pollute rivers.\n• HEP schemes flood large areas.\n• Acid rain damages needles and acidifies soils and lakes.\n• Human threats can be regulated; many also add to climate change, so threats are linked.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed understanding of climate change and other threats; balanced assessment weighing their scale and permanence; supported conclusion on the extent.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding of threats; partial assessment; conclusion weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; little or no assessment.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 11 shows open-pit oil sands mines, tailings ponds and roads cut into boreal forest near Fort McMurray, Alberta. The oil sands are among the largest oil reserves in the world and the industry employs tens of thousands of people.\n\nEvaluate the costs and benefits of exploiting the taiga.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nBenefits:\n• Jobs and income in remote areas (oil sands, logging, pulp mills); tax revenue and export earnings.\n• Energy security from oil and HEP; timber and paper for construction and consumers.\nCosts:\n• Loss of habitat for species such as woodland caribou; fragmentation by roads and pipelines.\n• Pollution, e.g. tailings ponds and river contamination affecting downstream communities and Wood Buffalo National Park.\n• Carbon released from cleared forest and peat; oil sands extraction is energy-intensive, adding to climate change.\n• Impacts on First Nations people's traditional hunting, fishing and land rights.\n• Some exploitation can be sustainable (selective logging, replanting, land reclamation), but mining damage is long-lasting.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed, balanced evaluation of costs and benefits using examples; reaches a well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some costs and benefits; evaluation partial or one-sided; judgement weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated points; little or no evaluation.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nFigure 22: in Costa Rica, ecotourism, payments to landowners and national parks have helped forest cover rise from about one-fifth to about half of the country.\n\nExamine the extent to which local-scale sustainable management can protect tropical rainforests.`, marks: 8, command: 'Examine', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nStrengths:\n• Ecotourism (e.g. Monteverde) gives local people income from standing forest and funds protection.\n• Sustainable forestry (selective logging, replanting, FSC certification) allows use without permanent loss.\n• Community management and indigenous reserves are often associated with lower deforestation rates.\n• Agroforestry and extractive reserves (e.g. rubber tapping, Brazil nuts) provide livelihoods.\nLimitations:\n• Small scale compared with commercial clearance for palm oil, soya or cattle.\n• Ecotourism brings limited numbers and can cause damage itself; income may leak to foreign companies.\n• Local schemes cannot stop global threats such as climate change or global demand for commodities.\n• Success depends on national government enforcement and global support (e.g. REDD+, CITES).\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed understanding of local strategies with examples; balanced examination of strengths and limits; well-supported conclusion on the extent.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding; partial examination; conclusion weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; little or no examination.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\nWood Buffalo National Park, Canada, protects about 44,800 km² of boreal forest, wetlands and the Peace-Athabasca Delta. In 2017 UNESCO raised concerns about the effects of oil sands development and upstream dams on the park.\n\nDiscuss the importance of protected areas in conserving the taiga.`, marks: 8, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nImportance:\n• National parks and wilderness areas ban or limit logging, mining and building, protecting habitats for species such as wood bison, whooping crane and caribou.\n• Large protected areas keep the forest unfragmented, allowing animals to migrate.\n• Protect carbon stores in trees and peat, helping to limit climate change.\n• Can support indigenous people's traditional ways of life.\nLimitations:\n• Only a small percentage of the taiga is strictly protected; vast remote areas are hard to monitor.\n• Pollution and water changes from outside, e.g. oil sands and upstream dams, still affect parks.\n• Climate change threats (fire, pests, permafrost thaw) do not respect boundaries.\n• Pressure for economic development may lead to boundaries being changed.\n• Other approaches are also needed: sustainable forestry, replanting, land reclamation after mining, global agreements.\n\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed, accurate understanding; balanced discussion with well-chosen examples; reaches a supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Some understanding; discussion partial or one-sided; conclusion weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; little or no discussion.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section D — Making a Geographical Decision.\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of the rainforest in Borneo, Indonesia. Indonesia is the world's largest producer of palm oil and much of its lowland rainforest in Borneo has been cleared for plantations, logging and mining. The forest is home to the critically endangered Bornean orangutan and to indigenous Dayak communities. Palm oil provides jobs for millions of Indonesians and is a major export. Forest fires on drained peatland have caused severe smoke pollution across South-East Asia.\nThree options for Indonesia's government to manage the rainforest more sustainably:\nOption 1 — Expand national parks and protected areas and increase patrols against illegal logging.\nOption 2 — Make greater use of international schemes such as REDD+, receiving payments for reducing forest loss and using CITES to stop the illegal wildlife trade.\nOption 3 — Require all palm oil and timber companies to be certified as sustainable and support community ecotourism and forest-friendly farming.\n\nSelect the option you think Indonesia's government ought to focus on first. Justify your choice. Use information from the Resource Booklet and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer. (12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `No option is better than another; credit is for the quality of justification.\nIndicative content:\nOption 1: directly protects habitats for orangutans and biodiversity; stops clearance in key areas; but expensive to patrol large, remote areas, may displace clearance elsewhere and may restrict local / Dayak people's use of the forest; little economic benefit.\nOption 2: brings in external funding; gives standing forest a financial value; supports satellite monitoring; CITES reduces wildlife trade. But payments depend on donor countries and political will; benefits may not reach local people; leakage.\nOption 3: works with the palm oil industry that employs millions, so it keeps jobs and export income while reducing damage; consumer demand for certified products; ecotourism gives local income from standing forest. But certification can be weakly enforced, does not restore lost forest, and ecotourism is small scale.\nGood answers consider economic, social and environmental impacts, short and long term, and why the chosen option is better than the others.\n\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Thorough, accurate knowledge and understanding applied throughout; detailed, balanced justification of the chosen option with comparison against the other options; uses a wide range of evidence from the resources and wider course; well-reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Accurate knowledge applied; clear justification with some comparison; good use of evidence; conclusion supported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant knowledge; partial justification, mostly of one option; some evidence; conclusion weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited or no justification; little evidence.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4 marks): spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; grammar used to control meaning effectively; wide range of specialist terms used appropriately.\nIntermediate performance (2–3 marks): considerable accuracy in spelling and punctuation; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): reasonable accuracy; no serious hindrance to meaning; limited specialist terms.\n0 marks: no response, or does not reach threshold.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.3': {
    name: 'Consuming Energy Resources',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 1 — energy consumption per person, 2022, gigajoules (GJ) per person (data written for this practice question):\nCanada 380 | USA 275 | China 110 | UK 105 | Brazil 60 | India 27 | Nigeria 9\n\nStudy Figure 1. Identify the country with the lowest energy consumption per person.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Nigeria (1).\nAccept 9 GJ per person only if the country is also named.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nEnergy resources can be classified as non-renewable, renewable or recyclable.\n\nIdentify which one of the following is a recyclable energy resource.\nA  Coal\nB  Natural gas\nC  Nuclear power using reprocessed uranium fuel\nD  Oil from tar sands`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nConventional oil and gas are extracted from easily reached reservoirs. Unconventional fossil fuels need more complex methods to extract.\n\nIdentify which one of the following is an unconventional fossil fuel.\nA  Crude oil from Saudi Arabia's Ghawar field\nB  Bitumen from the Alberta tar sands, Canada\nC  Natural gas from the North Sea\nD  Coal from an open-cast mine`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 1 — energy consumption per person, 2022, gigajoules (GJ) per person (data written for this practice question):\nCanada 380 | USA 275 | China 110 | UK 105 | Brazil 60 | India 27 | Nigeria 9\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the range of energy consumption per person.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `371 GJ per person (1).\nWorking: 380 − 9 = 371.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 4 — a newspaper headline, 2022: "Oil price passes US$120 a barrel as war in Europe disrupts supplies."\n\nUsing your own knowledge, state one economic cause of a large fall in the global oil price.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `One from (1):\n• fall in demand during a global recession / economic slowdown (e.g. 2008–09 financial crisis)\n• oversupply, e.g. rapid growth of US shale oil production (2014–16)\n• OPEC countries increasing production / not cutting output\n• lockdowns reducing demand for transport fuel (2020)\n• increased use of alternative energy / greater fuel efficiency reducing demand.\nDo not accept political causes alone (e.g. war) unless linked to supply rising.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 5 — sources of electricity generated in the UK, 2023 (data written for this practice question): gas 34%, wind 29%, nuclear 14%, biomass 11%, solar 5%, hydro 2%, other 5%.\n\nStudy Figure 5. Identify the largest renewable source of electricity in the UK.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Wind (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 6 — a council leaflet: "Save energy, save money! Around a quarter of the heat in an uninsulated home is lost through the roof."\n\nGive one way in which households can reduce the amount of energy they use for heating.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `One from (1):\n• fit loft insulation\n• fit cavity wall insulation\n• install double / triple glazing\n• draught-proof doors and windows\n• turn down the thermostat / use a smart thermostat or timer\n• install a heat pump (more efficient heating).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 7 — fact file: the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) was formed in 1960. Its members agree how much oil each will produce, which affects the world price.\n\nName one country that is a member of OPEC.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `One from (1): Saudi Arabia, Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Venezuela, United Arab Emirates, Nigeria, Libya, Algeria, Gabon, Equatorial Guinea, Republic of the Congo.\nDo not accept the USA, Russia, Canada, Norway or the UK.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 8 — quotation from an energy minister: "Our country now buys almost half of its gas from abroad. We must improve our energy security."\n\nState what is meant by the term 'energy security'.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Having a reliable, uninterrupted supply of energy that is affordable / available at a reasonable price (1).\nAccept: having enough energy to meet demand without depending on unreliable imports.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 1 — energy consumption per person, 2022, gigajoules (GJ) per person (data written for this practice question):\nCanada 380 | USA 275 | China 110 | UK 105 | Brazil 60 | India 27 | Nigeria 9\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the mean energy consumption per person for the seven countries. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 380 + 275 + 110 + 105 + 60 + 27 + 9 = 966 (1); 966 ÷ 7 = 138.0 GJ per person (1).\nAward 2 marks for 138.0 with no working only if the total 966 is seen; accept 138.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 1 — energy consumption per person, 2022, gigajoules (GJ) per person (data written for this practice question):\nCanada 380 | USA 275 | China 110 | UK 105 | Brazil 60 | India 27 | Nigeria 9\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the ratio of energy consumption per person in the USA to that in India. Give your answer in the form x : 1, to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 275 ÷ 27 = 10.185... (1); ratio 10.2 : 1 (1).\nAccept 10.19 : 1 for 1 mark only if rounding instruction not followed.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 2 — annual average price of Brent crude oil, US$ per barrel (approximate values, rounded; data written for this practice question):\n2012: 112 | 2014: 99 | 2016: 44 | 2018: 71 | 2020: 42 | 2022: 99\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate the percentage (%) decrease in the price of oil between 2014 and 2016. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 99 − 44 = 55; (55 ÷ 99) × 100 (1) = 55.6% (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 2 — annual average price of Brent crude oil, US$ per barrel (approximate values, rounded; data written for this practice question):\n2012: 112 | 2014: 99 | 2016: 44 | 2018: 71 | 2020: 42 | 2022: 99\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate the percentage (%) increase in the price of oil between 2020 and 2022. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 99 − 42 = 57; (57 ÷ 42) × 100 (1) = 135.7% (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 3 — approximate proven oil reserves, 2020, billion barrels (rounded values; data written for this practice question): Venezuela 303.8 | Saudi Arabia 297.5 | Canada 168.1 | Iran 157.8 | Iraq 145.0. World total: about 1,732 billion barrels.\n\nUsing Figure 3, calculate Venezuela's share of the world's proven oil reserves as a percentage (%). Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: (303.8 ÷ 1,732) × 100 (1) = 17.5% (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 2 — annual average price of Brent crude oil, US$ per barrel (approximate values, rounded; data written for this practice question):\n2012: 112 | 2014: 99 | 2016: 44 | 2018: 71 | 2020: 42 | 2022: 99\n\nThe data shown in Figure 2 is presented in a table. Describe one other suitable method of data presentation to show the change in oil price between 2012 and 2022.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `A line graph (1) with year on the x-axis and price in US$ per barrel on the y-axis, so the rises and falls over time are clear (1).\nAccept a bar chart with one bar per year, developed with suitable axes (1 + 1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 9 — a fact file about Iceland: about 85% of Iceland's primary energy comes from geothermal power and hydro-electric power (HEP). Iceland lies on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and has many fast-flowing rivers fed by glacial meltwater.\n\nExplain one way in which physical factors affect a country's access to energy resources.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a physical factor + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• geology determines whether fossil fuels are present (1) — oil and gas are only found in certain sedimentary basins, so some countries such as Saudi Arabia have large reserves and others have none (1)\n• tectonic activity (1) — plate boundaries such as Iceland's bring heat close to the surface, so geothermal energy can be used (1)\n• climate / relief (1) — high rainfall and steep relief give fast-flowing rivers for HEP, or high sunshine totals make solar power productive (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 10 — about 600 million people in sub-Saharan Africa had no access to electricity in 2022. Many rural households rely on fuelwood and charcoal for cooking.\n\nExplain one reason why many people in developing countries have limited access to energy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• people cannot afford electricity or fuel (1) because incomes are low, so they use free fuelwood instead (1)\n• governments lack money to build power stations and grids (1) so rural areas far from towns are not connected (1)\n• dispersed rural populations (1) make it expensive to extend transmission lines to each village (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 11 — Alaska, USA: oil was discovered at Prudhoe Bay on the Arctic coast in 1968. The Trans-Alaska Pipeline carries the oil about 1,287 km south to the port of Valdez. Much of the land it crosses has permafrost.\n\nExplain one environmental impact of extracting oil in Alaska.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for impact + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• oil spills pollute the land and sea (1) — the Exxon Valdez tanker spill in 1989 released about 41 million litres of oil into Prince William Sound, killing seabirds, sea otters and fish (1)\n• heat from buildings / pipelines can melt permafrost (1), causing ground subsidence and damage to the fragile tundra (1)\n• pipelines and roads can disrupt the migration of caribou (1), affecting their breeding and the Indigenous people who hunt them (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 12 — the Alberta tar sands, Canada: bitumen is mined from huge open pits in the boreal forest near Fort McMurray. Waste water is stored in large tailings ponds beside the Athabasca River.\n\nExplain one environmental impact of extracting oil from tar sands.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for impact + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• deforestation / habitat loss (1) — large areas of boreal forest and peatland are cleared for open-pit mines, destroying habitats for species such as woodland caribou (1)\n• water pollution (1) — toxic chemicals can leak from tailings ponds into the Athabasca River, harming fish and communities downstream (1)\n• high greenhouse gas emissions (1) — large amounts of energy are needed to heat and separate the bitumen, so more CO₂ is released per barrel than for conventional oil (1)\n• large volumes of water are used (1), lowering river levels (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 13 — Whitelee wind farm, East Renfrewshire, near Glasgow, is the UK's largest onshore wind farm, with over 200 turbines on moorland.\n\nExplain one environmental impact of developing a renewable energy resource.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for impact + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• visual impact (1) — tall turbines on open moorland can spoil views in an area valued for its landscape (1)\n• wildlife (1) — birds and bats may be killed by turbine blades / peat habitats disturbed during construction (1)\n• positive: reduces greenhouse gas emissions (1) because electricity is generated without burning fossil fuels (1).\nAccept impacts of other named renewables, e.g. reservoirs for HEP flooding land.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 14 — in 2022, after Russia invaded Ukraine, supplies of Russian gas to Europe were cut, and gas prices in Europe rose to record levels.\n\nExplain one way in which political factors can affect energy security.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for factor + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• conflict / war (1) can cut off supplies from producing countries, so importing countries face shortages and price rises (1)\n• energy can be used as a political weapon (1) — a supplier may restrict exports to put pressure on importing countries, as with Russian gas in 2022 (1)\n• government policy (1) — a ban on fracking or new nuclear plants can reduce the domestic supply of energy (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 2 — annual average price of Brent crude oil, US$ per barrel (approximate values, rounded; data written for this practice question):\n2012: 112 | 2014: 99 | 2016: 44 | 2018: 71 | 2020: 42 | 2022: 99\n\nSuggest one reason for the fall in the price of oil between 2014 and 2016.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for reason + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• oversupply (1) — US shale oil production rose rapidly so supply exceeded demand (1)\n• OPEC / Saudi Arabia chose not to cut production (1), keeping supply high and pushing prices down (1)\n• slower economic growth in China (1) reduced demand for oil (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 2 — annual average price of Brent crude oil, US$ per barrel (approximate values, rounded; data written for this practice question):\n2012: 112 | 2014: 99 | 2016: 44 | 2018: 71 | 2020: 42 | 2022: 99\n\nSuggest one reason for the low price of oil in 2020.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for reason + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• the COVID-19 pandemic (1) — lockdowns reduced travel and industry, so demand for oil fell sharply while supply stayed high (1)\n• oil storage filled up (1), so producers had to sell at very low prices (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 15 — two cars (data written for this practice question): Car A uses 6.0 litres of petrol per 100 km; Car B uses 4.5 litres per 100 km. A family drives 12,000 km a year.\n\nCalculate how many litres of petrol the family would save in one year by using Car B instead of Car A. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: difference = 6.0 − 4.5 = 1.5 litres per 100 km; 12,000 ÷ 100 = 120 (1); 120 × 1.5 = 180 litres (1).\nAlternative: Car A 720 litres, Car B 540 litres, saving 180 litres.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 16 — a householder's solar panels (data written for this practice question): installation cost £6,000; savings on electricity bills £450 per year.\n\nCalculate how many years it would take for the savings to pay back the cost of installation. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 6,000 ÷ 450 (1) = 13.3 years (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 17 — primary energy consumption in China (approximate values; data written for this practice question): 2000: 42 exajoules (EJ); 2022: 159 EJ.\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) increase in China's energy consumption between 2000 and 2022. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 159 − 42 = 117; (117 ÷ 42) × 100 (1) = 278.6% (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 18 — students carried out a questionnaire of 40 households in their town about energy-saving measures (data written for this practice question). 18 households had loft insulation, 31 had double glazing and 7 had solar panels.\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) of households surveyed that had loft insulation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: (18 ÷ 40) × 100 (1) = 45% (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 19 — Germany closed its last three nuclear power stations in April 2023 and plans to phase out coal power.\n\nExplain one disadvantage of nuclear power as an energy source.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for disadvantage + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• radioactive waste (1) remains dangerous for thousands of years, so it must be stored securely, which is expensive (1)\n• risk of accidents (1) — e.g. Fukushima, Japan, 2011, where a tsunami caused meltdowns and people were evacuated (1)\n• very high construction / decommissioning costs (1) and long building times (1)\n• uranium is a finite resource (1) that must be imported by many countries (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 1 — energy consumption per person, 2022, gigajoules (GJ) per person (data written for this practice question):\nCanada 380 | USA 275 | China 110 | UK 105 | Brazil 60 | India 27 | Nigeria 9\n\nStudy Figure 1. Identify two countries that have energy consumption per person below 100 GJ.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Two from (1 + 1): Brazil, India, Nigeria.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 20 — fact file: global oil demand rose from about 77 million barrels per day in 2000 to about 100 million barrels per day in 2023, with the fastest growth in Asia.\n\nExplain one reason why global demand for oil has increased.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for reason + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• economic growth in emerging countries such as China and India (1) increases industrial production and transport of goods (1)\n• rising car ownership (1) as incomes rise, increasing demand for petrol and diesel (1)\n• population growth (1) means more people need fuel and plastics (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 5 — an incomplete pie chart of the sources of electricity generated in the UK, 2023 (data written for this practice question), showing gas 34%, wind 29%, nuclear 14% and biomass 11%; the remaining 12% is blank.\n\nComplete the pie chart using this information: solar 5%, hydro 2%, other 5%. Describe how you would divide and label the blank 12% of the circle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for converting at least one value to degrees (1% = 3.6°): solar 18°, hydro 7.2° (about 7°), other 18° (1).\n1 mark for drawing the segments from the centre using a protractor, starting from the end of the last segment, in order (1).\n1 mark for labelling or shading each segment and adding it to the key (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 21 — Preston New Road, Lancashire: fracking for shale gas began in 2018. Small earthquakes, the largest of magnitude 2.9 in August 2019, led the UK government to pause fracking in England in November 2019.\n\nExplain one reason why developing unconventional fossil fuels can cause conflict between different groups of people.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for reason, with up to 2 further marks for development, e.g.:\nEnergy companies and some governments want the jobs, tax income and energy security that shale gas brings (1), but local residents and environmental groups oppose it (1) because of risks such as earthquakes, water contamination, lorry traffic and further greenhouse gas emissions (1).\nMaximum 1 mark for a list of groups without explanation.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 2 — annual average price of Brent crude oil, US$ per barrel (approximate values, rounded; data written for this practice question):\n2012: 112 | 2014: 99 | 2016: 44 | 2018: 71 | 2020: 42 | 2022: 99\n\nDescribe the changes in the price of oil shown in Figure 2. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for description of changes + 1 mark for use of data, e.g.:\n• price falls overall from 2012 to 2016 (1)\n• price fluctuates / rises and falls after 2016 (1)\n• largest fall between 2014 and 2016, from US$99 to US$44 (1 for data)\n• price recovers to US$99 in 2022, the same as 2014 (1 for data).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 22 — the Trans-Alaska Pipeline is raised on supports for much of its length across the permafrost.\n\nExplain one way in which the environmental impacts of oil extraction in Alaska have been reduced.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for strategy, with up to 2 further marks for development, e.g.:\nThe pipeline is raised on supports (1) so heat from the oil does not melt the permafrost and cause subsidence (1); raised sections and buried crossings allow caribou to migrate underneath (1).\nOr: tankers now must have double hulls (1) after the Exxon Valdez spill (1), reducing the risk of oil escaping if a ship hits rocks (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 23 — many countries have introduced low-emission zones and congestion charges in cities, such as London's Ultra Low Emission Zone (ULEZ).\n\nExplain one way in which energy use in transport can be reduced.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for method, with up to 2 further marks for development, e.g.:\nImproving public transport, e.g. trams and buses (1), gives people an alternative to cars (1), so fewer journeys use petrol or diesel, and each passenger uses less energy per km (1).\nOr: charging drivers to enter city centres (1) discourages car use (1), encouraging walking, cycling or public transport (1).\nAccept electric vehicles, car sharing, cycle lanes, fuel-efficiency standards.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 1 — energy consumption per person, 2022, gigajoules (GJ) per person (data written for this practice question):\nCanada 380 | USA 275 | China 110 | UK 105 | Brazil 60 | India 27 | Nigeria 9\n\nExplain two reasons why energy consumption per person varies between countries such as those in Figure 1.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• level of development / wealth (1) — people in high-income countries such as the USA own more cars, appliances and heating / air conditioning (1)\n• climate (1) — cold winters in Canada mean much energy is used for heating (1)\n• availability of energy resources (1) — countries with their own oil, gas or HEP, such as Canada, have cheaper energy so use more (1)\n• industrial structure (1) — energy-intensive industries such as oil sands processing raise consumption (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 3 — approximate proven oil reserves, 2020, billion barrels (rounded values; data written for this practice question): Venezuela 303.8 | Saudi Arabia 297.5 | Canada 168.1 | Iran 157.8 | Iraq 145.0. World total: about 1,732 billion barrels.\n\nExplain two reasons why oil reserves are unevenly distributed around the world.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• geology (1) — oil forms only where organic material was buried in sedimentary rocks and trapped under impermeable cap rock, so it is concentrated in places such as the Middle East (1)\n• past environments (1) — areas that were once warm, shallow seas rich in plankton had the conditions for oil to form (1)\n• exploration and technology (1) — reserves are only 'proven' when they have been found and can be extracted economically, so Canada's reserves increased once tar sands could be exploited (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 12 — the Alberta tar sands, Canada: Canada has the world's third largest proven oil reserves, mostly in the tar sands. Mining and processing employs many thousands of people in and around Fort McMurray.\n\nExplain one benefit and one cost of developing the Alberta tar sands.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for benefit + 1 development; 1 mark for cost + 1 development, e.g.:\nBenefit: jobs and income (1) — high wages attract workers and tax revenues fund services in Alberta (1); or energy security for Canada / USA (1) as oil comes from a stable, friendly country (1).\nCost: environmental damage (1) — clearance of boreal forest and toxic tailings ponds (1); or high greenhouse gas emissions (1) from the energy needed to process bitumen, adding to climate change (1); or health concerns among First Nations communities downstream, e.g. Fort Chipewyan (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 24 — the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge (ANWR) in north-east Alaska is home to caribou herds, polar bears and migrating birds. Some politicians want to allow oil drilling on its coastal plain.\n\nExplain two reasons why some people oppose oil exploitation in environmentally sensitive areas such as the Arctic.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• fragile ecosystems (1) — low temperatures mean plants grow and recover very slowly, so damage lasts for decades (1)\n• risk of oil spills (1) — cold water and sea ice make clean-up very difficult, as shown by the Exxon Valdez spill in 1989 (1)\n• disruption to wildlife (1), e.g. caribou calving grounds on the coastal plain (1)\n• impacts on Indigenous people such as the Gwich'in (1) who depend on caribou for food and culture (1)\n• climate change (1) — burning more fossil fuel adds to the warming that is already melting Arctic ice (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 25 — oil price rises: in 1973, OPEC cut oil exports to some Western countries and the oil price roughly quadrupled. In 2022, Brent crude rose above US$120 a barrel.\n\nExplain two ways in which a large increase in the price of oil can affect the economies of oil-importing countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each effect (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• higher transport costs (1) push up the price of goods, causing inflation (1)\n• businesses' costs rise (1), so they may cut jobs or production, leading to recession (1)\n• the cost of imports rises (1), worsening the country's trade balance / government debt (1)\n• households spend more on fuel and heating (1), leaving less to spend on other goods, so the economy slows (1).\nAccept a positive effect, e.g. investment in renewables becomes more attractive (1 + 1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 26 — Nigeria is one of Africa's largest oil producers. Oil provides most of its export earnings, but oil spills and gas flaring have damaged the Niger Delta.\n\nExplain two ways in which an oil-exporting country can be affected by a fall in the price of oil.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each effect (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• lower export earnings (1) — government revenue falls, so spending on schools, hospitals and infrastructure is cut (1)\n• job losses in the oil industry (1) as companies stop drilling expensive new wells (1)\n• currency weakens (1), making imported food and goods more expensive (1)\n• increased debt (1) as the government borrows to cover lost income (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 27 — energy use in UK homes: space heating accounts for over half of household energy use. Many homes were built before 1950 with solid walls and single glazing.\n\nExplain two ways in which energy can be conserved in homes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each way (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• insulation of lofts and walls (1) reduces heat loss, so less gas is burned to keep homes warm (1)\n• smart meters / thermostats (1) show energy use, encouraging people to turn heating down or off when not needed (1)\n• energy-efficient appliances and LED bulbs (1) use less electricity for the same output (1)\n• heat pumps / solar panels (1) reduce demand for fossil fuel energy (1)\n• building regulations for new homes (1) set high standards of energy efficiency (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 13 — Whitelee wind farm near Glasgow generates electricity for hundreds of thousands of homes.\n\nExplain two advantages of using renewable energy resources instead of fossil fuels.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each advantage (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• lower carbon emissions (1) — no fossil fuels are burned during generation, reducing the enhanced greenhouse effect (1)\n• they will not run out (1), so supply is sustainable for future generations (1)\n• improved energy security (1) — less dependence on imported oil and gas from unstable regions (1)\n• low running costs once built (1), as the 'fuel' (wind, sunlight) is free (1)\n• less air pollution (1), improving health (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 28 — UK electricity generation from wind and solar varies from day to day. On calm, cloudy winter evenings, gas power stations supply much of the demand.\n\nExplain two disadvantages of relying on renewable energy resources such as wind and solar.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each disadvantage (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• intermittent supply (1) — no electricity when the wind does not blow or at night, so back-up or storage is needed (1)\n• high initial costs (1) of building turbines, panels and grid connections (1)\n• large land / sea areas needed (1), which may conflict with farming, tourism or wildlife (1)\n• visual and noise impacts (1) lead to local opposition and planning delays (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 29 — a quotation from an environmental pressure group: "The world must leave most of its remaining fossil fuels in the ground." A quotation from an oil company chief executive: "Oil and gas will be needed for decades to come."\n\nExplain two reasons why different stakeholders have different views about how energy should be used in the future.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• economic interests (1) — oil companies (TNCs) and their shareholders make profits from fossil fuels so want production to continue (1)\n• environmental concern (1) — climate scientists and pressure groups such as Greenpeace see burning fossil fuels as the main cause of climate change (1)\n• governments must balance priorities (1) such as keeping energy affordable for voters and meeting emissions targets (1)\n• consumers (1) may support renewables in principle but want cheap, reliable energy (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 30 — the Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze River, China, has a capacity of about 22,500 MW, making it the world's largest hydro-electric power station. Over one million people were moved to make way for its reservoir.\n\nExplain one benefit and one cost of developing hydro-electric power.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for benefit + 1 development; 1 mark for cost + 1 development, e.g.:\nBenefit: large amounts of renewable electricity (1) reduce the need for coal power stations and so cut air pollution and CO₂ emissions (1); or flood control downstream (1).\nCost: people displaced (1) as towns and farmland are flooded by the reservoir (1); or loss of habitats / cultural sites (1); or sediment trapped behind the dam (1) reduces fertility downstream (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 31 — Country X's energy mix, 2023 (data written for this practice question): oil 38%, natural gas 30% (60% of this gas is imported from one neighbouring country), coal 12%, nuclear 8%, renewables 12%.\n\nSuggest two reasons why Country X may suffer from energy insecurity.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), each using evidence from Figure 31, e.g.:\n• heavy reliance on fossil fuels — 80% of its energy (38 + 30 + 12) (1) — which are finite and affected by global price rises (1)\n• dependence on a single supplier for much of its gas (1) means supply could be cut by conflict or political disputes (1)\n• only 12% from renewables (1), so little secure domestic supply (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 32 — the Deepwater Horizon drilling rig exploded in the Gulf of Mexico in 2010, killing 11 workers and leaking oil for 87 days.\n\nExplain two reasons why oil companies are drilling in deep water and other difficult environments.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each reason (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• easily reached conventional reserves are running out (1), so companies must look further offshore / deeper to keep up production (1)\n• rising demand for oil (1), especially from emerging economies, keeps prices high (1)\n• high oil prices (1) make expensive deep-water extraction profitable (1)\n• new technology (1), e.g. floating platforms and directional drilling, makes extraction possible at greater depths (1)\n• governments want energy security (1), so encourage domestic production (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 33 — in 2022, around 2.3 billion people worldwide still cooked using fuelwood, charcoal, crop waste or kerosene.\n\nExplain two impacts on people of having limited access to modern energy.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each impact (up to 2) and 1 further mark for each development (up to 2), e.g.:\n• health (1) — smoke from indoor cooking fires causes respiratory diseases (1)\n• time (1) — women and children spend hours collecting fuelwood, reducing time for school or paid work (1)\n• education (1) — no electric light means children cannot study after dark (1)\n• economic (1) — businesses cannot use machinery or refrigeration, limiting income (1)\n• environment (1) — fuelwood collection causes deforestation and soil erosion (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 12 — the Alberta tar sands, Canada: oil sands production rose from about 1.6 million barrels per day in 2010 to about 3.3 million barrels per day in 2022 (approximate values; data written for this practice question). The Keystone XL pipeline, planned to carry the oil to the USA, was cancelled in 2021.\n\nEvaluate the costs and benefits of developing unconventional fossil fuels such as tar sands.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Benefits (AO3 using evidence): production more than doubled (+106% from 1.6 to 3.3 million barrels per day), bringing jobs, wages and tax revenue for Alberta and Canada; reduces North American dependence on oil from less stable regions; energy security; supports pipelines and related industries.\n• Costs: open-pit mining destroys boreal forest and peatland; tailings ponds threaten the Athabasca River and First Nations communities; extraction uses large amounts of water and gas; higher greenhouse gas emissions per barrel than conventional oil; land reclamation is slow.\n• Evaluation: benefits are mainly economic and short to medium term; costs are environmental and long term; viability depends on the oil price (costly to extract, so less profitable when price falls); policies such as cancellation of Keystone XL show pressure from climate concerns.\n• A balanced judgement, e.g. benefits outweigh costs economically for Alberta, but globally costs are greater because of climate change.\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding to the issue with limited relevance; a judgement, if made, has little or no supporting evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to make some logical connections; a judgement is made but is only partially supported by evidence and may be unbalanced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to make fully logical and relevant connections; a balanced judgement is reached that is fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 11 — Alaska, USA: oil from Prudhoe Bay has been transported through the Trans-Alaska Pipeline since 1977. In 2023 the US government approved the Willow oil project in the National Petroleum Reserve–Alaska.\n\nAssess the impacts of oil extraction in a named environmentally sensitive area.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (Alaska or another named area, e.g. Alberta tar sands, Niger Delta):\n• Environmental: oil spills (Exxon Valdez 1989, about 41 million litres, Prince William Sound); damage to tundra and permafrost; disruption to caribou migration; air pollution and gas flaring; contribution to climate change.\n• Economic: thousands of jobs; Alaska Permanent Fund pays an annual dividend to residents from oil revenue; state government income; US energy security.\n• Social: impacts on Indigenous communities — some (e.g. Iñupiat) gain income, others (e.g. Gwich'in) fear loss of caribou and culture.\n• Assessment: impacts vary in scale and duration; spills are rare but very damaging; mitigation (raised pipeline, double-hulled tankers) reduces some impacts; judgement on whether economic gains justify environmental risks.\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding to the issue with limited relevance; a judgement, if made, has little or no supporting evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to make some logical connections; a judgement is made but is only partially supported by evidence and may be unbalanced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to make fully logical and relevant connections; a balanced judgement is reached that is fully supported by evidence.\nTo reach Level 3, answers must refer to a named area.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 34 — two approaches to future energy use:\nBusiness as usual: keep using mainly fossil fuels, maximise economic growth and develop new reserves, including unconventional sources.\nSustainable approach: reduce energy use, increase efficiency and switch to renewable energy to cut carbon emissions.\n\nEvaluate the business as usual and sustainable approaches to meeting future energy needs.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Business as usual: cheap, reliable energy with existing infrastructure; supports jobs in fossil fuel industries; fast economic growth, especially for emerging countries; but finite resources, rising extraction costs, price volatility, climate change, air pollution, exploitation of sensitive areas.\n• Sustainable: lower emissions; renewable resources will not run out; improved energy security from domestic supply; new green jobs; but high initial costs, intermittent supply needs storage / back-up, requires behaviour change and government investment.\n• Evaluation: may refer to different country contexts (e.g. Germany's Energiewende vs China's continued coal use alongside large solar growth); timescales; who benefits and who pays.\n• Balanced judgement about which approach is better / a combination.\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding to the issue with limited relevance; a judgement, if made, has little or no supporting evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to make some logical connections; a judgement is made but is only partially supported by evidence and may be unbalanced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to make fully logical and relevant connections; a balanced judgement is reached that is fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 2 — annual average price of Brent crude oil, US$ per barrel (approximate values, rounded; data written for this practice question):\n2012: 112 | 2014: 99 | 2016: 44 | 2018: 71 | 2020: 42 | 2022: 99\n\nAssess the factors that cause the global price of oil to change.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Demand: global economic growth raises demand (e.g. China and India); recessions reduce demand (2008–09); COVID-19 lockdowns in 2020 (price US$42 average).\n• Supply: OPEC decisions to raise or cut production; growth of US shale oil from about 2010 contributed to oversupply and the fall from US$99 (2014) to US$44 (2016).\n• Political: conflict and instability in producing regions (1973 embargo; 2022 Russia–Ukraine war, price back to US$99 average).\n• Other: cost of extraction, technology, switch to renewables and efficiency.\n• Assessment: supply and demand together determine price; political events cause the sharpest short-term spikes; judgement about which factor is most important, supported with data from Figure 2.\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding to the issue with limited relevance; a judgement, if made, has little or no supporting evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to make some logical connections; a judgement is made but is only partially supported by evidence and may be unbalanced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to make fully logical and relevant connections; a balanced judgement is reached that is fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 35 — Germany's 'Energiewende' (energy transition): renewables provided over half of Germany's electricity in 2023; nuclear power was phased out in April 2023; coal and imported gas still provided much of the rest.\n\nEvaluate the success of strategies used by a named developed country to reduce its reliance on fossil fuels.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (Germany, UK or another named developed country):\n• Strategies: subsidies / feed-in tariffs for solar and wind; offshore wind expansion; energy efficiency standards for buildings and appliances; carbon taxes; electric vehicle incentives; public transport.\n• Successes: large rise in renewable share; falling carbon emissions since 1990; new jobs in renewable industries; greater public awareness.\n• Limitations: closing nuclear meant continued use of coal (lignite) and gas; high electricity prices for households; dependence on imported Russian gas exposed in 2022; intermittent supply and slow grid expansion.\n• Judgement on overall success, using evidence.\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding to the issue with limited relevance; a judgement, if made, has little or no supporting evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to make some logical connections; a judgement is made but is only partially supported by evidence and may be unbalanced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to make fully logical and relevant connections; a balanced judgement is reached that is fully supported by evidence.\nTo reach Level 3, answers must refer to a named country.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 36 — China fact file: China is the world's largest consumer of energy and largest emitter of CO₂. Coal still generates over half of its electricity, but China has installed more solar and wind capacity than any other country.\n\nAssess the attitudes towards energy use in two contrasting countries.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (two contrasting countries, e.g. China / India and the USA / UK / Germany):\n• Emerging country, e.g. China: priority on economic growth and lifting people out of poverty, so coal remains important; but pollution and energy security concerns drive massive renewable and nuclear investment; government controls energy planning.\n• Developed country, e.g. USA: high consumption per person; strong fossil fuel lobby and shale boom; attitudes vary between political parties and states (e.g. California's renewable targets); consumer preference for large cars.\n• Or UK / Germany: legally binding net-zero targets; public support for renewables; debates over fracking and nuclear.\n• Assessment: attitudes shaped by development level, resource availability, politics and public opinion; contrasts and similarities; judgement.\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding to the issue with limited relevance; a judgement, if made, has little or no supporting evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to make some logical connections; a judgement is made but is only partially supported by evidence and may be unbalanced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to make fully logical and relevant connections; a balanced judgement is reached that is fully supported by evidence.\nTo reach Level 3, answers must refer to two contrasting named countries.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 21 — Preston New Road, Lancashire: fracking for shale gas was paused in England in 2019 after small earthquakes. In 2022 the ban was briefly lifted, then reinstated. The UK imports around half of the gas it uses.\n\nEvaluate whether the UK should develop its shale gas resources.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• For: improves energy security by reducing imports; jobs and investment; tax revenue; gas emits less CO₂ than coal when burned, so could be a 'bridge' fuel; USA example of lower gas prices.\n• Against: earthquakes and local opposition (Preston New Road); risk of groundwater contamination; lorry traffic, noise; methane leaks; delays the shift to renewables and conflicts with net-zero targets; UK geology complex and reserves uncertain; densely populated country.\n• Evaluation: weighing short-term security against long-term climate goals; different stakeholder views (Cuadrilla, residents, government, environmental groups); judgement.\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding to the issue with limited relevance; a judgement, if made, has little or no supporting evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to make some logical connections; a judgement is made but is only partially supported by evidence and may be unbalanced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to make fully logical and relevant connections; a balanced judgement is reached that is fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\nFigure 31 — Country X's energy mix, 2023 (data written for this practice question): oil 38%, natural gas 30% (60% of this gas is imported from one neighbouring country), coal 12%, nuclear 8%, renewables 12%.\n\nAssess the strategies that Country X could use to improve its energy security.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Diversify suppliers of gas (e.g. liquefied natural gas from several countries) to reduce reliance on one neighbour (60% of gas).\n• Increase renewables from 12% — wind, solar, HEP or geothermal depending on physical geography; reduces imports and emissions but intermittent and costly to build.\n• Nuclear expansion (8% now) — reliable low-carbon baseload but expensive, slow and produces radioactive waste.\n• Develop domestic fossil fuels, including unconventional — improves short-term security but environmental costs.\n• Energy conservation and efficiency — reduces demand, cheap, but needs behaviour change.\n• Storage (gas reserves, batteries) and interconnectors.\n• Assessment: combination needed; short-term vs long-term; costs; judgement on most effective strategy using evidence from Figure 31 (fossil fuels 80% of mix).\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding to the issue with limited relevance; a judgement, if made, has little or no supporting evidence.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to make some logical connections; a judgement is made but is only partially supported by evidence and may be unbalanced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to make fully logical and relevant connections; a balanced judgement is reached that is fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section D — Making a Geographical Decision. Resource Booklet — the issue: meeting Country Y's future energy needs (data written for this practice question). Country Y is an emerging country of 60 million people; its energy demand is forecast to rise by 40% by 2040. It currently gets 70% of its electricity from imported coal and oil. It has a long, windy coastline, high sunshine totals inland and a large undeveloped gas field beneath a coastal wetland that is a national park.\nThree options:\nOption 1 — develop the gas field beneath the coastal wetland, with strict environmental regulations.\nOption 2 — invest in large-scale wind and solar power, with battery storage and an improved national grid.\nOption 3 — a national energy-efficiency programme: insulate homes, improve public transport and set efficiency standards for industry and appliances.\n\nSelect the option you think would be best for Country Y's future energy needs. Justify your choice. Use information from the Resource Booklet and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer. (12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Select', markScheme: `Indicative content — no option is correct; credit any well-justified choice.\n• Option 1: domestic supply improves energy security and reduces imports; gas is lower-carbon than coal; jobs and revenue; but damages a protected wetland ecosystem, risk of spills / leaks, still a finite fossil fuel, conflicts with climate targets; compare Alaska / tar sands impacts.\n• Option 2: uses natural advantages (windy coast, high sunshine); renewable and low-carbon; reduces dependence on imports; new jobs; but high initial costs, intermittency needs storage and grid investment, land use and visual impacts; may not meet a 40% demand rise quickly.\n• Option 3: reduces the growth in demand cheaply; cuts emissions and household bills; but does not create new supply; depends on behaviour change; benefits slow to appear.\n• Good answers compare options, recognise trade-offs (economic, social, environmental; short and long term), consider different stakeholders, and use evidence from the booklet (60 million people, 40% rise, 70% imported coal and oil).\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies understanding to the resources with limited relevance. Makes an unsupported or generic choice with little use of evidence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies understanding to make some links with the resources. Justification is partial, with limited evidence; other options may be ignored.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding that is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies understanding to make logical connections with the resources. Justification uses evidence from the booklet and wider course; some consideration of the other options.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies understanding to make fully logical connections between the resources and the decision. A clear, balanced justification fully supported by evidence, recognising the strengths and weaknesses of the chosen option and why it is preferred to the others.\n\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4 marks): spelling and punctuation with consistent accuracy; rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used adeptly.\nIntermediate performance (2–3 marks): spelling and punctuation with considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spelling and punctuation with reasonable accuracy; some control of meaning, errors do not significantly hinder meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\n0 marks: no rewardable SPaG, or response does not relate to the question.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.4': {
    name: 'Making Geographical Decisions',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 1: a map of South-East Asia. Borneo is shown between the South China Sea to the north and the Java Sea to the south. A line of latitude labelled 0° crosses the middle of the island, passing through the city of Pontianak on the west coast of Kalimantan.\n\nStudy Figure 1. Identify the line of latitude that passes through Pontianak.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `The Equator (1)\nAllow 0° / 0 degrees latitude.\nDo not credit Tropic of Cancer / Tropic of Capricorn.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 2 lists some of the components of a tropical rainforest ecosystem in Borneo: hot, humid air; orangutans; strangler figs; heavy rainfall; latosol soil; leaf-cutter ants.\n\nWhich one of the following is a biotic component of the Borneo rainforest ecosystem?\n\nA  heavy rainfall\nB  latosol soil\nC  strangler figs\nD  hot, humid air`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `C (strangler figs) (1)\nDo not credit more than one answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 3: climate data for Pontianak, Kalimantan (data written for this practice question). Mean monthly rainfall (mm): Jan 270, Feb 210, Mar 240, Apr 280, May 270, Jun 220, Jul 160, Aug 190, Sep 230, Oct 360, Nov 390, Dec 320. Mean monthly temperature stays between 26 °C and 28 °C all year.\n\nUsing Figure 3, calculate the range of mean monthly rainfall in Pontianak.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `230 mm (1)\n(390 − 160 = 230)\nUnits not required. Do not credit '160–390'.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 4: area of oil palm plantation in Kalimantan (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | Area (million hectares)\n2000 | 1.2\n2005 | 2.1\n2010 | 3.8\n2015 | 5.6\n2020 | 6.9\n\nStudy Figure 4. Identify the first year shown in which the area of oil palm plantation was more than 5 million hectares.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `2015 (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 5: sources of Kenya's electricity generation, 2022 (data written for this practice question). Geothermal 45%; hydroelectric 22%; wind 17%; oil-fired thermal 11%; solar 5%.\n\nStudy Figure 5. Identify the energy source that generated the largest share of Kenya's electricity.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Geothermal (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 6 states that oil sands are an unconventional fossil fuel because the bitumen is too thick to flow and must be mined or heated before it can be used.\n\nUsing your own knowledge, name one unconventional fossil fuel source other than oil sands.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Any one from:\n• shale gas (1)\n• shale oil / tight oil / oil shale (1)\n• coal bed methane (1)\n• deep-water / ultra-deep offshore oil (1)\n• Arctic oil (1)\n• methane hydrates (1)\nDo not credit oil sands / tar sands (given) or conventional oil / coal / natural gas.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 7: forest cover in Kalimantan (data written for this practice question). 1990: 34.0 million hectares; 2000: 31.2 million hectares; 2010: 28.6 million hectares; 2020: 26.1 million hectares.\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) decrease in forest cover in Kalimantan between 1990 and 2020. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `34.0 − 26.1 = 7.9; 7.9 ÷ 34.0 × 100 (1)\n= 23.2% (1)\nAward 2 marks for 23.2 with or without working.\nAward 1 mark for 23.235… / 23 / 23.24 (incorrect rounding) or for correct method with an arithmetic slip.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 8: percentage of households with an electricity connection in six Kenyan counties, 2022 (data written for this practice question).\n\nCounty | Households with electricity (%)\nNairobi | 94\nMombasa | 88\nNakuru | 70\nKisumu | 62\nTurkana | 15\nMandera | 11\n\nCalculate the mean percentage of households with electricity for the six counties. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `94 + 88 + 70 + 62 + 15 + 11 = 340; 340 ÷ 6 (1)\n= 56.7% (1)\nAward 1 mark for 56.6 / 57 / 56.67 (incorrect rounding) or for correct method with an arithmetic slip.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 9: electricity consumption per person, 2021 (data written for this practice question). Kenya: 170 kWh per year; United Kingdom: 4,250 kWh per year.\n\nCalculate the ratio of electricity consumption per person between Kenya and the United Kingdom. Give your answer in the form 1:n. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `4,250 ÷ 170 (1)\n= 1:25 (1)\nAward 1 mark for 25 or 25:1 or 1/25 without the ratio in the required form.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 10: oil production from the Alberta oil sands (data written for this practice question). 2000: 0.6 million barrels per day; 2005: 1.1; 2010: 1.6; 2015: 2.5; 2020: 3.0.\n\nCalculate the percentage (%) increase in oil sands production between 2010 and 2020. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3.0 − 1.6 = 1.4; 1.4 ÷ 1.6 × 100 (1)\n= 87.5% (1)\nAward 1 mark for correct method with an arithmetic slip, or for 187.5% (3.0 ÷ 1.6 × 100).` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 4 is a table of the area of oil palm plantation in Kalimantan (data written for this practice question): 2000, 1.2 million hectares; 2005, 2.1; 2010, 3.8; 2015, 5.6; 2020, 6.9.\n\nThe data in Figure 4 is shown in a table. Describe one other suitable method of data presentation to show the change in the area of oil palm plantation over time.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a suitable method and 1 mark for a description of how it would be drawn / why it suits the data:\n• line graph (1) with year on the x-axis and area on the y-axis, the points joined to show the trend over time (1)\n• bar chart (1) with one bar for each year, bar height showing the area in million hectares (1)\n• proportional symbols on a map of Kalimantan (1) with circle size scaled to the area for each year (1)\nDo not credit pie chart (data is not parts of a whole).` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 11: a nutrient cycle diagram for the Borneo rainforest. The biomass store is drawn as a very large circle, the litter store as a very small circle and the soil store as a small circle. Thick arrows show nutrients moving quickly from litter to soil and from soil to biomass.\n\nUsing your own knowledge, explain one reason why most of the nutrients in the Borneo rainforest are stored in the biomass.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• The hot, wet climate means dead leaves decompose very quickly (1) so nutrients pass rapidly through the litter and soil and are taken up again by plants (1)\n• Plants grow all year and have dense shallow root systems (1) so they absorb nutrients from the soil almost as soon as they are released (1)\n• Heavy rainfall leaches nutrients from the soil (1) so little is left stored in the latosol, leaving the vegetation as the main store (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 12: much of the lowland of southern and western Kalimantan is peat swamp forest. Peat up to 20 m deep has built up over thousands of years from waterlogged, partly decayed plant material (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain one ecosystem service provided by Borneo's peat swamp forests.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for an ecosystem service and 1 mark for development:\n• Carbon storage (1) because waterlogged peat stops dead plant material decaying, locking carbon away rather than releasing it as CO₂ (1)\n• Water regulation / flood control (1) as peat soaks up heavy rainfall like a sponge and releases it slowly to rivers (1)\n• Habitat / supporting biodiversity (1) such as orangutans, which depend on the forest for food and nesting (1)\n• Provision of fish / clean water for local communities (1) because the swamps filter water flowing to rivers (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 3 shows that Pontianak, on the Equator, receives over 150 mm of rainfall in every month of the year and has mean temperatures of 26–28 °C (data written for this practice question).\n\nSuggest one reason why Pontianak has high rainfall throughout the year.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• The Sun is high in the sky all year so the ground heats strongly (1) causing warm, moist air to rise, cool and condense, giving daily convectional rainfall (1)\n• It lies close to the ITCZ / low pressure belt at the Equator (1) where trade winds converge and air rises, forming cumulonimbus clouds (1)\n• Evapotranspiration from the dense rainforest adds moisture to the air (1) which is recycled as rain (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 13: Kenya fact file (data written for this practice question). Population: 54 million in 2022, growing at 2.0% a year. Urban population: 29% of the total, growing faster than the rural population. GDP growth: about 5% a year. Electricity demand has grown faster than the population since 2010.\n\nUsing evidence from Figure 13, suggest one reason why Kenya's demand for electricity is increasing.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason linked to Figure 13 and 1 mark for development:\n• Population growth of 2.0% a year (1) means more households and businesses need electricity (1)\n• Rapid urbanisation (1) as people moving to cities such as Nairobi tend to use more electric appliances, lighting and transport (1)\n• Economic growth of about 5% a year (1) increases industry and incomes, so people can afford more electrical goods (1)\nMax 1 mark if no reference to the evidence in Figure 13.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 14: a photograph of a rural area near the Mau Forest, Kenya. Women are carrying bundles of firewood along a track. Tree stumps can be seen on a hillside in the background, where patches of soil are bare.\n\nExplain one environmental impact of households using fuelwood for cooking.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for an impact and 1 mark for development:\n• Deforestation (1) as trees are cut faster than they can regrow, reducing habitat and biodiversity (1)\n• Soil erosion (1) because without tree roots and canopy cover, heavy rain washes away bare soil (1)\n• Air pollution / greenhouse gas emissions (1) as burning wood releases CO₂ and smoke, contributing to climate change (1)\n• Reduced water supply to rivers (1) as the forest catchment (e.g. the Mau Forest 'water tower') stores and releases less water (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 15: Olkaria geothermal power station lies in Hell's Gate National Park in the Great Rift Valley, near Lake Naivasha. Steam vents and hot springs are found in the area.\n\nExplain one reason why the Rift Valley is a suitable location for geothermal power.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 mark for development:\n• It lies on a divergent / constructive plate boundary where the crust is thin (1) so hot rock / magma is close to the surface and can heat water into steam (1)\n• There is volcanic activity with hot springs and steam vents (1) showing high-temperature water that can be piped to turn turbines (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 16: facts about the boreal forest in northern Alberta. Winters last up to eight months, with temperatures below −30 °C; the growing season is about 50–100 days. Trees are mainly a few species of conifer. Soils are thin, acidic podzols, and in places the ground is permanently frozen.\n\nUsing evidence from Figure 16, explain one reason why the taiga recovers slowly after it has been disturbed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason linked to Figure 16 and 1 mark for development:\n• The short growing season / cold temperatures (1) mean trees grow very slowly, so it can take decades to over a century to regrow (1)\n• Thin, acidic, nutrient-poor podzols (1) because needle litter decomposes slowly, so few nutrients are available for new growth (1)\n• Low biodiversity (1) means fewer species are available to recolonise cleared land (1)\n• Permafrost (1) can melt when vegetation is removed, causing subsidence and waterlogging that prevents regrowth (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 17: an aerial photograph of an open-pit oil sands mine next to the Athabasca River. The forest has been stripped from a large area. Large ponds of grey liquid waste, called tailings ponds, lie close to the river bank.\n\nUsing Figure 17, explain one way in which surface mining of oil sands could affect the Athabasca River.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for an impact and 1 mark for development:\n• Toxic substances could leak or seep from tailings ponds (1) polluting the river and harming fish and the people downstream who eat them (1)\n• Large volumes of water are taken from the river to separate bitumen (1) reducing river flow, especially in winter (1)\n• Removing forest and soil increases surface runoff (1) carrying more sediment into the river (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 18: Lake Turkana Wind Power in Marsabit County, northern Kenya, has 365 turbines with a capacity of 310 MW. It is in a remote, dry area with strong, steady winds, a long way from Nairobi, where most electricity is used.\n\nExplain one disadvantage of locating a large wind farm in a remote area such as Marsabit County.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a disadvantage and 1 mark for development:\n• A long transmission line is needed to carry electricity to Nairobi (1) which is expensive to build and some energy is lost over the distance (1)\n• Delays in building the power line meant electricity could not be used at first (1) wasting the investment (1)\n• Local pastoral communities may lose access to grazing land (1) causing conflict over land rights (1)\n• Few local people may benefit (1) because they are not connected to the grid / jobs go to outside workers (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 4: area of oil palm plantation in Kalimantan (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | Area (million hectares)\n2000 | 1.2\n2005 | 2.1\n2010 | 3.8\n2015 | 5.6\n2020 | 6.9\n\nDescribe the trend shown in Figure 4. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the overall trend, 1 mark for a further descriptive point, 1 mark for use of data:\n• The area of oil palm plantation increased throughout / every year shown (1)\n• The fastest increase was between 2010 and 2015 (+1.8 million ha); allow 2005 to 2010 (+1.7 million ha) (1)\n• The rate of increase slowed between 2015 and 2020 (+1.3 million ha) (1)\n• Data use: rose from 1.2 to 6.9 million hectares / increased by 5.7 million hectares / more than five times larger (1)\nMax 2 marks without data.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 19: an incomplete pie chart of the main cooking fuel used by households in Kenya (data written for this practice question). It shows LPG (bottled gas) 24%, kerosene 6% and other 5%; the remaining 65% of the circle is blank.\n\nComplete Figure 19 using this information: fuelwood 50%, charcoal 15%. Describe how you would divide and label the blank 65% of the circle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Convert percentages to degrees by multiplying by 3.6: fuelwood 50% = 180°, charcoal 15% = 54° (1)\n• Use a protractor from the edge of the last segment to measure each sector; fuelwood should take up half the circle (1)\n• Label or shade each sector and add it to the key, using a different shading from the existing sectors (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 20: a scatter graph of GDP per person (US$) against energy use per person (GJ per year) for eight countries (data written for this practice question). Kenya: US$2,100, 6 GJ; Ethiopia: US$1,000, 3 GJ; Ghana: US$2,400, 9 GJ; India: US$2,400, 25 GJ; Brazil: US$9,000, 60 GJ; China: US$12,500, 110 GJ; UK: US$46,000, 110 GJ; USA: US$70,000, 280 GJ.\n\nDescribe the relationship shown in Figure 20. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Positive relationship / as GDP per person increases, energy use per person increases (1)\n• Data use, e.g. Ethiopia US$1,000 uses 3 GJ while the USA at US$70,000 uses 280 GJ (1)\n• Anomaly / not a perfect relationship: the UK and China use the same energy (110 GJ) despite the UK's GDP per person being almost four times higher (1)\n• Countries with GDP under US$3,000 all use less than 30 GJ per person (1)\nMax 2 marks without data.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 21: global production of palm oil rose from about 15 million tonnes in 1995 to over 70 million tonnes in 2020 (data written for this practice question). Palm oil is used in foods such as margarine and biscuits, in soap and cosmetics, and as a biofuel.\n\nExplain one reason why global demand for palm oil has increased.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and up to 2 further marks for development:\n• Rising populations and incomes in emerging countries such as China and India (1) mean more people buy processed food (1) which commonly contains palm oil because it is cheap and stable at room temperature (1)\n• Demand for biofuels has grown (1) because countries want to cut use of fossil fuels / meet renewable energy targets (1) and palm oil can be made into biodiesel (1)\n• Oil palm gives a higher yield per hectare than other vegetable oil crops (1) so it is the cheapest vegetable oil (1) and manufacturers switched to it from other oils / trans fats (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 22: the Dayak peoples are indigenous to Borneo. Many live in longhouse villages along rivers and practise shifting cultivation of rice, collect rattan, wild fruit and honey, hunt, and fish in forest rivers.\n\nExplain two ways in which indigenous people in Borneo depend on the rainforest.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each way (1 mark for the way, 1 for development), 2 × (1+1):\n• Food (1) from hunting wild pigs, fishing in forest rivers and gathering fruit and honey, which provides a balanced diet without needing money (1)\n• Building materials (1) such as timber and rattan used for longhouses, boats and baskets (1)\n• Income (1) from selling forest products such as rattan or honey, or from ecotourism (1)\n• Farmland (1) via shifting cultivation, where small plots are cleared and left to regrow so soil fertility recovers (1)\n• Medicines (1) from forest plants used in traditional healing (1)\n• Cultural / spiritual identity (1) as ancestral land and burial sites are in the forest (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 23: estimated number of Bornean orangutans (data written for this practice question): 1999, 230,000; 2015, 100,000. In 2015, El Niño caused a long dry season and fires burned over 2 million hectares of drained peatland in Kalimantan, covering South-East Asia in haze.\n\nUsing evidence from Figure 23, explain two threats to biodiversity in Borneo's rainforest.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1+1); max 3 marks if no reference to Figure 23:\n• Fires (1) on drained peatland destroy habitat and kill animals that cannot escape, e.g. 2 million hectares burned in 2015 (1)\n• Climate change / El Niño drought (1) makes the forest drier and more likely to burn, and stresses species adapted to constant rainfall (1)\n• Clearing for oil palm plantations (1) replaces a forest with thousands of species with a monoculture, so orangutans lose food and nest sites; numbers fell from 230,000 to 100,000 (1)\n• Logging / roads (1) fragment the forest, cutting off populations so they cannot breed and become more vulnerable (1)\n• Hunting / illegal pet trade (1) becomes easier as roads open up the forest (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 12: peat swamp forest covers large areas of lowland Kalimantan. To plant oil palm, the peat is drained with canals and the forest is often cleared by burning.\n\nExplain two ways in which deforestation in Borneo could contribute to climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1+1):\n• Burning forest releases carbon dioxide (1) adding to the enhanced greenhouse effect and global warming (1)\n• Draining peat allows it to dry out and decompose / oxidise (1) releasing thousands of years of stored carbon as CO₂ (1)\n• Fewer trees means less photosynthesis (1) so less CO₂ is absorbed from the atmosphere – the carbon sink is reduced (1)\n• Reduced evapotranspiration (1) alters the water cycle, leading to drier local conditions and more fires, releasing more carbon (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 5: in 2022, geothermal power produced about 45% of Kenya's electricity (data written for this practice question). Kenya imports almost all of its oil. The government plans to expand the Olkaria geothermal fields in the Rift Valley.\n\nExplain two ways in which Kenya's economy might benefit from expanding geothermal power.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1+1):\n• Less money spent on imported oil (1) improving the balance of trade and energy security (1)\n• Geothermal gives reliable 24-hour baseload power unlike hydro in droughts (1) so factories suffer fewer power cuts and productivity rises (1)\n• Cheaper electricity in the long term (1) once built, running costs are low, attracting foreign investment / TNCs (1)\n• Creates jobs (1) in construction, engineering and maintenance at Olkaria, boosting the multiplier effect (1)\n• Surplus steam / heat can be used directly (1) e.g. for flower-growing greenhouses near Lake Naivasha, supporting exports (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 24: views about Lake Turkana Wind Power (data written for this practice question).\nGovernment energy official: "The wind farm adds 310 MW of clean power and cuts our need for oil-fired power stations."\nLocal herder: "Our grazing land was fenced off and few of us have electricity in our homes."\nInvestor: "Strong, steady winds make this one of the best wind sites in Africa."\nConservation group: "The turbines lie on a migration route for birds."\n\nUsing Figure 24, explain two reasons why stakeholders hold different views about the Lake Turkana wind farm.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1+1); max 3 marks without reference to Figure 24:\n• Stakeholders gain different benefits (1) e.g. the government gains clean energy and energy security, while the herder loses grazing land (1)\n• Benefits and costs happen at different scales / places (1) national grid users in Nairobi benefit, but local people bear the costs and are not connected (1)\n• Different priorities / values (1) the investor focuses on profit from strong winds, whereas the conservation group values wildlife such as migrating birds (1)\n• Different levels of power in decision-making (1) local herders may not have been consulted, unlike the government and investors (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 8: percentage of households with an electricity connection, 2022 (data written for this practice question). Nairobi (the capital city): 94%. Turkana (a large, remote, dry county in the north-west with a low population density): 15%.\n\nUsing Figure 8 and your own knowledge, explain two reasons why access to electricity varies between Nairobi and Turkana.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1+1):\n• Turkana is remote and sparsely populated (1) so building grid power lines to scattered homes is very expensive per person connected (1)\n• Higher incomes in Nairobi (1) mean more households can afford connection fees and electricity bills (1)\n• Nairobi is the political and economic centre (1) so investment in infrastructure has been concentrated there (1)\n• Many Turkana households are nomadic pastoralists (1) who move with their herds, so a fixed grid connection is less useful (1)\nAllow references to the 79-percentage-point difference as development.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 25: Fort Chipewyan is a community of about 1,000 people, mostly First Nations (Mikisew Cree and Athabasca Chipewyan) and Métis, on Lake Athabasca, more than 150 km downstream of the oil sands mines. Many residents traditionally hunt, trap and fish.\n\nExplain two ways in which oil sands development may affect indigenous communities such as Fort Chipewyan.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1+1); credit positive and negative effects:\n• Pollution of the Athabasca River / lake (1) may contaminate fish that people eat, causing health concerns (1)\n• Loss of forest and wetland habitat (1) reduces caribou, moose and other animals, threatening traditional hunting and trapping and culture (1)\n• Jobs and income (1) some indigenous people work in, or run businesses supplying, the oil sands industry, raising incomes (1)\n• Lower water levels in the delta (1) make it harder to travel by boat to traditional hunting grounds (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 26: Wood Buffalo National Park, downstream of the oil sands, is Canada's largest national park and a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Mining companies must restore mined land to forest, and some Alberta timber companies have their forests certified by the Forest Stewardship Council (FSC).\n\nExplain two ways in which the taiga in Alberta can be managed more sustainably.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1+1):\n• Protected areas / national parks such as Wood Buffalo (1) ban mining and logging so habitats for species such as wood bison are conserved (1)\n• Land reclamation after mining (1) replacing soil and replanting native trees so the forest ecosystem can eventually recover (1)\n• FSC certification / selective logging (1) means only some trees are felled and replanting is required, so timber can be harvested without permanent loss (1)\n• Stricter rules on tailings ponds and water use (1) reduce pollution reaching rivers and the delta (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 27: Canada fact file (data written for this practice question). The oil and gas sector provides about 5% of Canada's GDP. Most oil sands crude is exported by pipeline to the USA. Producing oil from oil sands releases more greenhouse gas per barrel than most conventional oil.\n\nExplain one cost and one benefit for Canada of developing its oil sands.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1+1 for a cost, 1+1 for a benefit:\nBenefit:\n• Export earnings (1) from selling oil to the USA, boosting GDP and government tax revenue (1)\n• Energy security (1) Canada does not need to import oil, so is protected from global supply problems (1)\n• Jobs (1) in mining, engineering and transport, with a multiplier effect in towns such as Fort McMurray (1)\nCost:\n• High greenhouse gas emissions (1) as heating / steam extraction uses large amounts of natural gas, making climate targets harder to meet (1)\n• Environmental damage (1) clearing boreal forest and peat bogs and creating tailings ponds (1)\n• Reliance on one export market / oil price (1) a fall in global oil prices can cause job losses (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 28: Indonesia fact file (data written for this practice question). Population: 275 million. Indonesia is the world's largest producer of palm oil, which earns about US$25 billion a year in exports. About 16 million people work in the palm oil industry, including many smallholder farmers.\n\nUsing evidence from Figure 28, explain two reasons why Indonesia's government has supported the expansion of oil palm plantations.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × (1+1); max 3 marks without reference to Figure 28:\n• Export earnings (1) of about US$25 billion a year help pay for development / reduce debt (1)\n• Employment (1) about 16 million jobs give incomes to rural people and reduce poverty (1)\n• Smallholders can earn more than from subsistence farming (1) helping to slow rural–urban migration to Jakarta (1)\n• Palm oil can be used as a biofuel (1) reducing Indonesia's need to import oil for its large population (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — People and the Biosphere.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 29: Borneo's rainforest provides timber, rattan, fish and medicinal plants for local people; it regulates the water supply to rivers; its peat stores very large amounts of carbon; and it is a habitat for about 15,000 plant species, many found nowhere else (data written for this practice question).\n\nAssess the importance of Borneo's rainforest for people at local and global scales.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2 4, AO3 4):\nLocal: food, building materials, rattan and medicines for Dayak communities; income from forest products / ecotourism; water regulation and flood control for river communities; soil protection; cultural value.\nGlobal: carbon storage in biomass and peat helps regulate climate; biodiversity – genetic resources for medicines and crops; source of globally traded commodities (timber, palm oil from cleared land); influence on regional rainfall.\nAssessment: local importance is direct and immediate for survival; global importance (carbon storage) may be greater in scale, especially for climate change; the two are linked – loss of local services through fire also releases global carbon; importance varies depending on stakeholder.\nCredit judgements on which scale is more important if supported.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide a logical, well-supported assessment of the issue; uses the resources and own knowledge in a balanced way to reach a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies understanding to provide a partially developed assessment; uses some evidence from the resources and/or own knowledge; judgement is partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited application; judgement, if present, is unsupported or generic.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: the future of Borneo's rainforest. Borneo, the world's third-largest island, lies on the Equator in South-East Asia and is shared by Indonesia (whose part is called Kalimantan), Malaysia and Brunei. Much of the island was once covered in tropical rainforest and peat swamp forest, home to the Bornean orangutan. Since the 1970s large areas have been logged, drained and cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nFigure 30: causes of forest loss in Kalimantan, 2000–2020 (data written for this practice question). Industrial oil palm plantations 40%; smallholder farming 20%; logging 15%; fires 15%; mining and roads 10%.\n\nEvaluate the threats to the rainforest in Borneo.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2 4, AO3 4):\n• Commercial oil palm plantations are the largest single cause (40%) – driven by global demand for food, cosmetics and biofuel; permanent conversion to monoculture.\n• Smallholder farming (20%) – population growth and poverty; often smaller scale but widespread.\n• Logging (15%) – selective but opens up the forest with roads, making further clearance easier.\n• Fires (15%) – linked to drained peat and El Niño droughts; climate change may increase these; huge carbon release.\n• Mining and roads (10%) – coal and gold; new capital city projects in East Kalimantan.\n• Threats are interlinked: logging roads → access for farmers and plantations; draining → fire.\nJudgement: e.g. oil palm is the most serious because it is permanent and largest, or fires may be the most serious in the future because climate change worsens them.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide a logical, well-supported assessment of the issue; uses the resources and own knowledge in a balanced way to reach a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies understanding to provide a partially developed assessment; uses some evidence from the resources and/or own knowledge; judgement is partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited application; judgement, if present, is unsupported or generic.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: meeting Kenya's growing energy demand. Kenya is an East African country crossed by the Equator and the Great Rift Valley. Its population and economy are growing quickly and the government's Vision 2030 plan aims for every household to have electricity. Most electricity is generated from renewable sources, such as geothermal power at Olkaria and hydroelectricity, but many rural households still cook with fuelwood and charcoal.\n\nFigure 31: Kenya energy fact file (data written for this practice question). About 90% of electricity is renewable (geothermal, hydro, wind, solar). Only about 75% of households have an electricity connection. Hydro output falls during droughts. Kenya imports almost all its oil for transport. About 70% of households still cook with fuelwood or charcoal.\n\nAssess the extent to which renewable energy can meet Kenya's growing energy demand.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2 4, AO3 4):\nFor: large geothermal potential in the Rift Valley gives reliable baseload; strong winds at Lake Turkana; high solar radiation near the Equator – off-grid solar home systems can reach remote counties such as Turkana; already ~90% renewable electricity; reduces costly oil imports and emissions.\nAgainst: hydro unreliable in droughts (climate change may worsen); wind and solar intermittent without storage; high capital costs and need for long transmission lines; transport depends on imported oil; cooking energy largely fuelwood – a renewable resource but used unsustainably; demand growing with population (2% a year) and urbanisation.\nJudgement: renewables can meet most electricity demand but less of total energy demand (transport, cooking) without investment and changes in technology.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide a logical, well-supported assessment of the issue; uses the resources and own knowledge in a balanced way to reach a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies understanding to provide a partially developed assessment; uses some evidence from the resources and/or own knowledge; judgement is partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited application; judgement, if present, is unsupported or generic.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section C — Consuming Energy Resources.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 10: oil sands production rose from 0.6 to 3.0 million barrels per day between 2000 and 2020 (data written for this practice question). Figure 17: open-pit mines and tailings ponds lie next to the Athabasca River. Figure 27: oil and gas provide about 5% of Canada's GDP; oil sands crude releases more greenhouse gas per barrel than most conventional oil.\n\nEvaluate the costs and benefits of developing Alberta's oil sands.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2 4, AO3 4):\nBenefits: export income and tax revenue; tens of thousands of jobs and growth of Fort McMurray; energy security for Canada and North America; large reserves give long-term supply; some jobs and business contracts for First Nations.\nCosts: destruction of boreal forest and peat bogs, loss of caribou habitat; slow recovery of taiga makes reclamation difficult; tailings ponds pollute water and harm birds; large water withdrawals from the Athabasca; high greenhouse gas emissions per barrel; health and cultural concerns downstream at Fort Chipewyan; dependence on oil price.\nEvaluation: benefits are mainly economic and national/short-term; costs are environmental, local and global/long-term; balance depends on stakeholder and timescale; judgement should be supported, e.g. costs outweigh benefits given climate targets, or benefits outweigh costs if strict reclamation is enforced.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide a logical, well-supported assessment of the issue; uses the resources and own knowledge in a balanced way to reach a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies understanding to provide a partially developed assessment; uses some evidence from the resources and/or own knowledge; judgement is partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited application; judgement, if present, is unsupported or generic.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section B — Forests under Threat.\n\nResource Booklet — the issue: oil sands and the boreal forest of Alberta, Canada. Northern Alberta is covered by boreal forest (taiga) of spruce, pine and aspen with large peat bogs. Beneath it lie the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil reserves in the world. Bitumen near the surface is dug out by open-pit mining; deeper deposits are extracted by pumping steam underground. The Athabasca River flows north from the mining area past the community of Fort Chipewyan into the Peace-Athabasca Delta in Wood Buffalo National Park.\n\nFigure 26: Wood Buffalo National Park protects 44,800 km² of boreal forest and wetland. Mining companies must reclaim land after mining, but only a small proportion of the disturbed land has been certified as reclaimed (data written for this practice question). Some logging companies use FSC certification.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of strategies used to protect the taiga in Alberta.`, marks: 8, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2 4, AO3 4):\n• National parks / protected areas: effective at stopping logging and mining within the park; but pollution and reduced flows from upstream (oil sands, dams) still affect the Peace-Athabasca Delta, so protection is incomplete.\n• Land reclamation: legally required, and some land has been replanted; but only a small proportion certified as reclaimed; taiga regrows slowly due to the short growing season and poor podzol soils; peat bogs very hard to recreate.\n• FSC certification / sustainable forestry: replanting and selective felling maintain forest cover; relies on consumer demand and voluntary participation.\n• International agreements on climate change can reduce demand for oil sands in the long term, but are hard to enforce.\nJudgement: e.g. protected areas most effective for preserving the ecosystem, while reclamation is least effective because of slow recovery.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies understanding to deconstruct information and provide a logical, well-supported assessment of the issue; uses the resources and own knowledge in a balanced way to reach a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies understanding to provide a partially developed assessment; uses some evidence from the resources and/or own knowledge; judgement is partially supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited application; judgement, if present, is unsupported or generic.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section D — Making a Geographical Decision.\n\nUse all the information about Borneo given in the Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question). Kalimantan's forest cover fell from 34.0 to 26.1 million hectares between 1990 and 2020, while oil palm plantations grew from 1.2 to 6.9 million hectares between 2000 and 2020. Palm oil earns Indonesia about US$25 billion a year and employs about 16 million people. Bornean orangutan numbers have more than halved since 1999, and peat fires in 2015 burned over 2 million hectares.\n\nThree options for the future of Borneo's rainforest:\nOption 1 — Enforce a complete ban on clearing any remaining forest or peatland for new plantations, with satellite monitoring and fines.\nOption 2 — Expand protected areas, ecotourism and international carbon payments (REDD+), so that standing forest earns money for local communities.\nOption 3 — Help smallholder farmers increase yields on land that has already been cleared, using certified sustainable palm oil (RSPO) and agroforestry, so no new forest needs to be cut.\n\nSelect the option that you think would be the best way to secure a sustainable future for Borneo's rainforest. Justify your choice.\n\nUse information from the Resource Booklet and knowledge and understanding from the rest of your geography course to support your answer.\n\n(12 marks + 4 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 12, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO2 4, AO3 8). No option is better than another; credit is for the quality of justification.\nOption 1: protects remaining carbon stores and orangutan habitat immediately; prevents further peat drainage and fires; builds on Indonesia's moratorium on new licences in primary forest and peatland. But risks loss of jobs and export income; hard to enforce over remote areas – corruption and illegal clearance; smallholders may suffer; may push clearance to other countries.\nOption 2: gives economic value to standing forest; jobs for local and Dayak communities as guides; international funding (REDD+) links local and global scales; supports biodiversity. But tourism numbers are limited and seasonal; carbon payments depend on rich countries' commitment; benefits may not reach the poorest; protected areas can exclude indigenous people.\nOption 3: maintains palm oil income and 16 million jobs while reducing need for new land; RSPO certification can win access to markets such as the EU; agroforestry improves soils and biodiversity. But certification costs are high for smallholders; higher yields may make expansion more profitable; does not restore lost forest; does not on its own prevent fires.\nCredit: use of data from the booklet (e.g. 23.2% loss of forest cover); links to own knowledge from People and the Biosphere, Forests under Threat and Consuming Energy Resources (e.g. biofuel demand, carbon cycle, ecosystem services); consideration of different stakeholders, scales and timescales; explanation of why the other options were rejected.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Applies knowledge and understanding to find fully logical and relevant connections between a range of information from the resources and own knowledge. Evaluates the chosen option and the rejected options in a balanced way, considering costs and benefits for different stakeholders and timescales, and reaches a fully justified, substantiated decision.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Finds mostly logical and relevant connections between information. Makes a reasoned evaluation of the chosen option, with some reference to the other options; decision is mostly supported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Finds some relevant connections; partially developed evaluation, mainly of the chosen option; decision is partially justified with some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of understanding; limited or generic reasons for the choice with little use of the resources.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.\nMax top of Level 2 if there is no reference to the information in the Resource Booklet. Max top of Level 3 if the other options are not considered.\nSPaG (4 marks):\nHigh performance (4 marks): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate performance (2–3 marks): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; uses a good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning; uses a limited range of specialist terms.\n0 marks: no answer, or the answer does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` }
    ]
  }
};
